/* ============================================================
   ECG & CVRN Review Course
   PRACTICE AND RECALL DECK

   500 items. This is the deck the study sessions and the
   spaced recall queue draw from. It is deliberately separate from
   the reserved exam pool and from both diagnostic pools, so a
   practice exam score stays an estimate rather than a measure of
   how many answers a learner has already seen.

   Rule: an item lives in exactly one pool. Nothing here appears
   in cvrn-exam-bank.js, cvrn-diagnostic-bank.js, or
   cvrn-practical-bank.js. That is checked on every build.

   Blueprint proportional across all fourteen domains.
   Fields: id, d (domain), c (competency), dok, q, o, a, why.
   ============================================================ */
window.CVRN_PRACTICE_POOL = [
{id:"P4041",d:"D1",c:"D1.C1",dok:1,q:"Which term describes chest discomfort that is classically described as pressure, squeezing, or tightness rather than sharp or stabbing pain?",
 o:["Anginal-type chest pain","Pleuritic chest pain","Musculoskeletal chest pain","Neuropathic chest pain"],a:0,
 why:"Anginal-type chest pain classically presents as pressure, squeezing, tightness, or heaviness rather than a sharp sensation, reflecting the visceral nature of cardiac ischemic pain. Pleuritic chest pain is typically sharp and worsens with deep breathing or coughing, pointing to a pulmonary or pleural source rather than cardiac ischemia. Musculoskeletal chest pain is usually reproducible with palpation or movement, a feature not typical of cardiac pain. Neuropathic chest pain is often described as burning or shooting along a nerve distribution, which differs from the classic pressure sensation of angina."},
{id:"P4042",d:"D1",c:"D1.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient describes chest discomfort that worsens with deep inspiration and improves when leaning forward. Which conclusion is best supported by this description?",
 o:["This is a classic presentation of stable angina","The pattern suggests a pleuritic or pericardial source rather than typical ischemic cardiac pain","This pattern confirms an acute myocardial infarction","Positional and respiratory variation always rules out any cardiac cause"],a:1,
 why:"Pain that worsens with inspiration and improves with leaning forward is characteristic of pleuritic or pericardial pain, such as pericarditis, rather than the pressure type discomfort typical of myocardial ischemia. Stable angina is classically related to exertion and relieved by rest or nitroglycerin, not affected by breathing or position. This pattern does not fit the presentation of an acute myocardial infarction, which typically presents as persistent pressure unrelated to respiration or position. While this pattern points away from typical ischemic pain, it does not completely rule out all cardiac causes, since pericarditis itself is a cardiac condition, so the finding should be interpreted as suggestive rather than absolute."},
{id:"P4043",d:"D1",c:"D1.C1",dok:2,q:"A male patient reports substernal chest pressure that radiates to the left arm and jaw, accompanied by diaphoresis and nausea. Which symptom pattern classification does this represent?",
 o:["An atypical presentation more common in older women","A presentation with no known association with coronary ischemia","A classic or typical presentation of angina","A pattern specific to gastrointestinal disease only"],a:2,
 why:"Substernal pressure radiating to the left arm and jaw with diaphoresis and nausea represents the classic or typical presentation of angina most commonly described in the cardiac literature, particularly in men. Atypical presentations, more frequently seen in women, older adults, and patients with diabetes, tend to feature symptoms such as fatigue, dyspnea, or vague discomfort rather than this classic radiating pressure pattern. This constellation of symptoms is strongly, not weakly, associated with coronary ischemia. While gastrointestinal disease can occasionally mimic cardiac pain, this specific radiating pattern with diaphoresis is a well recognized ischemic pattern, not a pattern specific to gastrointestinal causes."},
{id:"P4046",d:"D1",c:"D1.C1",dok:2,q:"When taking a cardiac history, the nurse asks the patient to rate chest pain severity, describe its quality, identify aggravating and relieving factors, and note the timing of onset. Which assessment framework does this represent?",
 o:["A general review of systems unrelated to symptom characterization","A structured symptom analysis such as the PQRST or OLDCART approach","A cranial nerve assessment","A nutritional screening tool"],a:1,
 why:"Systematically assessing provocation, quality, region and radiation, severity, and timing follows a structured symptom analysis framework such as PQRST, which is specifically designed to fully characterize a symptom like chest pain. A general review of systems is a broader survey across body systems and is not the focused, structured symptom characterization being described here. A cranial nerve assessment evaluates neurological function and has no relationship to chest pain history taking. A nutritional screening tool assesses dietary intake and risk, which is unrelated to characterizing the quality and pattern of a presenting symptom."},
{id:"P4044",d:"D1",c:"D1.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient with known coronary artery disease reports that chest pain now occurs after walking half a block, whereas previously it took two blocks of walking to provoke symptoms. The pain is relieved with rest as before. Which interpretation is most appropriate?",
 o:["This is expected variability in stable angina and requires no additional action","The change indicates the pain is no longer cardiac in origin","This pattern indicates improvement in the underlying coronary disease","This represents a change in anginal threshold consistent with progression to unstable or worsening angina requiring prompt evaluation"],a:3,
 why:"A decrease in the amount of exertion needed to provoke angina represents a change in anginal threshold, which is a hallmark of worsening or unstable angina and warrants prompt evaluation rather than routine reassurance. This is not expected variability, since a consistent reduction in exertional tolerance reflects a meaningful clinical change, not random day-to-day fluctuation. The pain remains provoked by exertion and relieved by rest, both features consistent with a cardiac origin, so this pattern does not suggest a noncardiac cause. Needing less exertion to trigger pain indicates worsening, not improving, coronary blood flow reserve."},
{id:"P4045",d:"D1",c:"D1.C1",dok:3,q:"During a symptom history, a patient reports intermittent shortness of breath and unusual fatigue with exertion but explicitly denies any chest pain, pressure, or discomfort. The nurse recognizes these as potential anginal equivalents. Which patient population is this presentation most classically associated with?",
 o:["Older adults, women, and patients with diabetes","Young athletic men without other risk factors","Patients with a prior history of anxiety disorder only","Patients who have never smoked"],a:0,
 why:"Anginal equivalents, meaning ischemic symptoms without classic chest pain such as dyspnea or fatigue, are most classically associated with older adults, women, and patients with diabetes, populations known to have altered pain perception or atypical symptom presentation related to ischemia. Young athletic men without risk factors are the group most likely to present with classic exertional chest pain rather than anginal equivalents. A history of anxiety alone does not define the classic population for anginal equivalents, and attributing symptoms to anxiety without cardiac workup risks missing true ischemia. Smoking history is a risk factor for coronary disease generally but is not specifically tied to the anginal equivalent presentation pattern."},
{id:"P4047",d:"D1",c:"D1.C2",dok:1,q:"In an adult without cardiac enlargement, the point of maximal impulse is normally palpated in which location?",
 o:["The second intercostal space at the right sternal border","The fourth intercostal space at the left sternal border","The fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line","The seventh intercostal space at the anterior axillary line"],a:2,
 why:"The point of maximal impulse, reflecting the apex of the left ventricle, is normally located in the fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line in an adult without cardiac enlargement. The second intercostal space at the right sternal border corresponds to the aortic auscultation area, not the normal PMI location. The fourth intercostal space at the left sternal border corresponds to the tricuspid auscultation area. A PMI displaced as far as the seventh intercostal space at the anterior axillary line would indicate cardiomegaly, not a normal finding."},
{id:"P4048",d:"D1",c:"D1.C2",dok:2,q:"On palpation of the precordium, the nurse feels a sustained, forceful outward movement over the left ventricular area. What does this finding most likely indicate?",
 o:["A normal finding requiring no further assessment","Right ventricular volume overload exclusively","A pericardial friction rub","Left ventricular hypertrophy causing a sustained heave"],a:3,
 why:"A sustained, forceful outward movement over the left ventricular area, known as a heave or lift, indicates increased left ventricular mass or hypertrophy generating a stronger and more prolonged impulse than normal. This is not a normal finding, since the normal PMI is a brief, localized tap rather than a sustained forceful movement. While right ventricular overload can produce a parasternal heave, a heave specifically over the left ventricular area points to left-sided, not right-sided, pathology. A pericardial friction rub is an auscultated sound, not a palpable precordial movement, so it would not be described this way."},
{id:"P4049",d:"D1",c:"D1.C2",dok:2,q:"While palpating the precordium, the nurse feels a fine, vibratory sensation, often described as feeling like a cat purring, over the area of a known murmur. What does this palpable finding represent?",
 o:["A thrill, caused by turbulent blood flow, correlating with a loud murmur","A normal finding present in most healthy adults","A sign of decreased cardiac output only","An indication that the murmur is innocent and benign"],a:0,
 why:"A palpable thrill is a fine, vibratory sensation caused by turbulent blood flow across an abnormal valve or structure and typically correlates with a loud, grade 4 or higher murmur, making it a significant finding rather than incidental. This is not a normal finding in healthy adults, since thrills reflect turbulent, abnormal flow patterns. A thrill reflects turbulence at a specific structural site rather than being a general marker of reduced cardiac output. Because a thrill indicates a loud, hemodynamically significant murmur, it is associated with pathologic rather than innocent murmurs."},
{id:"P4050",d:"D1",c:"D1.C2",dok:2,q:"Bilateral lower extremity pitting edema that leaves a persistent indentation after two millimeters of pressure for a few seconds is typically graded as which level?",
 o:["2+","1+","3+","4+"],a:1,
 why:"A barely detectable indentation of about two millimeters that disappears rapidly is classically graded as 1+ pitting edema, the mildest level on the standard four point scale. A 2+ grade corresponds to a deeper indentation, typically around four millimeters, that takes slightly longer to rebound. A 3+ grade reflects a noticeably deep indentation of about six millimeters that lasts longer. A 4+ grade represents a very deep indentation of eight millimeters or more that can last more than two minutes, indicating severe edema."},
{id:"P4051",d:"D1",c:"D1.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient with long-standing heart failure has new bilateral lower extremity edema that is no longer pitting despite significant swelling. The skin also appears thickened and discolored. Which interpretation is best supported by these findings?",
 o:["This finding indicates the edema has completely resolved","The nonpitting quality indicates the edema is unrelated to cardiac function","Chronic venous insufficiency with fibrotic tissue changes has developed, altering the character of the edema over time","This pattern is diagnostic of an acute arterial occlusion"],a:2,
 why:"Chronic edema over time can lead to fibrotic and thickened skin changes, causing the edema to become nonpitting even though significant swelling and fluid accumulation remain present, a pattern classically seen in chronic venous insufficiency associated with long-standing heart failure. The presence of significant swelling and skin changes indicates ongoing pathology, not resolution of edema. Nonpitting character does not mean the edema is unrelated to cardiac function, since long-standing cardiac related venous congestion is a known cause of this chronic change. Acute arterial occlusion typically presents with sudden pain, pallor, and coolness rather than chronic thickened, discolored, nonpitting swelling, so this pattern does not fit an acute arterial process."},
{id:"P4052",d:"D1",c:"D1.C2",dok:3,q:"A nurse is performing a precordial assessment on a patient with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and long-standing pulmonary hypertension. On inspection there is a visible heave along the lower left sternal border, and on palpation the point of maximal impulse cannot be located in its usual position. The patient also has jugular venous distension and peripheral edema. Which integrated interpretation best accounts for this full clinical picture?",
 o:["These findings are isolated and unrelated to each other, each requiring separate, unconnected workups","The findings indicate primary left ventricular failure unrelated to the pulmonary history","The parasternal heave and jugular venous distension are incidental findings with no clinical significance in this patient","Right ventricular hypertrophy and failure secondary to chronic pulmonary hypertension, producing a parasternal heave, a shifted or obscured PMI, and systemic venous congestion"],a:3,
 why:"In chronic pulmonary hypertension from lung disease, the right ventricle hypertrophies and can fail, producing a parasternal heave from the enlarged right ventricle, a PMI that is difficult to locate in its usual left ventricular position because right heart findings dominate, and systemic venous congestion signs such as jugular venous distension and peripheral edema, so these findings fit together as a single right heart failure picture, a condition known as cor pulmonale. Treating these findings as unrelated ignores the pathophysiologic link between pulmonary hypertension and right heart strain that ties the exam findings together. The clinical picture, with a parasternal rather than apical heave and a background of pulmonary hypertension, points to right-sided rather than primary left-sided failure. Jugular venous distension and a parasternal heave in this context are significant findings reflecting right heart strain, not incidental or insignificant observations."},
{id:"P4053",d:"D1",c:"D1.C3",dok:1,q:"The first heart sound, S1, is produced by closure of which valves?",
 o:["The mitral and tricuspid valves","The aortic and pulmonic valves","The mitral and aortic valves","The tricuspid and pulmonic valves"],a:0,
 why:"S1 is produced by the closure of the atrioventricular valves, the mitral and tricuspid valves, marking the beginning of systole. The aortic and pulmonic valves are the semilunar valves whose closure produces S2, not S1. The mitral and aortic valves are on different sides of the closure timing, one atrioventricular and one semilunar, so pairing them together does not describe either normal heart sound accurately. The tricuspid and pulmonic valves also mix an atrioventricular and a semilunar valve and do not correspond to a single heart sound together."},
{id:"P4057",d:"D1",c:"D1.C3",dok:1,q:"Which part of the stethoscope is best used to auscultate low-pitched sounds such as an S3 gallop or the murmur of mitral stenosis?",
 o:["The bell","The diaphragm","The tubing","The earpieces"],a:0,
 why:"The bell of the stethoscope is designed to transmit low-pitched sounds such as an S3 gallop or the murmur of mitral stenosis when applied lightly to the skin. The diaphragm is designed for higher-pitched sounds such as S1, S2, and most systolic murmurs, and pressing it firmly can actually filter out low-pitched sounds. The tubing simply conducts sound to the earpieces and does not itself alter pitch sensitivity in a way that aids sound selection. The earpieces deliver sound to the examiner but do not have a role in selecting for low versus high frequency sounds."},
{id:"P4054",d:"D1",c:"D1.C3",dok:2,q:"An extra heart sound is heard immediately after S2, best heard at the apex with the bell of the stethoscope, in a patient with new onset volume overload from decompensated heart failure. Which sound is this most consistent with?",
 o:["An S4 gallop","An S3 gallop","A split S1","A pericardial friction rub"],a:1,
 why:"An S3 gallop occurs early in diastole just after S2 and is associated with rapid ventricular filling into a volume overloaded or dilated ventricle, making it a classic finding in decompensated heart failure. An S4 gallop occurs later, just before S1, and is associated with a stiff, noncompliant ventricle rather than volume overload timing right after S2. A split S1 would be heard at the very beginning of systole, not after S2, and reflects asynchronous closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves. A pericardial friction rub has a distinctive scratchy, grating quality tied to pericardial inflammation, not the specific early diastolic timing and quality described here."},
{id:"P4055",d:"D1",c:"D1.C3",dok:2,q:"A murmur is auscultated that is loud, easily heard as soon as the stethoscope touches the chest, and accompanied by a palpable thrill. Using the standard grading scale, which grade best fits this description?",
 o:["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 4","Grade 6"],a:2,
 why:"A murmur that is loud with an associated palpable thrill is classically graded as 4 on the standard 1 through 6 scale, since a thrill is specifically the defining feature that separates grade 4 from softer murmurs. A grade 1 murmur is very faint and heard only with special effort, which does not match a loud murmur with a thrill. A grade 2 murmur is faint but heard immediately, still without an associated thrill, so it does not fit this description either. A grade 6 murmur is audible even with the stethoscope lifted slightly off the chest, which is louder than what is described here."},
{id:"P4056",d:"D1",c:"D1.C3",dok:3,q:"A nurse auscultates a diastolic murmur best heard at the left lower sternal border with the patient leaning forward and the breath held in exhalation. Which conclusion is best supported?",
 o:["The timing and technique confirm this is a normal physiologic murmur","This pattern is diagnostic of mitral stenosis specifically","The positioning and breath-holding technique are irrelevant to the finding","The murmur pattern is more consistent with aortic regurgitation than with a systolic ejection murmur"],a:3,
 why:"A diastolic murmur best heard at the left sternal border with the patient leaning forward, a position and technique specifically used to enhance the sound of aortic regurgitation, fits the classic presentation and auscultation technique for that condition, distinguishing it from a systolic ejection murmur which occurs at a different point in the cardiac cycle. Diastolic murmurs are not considered normal physiologic findings the way some soft systolic murmurs in children or young adults can be. Mitral stenosis produces a diastolic murmur as well, but it is classically best heard at the apex with the bell in the left lateral position, not at the left lower sternal border with forward leaning, so this specific technique and location fit aortic regurgitation better. The positioning and breath-holding technique are deliberately used to bring out this specific murmur and are directly relevant to correctly identifying it."},
{id:"P4058",d:"D1",c:"D1.C3",dok:4,q:"A patient with known aortic stenosis reports new exertional syncope in addition to previously stable exertional chest pain and dyspnea. On auscultation the murmur, previously a loud grade 4 systolic ejection murmur, now sounds softer, and the carotid upstrokes are noted to be diminished and delayed. Which interpretation best integrates these findings?",
 o:["The softer murmur indicates the aortic stenosis has improved and is now less severe","The combination of new syncope, a softer murmur, and diminished delayed carotid upstrokes suggests critical, worsening aortic stenosis with declining stroke volume, a finding requiring urgent evaluation","The new syncope is unrelated to the cardiac examination findings and reflects a separate neurologic process only","Diminished carotid upstrokes are an expected normal aging change unrelated to the murmur findings"],a:1,
 why:"In severe or critical aortic stenosis, a paradoxically softer murmur can occur because declining stroke volume reduces the turbulent flow generating the sound, and this combined with new exertional syncope, a classic symptom of critical obstruction, and diminished, delayed carotid upstrokes together point to worsening, critical aortic stenosis that needs urgent evaluation rather than reassurance. A softer murmur in this clinical context does not mean improvement, since paradoxically softening murmurs with worsening symptoms is a recognized red flag pattern in severe aortic stenosis. Exertional syncope in a patient with known aortic stenosis is a classic symptom of critical obstruction and should not be dismissed as unrelated to the cardiac findings without full cardiac evaluation. Diminished and delayed carotid upstrokes, known as pulsus parvus et tardus, are a specific finding associated with severe aortic stenosis, not a benign or expected aging change."},
{id:"P4059",d:"D1",c:"D1.C4",dok:1,q:"Capillary refill time is considered delayed, and a possible indicator of poor peripheral perfusion, when it exceeds how many seconds?",
 o:["1 second","5 minutes","3 seconds","30 seconds"],a:2,
 why:"A capillary refill time greater than approximately 3 seconds is generally considered delayed and may indicate reduced peripheral perfusion. A refill time of about 1 second or less is within the normal expected range and would not be considered delayed. A value in minutes is far outside any reasonable clinical measurement for this bedside test, making it an implausible threshold. A value of 30 seconds is also far too long to represent the recognized clinical cutoff for delayed capillary refill."},
{id:"P4060",d:"D1",c:"D1.C4",dok:2,q:"A nurse grades a patient dorsalis pedis pulse as 1+ on a standard 0 to 4+ scale. What does this finding indicate?",
 o:["An absent pulse requiring immediate emergency escalation","A completely normal, strong pulse","A bounding pulse suggestive of high output states","A weak, diminished pulse that is palpable but reduced compared to normal"],a:3,
 why:"A grade of 1+ on the standard pulse scale indicates a weak or diminished pulse that is still palpable but reduced in strength compared to a normal finding, warranting continued monitoring and correlation with other perfusion signs. An absent pulse is graded as 0, not 1+, and represents a more urgent finding. A normal pulse is typically graded as 2+, representing an easily palpable pulse of expected strength. A bounding pulse associated with high output states is graded as 3+ or 4+, reflecting an exaggerated rather than diminished pulse."},
{id:"P4062",d:"D1",c:"D1.C4",dok:2,q:"Before drawing an arterial blood gas from the radial artery, the nurse performs an Allen test. What is the purpose of this assessment?",
 o:["To measure blood pressure in that extremity","To confirm adequate collateral circulation through the ulnar artery before puncturing the radial artery","To assess for deep vein thrombosis in the arm","To evaluate cardiac rhythm prior to the procedure"],a:1,
 why:"The Allen test assesses collateral blood flow through the ulnar artery by observing hand color return after radial artery compression is released, confirming adequate collateral circulation before the radial artery is punctured for a blood draw. It does not measure blood pressure, which requires a blood pressure cuff and different technique. It is not a test for deep vein thrombosis, which involves different assessment techniques for the venous system rather than arterial collateral flow. The Allen test has no role in evaluating cardiac rhythm and is specific to hand perfusion assessment before an arterial procedure."},
{id:"P4061",d:"D1",c:"D1.C4",dok:3,q:"Following a cardiac catheterization via the femoral approach, a patient reports increasing pain in the affected leg that is out of proportion to the procedure, along with new numbness and a leg that feels tense to palpation. The pedal pulse is still palpable. Which analysis best reflects the priority nursing concern?",
 o:["These findings raise concern for compartment syndrome or retroperitoneal bleeding, which can occur even with a palpable distal pulse and require urgent evaluation","Since the pedal pulse is still palpable, no vascular complication is possible and routine monitoring can continue","The findings are expected post-procedure discomfort and require only oral analgesia","Numbness in the leg after catheterization is a normal finding related to positioning during the procedure"],a:0,
 why:"Pain out of proportion, new numbness, and a tense, swollen limb are classic warning signs of compartment syndrome or bleeding such as a retroperitoneal hematoma after femoral access, and a palpable distal pulse does not rule these out because compartment pressure can compromise tissue perfusion before it eliminates a major arterial pulse. Assuming no complication is possible because the pulse is present ignores this critical point about how compartment syndrome and early ischemia can present. These findings represent a vascular emergency, not expected discomfort, so treating with analgesia alone without further evaluation delays necessary intervention. New numbness combined with tense swelling and disproportionate pain is a red flag finding, not a benign positional effect."},
{id:"P4063",d:"D1",c:"D1.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient has a lower extremity ulcer with a well-defined, punched-out appearance located over the lateral malleolus, with minimal surrounding drainage and a pale wound base. The leg is cool with diminished pulses. Which type of ulcer does this description most likely represent?",
 o:["A venous stasis ulcer","A diabetic neuropathic ulcer of the plantar foot","An arterial ulcer","A pressure injury from prolonged immobility"],a:2,
 why:"A well-defined, punched-out ulcer with a pale base, minimal drainage, and an associated cool limb with diminished pulses is characteristic of an arterial ulcer, which results from insufficient blood supply to the tissue. Venous stasis ulcers typically occur near the medial malleolus with irregular borders, more drainage, and surrounding hemosiderin skin staining, a different pattern than described here. Diabetic neuropathic ulcers classically occur on the plantar surface of the foot over pressure points, not the lateral malleolus, and relate to sensory loss rather than primarily arterial insufficiency. A pressure injury develops over bony prominences from prolonged pressure and is not primarily characterized by the arterial perfusion pattern described in this case."},
{id:"P4064",d:"D1",c:"D1.C4",dok:4,q:"A patient recovering from an aortic dissection repair develops sudden severe abdominal pain, and the nurse notes cool, mottled skin below the umbilicus with absent femoral pulses bilaterally, though upper extremity pulses remain strong. Blood pressure is preserved at 128 over 76. Which interpretation best integrates these findings and directs the priority action?",
 o:["Since blood pressure remains normal, these findings can be monitored on the routine schedule without urgent escalation","The absent femoral pulses with strong upper extremity pulses indicate a benign positional artifact from patient repositioning","The abdominal pain and mottling are expected postoperative findings unrelated to the vascular findings","The pattern suggests acute lower extremity and possible mesenteric malperfusion from extension or complication of the aortic process, requiring emergent provider notification despite a currently preserved blood pressure"],a:3,
 why:"Absent femoral pulses with preserved upper extremity pulses, combined with new severe abdominal pain and mottled skin below the umbilicus, is a pattern consistent with malperfusion below the level of a complication in the aorta, such as extension of dissection or thrombosis, and this constitutes a vascular emergency requiring urgent notification even though the blood pressure is currently normal, since blood pressure can remain preserved despite critical downstream ischemia. Relying on a normal blood pressure to defer escalation ignores that malperfusion can be present with hemodynamically normal central pressures, especially early in the process. A clear pulse asymmetry between upper and lower extremities with acute pain and skin changes is not an artifact of positioning and reflects a real perfusion difference. Sudden severe abdominal pain with new pulse loss and skin mottling after aortic repair is a significant finding pointing to a vascular complication, not an expected, benign postoperative pattern."},
{id:"P4066",d:"D1",c:"D1.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient with heart failure reports needing to sleep propped up on three pillows to breathe comfortably, a change from sleeping flat just a week ago. What does this finding represent?",
 o:["Paroxysmal nocturnal hypertension unrelated to fluid status","Orthopnea, suggesting worsening fluid status and possible decompensation","A normal and expected sleep pattern change with no clinical significance","A sign of improved cardiac function requiring no follow-up"],a:1,
 why:"The need for an increasing number of pillows to breathe comfortably while lying down, known as orthopnea, reflects worsening fluid redistribution to the lungs when supine and is a recognized sign of worsening heart failure or decompensation. There is no clinical entity called paroxysmal nocturnal hypertension relevant here, and the finding is not primarily a blood pressure phenomenon. This is not a normal, insignificant sleep change, since a new requirement for more pillows in a heart failure patient reflects a meaningful physiologic change. This finding indicates worsening, not improved, cardiac and fluid status, and warrants follow-up rather than being dismissed."},
{id:"P4069",d:"D1",c:"D1.C5",dok:2,q:"Over a four hour period, a patient trended vital signs show heart rate increasing from 78 to 118, systolic blood pressure narrowing from 130 over 70 to 100 over 86, and urine output declining from 50 mL per hour to 10 mL per hour, while the patient remains verbally appropriate. Which interpretation best reflects the significance of this trend?",
 o:["The narrowing pulse pressure, rising heart rate, and falling urine output together suggest early compensated shock despite preserved mental status","Because the patient remains verbally appropriate, these vital sign trends can be considered reassuring and require no escalation","The findings represent normal variation with no concerning pattern","Only the urine output trend is clinically meaningful, and the other changes can be disregarded"],a:0,
 why:"A narrowing pulse pressure, rising heart rate, and declining urine output together reflect compensatory mechanisms attempting to maintain perfusion in early shock, and mental status can remain intact until compensation begins to fail, so preserved orientation should not be reassuring in the presence of these other trending changes. Relying on intact mental status alone ignores that altered mentation is often a late sign of shock, appearing after other compensatory mechanisms have already been recruited. This pattern of trending vital signs together represents a clinically significant and concerning trajectory, not normal variation. All three trends, not just urine output, reflect the same underlying physiologic process of developing shock and should be interpreted together rather than in isolation."},
{id:"P4070",d:"D1",c:"D1.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient on telemetry develops a new run of ventricular tachycardia lasting eight seconds that self-terminates, and the patient reports feeling lightheaded during the episode but is now asymptomatic with stable vital signs. What is the most appropriate nursing action?",
 o:["No action is needed since the rhythm self-terminated and the patient is currently asymptomatic","Notify the provider promptly to report the event and stable vital sign findings, since even self-terminating ventricular tachycardia requires clinical evaluation","Document the event only in the nursing note without notifying the provider","Immediately begin cardiopulmonary resuscitation since any ventricular tachycardia requires it"],a:1,
 why:"Even a self-terminating run of ventricular tachycardia associated with symptoms such as lightheadedness represents a clinically significant event that requires prompt provider notification for further evaluation, since it may indicate underlying electrical instability that could recur or worsen. Assuming no action is needed because the episode resolved and the patient looks stable now overlooks the importance of investigating a symptomatic ventricular arrhythmia. Documenting without notifying the provider fails to ensure timely clinical evaluation of a potentially serious rhythm event. Cardiopulmonary resuscitation is reserved for patients without a pulse or in cardiac arrest, not for a patient who is currently alert, stable, and has a self-terminated rhythm."},
{id:"P4065",d:"D1",c:"D1.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with heart failure at home reports a 3 pound weight gain over two days. What does this finding most likely indicate?",
 o:["Fluid retention and possible early decompensation","Sudden dehydration","Normal daily weight fluctuation with no clinical significance","Improved nutritional intake with muscle gain"],a:0,
 why:"A rapid weight gain of several pounds over a short period in a heart failure patient most likely reflects fluid retention and can be an early warning sign of decompensation, prompting closer monitoring or provider contact per the patient action plan. This pattern reflects fluid gain, not dehydration, which would present as weight loss instead. This amount of weight change over just two days is not within the range of normal daily fluctuation and should be taken seriously in a heart failure patient. Muscle gain does not occur rapidly enough to account for a multi-pound change in two days, making this an implausible explanation for the finding."},
{id:"P4067",d:"D1",c:"D1.C5",dok:3,q:"A nurse caring for a patient with acute decompensated heart failure notes new confusion and lethargy in a patient who was alert and oriented on the previous shift, along with a blood pressure of 84 over 52 and cool extremities. Which action is the priority?",
 o:["Document the finding and reassess again at the next scheduled vital sign check","Attribute the confusion to poor sleep and reassure the patient","Notify the provider immediately using a structured communication tool such as SBAR to report the change in perfusion status","Increase the head of bed and wait to see if the confusion resolves on its own"],a:2,
 why:"New confusion combined with hypotension and cool extremities suggests worsening cardiac output and cerebral hypoperfusion, representing a significant change that requires immediate provider notification using a structured tool such as SBAR to ensure timely intervention. Waiting until the next scheduled check delays recognition of a potentially urgent decline in perfusion status. Attributing new confusion to poor sleep without considering the hemodynamic findings risks missing a serious physiologic cause. Simply repositioning and waiting does not address the underlying perfusion problem suggested by the hypotension and cool extremities and delays needed escalation."},
{id:"P4068",d:"D1",c:"D1.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient recovering from an inferior myocardial infarction develops a new holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border, along with sudden hypotension and worsening dyspnea. Which interpretation best reflects the priority nursing concern?",
 o:["The new murmur is an incidental finding unrelated to the recent infarction","This pattern reflects expected healing of the infarcted tissue and requires routine monitoring only","The hypotension is best explained by opioid pain medication alone, and the murmur can be addressed later","This presentation raises concern for a mechanical complication such as a ventricular septal rupture, requiring emergent provider notification"],a:3,
 why:"A new holosystolic murmur with sudden hypotension and worsening dyspnea after a myocardial infarction is a classic presentation of a mechanical complication such as ventricular septal rupture or acute mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle involvement, both of which are life threatening and require emergent provider notification. A new murmur appearing in this specific post-infarction context is a significant finding, not an incidental one. This combination of findings does not reflect normal healing and instead suggests a serious structural complication requiring urgent evaluation. While opioids can contribute to hypotension, the combination of a new murmur with acute hypotension and dyspnea points to a cardiac structural cause that should not be dismissed or deferred."},
{id:"P3001",d:"D2",c:"D2.C1",dok:1,q:"Which waveform component on the ECG represents depolarization of the ventricles?",
 o:["The P wave","The QRS complex","The ST segment","The T wave"],a:1,
 why:"The QRS complex reflects the spread of the depolarization wave through the ventricular muscle, which is why it is typically the largest, fastest deflection on the tracing. The P wave instead reflects atrial depolarization, the ST segment reflects the period between ventricular depolarization and repolarization, and the T wave reflects ventricular repolarization, so none of those three represent the event described."},
{id:"P3002",d:"D2",c:"D2.C1",dok:1,q:"A rhythm is described as normal sinus rhythm. Which relationship between the P waves and QRS complexes must be present for this label to apply?",
 o:["Each P wave is followed by one QRS complex in a consistent 1 to 1 pattern","P waves and QRS complexes occur at different rates with no fixed relationship","Two or more P waves precede each QRS complex in a fixed ratio","P waves appear only after every second or third QRS complex"],a:0,
 why:"Normal sinus rhythm requires that every P wave is followed by a QRS complex in a steady one to one relationship, showing that each atrial impulse is conducted to the ventricles without delay or block. An unrelated pattern between P waves and QRS complexes describes AV dissociation, a fixed ratio greater than one to one describes a conduction block such as second degree block, and P waves confined to every second or third QRS complex is not a feature of a normal conduction pattern."},
{id:"P3003",d:"D2",c:"D2.C1",dok:2,q:"Which term describes the portion of the ECG tracing measured from the end of the QRS complex to the beginning of the T wave?",
 o:["PR interval","QT interval","ST segment","TP segment"],a:2,
 why:"The ST segment spans from the end of the QRS complex, the J point, to the start of the T wave and reflects the early phase of ventricular repolarization, so it is the correct term for that specific portion of the tracing. The PR interval measures from the start of the P wave to the start of the QRS complex, the QT interval spans from the start of the QRS complex to the end of the T wave, and the TP segment is the isoelectric baseline between the end of one T wave and the next P wave, so each names a different portion of the cycle."},
{id:"P3004",d:"D2",c:"D2.C1",dok:2,q:"A monitor is calibrated so that 1 millivolt of signal produces a 5 mm deflection instead of the standard 10 mm. What effect will this have on the appearance of the QRS complexes?",
 o:["QRS amplitude will appear falsely low compared with true voltage","QRS amplitude will appear falsely high compared with true voltage","QRS duration will appear falsely prolonged","The baseline will drift upward over each cycle"],a:0,
 why:"Standard calibration is set so that 1 millivolt produces a 10 mm deflection, so halving that calibration to 5 mm per millivolt makes every waveform appear smaller in amplitude than the true electrical voltage. Calibration changes affect amplitude, not the timing of the waveform, so QRS duration is not altered, and a calibration change does not itself cause baseline drift, which is more typically caused by respiration or poor electrode contact."},
{id:"P3005",d:"D2",c:"D2.C1",dok:2,q:"For continuous bedside monitoring, lead II is frequently chosen as the primary monitoring lead. What is the main reason for this choice?",
 o:["It produces the tallest QRS complex of any available lead in every patient","Its electrical axis runs nearly parallel to the direction of normal atrial depolarization, producing a clear P wave","It is the only lead that can detect ST segment changes","It eliminates the need for a ground electrode"],a:1,
 why:"Lead II runs roughly parallel to the path the electrical impulse travels from the SA node through the atria, so it typically produces an upright, easily visible P wave that helps the nurse assess the relationship between atrial and ventricular activity. QRS height varies by patient and is not guaranteed to be tallest in lead II, other leads including modified chest leads can also show ST changes, and a ground electrode is still required regardless of which lead is monitored."},
{id:"P3006",d:"D2",c:"D2.C1",dok:3,q:"A telemetry technician notes that a patient QRS complexes measure only 3 mm tall on a strip where the calibration marker shows a 5 mm square for 1 millivolt of signal instead of the usual 10 mm square. How should the nurse interpret the true QRS amplitude?",
 o:["The true amplitude is approximately double the measured 3 mm, or about 6 mm at standard calibration","The true amplitude is exactly 3 mm regardless of calibration settings","The true amplitude cannot be estimated without a 12 lead ECG","The true amplitude is half of the measured value, or about 1.5 mm"],a:0,
 why:"Because the monitor is calibrated at half the standard gain, every measured amplitude on that strip represents only half of the true voltage, so the actual QRS amplitude is roughly double the 3 mm that was measured. Treating the measured value as accurate ignores the calibration setting entirely, halving it again compounds the error in the wrong direction, and while a 12 lead can confirm findings, the amplitude can still be reasonably estimated by accounting for the known calibration factor."},
{id:"P3007",d:"D2",c:"D2.C1",dok:3,q:"On a rhythm strip with a heart rate of 130 per minute, a small rounded deflection appears after each T wave and before the next QRS complex. What measurement pitfall should the nurse consider before labeling this deflection a P wave?",
 o:["At faster heart rates, a prominent U wave can be mistaken for the P wave of the following beat","This deflection can only represent atrial flutter waves","This deflection must represent a premature ventricular contraction","This finding always indicates third degree AV block"],a:0,
 why:"A U wave following the T wave can be subtle or prominent depending on the patient, and at faster heart rates the T wave, U wave, and next P wave can crowd together, making it easy to mistake a U wave for a true P wave unless intervals are measured carefully. Atrial flutter waves have a distinct sawtooth pattern rather than a single rounded deflection, a premature ventricular contraction would produce a wide bizarre QRS rather than an isolated small deflection, and third degree block requires evidence that P waves and QRS complexes are dissociated, which a single deflection does not establish."},
{id:"P3008",d:"D2",c:"D2.C2",dok:1,q:"A rhythm has an atrial and ventricular rate of 118 per minute, is regular, and shows a normal upright P wave before every QRS complex. What is this rhythm called?",
 o:["Sinus tachycardia","Sinus bradycardia","Atrial flutter","Junctional tachycardia"],a:0,
 why:"A regular rhythm with a normal P wave preceding every QRS and a rate above 100 per minute meets the definition of sinus tachycardia, since the impulse still originates from the SA node and conducts normally. Sinus bradycardia describes a rate below 60 per minute, atrial flutter shows sawtooth flutter waves rather than discrete upright P waves, and junctional tachycardia arises from the AV junction and typically shows an absent, inverted, or retrograde P wave rather than a normal upright one."},
{id:"P3009",d:"D2",c:"D2.C2",dok:2,q:"A nurse counts 8 QRS complexes within a 6 second segment of rhythm strip. Using the 6 second method, what is the estimated heart rate?",
 o:["48 per minute","60 per minute","80 per minute","96 per minute"],a:2,
 why:"The 6 second method estimates the rate by multiplying the number of QRS complexes counted in a 6 second strip by 10, so 8 complexes multiplied by 10 equals 80 per minute. The other answer choices come from arithmetic errors such as forgetting to multiply by 10 correctly or miscounting the complexes, and none of them reflect the correct multiplication of the counted complexes."},
{id:"P3010",d:"D2",c:"D2.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient who is febrile and anxious has a regular rhythm at 112 per minute with a normal PR interval and a normal, upright P wave before each QRS. What is the most likely explanation?",
 o:["Sinus tachycardia related to fever and anxiety","Atrial fibrillation related to fever","Ventricular tachycardia related to anxiety","Third degree AV block related to fever"],a:0,
 why:"A regular rhythm with a normal PR interval and a normal P wave before every QRS, occurring in the setting of fever and anxiety, is consistent with sinus tachycardia, since both fever and anxiety commonly increase sympathetic tone and SA node firing rate. Atrial fibrillation would show an irregularly irregular rhythm without discrete P waves, ventricular tachycardia would show a wide QRS without normal P waves, and third degree block would show P waves and QRS complexes at unrelated rates, none of which match the description given."},
{id:"P3011",d:"D2",c:"D2.C2",dok:2,q:"A rhythm is regular at 46 per minute with a normal PR interval and a normal upright P wave preceding every QRS complex. How should this rhythm be classified?",
 o:["Sinus bradycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Sinus arrest","Junctional rhythm"],a:0,
 why:"A regular rhythm with a rate below 60 per minute, a normal PR interval, and a normal P wave before each QRS meets the definition of sinus bradycardia, since the SA node is still generating the impulse but at a slower than usual rate. Sinus tachycardia refers to rates above 100 per minute, sinus arrest describes a pause in SA node firing rather than a sustained slow but regular rhythm, and a junctional rhythm would typically show an absent or inverted P wave rather than a normal upright one."},
{id:"P3014",d:"D2",c:"D2.C2",dok:2,q:"A previously regular sinus rhythm shows a single pause where one expected P-QRS-T cycle is missing entirely, but the underlying sinus rhythm resumes afterward at the original rate. What does this most likely represent?",
 o:["A sinus pause, in which the SA node briefly fails to fire","Third degree AV block","A premature ventricular contraction","Atrial flutter"],a:0,
 why:"A brief interruption in an otherwise regular sinus rhythm, followed by resumption of the same underlying rate, describes a sinus pause, which reflects a temporary failure of SA node impulse formation. Third degree block would show a persistent dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes rather than a single missing cycle followed by normal resumption, a premature ventricular contraction would add an early wide beat rather than remove a cycle, and atrial flutter would show a sustained sawtooth atrial pattern rather than an isolated pause."},
{id:"P3012",d:"D2",c:"D2.C2",dok:3,q:"A nurse notices that a patient rhythm has a normal upright P wave before every QRS and a normal PR interval, but the R to R interval gradually shortens during inspiration and lengthens during expiration. How should this pattern be interpreted?",
 o:["Sinus arrhythmia, a normal variation tied to the respiratory cycle","Premature atrial contractions requiring treatment","Atrial fibrillation requiring anticoagulation","Second degree AV block requiring a pacemaker"],a:0,
 why:"A cyclical change in rate that tracks with the respiratory cycle, while P waves and PR intervals remain normal, is the hallmark of sinus arrhythmia, a benign variant that is common in younger patients and does not require treatment. Premature atrial contractions would appear as early beats with a different P wave morphology rather than a smooth cyclical rate change, atrial fibrillation would lack organized P waves entirely, and second degree block would show intermittently dropped QRS complexes rather than a gradually changing rate."},
{id:"P3013",d:"D2",c:"D2.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient develops sinus tachycardia at 122 per minute two hours after abdominal surgery. Vital signs also show a temperature of 38.4 C and reported pain at 8 out of 10. What is the most appropriate nursing priority?",
 o:["Investigate and treat the underlying causes, such as pain and fever, rather than treating the rate itself","Administer a beta blocker immediately to slow the rate to under 100","Prepare for immediate synchronized cardioversion","Administer adenosine to terminate the rhythm"],a:0,
 why:"Sinus tachycardia is usually a physiologic response to an underlying stressor such as pain, fever, hypovolemia, or anxiety, so the priority is to identify and treat that cause rather than treating the rate in isolation. A beta blocker could mask a compensatory response and is not first line management for sinus tachycardia from a treatable cause, cardioversion is reserved for hemodynamically unstable reentrant tachyarrhythmias rather than sinus tachycardia, and adenosine is used to terminate reentrant supraventricular tachycardias, not sinus tachycardia, since it will not stop a sinus mechanism and will only cause a brief pause."},
{id:"P3015",d:"D2",c:"D2.C3",dok:1,q:"A single beat occurs earlier than expected, with a P wave that looks different from the patient normal sinus P wave, followed by a normal narrow QRS complex. What does this beat represent?",
 o:["A premature atrial contraction","A premature ventricular contraction","A junctional escape beat","A ventricular escape beat"],a:0,
 why:"An early beat originating from an ectopic atrial focus produces a P wave with a different shape than the normal sinus P wave, but because conduction still travels through the normal His Purkinje system, the QRS remains narrow, which is the definition of a premature atrial contraction. A premature ventricular contraction would show a wide, bizarre QRS without a preceding P wave, and both junctional and ventricular escape beats occur late, after a pause, rather than early, so neither fits a beat that arrives ahead of schedule."},
{id:"P3016",d:"D2",c:"D2.C3",dok:2,q:"An atrial rhythm shows a continuous sawtooth pattern of atrial activity at approximately 300 per minute, with no clear isoelectric baseline between atrial deflections. What is this atrial pattern called?",
 o:["Flutter waves","Fibrillatory waves","Normal P waves","U waves"],a:0,
 why:"A regular sawtooth pattern of atrial activity at approximately 300 per minute with no flat baseline between deflections is the classic description of flutter waves, seen in atrial flutter. Fibrillatory waves are chaotic and irregular in both timing and shape rather than forming a uniform sawtooth, normal P waves are discrete rounded deflections with a clear isoelectric baseline between them, and U waves are small deflections that follow the T wave rather than forming a continuous atrial pattern."},
{id:"P3017",d:"D2",c:"D2.C3",dok:2,q:"Flutter waves are present at an atrial rate of 300 per minute with a conduction ratio of 4 flutter waves to every 1 QRS complex. What is the ventricular rate?",
 o:["75 per minute","100 per minute","150 per minute","300 per minute"],a:0,
 why:"With an atrial rate of 300 per minute and only 1 of every 4 atrial impulses conducted to the ventricles, the ventricular rate is found by dividing 300 by 4, which equals 75 per minute. A rate of 150 would result from a 2 to 1 conduction ratio, a rate of 100 would result from a 3 to 1 ratio, and a rate of 300 would only occur if every flutter wave conducted, which is not the case here."},
{id:"P3018",d:"D2",c:"D2.C3",dok:2,q:"A rhythm is irregular, with P waves present before each QRS but showing at least three distinctly different shapes and sizes across the strip, along with varying PR intervals. What is this rhythm most likely to be?",
 o:["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus arrhythmia","First degree AV block"],a:0,
 why:"The presence of organized P waves with at least three different morphologies, varying PR intervals, and an irregular rate is the defining pattern of multifocal atrial tachycardia, which arises from multiple competing atrial foci. Atrial fibrillation lacks discrete, organized P waves entirely, sinus arrhythmia has a single consistent P wave shape that varies only in rate with respiration, and first degree block refers to a uniformly prolonged PR interval with a single P wave morphology, not multiple shapes."},
{id:"P3019",d:"D2",c:"D2.C3",dok:3,q:"A nurse is asked to distinguish multifocal atrial tachycardia from atrial fibrillation on an irregular rhythm strip. Which finding would confirm multifocal atrial tachycardia rather than atrial fibrillation?",
 o:["Presence of distinct, organized P waves with at least three different shapes before the QRS complexes","Absence of any identifiable P waves with a chaotic baseline","A ventricular rate that is perfectly regular","A PR interval that is fixed and identical for every beat"],a:0,
 why:"The key distinguishing feature is the presence of organized, discrete P waves, even though they vary in shape, since atrial fibrillation by definition lacks organized P waves and instead shows a chaotic fibrillatory baseline. A perfectly regular ventricular rate would argue against both of these irregular rhythms, and a fixed identical PR interval would suggest a single consistent atrial focus, which is inconsistent with the multiple morphologies seen in multifocal atrial tachycardia."},
{id:"P3020",d:"D2",c:"D2.C3",dok:3,q:"A rhythm strip shows a regular sinus rhythm that is interrupted once by a longer than expected pause. Careful review shows a distorted T wave just before the pause, suggesting a hidden early P wave that did not conduct. What is the best explanation?",
 o:["A blocked premature atrial contraction that was not conducted to the ventricles","A sinus pause with no atrial activity at all","Third degree AV block","A ventricular escape beat"],a:0,
 why:"An early atrial impulse that arrives so prematurely that the AV node is still refractory will distort the preceding T wave but fail to conduct a QRS, producing a pause that can be mistaken for a sinus pause unless the distorted T wave is recognized as a hidden P wave. A true sinus pause would not show any distortion of the preceding T wave, third degree block would show a sustained pattern of dissociation rather than a single isolated pause, and a ventricular escape beat would add a wide QRS during the pause rather than explain why a beat was skipped."},
{id:"P3021",d:"D2",c:"D2.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient develops new atrial fibrillation with a ventricular rate of 156 per minute, a blood pressure of 82 over 50, and confusion. What is the safest immediate action?",
 o:["Prepare for immediate synchronized cardioversion","Administer oral metoprolol and reassess in one hour","Administer a bolus of intravenous fluids only and observe","Administer adenosine as a rapid intravenous push"],a:0,
 why:"Atrial fibrillation with a rapid ventricular response causing hypotension and altered mental status meets the criteria for hemodynamic instability, which calls for immediate synchronized cardioversion rather than a slower oral medication approach. Oral metoprolol takes too long to act and does not address the emergency, fluids alone will not correct a rate related instability from rapid uncontrolled conduction, and adenosine is not appropriate for atrial fibrillation because it will not terminate this irregular rhythm and is reserved for reentrant supraventricular tachycardias."},
{id:"P3022",d:"D2",c:"D2.C3",dok:4,q:"A patient with new onset atrial fibrillation of unknown duration has a ventricular rate of 168, a blood pressure of 76 over 48, and is becoming lethargic. The care team must decide between immediate synchronized cardioversion and rate control while weighing that duration of the arrhythmia is unknown. What is the best overall approach?",
 o:["Proceed with immediate synchronized cardioversion despite the stroke risk, because hemodynamic instability takes priority over waiting to determine arrhythmia duration","Withhold cardioversion until a transesophageal echocardiogram excludes atrial thrombus, since the patient is currently breathing and awake","Give a full dose of intravenous diltiazem and reassess the blood pressure in thirty minutes before deciding on cardioversion","Delay any intervention until the exact onset time of the atrial fibrillation can be confirmed with certainty"],a:0,
 why:"When atrial fibrillation causes hemodynamic instability such as significant hypotension and a declining level of consciousness, current resuscitation priorities favor immediate synchronized cardioversion even though the unknown duration raises a theoretical stroke risk from cardioversion, because the immediate threat to perfusion outweighs that longer term risk. Waiting for a transesophageal echocardiogram or for confirmation of exact onset time delays lifesaving treatment in an unstable patient, and giving a rate controlling calcium channel blocker in an already hypotensive patient risks worsening the blood pressure further while not resolving the underlying instability as quickly as cardioversion would."},
{id:"P3023",d:"D2",c:"D2.C4",dok:1,q:"A rhythm is regular at 48 per minute with a QRS duration of 0.08 seconds. No P waves are visible before the QRS complexes, though an inverted P wave is seen immediately after some QRS complexes. What is this rhythm?",
 o:["Junctional rhythm","Sinus bradycardia","Idioventricular rhythm","Third degree AV block"],a:0,
 why:"A narrow QRS at a rate in the 40 to 60 range, with an absent P wave before the QRS or an inverted retrograde P wave appearing around the QRS, is characteristic of a junctional rhythm arising from the AV junction rather than the SA node. Sinus bradycardia requires a normal upright P wave before every QRS, idioventricular rhythm originates in the ventricles and produces a wide QRS, and third degree block would show independent, dissociated P waves and QRS complexes rather than the pattern described here."},
{id:"P3024",d:"D2",c:"D2.C4",dok:2,q:"A single beat interrupts an otherwise regular sinus rhythm. It has no preceding P wave, a wide and bizarre QRS shape, and is followed by a pause before the next sinus beat resumes. What is this beat called?",
 o:["A premature ventricular contraction","A premature atrial contraction","A junctional escape beat","A first degree AV block"],a:0,
 why:"An early, wide, bizarre QRS without a preceding P wave, followed by a compensatory pause, is the classic definition of a premature ventricular contraction, since the impulse originates within the ventricular myocardium and bypasses the normal narrow conduction pathway. A premature atrial contraction would have a different but present P wave and a narrow QRS, a junctional escape beat occurs late after a pause rather than early, and first degree block refers to a prolonged but constant PR interval rather than a single early wide beat."},
{id:"P3025",d:"D2",c:"D2.C4",dok:2,q:"A wide complex rhythm at 70 per minute appears shortly after a patient is successfully reperfused during treatment for a myocardial infarction. It is regular, has no clear preceding P waves, and resolves on its own within a few minutes. What is the most likely explanation?",
 o:["Accelerated idioventricular rhythm, a common and usually benign reperfusion rhythm","Ventricular fibrillation requiring immediate defibrillation","Third degree AV block requiring transcutaneous pacing","Torsades de pointes requiring magnesium sulfate"],a:0,
 why:"A regular wide complex rhythm with a rate roughly between 40 and 100 that appears around the time of reperfusion and resolves spontaneously is typical of accelerated idioventricular rhythm, a benign marker of restored blood flow that usually does not require treatment. Ventricular fibrillation is chaotic and disorganized rather than regular, third degree block would show dissociated P waves rather than simply an absence of visible P waves, and torsades de pointes has a distinctive twisting polymorphic QRS pattern rather than a uniform wide complex shape."},
{id:"P3026",d:"D2",c:"D2.C4",dok:2,q:"A wide complex rhythm at 220 per minute shows QRS complexes that continuously change in amplitude and appear to twist around the baseline in a spindle shaped pattern. What is this rhythm called?",
 o:["Torsades de pointes","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Accelerated idioventricular rhythm","Atrial flutter with aberrant conduction"],a:0,
 why:"A rapid wide complex rhythm in which the QRS amplitude and axis appear to twist around the isoelectric baseline in a spindle pattern is the classic description of torsades de pointes, a polymorphic form of ventricular tachycardia often associated with a prolonged QT interval. Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia has a uniform QRS shape from beat to beat rather than a changing twisting pattern, accelerated idioventricular rhythm runs at a much slower rate, and atrial flutter with aberrant conduction would still show an underlying regular sawtooth atrial pattern rather than a twisting ventricular waveform."},
{id:"P3029",d:"D2",c:"D2.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient with known cardiomyopathy has frequent PVCs arising from more than one shape and location, occurring several times per minute on the monitor. What is the most appropriate nursing action?",
 o:["Notify the provider and continue close monitoring, since multifocal PVCs in structural heart disease carry higher risk than isolated unifocal PVCs","Ignore the finding since PVCs are always benign regardless of underlying heart disease","Immediately defibrillate the patient","Restrain the patient to prevent movement artifact from mimicking PVCs"],a:0,
 why:"Multifocal PVCs, meaning they arise from more than one ventricular focus, are considered more clinically significant than isolated unifocal PVCs, especially in a patient with underlying structural heart disease such as cardiomyopathy, so notifying the provider and increasing monitoring is appropriate. PVCs are not always benign, particularly in structural heart disease, defibrillation is reserved for pulseless ventricular fibrillation or tachycardia rather than PVCs with a perfusing rhythm, and restraining the patient does not address the rhythm finding and is not an appropriate nursing response."},
{id:"P3027",d:"D2",c:"D2.C4",dok:3,q:"A rhythm strip shows a normal sinus beat, followed by a premature wide beat, followed by another normal sinus beat, followed by another premature wide beat, in a repeating pattern throughout the entire strip. How should the nurse document this pattern?",
 o:["Ventricular bigeminy","Ventricular trigeminy","A run of ventricular tachycardia","Sinus arrhythmia"],a:0,
 why:"A pattern in which every other beat is a premature ventricular contraction, alternating one to one with normal sinus beats, is defined as ventricular bigeminy. Trigeminy would show a PVC following every third beat rather than every second beat, a run of ventricular tachycardia requires at least three consecutive PVCs occurring together, and sinus arrhythmia describes a gradual rate change with normal narrow QRS complexes throughout, not alternating wide premature beats."},
{id:"P3028",d:"D2",c:"D2.C4",dok:3,q:"A premature ventricular contraction occurs and its QRS complex falls directly on the peak of the T wave of the preceding beat. Why is this timing considered clinically significant?",
 o:["It falls during the relative refractory period, which raises the risk of triggering ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation","It guarantees the patient will develop third degree AV block","It confirms the PVC originated from the atria rather than the ventricles","It has no clinical significance regardless of timing"],a:0,
 why:"A PVC that lands on the downslope of the preceding T wave falls during the relative refractory period of the ventricle, when cells are more vulnerable to being triggered into a self sustaining rapid rhythm such as ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation, a phenomenon known as R on T. This timing has no established relationship to AV block, the wide bizarre QRS of a PVC by definition indicates a ventricular rather than atrial origin, and the significance of this timing is well established rather than negligible, which is why the pattern is closely monitored."},
{id:"P3030",d:"D2",c:"D2.C5",dok:1,q:"A rhythm is regular with a normal QRS following every P wave, but the PR interval measures a constant 0.26 seconds on every beat. What does this represent?",
 o:["First degree AV block","Second degree AV block type I","Second degree AV block type II","Third degree AV block"],a:0,
 why:"A PR interval that is prolonged beyond 0.20 seconds but remains constant, with every P wave still followed by a QRS, defines first degree AV block, which reflects delayed but intact conduction through the AV node. Both types of second degree block involve at least one nonconducted P wave, which is not described here, and third degree block requires complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes rather than a constant, if prolonged, PR interval."},
{id:"P3032",d:"D2",c:"D2.C5",dok:2,q:"A conduction block shows a PR interval that lengthens slightly with each successive beat until a QRS is finally dropped, after which the cycle repeats. At which anatomic level does this type of block most commonly occur?",
 o:["At the level of the AV node","At the level of the bundle of His","At the level of the bundle branches","At the level of the Purkinje fibers"],a:0,
 why:"This pattern, known as Wenckebach or Mobitz type I block, most commonly occurs at the level of the AV node itself, which is why it is often associated with reversible causes such as increased vagal tone or inferior wall ischemia affecting the AV nodal blood supply. Blocks occurring at the bundle of His, bundle branches, or Purkinje fibers are considered infranodal and more typically produce the constant PR interval with sudden dropped beats seen in Mobitz type II rather than the gradual lengthening pattern described here."},
{id:"P3035",d:"D2",c:"D2.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient two days after an inferior wall myocardial infarction develops a heart rate of 32 per minute with P waves and QRS complexes occurring independently, along with dizziness and a systolic blood pressure of 74. What is the most appropriate immediate treatment?",
 o:["Prepare for transcutaneous pacing while giving atropine as a temporizing measure","Administer a beta blocker to slow the atrial rate","Observe without intervention since inferior MI related blocks always resolve spontaneously","Administer a calcium channel blocker to improve AV conduction"],a:0,
 why:"A symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable complete heart block requires preparation for transcutaneous pacing, with atropine given as a temporizing measure while pacing is arranged, since medication alone is often insufficient for infranodal or high grade block. A beta blocker or calcium channel blocker would further slow AV conduction and worsen the block rather than help it, and while inferior MI related AV block often does resolve over time, an unstable patient with symptomatic bradycardia and hypotension cannot simply be observed without intervention."},
{id:"P3031",d:"D2",c:"D2.C5",dok:3,q:"A nurse is comparing two types of second degree AV block. One shows a progressively lengthening PR interval before a dropped beat, while the other shows a constant PR interval with sudden, unpredictable dropped beats. Which of the two carries the greater risk of progressing suddenly to complete heart block?",
 o:["The type with a constant PR interval and sudden unpredictable dropped beats, because block is occurring below the AV node in the His Purkinje system","The type with a progressively lengthening PR interval, because it always occurs at the level of the ventricles","Both types carry an identical, low risk of progression","Neither type of second degree block can progress to complete heart block"],a:0,
 why:"The block associated with a constant PR interval and sudden, unpredictable dropped beats typically occurs below the AV node in the His Purkinje system, which is a less stable and less reliable escape pathway, making it more likely to progress suddenly to complete heart block. The progressively lengthening pattern usually reflects a more benign, reversible block at the level of the AV node itself rather than the ventricles, and it is inaccurate to say both carry identical low risk or that neither can progress, since the infranodal pattern specifically carries meaningfully higher risk."},
{id:"P3033",d:"D2",c:"D2.C5",dok:3,q:"A stable patient has P waves marching out regularly at 80 per minute and a completely separate, regular QRS rhythm at 38 per minute with a QRS duration of 0.15 seconds, with no relationship between the two. Based on the wide QRS and slow rate of the escape rhythm, where is this escape pacemaker most likely located?",
 o:["Low in the ventricles, since a wide QRS and slower rate suggest a ventricular escape focus rather than a junctional one","In the SA node, since the P waves are regular","In the AV junction, since junctional escape rhythms always produce a wide QRS","In the atria, since atrial escape rhythms are typically slower than 40 per minute"],a:0,
 why:"When third degree AV block occurs, the escape rhythm that takes over below the block can arise from the AV junction or lower in the ventricles, and a wide QRS paired with a slower rate around 30 to 40 per minute points toward a ventricular origin rather than a junctional one, since junctional escape rhythms are usually faster and narrower. The P waves reflect ongoing SA node activity above the block rather than describing the escape focus itself, junctional escape rhythms typically produce a narrow QRS because conduction still travels through the normal His Purkinje pathway, and atrial escape rhythms are not the source of the QRS complexes in complete heart block since the atria and ventricles are dissociated."},
{id:"P3034",d:"D2",c:"D2.C5",dok:3,q:"A nurse is trying to determine whether a patient intermittently dropped beats represent Mobitz type I or Mobitz type II second degree block, and the PR interval pattern is difficult to assess on a noisy strip. Which additional finding would most strongly support Mobitz type II over Mobitz type I?",
 o:["A wide QRS complex on the conducted beats, suggesting infranodal disease","A narrow QRS complex on the conducted beats, suggesting AV nodal disease","A heart rate that varies with respiration","A PR interval that shortens rather than lengthens before the dropped beat"],a:0,
 why:"Mobitz type II block usually arises from disease below the AV node in the bundle branches, so a wide QRS on the conducted beats supports that diagnosis, whereas Mobitz type I typically occurs at the AV node itself and is usually associated with a narrow QRS. A narrow QRS would actually point toward Mobitz type I rather than type II, a rate that varies with respiration describes sinus arrhythmia and is not a distinguishing feature of AV block type, and a shortening PR interval is not a recognized feature of either type of second degree block."},
{id:"P3036",d:"D2",c:"D2.C6",dok:2,q:"A monitored patient tracing shows a fine, regular, fuzzy oscillation of the baseline at a frequency far faster than any physiologic rhythm, most noticeable when a nearby infusion pump is running. What does this pattern most likely represent?",
 o:["Electrical interference artifact from nearby powered equipment","Ventricular fibrillation","Atrial fibrillation","Muscle tremor artifact from patient movement"],a:0,
 why:"A very fast, fine, regular oscillation of the baseline that correlates with the presence of nearby powered equipment is most consistent with electrical interference artifact, which has a fixed, uniform frequency unlike a true arrhythmia. Ventricular fibrillation is chaotic and irregular rather than uniformly fine and regular, atrial fibrillation would still show an underlying identifiable QRS pattern, and muscle tremor artifact tends to be coarser and tied to patient movement rather than nearby equipment."},
{id:"P3037",d:"D2",c:"D2.C6",dok:2,q:"A monitor alarm sounds for a wide, irregular rhythm, but the coarse, erratic baseline pattern only appears during episodes when the patient is repositioning in bed and disappears when the patient is still. What should the nurse do first?",
 o:["Assess the patient directly, including checking a pulse, and correlate the monitor pattern with patient movement before treating it as a true arrhythmia","Immediately call a code and begin chest compressions","Administer an antiarrhythmic medication based on the monitor pattern alone","Increase the alarm threshold so the alarm no longer sounds"],a:0,
 why:"Because the abnormal pattern correlates specifically with patient movement and resolves with stillness, the nurse should first assess the patient directly, including checking for a pulse and overall condition, to confirm whether this represents true artifact rather than a genuine life threatening arrhythmia before taking any action. Calling a code or giving medication based on an unconfirmed monitor pattern risks unnecessary interventions if this is simply movement artifact, and silencing the alarm by raising its threshold could cause a true emergency to be missed later and does not address the immediate need to assess the patient."},
{id:"P3040",d:"D2",c:"D2.C6",dok:2,q:"A telemetry strip shows a slow, smooth, undulating drift of the baseline that rises and falls with each breath, without affecting the shape of the QRS complexes themselves. What is the most appropriate troubleshooting step?",
 o:["Reposition the electrodes away from areas of heavy chest wall movement, such as off of the ribs and onto a flatter area","Immediately notify the provider of a new arrhythmia","Increase the defibrillator pad size in preparation for cardioversion","Discontinue cardiac monitoring entirely since the rhythm cannot be interpreted"],a:0,
 why:"A smooth baseline wander that rises and falls with respiration, without distorting the QRS complexes, is typically caused by electrode placement over an area with significant chest wall movement, so repositioning the electrodes to a flatter, more stable area is the appropriate troubleshooting step. This pattern is artifact rather than a new arrhythmia, so it does not warrant urgent provider notification or preparation for cardioversion, and the underlying rhythm can still generally be interpreted despite the baseline wander, so discontinuing monitoring entirely is unnecessary and would remove valuable clinical data."},
{id:"P3038",d:"D2",c:"D2.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient is brushing his teeth at the sink while wearing telemetry, and the monitor briefly displays what looks like a wide, chaotic, rapid rhythm resembling ventricular tachycardia. The patient remains alert, is talking normally, and denies any symptoms. What should the nurse do first?",
 o:["Go to the bedside to assess the patient directly and correlate the monitor finding with the activity before treating it as a true arrhythmia","Immediately activate the rapid response team for ventricular tachycardia","Administer amiodarone based on the monitor strip","Defibrillate the patient immediately"],a:0,
 why:"Repetitive motion such as tooth brushing commonly produces artifact that can mimic a wide complex tachycardia on the monitor, so the priority is to assess the patient directly and correlate the finding with the activity, since a patient who is alert, talking, and asymptomatic is very unlikely to actually be in a life threatening ventricular arrhythmia. Activating a rapid response, giving amiodarone, or defibrillating are all treatments for a genuine unstable ventricular arrhythmia, and applying any of them based solely on an uncorrelated monitor strip risks harming a patient who does not actually have that rhythm."},
{id:"P3039",d:"D2",c:"D2.C6",dok:3,q:"A monitor alarms for asystole, showing a flat line with no visible waveform, but the bedside pulse oximeter continues to display a normal waveform and reading. What is the most likely explanation and appropriate first action?",
 o:["A disconnected or displaced ECG lead is producing a false asystole alarm, so the nurse should assess the patient and check the lead connections before assuming cardiac arrest","The patient is in true asystole and chest compressions should begin immediately without further assessment","The pulse oximeter is malfunctioning and should be ignored","The monitor should be rebooted before any patient assessment occurs"],a:0,
 why:"A continuing, normal pulse oximetry waveform strongly suggests the patient still has a perfusing rhythm, which means the flat line on the ECG monitor is far more likely to represent a disconnected or displaced lead than true asystole, so the nurse should quickly assess the patient and check the leads before assuming cardiac arrest. Starting compressions without confirming the patient actual status could subject an alert patient to unnecessary and harmful intervention, dismissing the pulse oximeter reading ignores useful corroborating data, and rebooting the monitor delays the more urgent step of directly assessing the patient."},
{id:"P1001",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:1,q:"What is the earliest step in the development of atherosclerosis?",
 o:["Endothelial injury with lipid deposition into the vessel wall","Calcification of the arterial wall","Formation of a stable fibrous cap over a plaque","Acute thrombus formation occluding the lumen"],a:0,
 why:"Endothelial injury allows LDL particles to enter and become trapped in the vessel wall, which is the initiating event in atherosclerosis. Calcification and fibrous cap formation happen later as the plaque matures, and thrombus occlusion is the acute event that can end this process, not start it."},
{id:"P1002",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:1,q:"Which lipoprotein carries most of the cholesterol that becomes deposited in the arterial wall during atherosclerosis?",
 o:["Low density lipoprotein","High density lipoprotein","Triglyceride rich chylomicrons","Serum albumin"],a:0,
 why:"LDL is the main carrier of cholesterol into the arterial intima and is the primary target of lipid lowering therapy. HDL removes cholesterol from tissues rather than depositing it, chylomicrons mainly transport dietary fat rather than driving plaque cholesterol content, and albumin transports many substances but is not a lipoprotein involved in this process."},
{id:"P1003",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:1,q:"Which of the following is a nonmodifiable risk factor for coronary artery disease?",
 o:["Family history of premature coronary disease","Cigarette smoking","Uncontrolled hypertension","Sedentary lifestyle"],a:0,
 why:"Family history reflects genetic and inherited risk that cannot be changed by the patient. Smoking, hypertension control, and physical activity level are all behaviors or conditions that can be modified through treatment and lifestyle change."},
{id:"P1004",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient has a calculated 10 year ASCVD risk score of 12 percent. Which risk category does this represent?",
 o:["Intermediate risk","Low risk","Borderline risk","High risk"],a:0,
 why:"A 10 year risk of 7.5 to 19.9 percent is classified as intermediate risk under current risk stratification categories. Low risk is under 5 percent, borderline risk is 5 to 7.4 percent, and high risk is 20 percent or higher, so 12 percent falls into the intermediate range."},
{id:"P1005",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:2,q:"Which finding is considered a risk enhancing factor that can support starting statin therapy in a patient with borderline ASCVD risk?",
 o:["Chronic kidney disease","Occasional alcohol use","Blood pressure well controlled on one medication","A resting heart rate of 70 beats per minute"],a:0,
 why:"Chronic kidney disease is a recognized risk enhancing factor that can tip a borderline risk decision toward starting a statin. The other findings, occasional alcohol use, well controlled blood pressure, and a normal resting heart rate, are not established risk enhancers and do not carry the same weight in this decision."},
{id:"P1006",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient has abdominal obesity, a triglyceride level of 180 mg/dL, HDL of 38 mg/dL, blood pressure of 138/88, and a fasting glucose of 108 mg/dL. This combination of findings is best described as which condition?",
 o:["Metabolic syndrome","Familial hypercholesterolemia","Isolated hypertriglyceridemia","Chronic kidney disease"],a:0,
 why:"Metabolic syndrome is diagnosed when a patient has at least three of five criteria, including abdominal obesity, elevated triglycerides, low HDL, elevated blood pressure, and elevated fasting glucose, all of which are present here. Familial hypercholesterolemia is defined by a markedly elevated LDL rather than this pattern, isolated hypertriglyceridemia does not account for the other abnormal findings, and chronic kidney disease is not suggested by any of the values given."},
{id:"P1007",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:2,q:"A 55 year old with intermediate ASCVD risk and an LDL of 130 mg/dL is unsure about starting a statin. A coronary artery calcium score returns as zero. What does this result most reasonably support?",
 o:["It is reasonable to withhold statin therapy for now and reassess risk in several years","Start high intensity statin therapy immediately based on the LDL value alone","Add ezetimibe to statin therapy right away","No further cardiovascular risk assessment will ever be needed"],a:0,
 why:"A coronary artery calcium score of zero in an intermediate risk patient supports deferring statin therapy and rechecking risk in the future, since it suggests a lower current burden of atherosclerosis than the risk score alone implies. Starting high intensity therapy based on the LDL number alone ignores the additional information the calcium score provides, adding ezetimibe skips ahead of even starting a statin, and assuming no further assessment is ever needed overstates what a single zero score means since risk can still change over time."},
{id:"P1008",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:2,q:"A lipid panel shows a total cholesterol of 220 mg/dL, HDL of 40 mg/dL, and triglycerides of 150 mg/dL. What is the non-HDL cholesterol?",
 o:["180 mg/dL","150 mg/dL","220 mg/dL","30 mg/dL"],a:0,
 why:"Non-HDL cholesterol is calculated as total cholesterol minus HDL, which is 220 minus 40, equal to 180 mg/dL. Using the triglyceride value of 150 mistakes it for the non-HDL calculation, using the total cholesterol of 220 alone forgets to subtract HDL, and 30 does not reflect any correct step in this formula."},
{id:"P1009",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:2,q:"Which mechanism best explains why cigarette smoking accelerates atherosclerosis?",
 o:["It causes endothelial dysfunction and oxidative stress that promote plaque formation","It mechanically abrades the inner vessel wall","It increases HDL production","It decreases platelet aggregation"],a:0,
 why:"Smoking damages the endothelium and increases oxidative stress, both of which promote lipid deposition and plaque development. Smoking does not work through mechanical abrasion, and it actually lowers HDL and increases platelet aggregation rather than the opposite, making those two options factually reversed."},
{id:"P1010",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:3,q:"A 60 year old with type 2 diabetes has an LDL of 110 mg/dL, a blood pressure of 150/95 that is untreated, and a calculated 10 year ASCVD risk of 22 percent. The patient asks why a statin is being recommended when the LDL is not very high. Which explanation best addresses the reasoning behind this recommendation?",
 o:["Statin benefit is driven mainly by overall ASCVD risk category rather than by LDL level alone, and this patient falls into a high risk group where statin therapy reduces events","Statin therapy is unnecessary because an LDL this low means there is little cardiovascular risk","Statins are only indicated once LDL exceeds 190 mg/dL","Controlling blood pressure alone will fully address this risk without a statin"],a:0,
 why:"Current guidance bases much of the statin therapy decision on overall calculated risk rather than on LDL level alone, and this patient falls into a high risk category driven by diabetes, uncontrolled blood pressure, and a risk score over 20 percent. Assuming a moderate LDL means low risk anchors on a single lab value and ignores the overall risk profile, the 190 mg/dL threshold applies to a different clinical situation involving suspected familial hypercholesterolemia, and blood pressure control addresses only one risk pathway while leaving the lipid related risk untreated."},
{id:"P1011",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:3,q:"A 48 year old woman has an LDL of 145 mg/dL, a 10 year ASCVD risk of 6.5 percent, a father who had a myocardial infarction at age 50, and a high sensitivity CRP of 3.2 mg/L. How should these additional findings be factored into her risk stratification?",
 o:["The family history of premature coronary disease and the elevated CRP are risk enhancing factors that can support statin therapy even though her calculated risk alone is borderline","Because her calculated risk is below 7.5 percent, no other information can change the treatment decision","The elevated CRP alone is diagnostic of active coronary artery disease","Family history only counts as a risk enhancer when the relative was diagnosed after age 65"],a:0,
 why:"Premature coronary disease in a first degree relative, defined as before age 55 in men, and an elevated inflammatory marker such as CRP are both recognized risk enhancing factors that can shift management in a borderline risk patient toward statin therapy. Dismissing any role for risk enhancers ignores how the risk categories are meant to be used, treating CRP as diagnostic overstates what a nonspecific inflammatory marker can tell you, and reversing the age criterion misstates the actual definition of premature disease, which requires an early rather than late age of onset."},
{id:"P1012",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:3,q:"A coronary lesion on angiography is described as having a thin fibrous cap and a large lipid core, without causing a severe reduction in the vessel lumen. What is the clinical significance of this description?",
 o:["This is a vulnerable plaque with a high risk of rupture and acute thrombosis despite the lack of a severe stenosis","Because the lesion does not cause a critical narrowing, it poses little immediate risk","A thick fibrous cap would be more dangerous than a thin one","Lipid core size does not influence how a plaque behaves"],a:0,
 why:"A thin cap over a large lipid core describes a vulnerable plaque, which can rupture and trigger acute thrombosis even when it is not causing a tight stenosis. Assuming low risk based on the percent narrowing ignores plaque composition as a major driver of risk, a thick cap is actually more stable rather than more dangerous, and the size of the lipid core does directly influence plaque stability rather than being irrelevant."},
{id:"P1013",d:"D3",c:"D3.C1",dok:3,q:"A clinician is using a standard risk calculator to estimate a patient 10 year ASCVD risk. Which patient characteristic makes the calculator less reliable for guiding therapy in that individual?",
 o:["An LDL of 220 mg/dL suggestive of possible familial hypercholesterolemia","An age of 45 years","A blood pressure of 130/80 while on treatment","A nonsmoking status"],a:0,
 why:"A markedly elevated LDL suggestive of familial hypercholesterolemia is a situation where standard risk calculators can underestimate true lifetime risk, so these patients are generally treated as high risk regardless of the calculated score. Age, treated blood pressure, and nonsmoking status are simply normal inputs the calculator is designed to use, not exceptions that limit its reliability."},
{id:"P1014",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:1,q:"Which type of angina results from coronary artery spasm rather than a fixed atherosclerotic obstruction, and often occurs at rest during early morning hours?",
 o:["Prinzmetal, or variant, angina","Stable angina","Unstable angina","Silent ischemia"],a:0,
 why:"Prinzmetal angina is caused by transient coronary vasospasm rather than a fixed lesion, and it classically occurs at rest, often overnight or in the early morning. Stable angina is triggered by exertion from a fixed lesion, unstable angina involves plaque rupture and thrombus rather than primary spasm, and silent ischemia refers to ischemia without perceived symptoms, not a spasm mechanism."},
{id:"P1015",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:1,q:"Which term describes myocardial ischemia that occurs without the patient perceiving any chest pain or associated symptoms?",
 o:["Silent ischemia","Stable angina","The Levine sign","An anginal equivalent"],a:0,
 why:"Silent ischemia refers to objective evidence of ischemia occurring without any perceived symptoms at all. Stable angina by definition involves a symptom pattern, the Levine sign is a clenched fist gesture patients make when describing chest pain rather than a type of ischemia, and an anginal equivalent refers to a different symptom, such as dyspnea, standing in for pain rather than a complete absence of any symptom."},
{id:"P1016",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:2,q:"A 70 year old woman with diabetes presents with fatigue, nausea, and mild shortness of breath but denies any chest pain. Her troponin is elevated. Which concept best explains this presentation?",
 o:["An atypical presentation of ACS, which is more common in women and in patients with diabetes due to differences in pain perception","This cannot be ACS because chest pain must be present for the diagnosis","The symptoms point to a pulmonary rather than cardiac cause since chest pain is absent","An elevated troponin without chest pain is most likely a false positive result"],a:0,
 why:"Women and patients with diabetes, particularly those with autonomic neuropathy, often present with atypical symptoms such as fatigue, nausea, or dyspnea instead of classic chest pain, and the elevated troponin supports a cardiac cause here. Requiring chest pain for the diagnosis applies too strict a rule, assuming a pulmonary cause based only on the absence of pain closes off the workup prematurely, and dismissing a reliable biomarker because it does not match an expected symptom pattern ignores objective evidence."},
{id:"P1017",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient reports chest tightness only when climbing more than two flights of stairs quickly or during marked exertion, which resolves with rest, and has no symptoms with ordinary walking on level ground. Which classification best fits this pattern?",
 o:["Mild angina with only slight limitation of ordinary physical activity","Angina that occurs at rest","Angina with marked limitation walking even one level block","Inability to perform any physical activity without chest pain"],a:0,
 why:"Symptoms that occur only with strenuous or rapid exertion, sparing ordinary activity like level walking, fit a mild functional class with only slight limitation. Angina at rest represents a much more severe and unstable pattern, marked limitation with walking a single block describes greater functional impairment than this patient has, and inability to perform any activity without symptoms describes the most severe class, none of which match this description."},
{id:"P1018",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient with a history of stable angina reports that chest pain now occurs with far less exertion than before, lasts longer than usual, and once occurred while sitting quietly. What does this change in pattern most likely represent?",
 o:["A crescendo pattern suggesting progression toward unstable angina","A normal variation of stable angina that needs no further evaluation","Prinzmetal angina, since chest pain occurred at rest","An anxiety related symptom unrelated to cardiac disease"],a:0,
 why:"An increase in frequency, duration, or occurrence at rest represents a crescendo pattern that signals progression from stable to unstable angina and needs prompt evaluation. Treating this as a normal variation dismisses an important red flag, labeling it as Prinzmetal angina based on a single rest episode ignores the overall pattern of worsening exertional symptoms, and attributing it to anxiety without evaluation risks missing a true cardiac change."},
{id:"P1019",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient asks why nitroglycerin relieves anginal chest pain. What is the best explanation?",
 o:["It dilates veins and coronary arteries, which reduces preload and improves coronary blood flow while lowering myocardial oxygen demand","It directly dissolves the atherosclerotic plaque causing the pain","It increases heart rate to improve overall cardiac output","It thins the blood in a manner similar to aspirin"],a:0,
 why:"Nitroglycerin works mainly through vasodilation, reducing venous return and preload while improving coronary flow, both of which lower the oxygen demand driving the pain. It does not dissolve plaque, and while it can cause a reflex increase in heart rate as a side effect, that is not its therapeutic mechanism, and it works through vasodilation rather than through an antiplatelet effect like aspirin."},
{id:"P1020",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:2,q:"Which description of chest discomfort is most consistent with a cardiac rather than a musculoskeletal cause?",
 o:["Pressure or squeezing that radiates to the jaw or left arm and worsens with exertion","Sharp pain that worsens with a deep breath and with pressing on the chest wall","Pain that is reproduced by pressing directly on a specific rib","Brief stabbing pain lasting only a few seconds with no relation to activity"],a:0,
 why:"Pressure or squeezing discomfort that radiates and is brought on by exertion is the classic pattern of cardiac ischemic pain. Pain that worsens with breathing or palpation, or that is reproduced by pressing on a rib, points to a musculoskeletal or pleuritic cause, and a brief stabbing sensation unrelated to activity is a nonspecific pattern that does not fit typical angina."},
{id:"P1021",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:2,q:"In a patient with known coronary artery disease, which symptom should be treated with the same urgency as chest pain, as an anginal equivalent?",
 o:["New, unexplained shortness of breath on exertion","Occasional heartburn after eating spicy food","Mild ankle swelling that has been present and unchanged for months","Morning joint stiffness"],a:0,
 why:"New exertional dyspnea can represent myocardial ischemia without pain, especially in older adults and patients with diabetes, and should be evaluated with the same urgency as chest pain in a patient with known coronary disease. Heartburn after specific foods, longstanding stable ankle swelling, and joint stiffness are chronic or clearly unrelated symptoms that do not carry the same significance as a new anginal equivalent."},
{id:"P1022",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:3,q:"A 74 year old with type 2 diabetes and peripheral neuropathy is seen for a routine visit. An ECG obtained for preoperative clearance shows new Q waves in the inferior leads that were not present six months ago, though the patient denies any history of chest pain. What does this finding most likely represent?",
 o:["A silent myocardial infarction that occurred without recognized symptoms, which is more common in patients with diabetic neuropathy","A normal age related ECG variant that needs no further evaluation","A technical error in lead placement, since the patient has no symptoms","An old finding unrelated to coronary disease, since diabetes does not affect cardiac risk"],a:0,
 why:"New Q waves indicate prior myocardial injury, and patients with diabetic neuropathy can have blunted pain perception that allows an infarction to occur without recognized chest pain. Calling this a normal variant or a lead placement error dismisses a genuinely new and significant finding without evidence, and diabetes is in fact a major risk factor for coronary disease, not one that is unrelated to it."},
{id:"P1023",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient with stable angina develops chest tightness while shoveling snow in cold weather. The pain resolves within about three minutes of stopping the activity and using one sublingual nitroglycerin tablet, and does not recur for the rest of the day. How should this episode be classified?",
 o:["Typical stable angina, since it was triggered by a predictable increase in demand from cold exposure and exertion and resolved quickly","Unstable angina, since any need for an extra nitroglycerin dose signals instability","Myocardial infarction, since chest pain occurred during the episode","Prinzmetal angina, since the episode happened outdoors"],a:0,
 why:"Cold exposure combined with exertion is a classic and predictable trigger for demand related ischemia, and quick resolution with rest and nitroglycerin is consistent with typical stable angina rather than a change in pattern. Needing one extra nitroglycerin dose for an expected trigger is not itself a marker of instability, chest pain alone without prolonged symptoms or objective evidence of injury does not indicate infarction, and Prinzmetal angina is defined by a rest and nocturnal pattern with ECG changes rather than simply occurring outdoors."},
{id:"P1024",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:3,q:"A 40 year old with no traditional cardiac risk factors reports recurrent chest pain at rest, often between two and four in the morning, that resolves spontaneously within minutes. An ECG obtained during one episode shows transient ST elevation that resolves as the pain resolves, and troponin remains normal. Angiography shows no significant fixed stenosis. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
 o:["Prinzmetal, or vasospastic, angina","STEMI, since transient ST elevation was documented","Stable angina, since the pain resolved on its own","Anxiety related chest pain, since angiography was normal"],a:0,
 why:"Recurrent rest pain with transient, reversible ST elevation, a normal troponin, and no fixed obstructive lesion is the classic picture of coronary vasospasm. Calling this a STEMI misapplies criteria meant for a fixed, ongoing ischemic event rather than a transient reversible one, labeling it stable angina ignores the rest pattern and ECG changes that do not fit that diagnosis, and attributing it to anxiety dismisses objective ECG evidence of true ischemia."},
{id:"P1025",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient with unstable angina is being treated when, over the next hour, the pain becomes constant, troponin rises from normal to markedly elevated, and new ST depression develops in five leads. How should this evolution be interpreted?",
 o:["Progression from unstable angina to NSTEMI, reflecting ongoing myocardial injury","Expected fluctuation of unstable angina that does not represent a new diagnosis","A STEMI, based on the new ECG changes","A likely lab error in the troponin that should simply be repeated before any action is taken"],a:0,
 why:"A rising troponin along with worsening pain and new widespread ST depression indicates that myocardial injury has now occurred, meaning the diagnosis has evolved from unstable angina to NSTEMI. Calling this expected fluctuation dismisses clear biomarker evidence of injury, labeling it a STEMI misapplies criteria that require ST elevation rather than depression, and assuming a lab error ignores corroborating ECG and symptom changes that support a true evolving event."},
{id:"P1026",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient with known three vessel coronary disease reports several weeks of increasing chest pain frequency, now occurring with minimal exertion and occasionally at rest. A troponin and ECG obtained in the office during a pain free period are both normal. How should this presentation be classified and prioritized?",
 o:["Unstable angina based on the crescendo symptom pattern, warranting urgent cardiology referral despite the normal office troponin and ECG","Stable angina, because the office troponin and ECG obtained today are normal","No cardiac concern, since troponin is the definitive test and it is normal","A new secondary condition such as anxiety, since objective testing is unremarkable"],a:0,
 why:"Unstable angina is defined by history and symptom pattern and often has a normal troponin and ECG when the patient is not actively symptomatic, so a normal snapshot obtained between episodes does not rule it out, and this crescendo pattern warrants urgent referral. Relying only on a symptom free office assessment, treating troponin alone as definitive, or shifting to an alternative diagnosis all ignore the well established clinical definition of unstable angina, which depends heavily on the reported symptom pattern."},
{id:"P1027",d:"D3",c:"D3.C2",dok:4,q:"A patient with stable angina controlled on a beta blocker calls the clinic reporting three episodes of chest pressure at rest overnight, each lasting about ten minutes and relieved by an extra dose of sublingual nitroglycerin. The patient feels well at the time of the call and has no current pain. What is the most appropriate action?",
 o:["Instruct the patient to go to the emergency department immediately, since rest pain lasting several minutes represents a change consistent with unstable angina requiring urgent workup","Schedule a routine office visit within the next few days since the patient currently feels well","Advise the patient to increase the home nitroglycerin dose and call back only if pain becomes more frequent","Reassure the patient that this is a normal part of stable angina as long as nitroglycerin relieves it"],a:0,
 why:"New rest pain lasting several minutes and requiring extra nitroglycerin is a crescendo pattern that meets criteria for unstable angina, so the patient needs urgent evaluation rather than a delayed visit. Waiting for a routine appointment treats the current absence of pain as reassuring when the pattern itself is the red flag, having the patient self titrate nitroglycerin at home delays needed care for a potential ACS presentation, and normalizing the pattern ignores that a change from the patient known stable baseline is precisely what defines instability."},
{id:"P1028",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:1,q:"Approximately how soon after myocardial injury does a high sensitivity troponin typically begin to rise above the upper reference limit?",
 o:["About 2 to 4 hours","Immediately, within minutes of symptom onset","About 24 to 48 hours","After about 5 to 7 days"],a:0,
 why:"High sensitivity troponin typically begins to rise within about 2 to 4 hours of injury, which is why serial measurements are used rather than a single draw at presentation. Expecting an immediate rise at symptom onset ignores this lag time, 24 to 48 hours is closer to when older biomarkers such as CK-MB tend to peak, and 5 to 7 days is closer to when troponin levels are returning toward baseline rather than first rising."},
{id:"P1029",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:1,q:"Which diagnostic study directly visualizes the coronary arteries and is considered the gold standard for identifying the location and severity of coronary obstruction?",
 o:["Invasive coronary angiography","Transthoracic echocardiogram","Exercise treadmill testing without imaging","Chest x-ray"],a:0,
 why:"Invasive coronary angiography directly images the coronary lumen and is the gold standard for defining the location and severity of blockages. Echocardiography assesses wall motion and cardiac function rather than the coronary lumen directly, exercise testing without imaging only infers ischemia indirectly through symptoms and ECG changes, and a chest x-ray does not show coronary artery anatomy at all."},
{id:"P1030",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient high sensitivity troponin at presentation is within normal limits, and the repeat value one hour later is also normal with a change well below the assay defined threshold. Using a validated 0/1 hour protocol, how should this result be interpreted?",
 o:["Myocardial infarction is ruled out, and further workup for an alternative cause of chest pain should be pursued if clinically indicated","The patient definitely has unstable angina and needs immediate catheterization","A third troponin at 6 hours is mandatory before any disposition decision, regardless of clinical risk","The normal results are unreliable and should be disregarded"],a:0,
 why:"A validated 0/1 hour protocol with two values below threshold and a minimal change is designed to safely rule out MI in appropriate patients, allowing evaluation of other causes of chest pain. Assuming unstable angina requiring catheterization overinterprets a rule out result, requiring a mandatory extra draw regardless of clinical risk applies a rigid rule not supported by the validated protocol, and disregarding the results without justification ignores the evidence behind the algorithm."},
{id:"P1031",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:2,q:"What is the primary purpose of calculating a TIMI or GRACE risk score in a patient with a non-ST elevation acute coronary syndrome?",
 o:["To estimate the risk of death or recurrent ischemic events and help guide the timing of invasive management","To determine the exact culprit coronary artery before angiography","To decide whether the patient needs supplemental oxygen","To calculate the required dose of anticoagulation"],a:0,
 why:"TIMI and GRACE scores estimate a patient risk of death or recurrent ischemic events and are used to help decide how urgently to pursue an invasive strategy. These scores do not identify which specific artery is involved, they do not guide the oxygen decision, and they are not used to calculate medication dosing."},
{id:"P1032",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient with a resting ECG showing a left bundle branch block needs evaluation for suspected coronary disease but cannot exercise because of a knee injury. Which testing approach is most appropriate?",
 o:["Pharmacologic stress testing with imaging, such as a vasodilator nuclear or echocardiographic study","Standard exercise treadmill testing without imaging","Repeated 12 lead ECGs at rest on separate days","No further testing, since angiography is never indicated after a normal troponin"],a:0,
 why:"A pharmacologic stress test with imaging avoids the need for exercise and avoids the false positive ST changes that a left bundle branch block can cause on a standard exercise ECG. Exercise testing is not possible given the knee injury and would also be unreliable in the setting of this baseline ECG abnormality, resting ECGs alone cannot provoke or detect inducible ischemia, and ruling out further testing based only on a normal troponin ignores that this evaluation is intended to assess suspected disease outside of an acute event."},
{id:"P1033",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient troponin rises from 20 ng/L to 340 ng/L over three hours, accompanied by new ST depression and ongoing chest pain. What does this pattern most likely indicate?",
 o:["Acute myocardial injury consistent with NSTEMI","Chronic kidney disease causing a stable baseline troponin elevation","A false positive assay result requiring no action","Demand ischemia from anemia alone, unrelated to a primary coronary event"],a:0,
 why:"A large, rapid rise in troponin along with new ST depression and continued pain points to acute myocardial injury consistent with NSTEMI. Chronic kidney disease typically produces a stable, unchanging troponin elevation rather than this kind of rapid rise, dismissing the result as a false positive ignores a pattern with strong corroborating evidence, and attributing it only to anemia is a less supported alternative explanation given the clear dynamic rise and ECG changes pointing to a primary coronary event."},
{id:"P1034",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient with chest pain and possible heart failure has an elevated BNP level. What does this finding best support in the overall workup?",
 o:["Ongoing ventricular wall stress, which can result from ischemia related dysfunction or volume overload, helping assess for heart failure rather than confirming ACS directly","A definitive diagnosis of myocardial infarction","The exact location of a coronary blockage","The need to stop all antianginal medications"],a:0,
 why:"BNP reflects ventricular wall stress and is useful for assessing heart failure, but it is not a marker of myocardial necrosis and does not confirm ACS on its own. Confusing BNP with a marker of infarction such as troponin misassigns its role, BNP does not provide any information about the location of a coronary lesion, and an elevated BNP does not by itself indicate that antianginal medications should be stopped."},
{id:"P1035",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:2,q:"A 12 lead ECG shows ST depression in leads V1 through V4 with tall R waves, and no ST elevation is seen anywhere else on the tracing. What should the nurse suspect this pattern may represent?",
 o:["A posterior STEMI, since anterior ST depression with tall R waves can be a mirror image reciprocal change, and posterior leads should be obtained","An anterior STEMI, since the changes are located in the anterior leads","A normal variant that requires no further evaluation","Right ventricular infarction, since V1 is involved"],a:0,
 why:"ST depression with tall R waves in the anterior leads can be a reciprocal, mirror image finding of a posterior wall injury, so posterior leads should be added to confirm this. Calling it an anterior STEMI misreads a reciprocal change as the primary injury pattern, dismissing it as a normal variant ignores a recognized ischemic pattern, and right ventricular infarction is assessed with right sided leads such as V4R, not by the standard V1 placement."},
{id:"P1036",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient with NSTEMI has a GRACE score in the high risk range, ongoing chest pain despite maximal medical therapy, and dynamic ST changes on the monitor. Which approach to the timing of angiography best matches this risk profile?",
 o:["An immediate invasive strategy within about 2 hours, since the patient meets very high risk features","A routine invasive strategy delayed 25 to 72 hours, appropriate for a lower risk category than this patient","A conservative, ischemia guided strategy with stress testing before considering angiography","No angiography, since the first troponin was only mildly elevated"],a:0,
 why:"Ongoing pain despite therapy along with dynamic ECG changes and a high GRACE score are very high risk features that call for an immediate invasive strategy within about 2 hours. A delayed routine invasive approach is appropriate for lower acuity presentations, not this very high risk picture, a conservative ischemia guided strategy underestimates the urgency suggested by ongoing pain and ECG changes, and withholding angiography based on one mildly elevated troponin anchors on a single value while ignoring the rest of the high risk clinical picture."},
{id:"P1037",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient with end stage renal disease on hemodialysis has a chronically elevated baseline troponin. The patient now presents with chest pain, and troponin is drawn. Which approach best determines whether this represents a new acute event?",
 o:["Compare the current troponin to the patient known baseline and look for a significant rise or fall pattern along with ECG changes and symptoms","Conclude that no acute event is occurring because troponin is expected to be elevated in renal disease","Diagnose an acute MI based on the single elevated value alone, since it exceeds the reference range","Avoid checking troponin altogether in dialysis patients since it is considered unreliable"],a:0,
 why:"In patients with a known chronically elevated troponin, comparing the new value to the established baseline and looking for a significant change, along with correlating ECG and symptoms, is the appropriate way to detect a new acute event. Assuming no acute process because elevation is expected dismisses the possibility of a true new injury on top of a chronic baseline, diagnosing MI from a single absolute value ignores the known chronic elevation, and avoiding troponin testing altogether discards a useful trend based tool rather than simply interpreting it correctly."},
{id:"P1038",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient reports that chest pain has resolved, the ECG is now normal, but the troponin returns markedly elevated. How should the care team interpret this combination of findings?",
 o:["Myocardial injury has occurred even though the pain and ECG have normalized, and further workup for the cause and extent of injury is warranted","The elevated troponin can be disregarded since the ECG is now normal","The normal ECG proves the troponin elevation is a laboratory error","No infarction occurred because the pain resolved"],a:0,
 why:"A markedly elevated troponin indicates that myocardial injury has occurred, and this remains true even if pain and the ECG have since normalized, so further evaluation of the cause and extent of injury is needed. Disregarding the troponin because one other test normalized ignores solid biomarker evidence, assuming the troponin must be an error based on the ECG reverses which finding should be trusted, and equating symptom relief with no injury misunderstands how these findings relate to each other."},
{id:"P1039",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient with a low risk assessment score, normal serial troponin values, and no ischemic ECG changes is being prepared for discharge. Which follow up plan best reflects current practice?",
 o:["Outpatient follow up, with consideration of further noninvasive testing such as stress testing if symptoms recur or risk factors warrant it","Immediate inpatient catheterization regardless of the low risk assessment","No follow up is needed at all, since biomarkers were normal","Repeat troponin testing every day for a week before any discharge is considered"],a:0,
 why:"A validated low risk assessment with normal serial troponin and no ischemic ECG changes supports safe discharge with outpatient follow up and consideration of further testing if needed. Proceeding straight to catheterization overtreats a validated low risk classification, assuming no follow up is needed at all underestimates residual risk that these tools are designed to still account for, and repeating troponin daily for a week goes well beyond what validated rule out protocols call for."},
{id:"P1040",d:"D3",c:"D3.C3",dok:4,q:"A patient presents with intermittent chest discomfort for two days. The initial ECG is nondiagnostic, and the first troponin is normal. A one hour troponin rises modestly but stays under the diagnostic delta threshold, and the patient continues to have pain while blood pressure and heart rate remain stable. What is the most appropriate next step?",
 o:["Continue serial troponin measurement per protocol, maintain continuous ECG monitoring, and use ongoing clinical judgment rather than discharging based on the first two values alone","Discharge the patient because the 0/1 hour protocol technically did not meet the positive threshold","Proceed directly to fibrinolytic therapy since two troponin values have already been obtained","Repeat only a chest x-ray and stop further cardiac testing since troponin has not crossed the diagnostic threshold"],a:0,
 why:"Continued serial troponin testing, ongoing ECG monitoring, and clinical judgment are appropriate here because ongoing symptoms with a modestly rising trend are a warning sign that a rule out algorithm alone is not meant to override. Discharging based on a technical rule out ignores the persistent symptoms and rising trend, giving fibrinolytic therapy skips the ECG criteria that this therapy requires and misapplies STEMI specific treatment to a presentation without ST elevation, and stopping cardiac workup in favor of an unrelated test abandons evaluation of a patient who still has active symptoms and a concerning trend."},
{id:"P1041",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:1,q:"Which medication is given to nearly all patients with suspected ACS at first contact, unless contraindicated, primarily for its antiplatelet effect?",
 o:["Aspirin","Warfarin","Unfractionated heparin","Atorvastatin"],a:0,
 why:"Aspirin produces an immediate antiplatelet effect and is given to nearly all ACS patients at first contact unless contraindicated. Warfarin is not used for immediate antiplatelet effect in this setting, heparin is an anticoagulant with a different mechanism and is not given identically to every patient in this same first contact fashion, and a statin does not provide an immediate antiplatelet effect at all."},
{id:"P1042",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:1,q:"Supplemental oxygen should be administered to a patient with suspected ACS under which condition, according to current guidance?",
 o:["Oxygen saturation below 90 percent","All patients regardless of oxygen saturation","Only if the patient specifically requests it","Whenever chest pain is present regardless of oxygen saturation"],a:0,
 why:"Current guidance reserves supplemental oxygen for patients who are hypoxic, generally with a saturation below 90 percent, since routine oxygen in normoxic patients has not been shown to help and may cause harm. Giving oxygen to all patients regardless of saturation reflects an outdated habit, basing the decision on patient request is not a clinical criterion, and giving oxygen simply because chest pain is present without checking saturation applies a reflexive habit rather than the actual indication."},
{id:"P1043",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient with STEMI at a PCI capable hospital is being prepared for the cath lab. Which medication combination reflects standard acute pharmacologic therapy prior to intervention?",
 o:["Aspirin, a P2Y12 inhibitor, and an anticoagulant such as heparin","Aspirin and warfarin only","A P2Y12 inhibitor alone without aspirin","A thrombolytic plus a P2Y12 inhibitor"],a:0,
 why:"Standard preparation for primary PCI includes aspirin, a P2Y12 inhibitor, and an anticoagulant such as heparin to support antiplatelet and anticoagulant coverage during the procedure. Warfarin is not used acutely in this setting, a P2Y12 inhibitor alone leaves antiplatelet coverage incomplete, and giving a thrombolytic is not appropriate when primary PCI is available promptly at a capable center."},
{id:"P1044",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient receiving nitroglycerin for chest pain is found to have taken sildenafil for erectile dysfunction two hours earlier. What is the priority nursing concern?",
 o:["Severe, life threatening hypotension from the combined vasodilating effects of both drugs","An allergic reaction between the two medications","Decreased effectiveness of the nitroglycerin","Increased risk of bleeding"],a:0,
 why:"Nitrates combined with a PDE5 inhibitor such as sildenafil can cause profound, dangerous hypotension because both drugs cause vasodilation through related pathways. This interaction is not an allergic reaction, it does not reduce the effect of nitroglycerin, since the vasodilating effect is actually potentiated rather than blunted, and it is not primarily a bleeding risk interaction, which would apply to a different class of medications."},
{id:"P1045",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:2,q:"Morphine is used cautiously during acute management of ACS mainly because of which concern reflected in current guidance?",
 o:["It may mask ongoing ischemic symptoms and has been associated with reduced absorption of oral antiplatelet drugs and worse outcomes in some studies, so it is reserved for pain not relieved by nitrates","It directly worsens coronary artery spasm","It is contraindicated in all chest pain regardless of severity","It should be given before nitroglycerin in every patient"],a:0,
 why:"Morphine can mask symptoms that would otherwise signal ongoing ischemia and has been linked to slower absorption of oral antiplatelet agents along with worse outcomes in some studies, which is why it is reserved for pain that does not respond to nitrates. It does not directly cause coronary spasm, it is not banned outright for every patient with chest pain, and reversing the usual order to give it before nitroglycerin does not match the stepwise approach current guidance supports."},
{id:"P1046",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient with an inferior STEMI develops hypotension after receiving sublingual nitroglycerin. Which additional finding should prompt the nurse to suspect right ventricular infarction?",
 o:["Jugular venous distension with clear lung sounds","Crackles heard throughout both lung fields","Bounding peripheral pulses","Warm, dry skin"],a:0,
 why:"Jugular venous distension with clear lungs reflects the classic right ventricular infarction triad of hypotension, elevated jugular pressure, and clear lung fields, since the problem is right sided and preload dependent rather than pulmonary. Crackles throughout the lungs suggest left sided failure and pulmonary edema, which is the opposite pattern, bounding pulses are a nonspecific finding not part of this triad, and warm dry skin does not fit a hypotensive, preload dependent presentation like right ventricular infarction."},
{id:"P1047",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:2,q:"Right ventricular infarction changes the acute management plan primarily by making which class of medication relatively contraindicated?",
 o:["Nitrates and other preload reducing agents","Aspirin","Beta blockers whenever the heart rate is normal and there is no shock","P2Y12 inhibitors"],a:0,
 why:"Right ventricular infarction depends on adequate preload to maintain cardiac output, so nitrates and other preload reducing drugs can cause severe hypotension and are relatively contraindicated. Aspirin and P2Y12 inhibitors remain indicated as part of standard ACS therapy, and beta blockers are used with caution based on hemodynamics rather than being specifically restricted by the preload issue that defines this complication."},
{id:"P1048",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient develops a new S3 heart sound, bilateral crackles, and hypotension two days after a large anterior MI. Which complication is most consistent with this presentation?",
 o:["Cardiogenic shock from extensive left ventricular dysfunction","Pericarditis","Free wall rupture","Right ventricular infarction"],a:0,
 why:"An S3, bilateral crackles, and hypotension after a large anterior MI point to pump failure and cardiogenic shock from extensive left ventricular damage. Pericarditis typically presents with pleuritic pain and a friction rub rather than this pattern, free wall rupture usually presents suddenly with tamponade and hemodynamic collapse rather than this gradual picture, and right ventricular infarction would present with clear lungs and jugular venous distension rather than pulmonary crackles."},
{id:"P1049",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:2,q:"Which finding should prompt the nurse to hold a scheduled dose of a beta blocker in a patient recovering from an acute MI?",
 o:["A heart rate of 48 beats per minute with signs of poor perfusion","A heart rate of 78 with a normal blood pressure","Mild fatigue reported by the patient","A blood pressure of 128/76"],a:0,
 why:"A slow heart rate accompanied by signs of poor perfusion indicates the patient cannot tolerate additional negative chronotropic effect, so the dose should be held and the provider notified. A heart rate of 78 with normal blood pressure, mild fatigue alone, and a blood pressure of 128/76 are all within an acceptable range and do not by themselves indicate a need to withhold the medication."},
{id:"P1050",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient in the cath lab suddenly becomes hypotensive with distant heart sounds and distended neck veins shortly after a coronary intervention. Which complication should be suspected first?",
 o:["Cardiac tamponade from a coronary perforation","A vasovagal reaction","An allergic reaction to contrast dye","Hypoglycemia"],a:0,
 why:"Sudden hypotension with distant heart sounds and distended neck veins is the classic presentation of cardiac tamponade, which can occur from a coronary perforation during intervention. A vasovagal reaction typically presents with bradycardia and hypotension without this specific triad, an allergic reaction to contrast usually presents with hives or bronchospasm rather than distant heart sounds, and hypoglycemia produces a different symptom pattern unrelated to these findings."},
{id:"P1051",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient with NSTEMI has a heart rate of 52, a blood pressure of 92/58, and cool extremities. The admission order set includes a beta blocker to be given on arrival. What should the nurse do?",
 o:["Hold the beta blocker and notify the provider, since the patient shows signs of hemodynamic compromise that beta blockade could worsen","Give the beta blocker as ordered because it is standard therapy for all ACS patients regardless of hemodynamics","Give a double dose to better control the heart rate","Discontinue the beta blocker permanently without notifying the provider"],a:0,
 why:"Bradycardia, hypotension, and cool extremities indicate hemodynamic compromise that beta blockade could worsen, so the appropriate action is to hold the dose and notify the provider. Giving the medication as ordered applies a standard protocol without considering the patient current hemodynamics, giving a double dose is dangerous and would worsen both the bradycardia and hypotension, and discontinuing the medication permanently without provider input oversteps the nurse role when the correct action is to hold and communicate."},
{id:"P1052",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:3,q:"A STEMI patient received fibrinolytic therapy at a non-PCI facility 90 minutes ago. The patient now has recurrent chest pain, and the ST elevation on the repeat ECG has increased rather than resolved. What does this indicate, and what is the priority action?",
 o:["Failed fibrinolysis, and the patient should be urgently transferred for rescue PCI","Successful reperfusion, so the current plan should continue without change","A normal expected pattern after fibrinolysis that requires no action","The fibrinolytic dose should simply be repeated"],a:0,
 why:"Recurrent pain with worsening rather than resolving ST elevation after fibrinolysis indicates failed reperfusion, which calls for urgent transfer for rescue PCI. Interpreting this as successful reperfusion misreads the data, since resolution of pain and ST elevation are the expected signs of success, dismissing it as a normal pattern ignores a clear red flag, and repeating the fibrinolytic dose is not the recommended approach to a failed lytic attempt."},
{id:"P1053",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:3,q:"A STEMI patient arrives at a non-PCI hospital, and the estimated transfer time to a PCI capable center is 45 minutes, well within recommended timeframes. Which reperfusion strategy is preferred in this situation?",
 o:["Transfer for primary PCI","Immediate fibrinolytic therapy at the current facility","No reperfusion therapy until symptoms resolve on their own","Oral antiplatelet therapy alone without any reperfusion strategy"],a:0,
 why:"When transfer to a PCI capable center can be accomplished quickly, transfer for primary PCI is the preferred strategy because it generally produces better outcomes than fibrinolysis. Fibrinolytic therapy at the original facility is favored mainly when a substantial transfer delay is expected, not when transfer is fast, withholding reperfusion until symptoms resolve on their own creates unsafe delay, and oral antiplatelet therapy alone is not an adequate substitute for a reperfusion strategy in STEMI."},
{id:"P1054",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient with anterior STEMI undergoes successful primary PCI. Twelve hours later, the nurse notes a new harsh holosystolic murmur at the left sternal border, and catheterization data shows a step up in oxygen saturation between the right atrium and pulmonary artery. What complication does this most likely represent?",
 o:["Ventricular septal rupture","Papillary muscle rupture causing mitral regurgitation","Free wall rupture","Pericarditis"],a:0,
 why:"A step up in oxygen saturation between right heart chambers indicates a left to right shunt through a hole in the septum, which along with a new harsh murmur points to ventricular septal rupture. Papillary muscle rupture causes a murmur from mitral regurgitation but does not create this intracardiac oxygen step up pattern, free wall rupture presents with sudden tamponade and hemodynamic collapse rather than a murmur with this finding, and pericarditis produces a friction rub rather than a holosystolic murmur or an oxygen step up."},
{id:"P1055",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient with known severe aortic stenosis develops classic exertional angina. What is the priority consideration when planning nitrate therapy for this patient?",
 o:["Nitrates should be used cautiously or avoided, since preload reduction can cause severe hypotension in the setting of a fixed obstructive valve lesion","Nitrates are first line therapy with no special precaution needed","Nitrates should be given in higher than usual doses to overcome the obstruction","Nitrates are contraindicated only if the patient is also diabetic"],a:0,
 why:"In severe aortic stenosis, the fixed outflow obstruction depends on adequate preload to maintain cardiac output, so reducing preload with nitrates can cause severe, dangerous hypotension. Treating nitrates as routine first line therapy without precaution ignores this well known interaction, increasing the dose to overcome the obstruction is dangerous rather than helpful, and adding a diabetes qualifier introduces an irrelevant condition that has nothing to do with the actual mechanism of concern."},
{id:"P1056",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient two hours after primary PCI performed through the radial artery reports increasing hand pain, numbness, and a cool, pale hand distal to the access site. What should the nurse do first?",
 o:["Assess distal pulses and notify the provider promptly to evaluate for possible radial artery occlusion","Reposition the arm and reassess again in four hours","Apply a warm compress and elevate the extremity without further assessment","Loosen the compression band completely and discontinue monitoring"],a:0,
 why:"New pain, numbness, and a cool pale hand distal to a radial access site are signs of possible vascular compromise, so the priority is to assess pulses and notify the provider promptly. Waiting four hours to reassess delays care for a possible occlusion, applying a warm compress without further assessment does not address the underlying vascular concern, and loosening the band and stopping monitoring skips the proper stepwise assessment needed before adjusting a hemostasis device."},
{id:"P1057",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient being treated for STEMI develops a wide complex tachycardia with a heart rate of 190, a blood pressure of 70/40, and altered mental status. What is the priority intervention?",
 o:["Immediate synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous amiodarone, then reassessment in 15 minutes","Vagal maneuvers","Oral beta blocker administration"],a:0,
 why:"An unstable wide complex tachycardia with hypotension and altered mental status requires immediate synchronized cardioversion rather than a medication that takes time to act. Giving amiodarone and waiting 15 minutes delays needed emergent treatment for an unstable rhythm, vagal maneuvers are appropriate for a stable supraventricular tachycardia rather than unstable ventricular tachycardia, and an oral beta blocker acts far too slowly for this emergent, unstable presentation."},
{id:"P1058",d:"D3",c:"D3.C4",dok:4,q:"A 70 year old with NSTEMI becomes acutely dyspneic fifteen minutes after arrival. Blood pressure drops to 78/50, bilateral crackles are heard, a stat portable chest x-ray shows pulmonary edema, and the heart rate is 118 and irregular, consistent with new atrial fibrillation. What is the most appropriate sequence of priority actions?",
 o:["Support oxygenation and circulation first, including positioning and preparation for possible inotropic or vasopressor support, address the new arrhythmia contributing to hemodynamic compromise, and pursue urgent cardiology involvement for possible cardiogenic shock, rather than following a fixed sequence of oral home medications","Give the scheduled oral beta blocker first to control the heart rate before addressing oxygenation","Wait for the cardiology consult before initiating any oxygen support or hemodynamic intervention","Administer a fluid bolus first to raise the blood pressure, since hypotension is present"],a:0,
 why:"This picture reflects cardiogenic shock with pulmonary edema and a new arrhythmia, so the priority is supporting oxygenation and circulation, addressing the arrhythmia, and getting urgent cardiology involvement rather than following a rigid medication sequence meant for a stable patient. Giving an oral beta blocker first would further reduce contractility and worsen the hypotension in an already unstable patient, waiting for a consult before starting any support creates dangerous delay, and a fluid bolus can worsen pulmonary edema in a patient whose problem is pump failure rather than volume depletion."},
{id:"P1059",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:1,q:"Which medication class is recommended for nearly all post-MI patients to reduce mortality by blocking the cardiac effects of catecholamines?",
 o:["Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Loop diuretics","Alpha blockers"],a:0,
 why:"Beta blockers reduce mortality after MI by blunting the effects of catecholamines on heart rate, contractility, and oxygen demand, and are recommended for nearly all patients unless contraindicated. Calcium channel blockers, loop diuretics, and alpha blockers do not have this same mortality benefit through catecholamine blockade in the general post-MI population."},
{id:"P1060",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:1,q:"Which lifestyle change provides the greatest reduction in cardiovascular risk for a patient who currently smokes?",
 o:["Complete smoking cessation","Switching to a lower tar cigarette brand","Reducing daily cigarette count by half without quitting","Smoking only outdoors"],a:0,
 why:"Complete cessation produces the greatest reduction in cardiovascular risk, since even reduced exposure continues to cause harm. Switching brands or cutting back without quitting still exposes the patient to substantial risk and creates a false sense of having addressed the problem, and smoking only outdoors changes exposure to others but does not reduce the personal cardiovascular risk from inhaling the smoke."},
{id:"P1061",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient one year after drug eluting stent placement is stable on aspirin and a P2Y12 inhibitor, with no bleeding events and no high ischemic risk features. What is an appropriate next step to discuss regarding dual antiplatelet therapy duration?",
 o:["Consider transitioning to single antiplatelet therapy, since standard dual therapy duration for many stable patients is around 12 months","Continue dual antiplatelet therapy indefinitely for every patient regardless of bleeding or ischemic risk","Stop both antiplatelet agents completely at one year","Switch to warfarin in place of both antiplatelet agents"],a:0,
 why:"For many stable patients without high ischemic risk or bleeding concerns, dual antiplatelet therapy for about 12 months followed by transition to a single agent is consistent with current practice. Continuing dual therapy indefinitely for everyone applies a single duration without individualizing to risk, stopping both agents completely removes needed long term antiplatelet protection, and switching to warfarin uses the wrong drug class for this indication."},
{id:"P1062",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:2,q:"A post-MI patient with an LDL of 65 mg/dL on high intensity statin therapy asks whether the statin dose can be lowered since the LDL is already low. What is the best response?",
 o:["High intensity statin therapy should generally be continued regardless of how low the LDL falls in this very high risk secondary prevention group","The dose should be reduced once LDL falls below 70 to avoid lowering it too much","The statin can be stopped entirely since the goal has already been met","Switching to a low intensity statin will maintain the same benefit"],a:0,
 why:"In secondary prevention, the benefit of high intensity statin therapy is tied to the intensity of treatment itself, so it is generally continued even when LDL falls well below typical targets. Reducing the dose based on a made up lower limit is not supported by current guidance, stopping the statin removes ongoing protective therapy, and switching to a low intensity statin assumes intensity does not matter, which is not consistent with the evidence behind these recommendations."},
{id:"P1063",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:2,q:"Which additional therapy should be considered for a very high risk secondary prevention patient whose LDL remains at 85 mg/dL despite maximally tolerated statin therapy?",
 o:["Adding ezetimibe as the next step in a stepwise approach","Adding a second high intensity statin from a different class","Stopping the statin and starting a fibrate alone","No further therapy is needed, since LDL is below 100"],a:0,
 why:"Ezetimibe is the recommended next step when LDL remains above goal despite maximally tolerated statin therapy in a very high risk secondary prevention patient. Combining two high intensity statins is not standard practice, a fibrate primarily targets triglycerides rather than LDL and does not replace statin therapy for this purpose, and using a 100 mg/dL threshold applies an older, less stringent target than the very high risk goal used in current guidance."},
{id:"P1064",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:2,q:"Which patient is an appropriate candidate for referral to a supervised outpatient cardiac rehabilitation program after MI?",
 o:["A hemodynamically stable patient without uncontrolled arrhythmias, who is ready to begin a structured, monitored exercise and education program","A patient who is still in cardiogenic shock","A patient who has already returned to full, unrestricted activity independently and reports no further needs","A patient with uncontrolled unstable angina"],a:0,
 why:"A hemodynamically stable patient without uncontrolled arrhythmias is an appropriate candidate to begin a structured, monitored rehabilitation program. A patient still in cardiogenic shock or with uncontrolled unstable angina needs further stabilization before starting an exercise program, and a patient who has already returned to full activity independently, while doing well, does not represent the typical candidacy criteria this referral decision is based on, since the point is stability rather than current activity level alone."},
{id:"P1065",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient with diabetes and established coronary artery disease is already taking metformin and a statin. Which additional medication class has evidence for reducing cardiovascular events in this population, per current guidance?",
 o:["An SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 receptor agonist","A sulfonylurea","A thiazolidinedione","Insulin as the automatic next step regardless of other options"],a:0,
 why:"SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists have evidence for reducing cardiovascular events in patients with diabetes and established coronary disease, making them a preferred add on in this population. Sulfonylureas do not carry the same cardiovascular benefit, thiazolidinediones are associated with fluid retention and heart failure risk that makes them less favorable, and insulin is not the guideline directed choice specifically for cardiovascular risk reduction in this scenario."},
{id:"P1066",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient six months after MI has a blood pressure averaging 148/92 on two medications, an LDL of 78 mg/dL on high intensity statin, continues to smoke about half a pack of cigarettes daily, and has missed several cardiac rehab sessions due to transportation issues. Which factor should the care team prioritize as offering the greatest additional reduction in future cardiovascular risk?",
 o:["Smoking cessation support, since continued smoking is a major independent modifiable risk that is not addressed by the current medication regimen","Further LDL lowering, since the LDL has not reached an even lower number some patients aim for","Addressing transportation barriers to rehab alone, without addressing the ongoing smoking","Adding a third blood pressure medication before addressing smoking"],a:0,
 why:"Continued smoking is a major, independent, and fully modifiable risk factor that none of the current medications address, so prioritizing cessation support offers the greatest additional benefit. Focusing on an already well controlled LDL anchors on a single lab value in isolation rather than the biggest overall risk driver, addressing transportation alone without the smoking only partially addresses the patient overall risk, and adding another blood pressure medication before addressing smoking is reasonable eventually but should not take priority over the larger modifiable risk."},
{id:"P1067",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with a history of MI and a documented prior gastrointestinal bleed is being considered for long term secondary prevention antiplatelet therapy. Which approach best balances the ischemic and bleeding risks involved?",
 o:["Use aspirin at the lowest effective dose, with consideration of a proton pump inhibitor for gastroprotection, weighing ongoing ischemic benefit against the bleeding history","Avoid all antiplatelet therapy indefinitely because of the prior bleed","Use full dose dual antiplatelet therapy indefinitely regardless of the bleeding history","Switch to routine anticoagulation with warfarin in place of antiplatelet therapy"],a:0,
 why:"Using low dose aspirin with gastroprotective therapy allows the patient to retain the ischemic benefit of antiplatelet therapy while addressing the elevated bleeding risk from the prior event. Avoiding all antiplatelet therapy removes proven secondary prevention benefit entirely, continuing full dose dual therapy indefinitely ignores the elevated bleeding risk, and switching to warfarin substitutes the wrong drug class when there is no separate indication for anticoagulation, such as atrial fibrillation."},
{id:"P1068",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient one week after MI has an LDL of 105 mg/dL despite being started on high intensity statin therapy at discharge and reports taking it every day. Blood pressure and blood glucose are well controlled. What is the most appropriate next step?",
 o:["Allow more time, generally 4 to 12 weeks, for the full statin effect before considering additional therapy, provided adherence is confirmed and no other issues are found","Conclude the statin has failed and switch immediately to a fibrate","Start a PCSK9 inhibitor immediately, since the LDL is already at a treatment failure threshold at one week","Reconsider the recent MI diagnosis, since the LDL is still elevated"],a:0,
 why:"Statins take several weeks to reach their full LDL lowering effect, so it is appropriate to confirm adherence and allow the recommended time frame before deciding whether additional therapy is needed. Switching to a fibrate misidentifies the appropriate next drug class for LDL lowering, starting an advanced therapy such as a PCSK9 inhibitor after only one week skips the necessary reassessment period, and questioning the MI diagnosis based on an LDL value is illogical since the two are unrelated at this stage."},
{id:"P1069",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with established coronary artery disease continues to have recurrent ischemic events despite an LDL at goal on maximally tolerated statin and ezetimibe, well controlled blood pressure, and having quit smoking. The provider is considering adding low dose colchicine. What is the primary rationale for this addition, based on evidence incorporated into current guidance?",
 o:["Colchicine has anti inflammatory effects that can reduce recurrent atherosclerotic cardiovascular events in patients with established coronary disease, independent of further LDL lowering","Colchicine further lowers LDL cholesterol beyond what statins and ezetimibe achieve","Colchicine is used primarily to treat statin associated muscle pain","Colchicine replaces the need for antiplatelet therapy"],a:0,
 why:"Colchicine reduces vascular inflammation and has evidence for lowering recurrent cardiovascular events in patients with established coronary disease, working through a pathway independent of further LDL lowering. It does not lower LDL cholesterol itself, its use here is not for treating statin related muscle symptoms, which is a different clinical problem, and it is intended as an addition to, not a replacement for, antiplatelet therapy."},
{id:"P1070",d:"D3",c:"D3.C5",dok:4,q:"A patient with recurrent MI has an LDL of 92 mg/dL on maximally tolerated statin plus ezetimibe, a blood pressure of 138/86 on two medications, an HbA1c of 8.2 percent on metformin alone, and ongoing tobacco use. The patient is being discharged after a readmission for unstable angina, and the team has limited time for discharge counseling. Which approach best sequences the priorities for reducing future risk?",
 o:["Address tobacco cessation and glycemic control as the highest yield modifiable targets first, arrange close outpatient follow up for lipid and blood pressure optimization including likely need for a PCSK9 inhibitor given the very high risk category, rather than focusing all counseling time on a single risk factor","Focus discharge counseling entirely on blood pressure, since it is closest to its goal","Delay all counseling until the next office visit since multiple issues cannot be addressed at discharge","Add a PCSK9 inhibitor as the sole intervention without addressing tobacco use or glycemic control"],a:0,
 why:"With several unaddressed risk factors and limited time, prioritizing tobacco cessation and glycemic control while arranging close follow up for the remaining lipid and blood pressure optimization addresses the highest yield modifiable risks without neglecting the rest. Focusing only on blood pressure misallocates limited time to the factor already closest to control while ignoring larger untreated risks, delaying all counseling wastes a critical discharge teaching opportunity known to support risk reduction, and treating only the lipid parameter with a PCSK9 inhibitor ignores the higher impact modifiable behaviors also present in this case."},
{id:"P6001",d:"D4",c:"D4.C1",dok:1,q:"Which blood pressure category, per the 2017 ACC/AHA guideline, is defined as systolic 120 to 129 mm Hg and diastolic less than 80 mm Hg?",
 o:["Normal blood pressure","Elevated blood pressure","Stage 1 hypertension","Stage 2 hypertension"],a:1,
 why:"Elevated blood pressure is defined by systolic 120 to 129 mm Hg with diastolic less than 80 mm Hg. Normal is less than 120/80, stage 1 is 130 to 139 systolic or 80 to 89 diastolic, and stage 2 is 140/90 or higher, so those ranges do not match the values given."},
{id:"P6002",d:"D4",c:"D4.C1",dok:2,q:"A nurse measures blood pressure with the patient arm unsupported and hanging at the side. What effect does this have on the reading?",
 o:["Falsely elevated systolic and diastolic readings due to isometric muscle contraction","Falsely low readings because gravity increases venous return","No effect, because arm position does not change intra arterial pressure","Falsely elevated systolic reading only, because diastolic pressure is unaffected by position"],a:0,
 why:"An unsupported, hanging arm requires isometric muscle contraction to hold its position, which raises both systolic and diastolic pressure readings. Gravity does not increase venous return in this position, arm position does measurably affect the reading, and both systolic and diastolic values are elevated rather than systolic alone."},
{id:"P6003",d:"D4",c:"D4.C1",dok:2,q:"A blood pressure cuff bladder that is too large for the patient arm circumference will most likely produce which result?",
 o:["A falsely low blood pressure reading","A falsely high blood pressure reading","No change in accuracy, since only pulse pressure is affected","An accurate reading, since bladder size does not affect cuff pressure transmission"],a:0,
 why:"An oversized cuff bladder spreads compression over a wider area than needed, so less external pressure is required to occlude the artery, producing a falsely low reading. A cuff that is too small does the opposite and overestimates pressure, and bladder size does directly affect measurement accuracy rather than only pulse pressure or nothing at all."},
{id:"P6006",d:"D4",c:"D4.C1",dok:2,q:"Before obtaining an accurate blood pressure measurement, how long should a patient sit quietly with feet flat on the floor and back supported?",
 o:["At least 5 minutes","At least 30 seconds","At least 15 minutes","No rest period is required if the patient walked slowly to the room"],a:0,
 why:"Guidelines recommend at least 5 minutes of quiet seated rest before measurement to allow blood pressure to stabilize. Thirty seconds is not long enough to avoid an artifactually elevated reading, 15 minutes is longer than necessary and impractical for routine care, and skipping rest entirely can falsely elevate the reading even after slow walking."},
{id:"P6004",d:"D4",c:"D4.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient office blood pressure readings are consistently 148/92 mm Hg, but home readings averaged over one week are 128/78 mm Hg. Which condition does this pattern describe?",
 o:["White coat hypertension","Masked hypertension","Sustained hypertension","Orthostatic hypotension"],a:0,
 why:"Blood pressure that is elevated only in the office setting but normal on home monitoring defines white coat hypertension. Masked hypertension is the reverse pattern, sustained hypertension would show elevated readings in both settings, and orthostatic hypotension refers to a drop in pressure with standing, not a difference between office and home settings."},
{id:"P6005",d:"D4",c:"D4.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient office blood pressure is 118/76 mm Hg, but ambulatory monitoring shows an average of 138/88 mm Hg during daily activity. Which condition is this?",
 o:["Masked hypertension","White coat hypertension","Resistant hypertension","Isolated systolic hypertension"],a:0,
 why:"Normal office readings paired with elevated out of office readings define masked hypertension, a pattern that carries real cardiovascular risk despite looking normal in clinic. White coat hypertension is the opposite pattern, resistant hypertension is defined by failure to control blood pressure on three or more optimally dosed agents, and isolated systolic hypertension refers to elevated systolic with normal diastolic pressure regardless of setting."},
{id:"P6007",d:"D4",c:"D4.C1",dok:4,q:"A patient has a blood pressure of 154/94 mm Hg in the right arm and 128/78 mm Hg in the left arm on repeated measurement. What is the most appropriate next step?",
 o:["Use the arm with the higher reading for future measurements and evaluate for arterial disease such as subclavian stenosis","Average the two readings and document a single value","Repeat only the lower reading, since the higher reading is likely due to cuff error","Diagnose white coat hypertension based on the discrepancy"],a:0,
 why:"An interarm difference this large raises concern for vascular pathology such as subclavian stenosis, so the higher reading arm should be used going forward and further vascular evaluation pursued. Averaging the values would obscure a clinically important finding, assuming cuff error is not justified without evidence, and this pattern does not describe white coat hypertension."},
{id:"P6008",d:"D4",c:"D4.C2",dok:1,q:"What is the most common cause of hypertension in adults?",
 o:["Primary (essential) hypertension with no single identifiable cause","Renal artery stenosis","Primary aldosteronism","Pheochromocytoma"],a:0,
 why:"Roughly 90 to 95 percent of adult hypertension is primary, or essential, hypertension with no single identifiable cause. Renal artery stenosis, primary aldosteronism, and pheochromocytoma are recognized secondary causes but together account for only a minority of cases."},
{id:"P6009",d:"D4",c:"D4.C2",dok:2,q:"A 28 year old with new stage 2 hypertension has hypokalemia not explained by diuretic use and a low plasma renin level. Which secondary cause is most likely?",
 o:["Primary aldosteronism","Pheochromocytoma","Renal artery stenosis","Coarctation of the aorta"],a:0,
 why:"Unexplained hypokalemia with suppressed renin reflects excess mineralocorticoid activity, which is characteristic of primary aldosteronism. Pheochromocytoma typically causes episodic symptoms from catecholamine excess rather than this electrolyte pattern, renal artery stenosis raises renin rather than suppressing it, and coarctation presents with a blood pressure differential between the arms and legs."},
{id:"P6010",d:"D4",c:"D4.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient reports episodic headaches, palpitations, and diaphoresis along with paroxysmal severe hypertension. Which secondary cause should be suspected?",
 o:["Pheochromocytoma","Primary aldosteronism","Cushing syndrome","Obstructive sleep apnea"],a:0,
 why:"The classic triad of episodic headache, palpitations, and diaphoresis with paroxysmal blood pressure spikes reflects catecholamine excess from a pheochromocytoma. Aldosteronism causes sustained rather than episodic hypertension with hypokalemia, Cushing syndrome typically presents with weight gain and striae, and obstructive sleep apnea is associated with snoring and nocturnal symptoms rather than catecholamine surges."},
{id:"P6013",d:"D4",c:"D4.C2",dok:2,q:"A young adult with hypertension has diminished and delayed femoral pulses compared with the radial pulses, with lower blood pressure measured in the legs than the arms. Which condition is most likely?",
 o:["Coarctation of the aorta","Renal artery stenosis","Primary aldosteronism","Pheochromocytoma"],a:0,
 why:"Radial to femoral pulse delay along with lower leg blood pressure than arm blood pressure is a classic finding of aortic coarctation, a narrowing that typically occurs distal to the left subclavian artery. Renal artery stenosis, primary aldosteronism, and pheochromocytoma do not produce this specific pulse and pressure differential pattern."},
{id:"P6011",d:"D4",c:"D4.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient with resistant hypertension, obesity, and loud snoring reports morning headaches and daytime sleepiness. Which secondary cause should be screened for first?",
 o:["Obstructive sleep apnea","Pheochromocytoma","Renal artery stenosis","Primary hyperparathyroidism"],a:0,
 why:"The combination of obesity, loud snoring, morning headache, and daytime sleepiness is the classic symptom cluster for obstructive sleep apnea, a common and treatable secondary cause of resistant hypertension. Pheochromocytoma, renal artery stenosis, and hyperparathyroidism each have distinct presentations that do not match this specific symptom pattern."},
{id:"P6012",d:"D4",c:"D4.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient started on an ACE inhibitor develops a significant rise in serum creatinine, and an abdominal bruit is heard on exam. Which secondary cause of hypertension is most likely?",
 o:["Renal artery stenosis","Primary aldosteronism","Cushing syndrome","Coarctation of the aorta"],a:0,
 why:"An acute rise in creatinine after starting an ACE inhibitor along with an abdominal bruit is characteristic of renal artery stenosis, because ACE inhibition removes the angiotensin II mediated efferent arteriolar constriction that maintains filtration pressure distal to the stenosis. Aldosteronism, Cushing syndrome, and coarctation do not typically produce this specific ACE inhibitor provoked kidney injury pattern."},
{id:"P6020",d:"D4",c:"D4.C3",dok:1,q:"What is the primary mechanism of action of thiazide diuretics in lowering blood pressure?",
 o:["Inhibiting sodium and chloride reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule","Blocking beta-1 adrenergic receptors in the heart","Inhibiting angiotensin converting enzyme","Blocking calcium influx into vascular smooth muscle"],a:0,
 why:"Thiazide diuretics act at the distal convoluted tubule to inhibit sodium and chloride reabsorption, promoting natriuresis and reducing plasma volume. Beta-1 receptor blockade describes beta blockers, angiotensin converting enzyme inhibition describes ACE inhibitors, and calcium channel blockade describes calcium channel blockers, not thiazides."},
{id:"P6014",d:"D4",c:"D4.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient with hypertension and heart failure with reduced ejection fraction needs additional antihypertensive therapy. Which class provides both blood pressure control and a mortality benefit in this population?",
 o:["Beta blockers such as carvedilol or metoprolol succinate","Non dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as verapamil","Alpha blockers such as doxazosin","Direct vasodilators such as hydralazine given alone"],a:0,
 why:"Evidence based beta blockers reduce mortality in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction while also lowering blood pressure. Non dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers have a negative inotropic effect that can worsen this type of heart failure, alpha blockers were shown in trial evidence to increase heart failure risk, and hydralazine given alone lacks the mortality benefit seen when combined with a nitrate."},
{id:"P6015",d:"D4",c:"D4.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient with hypertension and chronic kidney disease with significant proteinuria should preferentially receive which class of antihypertensive?",
 o:["An ACE inhibitor or ARB","A thiazide diuretic alone","A dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker alone","A beta blocker alone"],a:0,
 why:"ACE inhibitors and ARBs reduce intraglomerular pressure and specifically lower proteinuria, providing renoprotection beyond blood pressure control alone. Thiazide diuretics, dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, and beta blockers can lower blood pressure but do not provide this specific antiproteinuric benefit and are not preferred first line agents in this setting."},
{id:"P6016",d:"D4",c:"D4.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient with hypertension and a history of myocardial infarction needs an antihypertensive chosen for a compelling indication. Which class is preferred?",
 o:["Beta blocker","Alpha blocker","Direct renin inhibitor","Loop diuretic"],a:0,
 why:"Beta blockers reduce mortality and reinfarction risk after myocardial infarction, making them the preferred compelling indication choice in this population. Alpha blockers, direct renin inhibitors, and loop diuretics lack this specific post infarction mortality benefit and are not first line compelling indication agents."},
{id:"P6018",d:"D4",c:"D4.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient remains hypertensive despite maximally tolerated doses of a thiazide diuretic, an ACE inhibitor, and a calcium channel blocker. Which medication is recommended as the next add on agent for resistant hypertension?",
 o:["Spironolactone","Doxazosin","Clonidine","Hydralazine"],a:0,
 why:"Spironolactone, a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, is the preferred fourth line add on agent for resistant hypertension based on trial evidence, often reflecting unrecognized aldosterone excess in this population. Doxazosin, clonidine, and hydralazine can be effective but are reserved for later steps or carry more bothersome side effects, so they are not the preferred next choice."},
{id:"P6017",d:"D4",c:"D4.C3",dok:3,q:"Antihypertensive trials have shown that ACE inhibitor monotherapy tends to be less effective at lowering blood pressure in Black patients compared with White patients. What physiologic difference best explains this finding?",
 o:["Black patients more often have low renin, volume and salt sensitive hypertension","Black patients metabolize ACE inhibitors more rapidly through hepatic enzymes","Black patients have a higher prevalence of renal artery stenosis","Black patients have higher baseline renin activity, making renin angiotensin blockade more effective"],a:0,
 why:"Low renin, volume expanded, salt sensitive hypertension is more prevalent on average among Black patients, so agents that block the renin angiotensin system are relatively less potent while diuretics and calcium channel blockers work better. Faster drug metabolism, a higher prevalence of renal artery stenosis, and higher baseline renin activity are not the established mechanisms behind this observed difference."},
{id:"P6019",d:"D4",c:"D4.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient blood pressure remains uncontrolled on lisinopril. The prescriber considers adding losartan for additional renin angiotensin blockade. What is the concern with this combination?",
 o:["Combining an ACE inhibitor with an ARB increases the risk of hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury without added cardiovascular benefit","This combination is synergistic and is the preferred method for resistant hypertension","ARBs cannot be combined with any other class of antihypertensive","This combination eliminates the need for potassium monitoring"],a:0,
 why:"Dual renin angiotensin system blockade with an ACE inhibitor plus an ARB increases the risk of hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury without proven additional cardiovascular benefit, so it is generally discouraged. It is not a preferred or synergistic strategy for resistant hypertension, ARBs can be combined with other non RAAS classes, and potassium monitoring remains necessary rather than unnecessary."},
{id:"P6021",d:"D4",c:"D4.C4",dok:1,q:"What is the recommended maximum daily sodium intake for blood pressure reduction according to general lifestyle guidance?",
 o:["Less than 2300 mg per day","Less than 6000 mg per day","Less than 500 mg per day","No restriction is necessary if blood pressure is controlled with medication"],a:0,
 why:"General lifestyle guidance recommends limiting sodium intake to less than 2300 mg per day, with further reduction toward 1500 mg providing even greater benefit for many patients. Six thousand mg is far above the recommendation, 500 mg is an unrealistically low general target, and sodium restriction remains beneficial even when a patient is on medication rather than becoming unnecessary."},
{id:"P6022",d:"D4",c:"D4.C4",dok:2,q:"A nurse is teaching a patient about the DASH eating plan for blood pressure control. Which description best characterizes this diet?",
 o:["High in fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and low fat dairy, with reduced saturated fat and sodium","A high protein, low carbohydrate diet emphasizing red meat","A diet that eliminates all dietary fat","A liquid only diet used for rapid short term weight loss"],a:0,
 why:"The DASH eating plan emphasizes fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and low fat dairy while reducing saturated fat and sodium, an approach shown to meaningfully lower blood pressure. A high protein, low carbohydrate, red meat emphasized diet, a diet that eliminates all fat, and a liquid only rapid weight loss diet do not reflect DASH principles and are not the evidence based recommendation."},
{id:"P6023",d:"D4",c:"D4.C4",dok:2,q:"A nurse counsels a patient with hypertension about alcohol intake. What is the recommended limit to help control blood pressure?",
 o:["No more than 2 drinks per day for men and 1 drink per day for women","No more than 5 drinks per day for men and 3 drinks per day for women","Complete abstinence is required for any degree of hypertension","Alcohol has no effect on blood pressure, so no limit is needed"],a:0,
 why:"Guidance recommends limiting alcohol to no more than 2 drinks per day for men and 1 drink per day for women to help control blood pressure. Five and three drinks per day exceed the recommended limit, complete abstinence is not required since the guidance is moderation rather than elimination, and alcohol does have a dose related pressor effect rather than none at all."},
{id:"P6024",d:"D4",c:"D4.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient with well controlled hypertension on a three drug regimen reports frequently forgetting doses because the medications are taken at different times throughout the day. Which intervention is most likely to improve adherence?",
 o:["Simplify the regimen using combination pills and once daily dosing when possible","Switch to a completely different drug class without addressing timing","Instruct the patient to set multiple daily alarms without changing the regimen","Discontinue one medication to reduce pill burden regardless of blood pressure control"],a:0,
 why:"Simplifying the regimen with combination pills and once daily dosing directly addresses the identified barrier of a complex, multi time schedule and is a proven strategy for improving adherence. Switching drug classes without addressing timing does not solve the underlying problem, alarms alone are less effective than true regimen simplification, and discontinuing a needed medication risks worsening blood pressure control."},
{id:"P6025",d:"D4",c:"D4.C4",dok:3,q:"A nurse recommends aerobic exercise for a patient with hypertension. Which regimen reflects general guideline recommendations for blood pressure benefit?",
 o:["At least 90 to 150 minutes per week of moderate intensity aerobic activity","Fewer than 10 minutes per week of vigorous activity","Only isometric resistance exercise such as heavy weightlifting","Exercise has no measurable effect on blood pressure independent of weight loss"],a:0,
 why:"General guidance recommends roughly 90 to 150 minutes per week of moderate intensity aerobic activity, which lowers blood pressure independent of any accompanying weight loss. Less than 10 minutes per week of vigorous activity is far below the recommended amount, isometric resistance exercise alone is not the standard first recommendation, and exercise does have a measurable independent blood pressure benefit."},
{id:"P6026",d:"D4",c:"D4.C5",dok:1,q:"Hypertensive urgency is defined as severely elevated blood pressure, typically 180/120 mm Hg or higher, occurring with which additional feature?",
 o:["No evidence of acute target organ damage","Acute target organ damage such as encephalopathy or pulmonary edema","A blood pressure that has been elevated for less than one hour","A blood pressure measured only in a home setting"],a:0,
 why:"Hypertensive urgency is markedly elevated blood pressure without acute target organ damage, which is what distinguishes it from hypertensive emergency. The presence of acute organ damage instead defines an emergency, and neither the specific duration of elevation nor the setting where blood pressure was measured is the defining feature of urgency."},
{id:"P6027",d:"D4",c:"D4.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient presents with a blood pressure of 198/118 mm Hg, is asymptomatic, and has no evidence of end organ damage on exam or labs. What is the most appropriate management?",
 o:["Adjust or resume oral antihypertensive medication and arrange close outpatient follow up","Administer intravenous nicardipine and admit to the intensive care unit","Rapidly lower blood pressure to normal within one hour using intravenous agents","Discharge with no medication changes and no follow up needed"],a:0,
 why:"Hypertensive urgency without evidence of organ damage is managed by adjusting or resuming oral antihypertensive therapy with close outpatient follow up, avoiding rapid reduction that could cause organ hypoperfusion. Intravenous agents and intensive care admission are reserved for true emergencies with organ damage, rapid reduction to normal within an hour is unsafe, and discharge with no plan at all leaves severely elevated pressure unaddressed."},
{id:"P6028",d:"D4",c:"D4.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with an acute aortic dissection and severe hypertension requires blood pressure reduction. Which approach is most appropriate?",
 o:["Give an intravenous beta blocker first to reduce heart rate and shear stress, then add a vasodilator, targeting systolic pressure below 120 mm Hg","Give a vasodilator alone as first line therapy to maximize the speed of pressure reduction","Withhold all antihypertensive therapy until surgical repair is completed","Target a systolic pressure of 160 mm Hg to preserve organ perfusion"],a:0,
 why:"In aortic dissection, beta blockade is given first (or with a vasodilator) to blunt reflex tachycardia and reduce aortic wall shear stress, with a target systolic pressure below 120 mm Hg. A vasodilator given alone can trigger reflex tachycardia that worsens shear stress, withholding all treatment is dangerous while awaiting surgery, and a target of 160 mm Hg is far above the level needed to limit propagation of the dissection."},
{id:"P6029",d:"D4",c:"D4.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with an acute ischemic stroke is being considered for thrombolytic therapy. Blood pressure is 198/112 mm Hg. What must occur before thrombolytics can be given?",
 o:["Blood pressure must be carefully lowered to below 185/110 mm Hg","Blood pressure must be lowered to below 120/80 mm Hg","Thrombolytics can be given regardless of blood pressure","Blood pressure must be allowed to rise further to improve cerebral perfusion"],a:0,
 why:"Guidelines require blood pressure below 185/110 mm Hg before thrombolytic administration to reduce the risk of hemorrhagic conversion. Lowering to a fully normotensive level is not required and could reduce cerebral perfusion, giving thrombolytics regardless of blood pressure is unsafe, and allowing pressure to rise further would increase rather than decrease hemorrhage risk."},
{id:"P6030",d:"D4",c:"D4.C5",dok:4,q:"A pregnant patient at 34 weeks gestation has a blood pressure of 172/114 mm Hg with headache and hyperreflexia. In addition to antihypertensive therapy with intravenous labetalol or hydralazine, which additional medication is indicated to prevent seizures?",
 o:["Magnesium sulfate","Diazepam","Phenytoin","Nifedipine"],a:0,
 why:"Magnesium sulfate is the standard of care for seizure prophylaxis in severe preeclampsia with features such as headache and hyperreflexia. Diazepam and phenytoin are not first line for eclampsia prophylaxis, and nifedipine is an antihypertensive agent that does not address seizure prevention even though it can also be used for blood pressure control."},
{id:"P6031",d:"D5",c:"D5.C1",dok:1,q:"Which type of cardiomyopathy is characterized by ventricular dilation and impaired systolic contractile function?",
 o:["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy"],a:0,
 why:"Dilated cardiomyopathy features chamber enlargement with reduced ejection fraction from primary systolic dysfunction. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy features wall thickening with usually preserved or hyperdynamic systolic function, restrictive cardiomyopathy features stiff walls with primarily diastolic dysfunction, and arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy features fibrofatty replacement with arrhythmia risk rather than global systolic failure."},
{id:"P6032",d:"D5",c:"D5.C1",dok:2,q:"Which cardiomyopathy is characterized by fibrofatty replacement of right ventricular myocardium and is a recognized cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes?",
 o:["Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Peripartum cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy"],a:0,
 why:"Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy is defined by fibrofatty replacement of right ventricular tissue that predisposes to ventricular arrhythmias and sudden death, often during exertion in young athletes. Restrictive cardiomyopathy does not show this fibrofatty pattern, peripartum cardiomyopathy is tied to pregnancy timing, and takotsubo cardiomyopathy is a stress induced condition with apical ballooning."},
{id:"P6033",d:"D5",c:"D5.C1",dok:2,q:"Peripartum cardiomyopathy is defined as new onset heart failure with reduced ejection fraction occurring during which time period?",
 o:["The last month of pregnancy through five months postpartum","Only during the first trimester of pregnancy","More than one year after delivery","Only in patients who have had three or more prior pregnancies"],a:0,
 why:"The accepted definition is new systolic heart failure toward the end of pregnancy or within about five months after delivery with no other identifiable cause. The first trimester, more than a year after delivery, and a required history of three or more pregnancies do not match the recognized diagnostic time window."},
{id:"P6034",d:"D5",c:"D5.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient has heart failure symptoms with normal or only mildly increased wall thickness, biatrial enlargement, and preserved systolic function with impaired ventricular filling. Which cardiomyopathy does this most likely represent?",
 o:["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy"],a:0,
 why:"Restrictive cardiomyopathy classically produces stiff, poorly compliant ventricles that impair filling and cause marked biatrial enlargement while systolic function and wall thickness remain relatively preserved. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy shows significant wall thickening, dilated cardiomyopathy shows chamber enlargement with reduced systolic function, and arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy primarily affects the right ventricle with arrhythmia rather than this diastolic filling pattern."},
{id:"P6035",d:"D5",c:"D5.C1",dok:3,q:"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is most commonly caused by which genetic pattern?",
 o:["Autosomal dominant mutations in genes encoding cardiac sarcomere proteins","Autosomal recessive mitochondrial DNA mutations","X-linked mutations affecting the dystrophin gene","An acquired, non-genetic postviral process"],a:0,
 why:"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is most often caused by autosomal dominant mutations in genes encoding cardiac sarcomere proteins, such as beta myosin heavy chain or myosin binding protein C. Mitochondrial DNA mutations and dystrophin gene mutations describe different or less common conditions, and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is a genetic rather than an acquired postviral process."},
{id:"P6036",d:"D5",c:"D5.C2",dok:2,q:"In hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, left ventricular outflow tract obstruction is primarily caused by which mechanism?",
 o:["Systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve against a thickened interventricular septum","Fixed calcific narrowing of the aortic valve","Fibrous scarring of the papillary muscles","Dilation of the mitral annulus reducing leaflet coaptation"],a:0,
 why:"Dynamic outflow tract obstruction in hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy results from septal hypertrophy combined with systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve leaflet, drawn toward the septum by altered flow dynamics. Fixed calcific narrowing describes aortic stenosis, and papillary muscle scarring or annular dilation describe mechanisms of mitral regurgitation, not this dynamic obstruction."},
{id:"P6037",d:"D5",c:"D5.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient with dilated cardiomyopathy is most likely to present with which combination of findings?",
 o:["A displaced, diffuse point of maximal impulse, an S3 gallop, and signs of biventricular heart failure","A loud early systolic click and a normal sized heart on exam","Isolated right ventricular findings with no left sided symptoms","A narrow pulse pressure with a forceful, sustained apical impulse"],a:0,
 why:"Dilated cardiomyopathy causes chamber enlargement and systolic dysfunction, producing a laterally displaced diffuse point of maximal impulse, an S3 from volume overload, and biventricular failure signs. A click with a normal sized heart, isolated right sided findings, and a forceful sustained impulse typical of pressure overload do not match the typical dilated cardiomyopathy presentation."},
{id:"P6038",d:"D5",c:"D5.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient with restrictive cardiomyopathy is most likely to present with which finding related to impaired ventricular filling?",
 o:["Elevated jugular venous pressure that rises further with inspiration, known as Kussmaul sign","Bounding peripheral pulses with a widened pulse pressure","A hyperdynamic apical impulse displaced to the axilla","Warm, flushed extremities from a hyperdynamic circulation"],a:0,
 why:"Restrictive cardiomyopathy impairs ventricular filling and elevates venous pressures, and the stiff right ventricle cannot accommodate the inspiratory increase in venous return, producing Kussmaul sign. Bounding pulses with a widened pulse pressure, a hyperdynamic displaced impulse, and warm hyperdynamic extremities are not typical of this low output, filling impaired state."},
{id:"P6039",d:"D5",c:"D5.C2",dok:3,q:"A 19 year old competitive athlete has recurrent palpitations and one episode of syncope during sprint training. ECG shows an epsilon wave and T wave inversions in the right precordial leads. Which cardiomyopathy is most consistent with this presentation?",
 o:["Arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Peripartum cardiomyopathy"],a:0,
 why:"Epsilon waves and right precordial T wave inversion with exertional arrhythmia and syncope in a young athlete are classic ECG and clinical features of arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy more typically shows voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy, restrictive cardiomyopathy often shows low voltage from infiltrative disease, and peripartum cardiomyopathy is tied to pregnancy timing and does not apply here."},
{id:"P6040",d:"D5",c:"D5.C2",dok:3,q:"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is thought to result primarily from which mechanism?",
 o:["A surge of catecholamines causing transient myocardial stunning, most pronounced at the ventricular apex","Atherosclerotic plaque rupture with acute thrombotic coronary occlusion","A genetic mutation in sarcomere proteins present from birth","Chronic pressure overload from long standing untreated hypertension"],a:0,
 why:"Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is driven by an acute catecholamine surge, often triggered by emotional or physical stress, causing transient myocardial stunning that is most pronounced at the apex and produces apical ballooning without significant coronary occlusion. Plaque rupture describes acute myocardial infarction, a sarcomere mutation describes inherited hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and chronic pressure overload describes hypertensive heart disease rather than this acute stress condition."},
{id:"P6041",d:"D5",c:"D5.C2",dok:3,q:"A 17 year old athlete reports exertional chest pain, dyspnea, and one episode of near syncope during basketball practice. Which pathophysiologic process, if present, is most concerning for hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy?",
 o:["Dynamic left ventricular outflow obstruction that worsens with exertion as ventricular volume decreases and contractility increases","Fixed valvular narrowing that remains constant regardless of activity level","Gradual coronary atherosclerosis causing demand ischemia","A vasovagal reflex triggered by prolonged standing"],a:0,
 why:"In hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, exertion increases contractility and decreases ventricular volume, both of which worsen the dynamic outflow obstruction and can precipitate symptoms or sudden death, fitting this presentation in a young athlete. Fixed valvular narrowing, atherosclerotic ischemia, and a simple vasovagal reflex are less likely in this age group and do not describe the load dependent obstruction typical of this condition."},
{id:"P6042",d:"D5",c:"D5.C3",dok:1,q:"Which diagnostic test is most commonly used as the initial imaging study to evaluate suspected cardiomyopathy?",
 o:["Transthoracic echocardiogram","Cardiac catheterization","Endomyocardial biopsy","Positron emission tomography"],a:0,
 why:"Echocardiography is the standard, noninvasive first line imaging test used to assess chamber size, wall thickness, and systolic and diastolic function when cardiomyopathy is suspected. Cardiac catheterization, endomyocardial biopsy, and positron emission tomography are more invasive or specialized tests reserved for further evaluation after initial imaging."},
{id:"P6043",d:"D5",c:"D5.C3",dok:2,q:"Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging is particularly useful in the evaluation of cardiomyopathy because it can provide which additional information beyond standard echocardiography?",
 o:["Detailed tissue characterization, including patterns of fibrosis or infiltration","Real time bedside assessment during an acute emergency","The lowest cost imaging option available","Direct measurement of coronary artery stenosis severity"],a:0,
 why:"Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging offers superior tissue characterization, such as late gadolinium enhancement patterns of fibrosis or infiltrative disease, that echocardiography cannot provide. It is not a rapid bedside emergency tool, it is more costly than echocardiography rather than the lowest cost option, and it does not directly quantify coronary stenosis the way angiography does."},
{id:"P6044",d:"D5",c:"D5.C3",dok:2,q:"A nurse anticipates that a B-type natriuretic peptide level will be obtained in a patient with suspected cardiomyopathy. What does an elevated level primarily indicate?",
 o:["Increased ventricular wall stress from volume or pressure overload","A specific diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Normal cardiac function requiring no further workup","Active bacterial endocarditis"],a:0,
 why:"B-type natriuretic peptide rises in response to ventricular wall stretch from volume or pressure overload regardless of the underlying cardiomyopathy type, so it supports a heart failure process rather than pinpointing a specific cause. It does not diagnose hypertrophic cardiomyopathy specifically, an elevated level indicates abnormal rather than normal function, and it is not a marker of infective endocarditis."},
{id:"P6045",d:"D5",c:"D5.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient has clinical and echocardiographic features suggestive of restrictive cardiomyopathy from suspected cardiac amyloidosis, but noninvasive testing remains inconclusive. Which procedure can provide a definitive tissue diagnosis?",
 o:["Endomyocardial biopsy","A repeat transthoracic echocardiogram","An exercise stress test","Ambulatory ECG monitoring"],a:0,
 why:"Endomyocardial biopsy allows direct histologic evaluation, including special staining for amyloid, providing a definitive tissue diagnosis when noninvasive testing is inconclusive. Repeating the echocardiogram does not add tissue diagnosis, and stress testing or ambulatory monitoring assess ischemia or rhythm rather than infiltrative tissue pathology."},
{id:"P6046",d:"D5",c:"D5.C3",dok:3,q:"Genetic testing is recommended for a patient newly diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy primarily for which purpose?",
 o:["To identify at risk first degree relatives who should undergo cascade screening","To determine the specific antihypertensive medication that should be prescribed","To replace the need for any echocardiographic follow up","To confirm the diagnosis, since echocardiography alone cannot detect the condition"],a:0,
 why:"Genetic testing in a patient with confirmed hypertrophic cardiomyopathy primarily enables cascade screening of first degree relatives who may carry the same pathogenic variant. It does not guide antihypertensive drug choice, it does not replace ongoing echocardiographic surveillance, and echocardiography, not genetic testing, remains the primary tool for diagnosing the condition itself."},
{id:"P6047",d:"D5",c:"D5.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient with newly diagnosed dilated cardiomyopathy and reduced ejection fraction should be started on which foundational therapy, consistent with guideline directed heart failure management?",
 o:["An ACE inhibitor or ARNI, a beta blocker, and a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist as tolerated","A short course of high dose loop diuretic as the only long term therapy","Digoxin as first line monotherapy","A calcium channel blocker as first line therapy"],a:0,
 why:"Guideline directed medical therapy for reduced ejection fraction heart failure, which underlies most dilated cardiomyopathy, includes renin angiotensin system inhibition, beta blockade, and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonism as tolerated because these classes improve survival. Diuretics alone treat congestion without modifying disease course, digoxin is adjunctive rather than first line, and non dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers can worsen this type of heart failure and are avoided."},
{id:"P6048",d:"D5",c:"D5.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient recovering from peripartum cardiomyopathy asks about the safety of a future pregnancy. Her ejection fraction has remained at 30 percent despite therapy. What is the most appropriate counseling?",
 o:["Future pregnancy carries a high risk of relapse or worsening heart failure and should be discussed carefully with a cardiologist before conceiving","Future pregnancy is completely safe once any heart failure medication is stopped","Peripartum cardiomyopathy never recurs in a subsequent pregnancy","Ejection fraction has no bearing on the safety of a future pregnancy"],a:0,
 why:"A persistently reduced ejection fraction after peripartum cardiomyopathy is associated with a substantially higher risk of relapse or death in a subsequent pregnancy, so careful specialist counseling before conceiving is essential. It is not accurate to call future pregnancy completely safe, recurrence is a recognized possibility rather than something that never happens, and ejection fraction recovery status is directly relevant to this counseling rather than irrelevant."},
{id:"P6051",d:"D5",c:"D5.C4",dok:2,q:"A nurse is counseling an adolescent patient with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy about participation in competitive sports. Which statement reflects current guideline based counseling?",
 o:["Decisions about competitive sports participation should involve shared decision making between the patient, family, and a specialist experienced in the condition, factoring in individual risk","All patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy are permanently barred from any physical activity","Competitive sports participation is always safe and requires no additional precautions","Only patients who have already had a cardiac arrest need any activity guidance"],a:0,
 why:"Current guidance favors individualized, shared decision making about sports participation based on personal risk factors rather than a blanket restriction. A permanent ban on all physical activity, an assumption that participation is always safe with no precautions, and waiting until after a cardiac arrest to give guidance all fail to reflect this individualized, shared decision making approach."},
{id:"P6049",d:"D5",c:"D5.C4",dok:3,q:"Which class of medication is considered first line for symptom relief in a patient with hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy who has exertional dyspnea from left ventricular outflow obstruction?",
 o:["Beta blockers or non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers to reduce contractility and heart rate","Loop diuretics to reduce preload","Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers to promote vasodilation","Positive inotropes to augment cardiac output"],a:0,
 why:"Beta blockers or non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers slow heart rate, prolong diastolic filling, and reduce contractility, which decreases the dynamic outflow gradient and relieves symptoms. Loop diuretics reduce preload and can worsen obstruction, dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers and other vasodilators reduce afterload in a way that can also worsen obstruction, and positive inotropes increase contractility, which likewise worsens the obstruction."},
{id:"P6050",d:"D5",c:"D5.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient is diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, and genetic testing identifies a pathogenic sarcomere gene variant. What counseling should be provided regarding first degree relatives?",
 o:["First degree relatives should undergo cascade genetic testing and, if genotype negative, may still need periodic clinical screening depending on age","First degree relatives require no evaluation unless they develop symptoms","Only relatives who are competitive athletes need to be screened","Screening is unnecessary once the proband starts medication"],a:0,
 why:"Cascade genetic testing of first degree relatives identifies who inherited the pathogenic variant, while genotype negative relatives typically need less follow up and genotype positive or untested relatives require ongoing periodic clinical screening due to age related penetrance. Waiting for symptoms misses presymptomatic risk, screening should not be limited only to competitive athletes, and starting medication in the proband does not change the risk to relatives."},
{id:"P6052",d:"D5",c:"D5.C5",dok:2,q:"Which of the following is a recognized major risk factor for sudden cardiac death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?",
 o:["A maximal left ventricular wall thickness of 30 mm or greater","A left ventricular ejection fraction of 60 percent","A resting heart rate of 70 beats per minute","A normal blood pressure response to exercise"],a:0,
 why:"Massive hypertrophy, defined as a maximal wall thickness of 30 mm or greater, is an established major risk factor for sudden cardiac death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. A normal ejection fraction, a normal resting heart rate, and a normal blood pressure response to exercise are reassuring findings rather than risk factors."},
{id:"P6053",d:"D5",c:"D5.C5",dok:2,q:"In a patient with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, which finding on ambulatory ECG monitoring raises concern for increased sudden cardiac death risk?",
 o:["Nonsustained ventricular tachycardia","Occasional isolated premature atrial contractions","A stable sinus rhythm with a normal PR interval","First degree atrioventricular block without symptoms"],a:0,
 why:"Nonsustained ventricular tachycardia is a recognized major risk marker for sudden cardiac death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and factors directly into risk stratification and defibrillator decisions. Isolated premature atrial contractions, a stable normal sinus rhythm, and asymptomatic first degree atrioventricular block are not major sudden death risk markers in this condition."},
{id:"P6054",d:"D5",c:"D5.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has had one episode of syncope during exertion with no clear vasovagal trigger identified. How should this finding be interpreted in sudden cardiac death risk assessment?",
 o:["Unexplained exertional syncope is considered a major risk factor and should prompt consideration of an implantable defibrillator","Syncope in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is never clinically significant and can be disregarded","Syncope only matters if it occurs more than five times per year","This single episode automatically excludes the patient from future competitive activity regardless of other findings"],a:0,
 why:"Unexplained syncope, especially when exertional and without an identified benign cause, is a recognized major risk factor in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy risk stratification and should prompt further assessment including possible defibrillator consideration. Syncope should never simply be disregarded, there is no five episode threshold required for significance, and activity decisions require broader individualized assessment rather than automatic exclusion based on one finding."},
{id:"P6055",d:"D5",c:"D5.C5",dok:4,q:"Primary prevention implantable defibrillator decisions differ between hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and nonischemic dilated cardiomyopathy. Which statement correctly contrasts the two approaches?",
 o:["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy relies on a multifactorial risk score incorporating features such as wall thickness, family history, and syncope, while dilated cardiomyopathy more heavily weighs a persistently reduced ejection fraction of 35 percent or less despite optimal therapy","Both conditions use ejection fraction alone as the sole determinant for defibrillator placement","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy never qualifies for a defibrillator regardless of risk factors","Dilated cardiomyopathy risk stratification ignores response to guideline directed medical therapy entirely"],a:0,
 why:"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy sudden death risk stratification is multifactorial, combining wall thickness, family history of sudden death, unexplained syncope, nonsustained ventricular tachycardia, and other features rather than relying on ejection fraction, whereas dilated cardiomyopathy primary prevention decisions are heavily anchored to a persistently reduced ejection fraction of about 35 percent or less after a trial of optimal guideline directed therapy. Ejection fraction alone is not the hypertrophic cardiomyopathy criterion, patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy can and do qualify for defibrillators, and dilated cardiomyopathy risk assessment specifically depends on response to medical therapy rather than ignoring it."},
{id:"P2001",d:"D6",c:"D6.C1",dok:1,q:"Which description best fits ACC/AHA Stage A heart failure?",
 o:["At risk for heart failure with no structural heart disease and no symptoms","Structural heart disease present but no current or prior heart failure symptoms","Structural heart disease with current or prior heart failure symptoms","Refractory symptoms at rest despite optimized therapy"],a:0,
 why:"Stage A describes patients with risk factors such as hypertension or diabetes but no structural heart changes and no symptoms, so the focus is prevention. Structural disease without symptoms describes Stage B, structural disease with current or prior symptoms describes Stage C, and refractory symptoms despite optimized therapy describe Stage D, so each remaining choice names a later stage rather than the earliest at-risk category."},
{id:"P2002",d:"D6",c:"D6.C1",dok:1,q:"What does New York Heart Association class I describe?",
 o:["Slight limitation of activity, with ordinary activity causing symptoms","No limitation of physical activity, with ordinary activity causing no symptoms","Marked limitation of activity, with less than ordinary activity causing symptoms","Symptoms present even at rest"],a:1,
 why:"Class I means the patient has structural heart disease but no symptom limitation with ordinary activity. Slight limitation with ordinary activity describes class II, marked limitation with less than ordinary activity describes class III, and symptoms present at rest describe class IV, each representing progressively greater symptom burden than the asymptomatic class I presentation."},
{id:"P2003",d:"D6",c:"D6.C1",dok:1,q:"Which ejection fraction range defines heart failure with mildly reduced ejection fraction (HFmrEF)?",
 o:["Less than or equal to 40 percent","Greater than or equal to 50 percent","41 percent to 49 percent","30 percent to 35 percent"],a:2,
 why:"HFmrEF is defined by the 2022 guideline as an ejection fraction of 41 to 49 percent, a category between reduced and preserved. The 40 percent or below range describes HFrEF, the 50 percent or above range describes HFpEF, and 30 to 35 percent falls within the reduced category rather than the mildly reduced range."},
{id:"P2004",d:"D6",c:"D6.C1",dok:1,q:"What defines heart failure with improved ejection fraction (HFimpEF)?",
 o:["An ejection fraction that has always been in the preserved range","An ejection fraction that drops from normal to reduced after a new cardiac event","An ejection fraction that fluctuates from visit to visit without any treatment change","A baseline ejection fraction of 40 percent or less that improves to greater than 40 percent on repeat measurement"],a:3,
 why:"HFimpEF specifically describes a documented recovery from a previously reduced ejection fraction after treatment, which is why GDMT is continued even after improvement. An ejection fraction that was always preserved does not represent recovery, a new drop in ejection fraction describes a new reduced state rather than improvement, and untreated fluctuation does not meet the definition of a therapy-associated improvement."},
{id:"P2005",d:"D6",c:"D6.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient has hypertension and type 2 diabetes, no history of cardiac symptoms, and a normal echocardiogram. Based on ACC/AHA staging, which stage applies?",
 o:["Stage A","Stage B","Stage C","Stage D"],a:0,
 why:"This patient has risk factors for heart failure but no structural abnormality and no symptoms, which fits Stage A exactly. Stage B requires structural disease on imaging, Stage C requires current or prior symptoms, and Stage D requires refractory symptoms despite maximal therapy, none of which apply here."},
{id:"P2006",d:"D6",c:"D6.C1",dok:2,q:"Why is classifying heart failure by ejection fraction category clinically useful?",
 o:["It is the only factor that determines a patient's symptoms","It identifies which patients have evidence-based benefit from specific GDMT pillar medications","It always predicts a patient's exact NYHA functional class","It replaces the need to ever reassess the patient again"],a:1,
 why:"Major heart failure trials enrolled patients by ejection fraction category, so EF classification guides which pillar therapies have proven mortality benefit for a given patient. Symptoms depend on many factors beyond EF alone, EF does not reliably predict NYHA class since the two systems measure different things, and ongoing reassessment is still required regardless of EF category."},
{id:"P2007",d:"D6",c:"D6.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient has an ejection fraction of 55 percent, exertional dyspnea, and echocardiographic evidence of diastolic dysfunction. Which classification fits this presentation?",
 o:["Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction","Heart failure with mildly reduced ejection fraction","Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction","Heart failure with improved ejection fraction"],a:2,
 why:"An ejection fraction at or above 50 percent with heart failure symptoms and diastolic dysfunction defines HFpEF. HFrEF requires an ejection fraction of 40 percent or below, HFmrEF requires 41 to 49 percent, and HFimpEF requires a documented prior reduced ejection fraction that has since improved, none of which describe this presentation."},
{id:"P2008",d:"D6",c:"D6.C1",dok:2,q:"Which statement about New York Heart Association functional class is accurate?",
 o:["It is assigned once at diagnosis and never reassessed","It is determined solely by the patient's ejection fraction","It replaces the need for ACC/AHA staging","It can change over time as symptoms improve or worsen with treatment"],a:3,
 why:"NYHA class reflects current symptom burden and is reassessed at clinical visits because it can improve or worsen with treatment response. It is not fixed at diagnosis, it is based on functional symptoms rather than EF alone, and it complements rather than replaces staging, which tracks structural disease progression separately."},
{id:"P2009",d:"D6",c:"D6.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient previously classified as ACC/AHA Stage C with an ejection fraction of 30 percent now has an ejection fraction of 45 percent after 18 months of optimized GDMT and reports no current symptoms. How should this patient be classified and managed?",
 o:["Remains Stage C with HFimpEF, and GDMT should be continued","Reclassified as Stage A, and GDMT can be stopped","Reclassified as Stage B, and GDMT can be stopped","Reclassified as HFpEF, since the ejection fraction is now higher"],a:0,
 why:"Once a patient has had symptomatic heart failure, staging does not revert to an earlier stage even if the ejection fraction improves, and the HFimpEF designation captures the improvement while signaling that GDMT must continue to prevent relapse. Reclassifying to Stage A or B and stopping medications ignores the history of symptoms and the documented risk of decompensation if GDMT is withdrawn, and calling this HFpEF confuses a recovered reduced ejection fraction with a heart that was never reduced."},
{id:"P2010",d:"D6",c:"D6.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient with longstanding hypertension and left ventricular hypertrophy on echocardiogram has never had heart failure symptoms and has been told they are Stage B. The patient asks why they are being treated for heart failure when they feel fine. What is the best explanation?",
 o:["The patient's symptoms are likely being underreported and should be treated as Stage C","Structural heart disease is already present, and treatment now aims to prevent progression to symptomatic heart failure","The echocardiogram findings are probably an error since the patient has no symptoms","Treatment can safely wait until symptoms actually develop"],a:1,
 why:"Stage B is defined by structural heart disease without symptoms, and early treatment is aimed at preventing the transition to symptomatic Stage C disease. Assuming underreported symptoms is not supported by the data given, dismissing the echocardiogram findings ignores objective diagnostic evidence, and waiting for symptoms to appear would forgo the preventive benefit that early intervention provides."},
{id:"P2011",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:1,q:"Which hormone system is activated early in heart failure to help maintain blood pressure by promoting sodium and water retention?",
 o:["Natriuretic peptide system","Parasympathetic nervous system","Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","Thyroid hormone system"],a:2,
 why:"The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system is a primary neurohormonal compensatory pathway that retains sodium and water and constricts vessels to support blood pressure when cardiac output falls. The natriuretic peptide system works in the opposite direction by promoting sodium and water excretion, the parasympathetic system is not the dominant compensatory driver in heart failure, and thyroid hormone is not part of this compensatory response."},
{id:"P2012",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:1,q:"What is the Frank-Starling mechanism?",
 o:["Increased heart rate compensates for a reduced stroke volume","Ventricular walls thicken over time to reduce wall stress","The heart shifts to anaerobic metabolism to conserve oxygen","Increased ventricular stretch from greater preload produces a stronger contraction"],a:3,
 why:"The Frank-Starling mechanism describes how greater diastolic filling stretches the myocardium and initially increases contractile force. Increased heart rate is a separate chronotropic compensatory response, wall thickening describes hypertrophic remodeling rather than acute stretch, and a shift to anaerobic metabolism is not part of this mechanism."},
{id:"P2013",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:1,q:"What triggers the release of B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) from the ventricles?",
 o:["Ventricular wall stretch from volume or pressure overload","Renal sodium reabsorption","Direct sympathetic nervous system stimulation of the ventricle","Aldosterone secretion from the adrenal gland"],a:0,
 why:"BNP is released by ventricular myocytes in response to increased wall stretch caused by volume or pressure overload. Renal sodium reabsorption and aldosterone secretion are downstream effects of other compensatory pathways rather than triggers of BNP release, and sympathetic stimulation is a separate compensatory mechanism that does not directly trigger BNP secretion."},
{id:"P2014",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:2,q:"Which effect does chronic aldosterone excess have in heart failure beyond promoting sodium retention?",
 o:["Bronchodilation that improves oxygenation","Myocardial fibrosis and adverse cardiac remodeling","Improved myocardial contractility over time","Increased clearance of natriuretic peptides"],a:1,
 why:"Beyond its role in sodium retention, sustained aldosterone exposure promotes fibrosis and structural remodeling that worsen cardiac function over time, which is why mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists are part of GDMT. Aldosterone has no bronchodilating effect, it does not improve contractility, and it is not known to directly increase natriuretic peptide clearance."},
{id:"P2015",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:2,q:"Why do natriuretic peptides fail to fully compensate for volume overload as heart failure progresses?",
 o:["Natriuretic peptide levels fall back to normal once compensation succeeds","The kidneys stop responding to all circulating hormones equally","Receptor downregulation and enzymatic breakdown reduce their effectiveness over time, allowing RAAS and sympathetic effects to dominate","Natriuretic peptides directly cause the fluid retention seen in heart failure"],a:2,
 why:"Chronic elevation leads to receptor downregulation and increased breakdown of natriuretic peptides, so their sodium and water excreting effects diminish even as RAAS and sympathetic activation persist. Natriuretic peptide levels actually rise rather than normalize as heart failure worsens, the kidneys do not lose responsiveness to every hormone uniformly, and natriuretic peptides oppose fluid retention rather than causing it."},
{id:"P2016",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient with heart failure has resting tachycardia. Which compensatory mechanism best explains this finding?",
 o:["Parasympathetic withdrawal acting alone","Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system activation directly increasing heart rate","A natriuretic peptide effect on the sinoatrial node","Sympathetic nervous system activation increasing heart rate to help maintain cardiac output"],a:3,
 why:"Sympathetic activation is the primary driver of compensatory tachycardia, increasing heart rate and contractility to help sustain cardiac output when stroke volume falls. Parasympathetic withdrawal alone does not fully account for the sustained increase, RAAS activation primarily affects vascular tone and volume rather than heart rate directly, and natriuretic peptides would tend to reduce rather than increase heart rate."},
{id:"P2017",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:2,q:"Which class of heart failure medication is specifically intended to blunt the harmful long-term effects of excess sympathetic activation on the heart?",
 o:["Beta blockers","SGLT2 inhibitors","Loop diuretics","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists"],a:0,
 why:"Beta blockers directly antagonize the sustained sympathetic stimulation that damages myocytes and worsens remodeling over time. SGLT2 inhibitors act through natriuretic and metabolic pathways, loop diuretics only relieve congestion without altering the neurohormonal cascade, and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists target the aldosterone pathway rather than sympathetic activation."},
{id:"P2018",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:2,q:"Ventricular remodeling in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is best described as which process?",
 o:["A reversible thickening that resolves completely once symptoms improve","A progressive change in ventricular size, shape, and mass in response to chronic wall stress, producing a more dilated and spherical chamber","A normal age-related change unrelated to heart failure","An immediate response that fully compensates for lost pump function"],a:1,
 why:"Remodeling is a maladaptive structural change driven by chronic neurohormonal activation and wall stress that progressively enlarges and reshapes the ventricle, which is why GDMT aims to slow or reverse it. It is not simply reversible thickening that disappears with symptom improvement, it is not a normal aging process, and it does not fully compensate for lost function since it ultimately worsens pump performance."},
{id:"P2019",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:2,q:"What is the primary hemodynamic consequence of the arterial vasoconstriction driven by RAAS and sympathetic activation in heart failure?",
 o:["Decreased afterload, which improves stroke volume","Improved coronary perfusion in all patients","Increased afterload, which further reduces stroke volume in a failing ventricle","Reduced myocardial oxygen demand"],a:2,
 why:"Vasoconstriction raises arterial resistance, or afterload, which a failing ventricle struggles to pump against, further reducing stroke volume. It does not decrease afterload, it does not reliably improve coronary perfusion in a failing heart, and it increases rather than reduces myocardial oxygen demand by making the heart work harder against higher resistance."},
{id:"P2020",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:2,q:"Why are ACE inhibitors, ARBs, and ARNIs considered central to guideline-directed therapy from a compensatory mechanism standpoint?",
 o:["They primarily increase heart rate to improve cardiac output","They act as direct inotropes that strengthen contractility like dobutamine","They work exclusively through the natriuretic peptide system","They interrupt the maladaptive renin-angiotensin-aldosterone cascade that drives vasoconstriction, sodium retention, and remodeling"],a:3,
 why:"These agents block the RAAS pathway that otherwise perpetuates harmful vasoconstriction, fluid retention, and structural remodeling. They do not raise heart rate to boost output, they are not inotropes that directly increase contractility, and only the ARNI component adds natriuretic peptide augmentation through neprilysin inhibition, while ACE inhibitors and ARBs alone do not act on that system."},
{id:"P2021",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:3,q:"A student nurse asks why a patient with heart failure and a normal blood pressure is being started on a medication that could lower blood pressure further. How should the preceptor explain this using compensatory mechanism principles?",
 o:["The current blood pressure is likely being sustained by harmful RAAS and sympathetic activation, so blocking these pathways protects the heart long term even with a modest blood pressure drop","The medication will have no measurable effect on blood pressure in this patient","Blood pressure should always be maximized in heart failure regardless of the mechanism maintaining it","The blood pressure reading was probably inaccurate and should be repeated before starting therapy"],a:0,
 why:"In heart failure, a seemingly normal blood pressure is often maintained by compensatory vasoconstriction that is ultimately damaging, so pharmacologically interrupting that pathway provides long-term cardiac protection even if it lowers blood pressure somewhat. Assuming no effect ignores the known mechanism, insisting blood pressure always be maximized ignores that the mechanism sustaining it is harmful, and dismissing the reading as inaccurate avoids addressing the actual physiologic concept being taught."},
{id:"P2022",d:"D6",c:"D6.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient asks why heart failure symptoms worsened gradually over several years rather than appearing all at once after their heart attack. Which explanation best reflects the underlying pathophysiology?",
 o:["Heart failure symptoms depend only on ongoing coronary artery disease and are unrelated to any compensatory response","Compensatory mechanisms such as RAAS and sympathetic activation initially maintained cardiac output, but their sustained activation caused progressive remodeling and worsening pump function over time","The initial heart injury alone determines the entire symptom trajectory regardless of any ongoing physiologic processes","Compensatory mechanisms have no measurable effect on how heart failure progresses"],a:1,
 why:"The gradual symptom progression reflects the fact that compensatory mechanisms are initially protective but become maladaptive with sustained activation, driving remodeling and decline over years. Attributing progression only to ongoing coronary disease ignores the neurohormonal contribution, claiming the initial injury alone determines the trajectory ignores the well documented role of remodeling, and denying any effect of compensatory mechanisms contradicts core heart failure pathophysiology."},
{id:"P2023",d:"D6",c:"D6.C3",dok:1,q:"What does hepatojugular reflux testing assess?",
 o:["Tenderness in the right upper quadrant during inspiration, indicating gallbladder disease","Calf pain with dorsiflexion of the foot, indicating deep vein thrombosis","Sustained rise in jugular venous pressure with sustained abdominal pressure, indicating volume overload","Point tenderness in the right lower abdomen, indicating appendicitis"],a:2,
 why:"Hepatojugular reflux testing applies sustained pressure over the abdomen and observes for a sustained rise in jugular venous pressure, which indicates right-sided volume overload in heart failure. The other options describe unrelated physical exam findings, Murphy sign for gallbladder disease, Homans sign for deep vein thrombosis, and McBurney point tenderness for appendicitis, none of which assess volume status."},
{id:"P2024",d:"D6",c:"D6.C3",dok:2,q:"Which finding reflects a neurohormonal compensatory response rather than a direct sign of fluid overload?",
 o:["Crackles heard at both lung bases","Elevated jugular venous pressure","Pitting edema of the lower extremities","Resting tachycardia with cool extremities"],a:3,
 why:"Resting tachycardia with cool extremities reflects sympathetic activation and reduced perfusion rather than fluid accumulation itself. Crackles, elevated jugular venous pressure, and pitting edema are all direct physical signs of volume overload and congestion, not markers of the compensatory perfusion response."},
{id:"P2025",d:"D6",c:"D6.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient with heart failure reports awakening suddenly at night gasping for air, which improves several minutes after sitting upright. What does this describe?",
 o:["Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea from fluid redistribution to the lungs during sleep","Orthopnea, which occurs immediately upon lying down before falling asleep","Cheyne-Stokes respiration, a cyclical breathing pattern unrelated to position","Anxiety-related hyperventilation unrelated to fluid status"],a:0,
 why:"Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea occurs after a period of lying flat, as fluid redistributes into the lungs during sleep, causing sudden breathlessness that improves with sitting up. Orthopnea instead occurs immediately upon lying down rather than after a delay, Cheyne-Stokes respiration is a distinct cyclical breathing pattern rather than a sudden awakening event, and attributing the episode to anxiety dismisses a well recognized congestive symptom."},
{id:"P2026",d:"D6",c:"D6.C3",dok:2,q:"Which chest x-ray finding is most specific for pulmonary venous congestion in heart failure?",
 o:["Lung hyperinflation","Kerley B lines","Pleural thickening","Widened mediastinum"],a:1,
 why:"Kerley B lines represent thickened interlobular septa from interstitial fluid and are a specific radiographic sign of pulmonary venous congestion. Hyperinflation is typical of obstructive lung disease rather than congestion, pleural thickening reflects a chronic process rather than acute fluid overload, and a widened mediastinum suggests aortic or mediastinal pathology rather than venous congestion."},
{id:"P2027",d:"D6",c:"D6.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient with heart failure and chronic kidney disease has an NT-proBNP result that is higher than expected for the apparent degree of clinical congestion. What most likely explains this?",
 o:["Chronic kidney disease has no known effect on natriuretic peptide levels","The natriuretic peptide assay is invalid in any patient with kidney disease","Reduced renal clearance of natriuretic peptides in chronic kidney disease raises baseline levels independent of congestion severity","Chronic kidney disease always lowers natriuretic peptide levels, masking true congestion"],a:2,
 why:"Natriuretic peptides are cleared partly by the kidneys, so reduced renal function raises baseline levels even without a proportional increase in congestion, which is why results must be interpreted alongside renal function and clinical exam. It is not true that kidney disease has no effect, the assay remains clinically useful with adjusted interpretation rather than invalid, and kidney disease raises rather than lowers natriuretic peptide levels."},
{id:"P2028",d:"D6",c:"D6.C3",dok:2,q:"Which physical exam finding is a late but specific sign of significant right-sided volume overload?",
 o:["Mild bilateral ankle edema","General fatigue","A dry, nonproductive cough","Ascites with hepatomegaly"],a:3,
 why:"Ascites and hepatomegaly reflect significant systemic venous congestion and typically appear later in the course of right-sided volume overload, making them more specific findings. Mild ankle edema, fatigue, and a dry cough are common and nonspecific, and each can result from many conditions unrelated to advanced volume overload."},
{id:"P2029",d:"D6",c:"D6.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient denies orthopnea and reports not needing extra pillows to sleep, but the nurse notes jugular venous distension to the angle of the jaw and 2+ pitting edema to the knees. How should the nurse interpret the absence of orthopnea in this context?",
 o:["The absence of orthopnea does not rule out congestion, since the objective findings of jugular venous distension and edema still indicate significant volume overload","Significant congestion is unlikely since orthopnea is always present when congestion is clinically significant","The jugular venous distension and edema findings should be disregarded since they conflict with the patient's symptom report","The patient should only be reassessed for congestion if orthopnea eventually develops"],a:0,
 why:"No single symptom is required for congestion to be present, and objective findings such as marked jugular venous distension and significant edema are reliable indicators of volume overload on their own. Assuming orthopnea must always be present overrelies on one symptom, dismissing the objective exam findings ignores valid clinical data, and waiting for orthopnea to develop would delay recognizing existing congestion."},
{id:"P2030",d:"D6",c:"D6.C3",dok:3,q:"A nurse is assessing volume status in a patient with heart failure and obesity, where peripheral edema is difficult to evaluate due to body habitus. Which combination of findings would best support a determination of volume overload despite this limitation?",
 o:["An elevated body mass index alone","Elevated jugular venous pressure, a positive hepatojugular reflux, and an S3 gallop","A normal blood pressure and heart rate","Patient-reported thirst"],a:1,
 why:"Jugular venous pressure, hepatojugular reflux, and an S3 gallop are exam findings that remain reliable indicators of volume status even when peripheral edema is hard to assess due to body habitus. Body mass index alone is not a congestion marker, normal vital signs are reassuring but do not rule congestion in or out, and thirst is a nonspecific symptom that does not reliably indicate volume status."},
{id:"P2031",d:"D6",c:"D6.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient admitted with heart failure has crackles at both lung bases, an S3 gallop, and 3+ edema, with a 3 pound weight gain over the prior two days, but denies feeling short of breath. Which action best reflects sound clinical judgment?",
 o:["Withhold diuresis since the patient is asymptomatic","Plan for discharge since the patient reports feeling well","Continue to treat the patient as clinically congested based on the objective exam and weight findings, since symptom perception can lag behind measurable fluid status","Rely solely on the patient's symptom report going forward to guide therapy"],a:2,
 why:"Objective findings such as lung crackles, an S3 gallop, significant edema, and rapid weight gain reliably indicate clinically significant congestion even when a patient does not yet perceive breathlessness, so treatment should proceed based on these findings. Withholding diuresis, planning discharge, or relying only on symptom report would each ignore strong objective evidence of volume overload and risk clinical deterioration."},
{id:"P2032",d:"D6",c:"D6.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient's BNP returns markedly elevated during an emergency department visit for dyspnea. Which additional information is most important for the nurse to gather before assuming this confirms acute decompensated heart failure?",
 o:["The exact time of day the blood sample was drawn","The patient's exercise tolerance six months prior to this visit","The patient's dietary sodium intake over the past week","The patient's baseline BNP value and current renal function, since both chronic heart failure and reduced kidney function can chronically elevate BNP independent of acute decompensation"],a:3,
 why:"Because BNP can be chronically elevated in patients with preexisting heart failure or reduced renal function, comparing the current value to a personal baseline and current renal function is essential to correctly interpret an elevated result. Time of day has no meaningful effect on BNP interpretation, prior exercise tolerance from months earlier is less relevant to interpreting an acute result, and while dietary sodium intake is clinically relevant to congestion, it is not the most important piece of information for interpreting the BNP number itself."},
{id:"P2033",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:1,q:"Which medication class provides symptom relief in heart failure but is not considered one of the four foundational GDMT pillars?",
 o:["Loop diuretics","SGLT2 inhibitors","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists","Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitors"],a:0,
 why:"Loop diuretics relieve congestive symptoms but have not been shown to reduce mortality the way the four pillar classes have, so they are not counted among the foundational pillars. SGLT2 inhibitors, mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists, and ARNIs are each one of the four pillar classes with proven mortality benefit in HFrEF."},
{id:"P2034",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:2,q:"Which beta blocker lacks outcome trial evidence for use in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction and is therefore avoided for that indication?",
 o:["Carvedilol","Metoprolol tartrate (immediate release)","Bisoprolol","Metoprolol succinate (extended release)"],a:1,
 why:"Immediate release metoprolol tartrate was not studied in the pivotal heart failure mortality trials and lacks the evidence base that supports its use, unlike the extended release succinate formulation. Carvedilol, bisoprolol, and metoprolol succinate are each supported by outcome trial evidence and are the recommended beta blockers for HFrEF."},
{id:"P2035",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:2,q:"What is the primary rationale for using SGLT2 inhibitors in heart failure regardless of a patient's diabetes status?",
 o:["They are used solely to manage hyperglycemia in patients who also have diabetes","They replace the need for diuretic therapy in most patients","They provide natriuretic and cardiorenal protective effects that are independent of glucose lowering","They primarily work by lowering heart rate similar to a beta blocker"],a:2,
 why:"SGLT2 inhibitors reduce heart failure hospitalizations and offer cardiorenal protection through mechanisms independent of their glucose lowering effect, which is why they benefit patients without diabetes as well. Limiting their purpose to glycemic control misses this broader benefit, they do not eliminate the need for diuretics, and their mechanism does not involve lowering heart rate."},
{id:"P2036",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient starting an SGLT2 inhibitor for heart failure should be educated about which potential adverse effect?",
 o:["Significant weight gain","Bradycardia","Hyperkalemia as the primary concern","Genital mycotic infections related to increased urinary glucose excretion"],a:3,
 why:"Increased glucose in the urine from SGLT2 inhibition creates an environment that increases the risk of genital mycotic infections, which patients should be taught to recognize. SGLT2 inhibitors typically cause modest weight loss rather than gain, they are not associated with bradycardia, and hyperkalemia is more characteristically linked to mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists rather than SGLT2 inhibitors."},
{id:"P2037",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:2,q:"Which statement about starting beta blockers in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is accurate?",
 o:["Therapy should begin at a low dose and be titrated slowly as tolerated","The full target dose should be started immediately for the fastest benefit","Beta blockers should only be added once the patient is completely symptom free","Beta blockers should be avoided in patients who are also taking an ARNI"],a:0,
 why:"Beta blockers are started at a low dose and up-titrated gradually because starting at full dose can precipitate acute decompensation in a patient with reduced reserve. Waiting for complete symptom resolution would unnecessarily delay a therapy with proven mortality benefit, and there is no contraindication to combining a beta blocker with an ARNI, since both are foundational pillars meant to be used together."},
{id:"P2038",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:2,q:"Which combination is an alternative option for patients who cannot tolerate an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or ARNI due to significant renal dysfunction or hyperkalemia risk?",
 o:["A higher dose of the current beta blocker alone","Hydralazine and isosorbide dinitrate","A second mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist added to the first","A loop diuretic used in place of RAAS blockade"],a:1,
 why:"Hydralazine combined with isosorbide dinitrate is a guideline-supported alternative that provides vasodilating benefit without the renal and potassium risks of RAAS blockade. Increasing the beta blocker dose does not substitute for RAAS blockade, adding a second MRA would worsen hyperkalemia risk rather than avoid it, and diuretics manage congestion but do not provide the mortality benefit associated with RAAS-directed therapy."},
{id:"P2039",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:2,q:"What monitoring is recommended after initiating or increasing the dose of a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist?",
 o:["Liver function tests as the primary concern","Thyroid function studies","Serum potassium and renal function within about one to two weeks","A complete blood count within 24 hours"],a:2,
 why:"Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists carry a risk of hyperkalemia and worsening renal function, so potassium and creatinine are checked within one to two weeks of starting or adjusting the dose. Liver function, thyroid studies, and a complete blood count are not the primary monitoring concerns for this drug class."},
{id:"P2040",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient with HFrEF is on a beta blocker and an SGLT2 inhibitor but has not started an ARNI or MRA. Blood pressure is 128/78 and renal function is normal. What is the most appropriate next consideration?",
 o:["Wait until the current two medications reach maximum dose before adding any others","Avoid adding more medications since the patient is currently stable","Add only a diuretic, since blood pressure is already well controlled","Initiate the remaining pillar therapies, since all four classes provide independent mortality benefit and should be added as tolerated rather than viewed as sequential alternatives"],a:3,
 why:"Current guidance favors initiating all four pillar classes as soon as reasonably tolerated rather than maximizing one class before starting the next, since each contributes independent benefit. Waiting for maximum dose of the current medications unnecessarily delays proven benefit, avoiding new medications because the patient feels stable forgoes further risk reduction, and adding only a diuretic does not address the two missing mortality-benefit pillars."},
{id:"P2041",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient newly started on sacubitril-valsartan reports facial and lip swelling within hours of the first dose. What is the priority nursing action?",
 o:["Stop the medication and seek emergency evaluation for angioedema","Reassure the patient that this is an expected mild reaction that will resolve on its own","Administer an antihistamine and continue the medication as scheduled","Plan to decrease the dose at the next scheduled visit"],a:0,
 why:"Facial and lip swelling after starting an ARNI raises concern for angioedema, which can progress to airway compromise and requires stopping the drug and seeking emergency evaluation. Reassuring the patient without action risks a life-threatening delay, treating with an antihistamine while continuing the drug does not remove the causative agent, and waiting until the next visit to adjust the dose is an unsafe delay for a potentially rapidly progressing reaction."},
{id:"P2042",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient with HFrEF and type 2 diabetes on metformin is being started on an SGLT2 inhibitor. Which teaching point is most important to prevent a serious complication?",
 o:["Monitor only for hypoglycemia, since SGLT2 inhibitors lower blood sugar significantly on their own","Seek care for nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain even if blood glucose is not markedly elevated, since SGLT2 inhibitors can cause euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis","Stop metformin permanently once the SGLT2 inhibitor is started","Expect significant weight gain as a common side effect of this medication"],a:1,
 why:"SGLT2 inhibitors carry a risk of euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis, where ketoacidosis symptoms occur without markedly elevated glucose, so patients need to recognize these symptoms regardless of their glucose reading. SGLT2 inhibitors alone carry a relatively low hypoglycemia risk so that framing overstates the concern, there is no requirement to permanently stop metformin when starting an SGLT2 inhibitor, and this drug class typically causes modest weight loss rather than gain."},
{id:"P2043",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient with HFrEF, an ejection fraction of 25 percent, and persistent NYHA class II symptoms remains symptomatic despite optimized doses of all four GDMT pillars. Which action is most appropriate to consider next per current guidelines?",
 o:["Increase the loop diuretic dose as the next disease-modifying step","Discontinue the SGLT2 inhibitor to simplify the medication regimen","Referral for consideration of additional agents such as ivabradine or vericiguat as add-on therapy","Switch the ARNI back to a standalone ACE inhibitor"],a:2,
 why:"When a patient remains symptomatic on optimized four-pillar therapy, guidelines support considering additional agents such as ivabradine for elevated heart rate or vericiguat for further risk reduction. Increasing the diuretic dose addresses congestion symptoms but is not a disease-modifying step, discontinuing the SGLT2 inhibitor removes a proven-benefit pillar, and switching the ARNI back to an ACE inhibitor downgrades therapy without any benefit."},
{id:"P2044",d:"D6",c:"D6.C4",dok:4,q:"A patient with HFrEF (EF 25%) is admitted for elective surgery. Home medications include sacubitril-valsartan, carvedilol, spironolactone, and dapagliflozin. Preoperative labs show potassium 5.0 mmol/L, baseline creatinine, and blood pressure 110/68. The surgical team asks which medications should be held the morning of surgery and why. Which response best integrates GDMT principles with perioperative risk?",
 o:["Hold all four GDMT medications the morning of surgery to minimize any perioperative risk","Continue all four medications unchanged since the patient is hemodynamically stable","Hold only the beta blocker since it is most likely to cause hypotension under anesthesia","Hold the SGLT2 inhibitor for the recommended interval before surgery to reduce ketoacidosis and dehydration risk, hold the mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist given the potassium and expected fluid shifts, continue the beta blocker to avoid rebound tachycardia and withdrawal effects, and reassess the ARNI based on blood pressure and renal trends"],a:3,
 why:"This response reflects the specific perioperative risks of each pillar: SGLT2 inhibitors are held before surgery due to ketoacidosis and volume risk, MRAs are held given the borderline potassium and anticipated fluid shifts, and beta blockers are continued because abrupt withdrawal can cause dangerous rebound tachycardia and ischemia. Holding all four medications unnecessarily stops the beta blocker and risks withdrawal, continuing all four unchanged ignores real SGLT2 inhibitor and MRA perioperative risks, and holding only the beta blocker gets the priority backwards since abrupt beta blocker withdrawal is the more dangerous omission while ignoring the SGLT2 inhibitor and MRA concerns."},
{id:"P2045",d:"D6",c:"D6.C5",dok:2,q:"Which factor is a common precipitant of acute decompensated heart failure?",
 o:["Dietary sodium indiscretion","Routine annual influenza vaccination","Regular low-intensity walking","Consistent adherence to prescribed GDMT"],a:0,
 why:"Excess dietary sodium intake increases fluid retention and is one of the most common preventable precipitants of acute decompensation. Influenza vaccination is actually recommended to reduce illness-related decompensation risk, regular low-intensity activity is generally beneficial rather than harmful, and consistent GDMT adherence protects against decompensation rather than causing it."},
{id:"P2046",d:"D6",c:"D6.C5",dok:2,q:"Which class of medication is typically held or used with caution during an episode of acute decompensated heart failure accompanied by hypotension or low output?",
 o:["Loop diuretics","Beta blockers","Supplemental oxygen","IV vasodilators for a hypertensive presentation"],a:1,
 why:"Beta blockers can further reduce contractility and are typically held or cautiously continued when a patient shows signs of hypotension or low cardiac output during acute decompensation. Loop diuretics remain a mainstay of ADHF treatment, supplemental oxygen is supportive care that is given rather than withheld, and IV vasodilators are an appropriate treatment option specifically for a hypertensive congestive presentation."},
{id:"P2047",d:"D6",c:"D6.C5",dok:2,q:"What is the primary goal of intravenous diuretic therapy in acute decompensated heart failure?",
 o:["Lower blood pressure to a specific numeric target","Increase heart rate to improve cardiac output","Achieve decongestion by removing excess extracellular fluid","Eliminate the need for ongoing daily weight monitoring"],a:2,
 why:"The main purpose of IV diuretic therapy in ADHF is to relieve congestion by removing excess extracellular fluid. It is not primarily aimed at a specific blood pressure target, it has no role in increasing heart rate, and daily weight monitoring remains important both during and after diuresis to track ongoing fluid status."},
{id:"P2048",d:"D6",c:"D6.C5",dok:2,q:"Which finding during IV diuresis for acute decompensated heart failure would most concern the nurse?",
 o:["A 2 pound weight loss over 24 hours with improved breathing","Mild thirst reported by the patient","Increased urine output with looser fitting rings","A rising creatinine accompanied by minimal urine output despite adequate diuretic dosing"],a:3,
 why:"A rising creatinine with minimal urine output despite adequate dosing suggests worsening renal perfusion or diuretic resistance and needs prompt evaluation. Weight loss with improved breathing, mild thirst, and increased urine output with looser rings are all expected signs that diuresis is working effectively rather than causes for concern."},
{id:"P2049",d:"D6",c:"D6.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with acute decompensated heart failure has a blood pressure of 82/50, cool clammy skin, and confusion, and has already received diuretics without improvement. What is the most appropriate next step?",
 o:["Consider inotropic support such as dobutamine or milrinone for a cold and hypoperfused presentation","Increase the diuretic dose further","Administer a beta blocker to reduce myocardial oxygen demand","Give a large volume normal saline bolus"],a:0,
 why:"Hypotension, cool skin, and confusion indicate a low output, hypoperfused state where inotropic support is needed to improve perfusion rather than further diuresis. Increasing diuretics would worsen hypotension without addressing perfusion, beta blockers are contraindicated in hypotensive low-output states because they further reduce contractility, and a large fluid bolus risks worsening pulmonary congestion without correcting the underlying pump failure."},
{id:"P2050",d:"D6",c:"D6.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient receiving high dose IV loop diuretic therapy for acute decompensated heart failure shows diminishing urine output despite dose increases. Which strategy reflects an evidence-based approach to overcoming diuretic resistance?",
 o:["Switch to an oral loop diuretic at the same total daily dose","Add a second diuretic with a different mechanism, such as a thiazide-type diuretic, for sequential nephron blockade","Stop diuretics entirely and rely on fluid restriction alone","Start an SGLT2 inhibitor acutely for additional diuretic effect"],a:1,
 why:"Adding a diuretic that blocks sodium reabsorption at a different nephron segment, such as a thiazide-type agent, produces sequential nephron blockade that can overcome resistance to loop diuretics alone. Switching to an oral loop diuretic is unreliable in a congested gut with reduced absorption, stopping diuretics and relying on fluid restriction is far too slow for acute decompensation, and starting an SGLT2 inhibitor during active hemodynamic instability is not the recommended acute management strategy."},
{id:"P2051",d:"D6",c:"D6.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient is being transitioned from IV to oral diuretics prior to discharge for acute decompensated heart failure. Which finding best supports readiness for this transition?",
 o:["The patient reports feeling ready to go home","Blood pressure has returned to the patient's prehospital baseline alone","The patient has reached a stable euvolemic weight with consistent urine output on a stable IV dose for 24 to 48 hours","The patient has not required supplemental oxygen for the past two hours"],a:2,
 why:"A stable euvolemic weight with consistent response on a steady IV dose over one to two days provides objective evidence that fluid status is controlled and ready for oral conversion. Feeling ready to go home is subjective and not a clinical readiness marker, blood pressure alone does not confirm volume status, and a short period without oxygen need is reassuring but insufficient on its own to confirm readiness."},
{id:"P2052",d:"D6",c:"D6.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with acute decompensated heart failure and worsening renal function despite diuresis is diagnosed with cardiorenal syndrome. Which explanation best describes this process to a new nurse?",
 o:["The kidneys are failing due to an unrelated primary renal disease","Diuretics are directly toxic to the kidneys in all patients and should always be discontinued","Renal function does not affect diuretic dosing decisions","Reduced cardiac output and venous congestion both impair kidney perfusion and function, creating a cycle where treating congestion can also stress the kidneys"],a:3,
 why:"Cardiorenal syndrome reflects the interaction between poor forward flow and venous congestion, both of which impair renal perfusion, so aggressive decongestion can sometimes further stress kidney function even as it treats the heart failure. Attributing it to an unrelated primary renal disease misses this cardiac-renal interaction, claiming diuretics are always directly toxic overgeneralizes an isolated effect into a rule that would prevent needed treatment, and renal function actually directly informs diuretic dosing decisions rather than being irrelevant."},
{id:"P2053",d:"D6",c:"D6.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with acute decompensated heart failure and significant respiratory distress from pulmonary edema is being considered for noninvasive positive pressure ventilation. What is the primary physiologic benefit of this intervention?",
 o:["It reduces the work of breathing and improves oxygenation by decreasing preload and afterload while recruiting collapsed alveoli","It directly removes excess fluid from the lungs in the same way diuretics do","It replaces the need for diuretic or vasodilator therapy","It primarily works by lowering the patient's heart rate"],a:0,
 why:"Positive pressure ventilation improves oxygenation and reduces the work of breathing by decreasing venous return and afterload while helping reopen collapsed alveoli. It does not physically remove fluid the way a diuretic does, it is an adjunct rather than a replacement for diuretic and vasodilator therapy, and its mechanism does not primarily involve lowering heart rate."},
{id:"P2054",d:"D6",c:"D6.C5",dok:4,q:"A patient is admitted with acute decompensated heart failure. Over the first 24 hours, the nurse observes blood pressure dropping from 118/74 to 88/56, urine output falling despite an increased furosemide infusion rate, extremities becoming cooler, and mild new confusion, while lung crackles are improving. How should the nurse synthesize this overall trend?",
 o:["The improving crackles indicate the current treatment plan is working well and should continue unchanged","The patient appears to be transitioning from a congested but adequately perfused state toward a low output, hypoperfused state, and the care team should be notified to reassess the treatment plan rather than continuing to escalate diuretics alone","The falling blood pressure is an expected and desired response to diuresis and requires no action","The new confusion is most likely unrelated sundowning and does not need to be correlated with the hemodynamic trend"],a:1,
 why:"Taken together, falling blood pressure, declining urine output despite escalating diuretic dosing, cooling extremities, and new confusion point toward worsening perfusion even though the lungs are clearing, so the trend as a whole warrants prompt reassessment rather than continued diuretic escalation alone. Focusing only on the improving crackles ignores the concerning perfusion signs, treating the blood pressure drop as simply expected dismisses a potential warning sign of hypoperfusion, and dismissing the confusion as unrelated sundowning fails to integrate it with the rest of the worsening hemodynamic picture."},
{id:"P2055",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:1,q:"Which vaccination is routinely recommended for patients with heart failure to help reduce the risk of illness-related decompensation?",
 o:["Live attenuated intranasal vaccine only, regardless of formulation preference","Varicella vaccine","Annual influenza vaccine","No vaccines are specifically recommended for heart failure patients"],a:2,
 why:"Annual influenza vaccination is recommended for heart failure patients because respiratory illness is a common trigger for decompensation. Specifying only the live intranasal formulation is inaccurate since inactivated formulations are typically preferred in this population, varicella vaccine is not a heart failure specific recommendation, and vaccination is in fact an important part of preventive care in heart failure rather than unnecessary."},
{id:"P2056",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:2,q:"What is the general daily sodium restriction recommended for most patients with symptomatic heart failure?",
 o:["Less than 500 milligrams per day","No restriction is needed if the patient is taking a diuretic","Less than 6 grams per day","Less than about 2 to 3 grams per day"],a:3,
 why:"A moderate sodium restriction of roughly 2 to 3 grams per day is generally recommended to help reduce fluid retention in symptomatic heart failure. A restriction below 500 milligrams is overly strict and difficult to sustain, diuretics do not eliminate the benefit of sodium restriction, and 6 grams per day is close to average intake and not a meaningful therapeutic restriction."},
{id:"P2057",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:2,q:"Which statement about cardiac rehabilitation for heart failure patients is accurate?",
 o:["Supervised exercise training can improve functional capacity and quality of life in stable heart failure patients","Exercise should be avoided entirely to protect the heart from further damage","Cardiac rehabilitation is appropriate only after a myocardial infarction and not for heart failure","Patients should wait until they reach NYHA class IV before beginning any structured activity"],a:0,
 why:"Supervised, individualized exercise training has been shown to improve functional capacity and quality of life in stable heart failure patients and is a recommended component of care. Avoiding exercise entirely is an outdated view not supported by current evidence, heart failure is itself a recognized indication for cardiac rehabilitation independent of myocardial infarction, and waiting until class IV symptoms develop is backwards since earlier, appropriately supervised activity is generally beneficial rather than something to delay."},
{id:"P2058",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:2,q:"When should a follow-up appointment typically be scheduled after discharge for a heart failure hospitalization to help reduce readmission risk?",
 o:["Within 60 days of discharge","Within about 7 to 14 days of discharge","Only if symptoms return","No follow-up is needed if the patient felt well at discharge"],a:1,
 why:"Early follow-up within about 7 to 14 days after discharge addresses the high-risk vulnerable period and has been associated with lower readmission rates. Waiting 60 days misses this critical early window, scheduling follow-up only if symptoms return is a reactive approach rather than the recommended proactive one, and skipping follow-up because the patient felt well at discharge ignores the known risk of early decompensation after hospitalization."},
{id:"P2059",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient with advanced heart failure asks about a device that can be implanted to help detect worsening congestion before symptoms become severe. Which technology is the nurse describing when explaining an implantable pulmonary artery pressure monitoring system?",
 o:["An implantable cardioverter defibrillator that treats life-threatening arrhythmias","A cardiac resynchronization therapy device that improves ventricular synchrony","An implantable hemodynamic monitor that transmits pulmonary artery pressure data remotely to guide early medication adjustment","A left ventricular assist device that mechanically pumps blood"],a:2,
 why:"An implantable pulmonary artery pressure monitor transmits hemodynamic data remotely so clinicians can detect rising pressures and adjust medications before overt symptoms develop. An implantable cardioverter defibrillator treats arrhythmias rather than monitoring pressure, a resynchronization device improves the timing of ventricular contraction rather than transmitting pressure data, and a ventricular assist device is a mechanical pump rather than a monitoring tool."},
{id:"P2060",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient with an ejection fraction of 30 percent and a wide QRS complex on ECG is being evaluated for a device to improve ventricular synchrony and symptoms. Which device is most appropriate to discuss?",
 o:["A standard single chamber pacemaker","An implantable loop recorder","A left ventricular assist device","Cardiac resynchronization therapy (biventricular pacemaker)"],a:3,
 why:"Cardiac resynchronization therapy uses biventricular pacing to correct the dyssynchrony reflected by a wide QRS complex in a patient with reduced ejection fraction, improving symptoms and outcomes. A standard single chamber pacemaker does not address ventricular dyssynchrony, an implantable loop recorder is a diagnostic monitoring tool rather than a treatment, and a ventricular assist device is generally reserved for more advanced, refractory disease rather than being the first option for this indication."},
{id:"P2061",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient with end-stage heart failure who is not a candidate for transplant or mechanical circulatory support expresses a wish to focus on comfort and staying out of the hospital. What is the most appropriate nursing response?",
 o:["Initiate a palliative care referral to align the plan of care with the patient's goals while continuing to manage symptoms","Explain that palliative care means all heart failure treatment must stop immediately","Tell the patient this decision cannot be discussed until they are actively dying","Continue escalating aggressive interventions, since stopping is not an option"],a:0,
 why:"A palliative care referral supports symptom management and goal-concordant care and can be introduced alongside ongoing heart failure treatment rather than only at the very end of life. Stating that all treatment must stop misrepresents what palliative care involves, delaying the conversation until the patient is actively dying misses an opportunity for earlier goal-aligned planning, and continuing to escalate aggressive interventions disregards the patient's expressed wishes and autonomy."},
{id:"P2062",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient recently discharged after a heart failure hospitalization asks why they need to know both their discharge medications and the reason for each one. What is the best explanation of why this improves outcomes?",
 o:["This information is only required for insurance documentation purposes","Understanding the purpose of each medication supports adherence and helps the patient recognize when a change or missed dose could affect their symptoms, reducing readmission risk","Medication reconciliation is the pharmacist's responsibility alone and does not require patient understanding","Knowing the drug names alone is sufficient without understanding their purpose"],a:1,
 why:"When patients understand why each medication is prescribed, they are more likely to adhere to the regimen and recognize warning signs related to missed doses or changes, which helps prevent readmission. Framing this as only an insurance requirement misses the clinical rationale, treating medication understanding as solely the pharmacist's role undervalues the patient's active role in their own care, and knowing only drug names without understanding their purpose leaves the patient unable to connect medication changes to symptom changes."},
{id:"P2063",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient being considered for a left ventricular assist device as destination therapy asks what this means. What is the most accurate explanation?",
 o:["It is a temporary device used only until a donor heart becomes available","It is a fully implantable replacement heart that eliminates the need for any further heart failure medications","The device is intended as long-term support rather than a bridge to transplant, for patients who are not transplant candidates","It can be removed once heart function recovers in nearly all patients"],a:2,
 why:"Destination therapy means the device is intended to provide long-term mechanical circulatory support for patients who are not eligible for heart transplant, rather than serving as a temporary bridge. Describing it as temporary until a donor heart is found actually describes bridge to transplant, describing it as a full heart replacement that removes the need for ongoing medications overstates the device since patients still require GDMT and anticoagulation, and expecting removal after recovery in nearly all patients is inaccurate since recovery allowing device removal is uncommon in this population."},
{id:"P2064",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:4,q:"A patient with recurrent heart failure hospitalizations lives alone, has limited health literacy, and has missed several follow-up appointments. The interdisciplinary team is planning the next discharge. Which combination of interventions would most comprehensively address this patient's risk for readmission?",
 o:["Providing a detailed written discharge packet alone","Scheduling the routine 4 to 6 week follow-up appointment without any additional support","Discharging with instructions to call if symptoms worsen and no additional referrals","Teach-back education tailored to health literacy level, a home health or transitional care referral, an early follow-up appointment with transportation assistance arranged, and social work involvement to address barriers to attending appointments"],a:3,
 why:"This patient has multiple identified risk factors, including limited health literacy, social isolation, and a pattern of missed appointments, so a comprehensive plan combining teach-back education, transitional support, an early accessible follow-up visit, and social work involvement addresses each barrier directly. A written packet alone does not accommodate limited health literacy, the standard longer-interval follow-up misses the high-risk early period and ignores the identified barriers, and a reactive call-if-worse instruction without added support fails to address the access and literacy barriers already identified."},
{id:"P2065",d:"D6",c:"D6.C6",dok:4,q:"A home health nurse is developing an individualized self-care education plan for a patient with heart failure, chronic kidney disease, and a recent diagnosis of mild cognitive impairment who lives with a supportive spouse. Which approach best integrates all of these factors into the education plan?",
 o:["Simplify and repeat teaching using written and visual aids, involve the spouse as a care partner for daily weight and symptom tracking, and coordinate medication and fluid management decisions with renal function trends","Provide standard heart failure education materials identical to those used for cognitively intact patients","Direct all education solely to the patient to promote independence despite the cognitive impairment","Focus education only on heart failure self-care without factoring in the coexisting kidney disease"],a:0,
 why:"This plan accounts for the cognitive impairment by simplifying and repeating teaching with visual aids and involving the spouse as a care partner, while also recognizing that kidney disease affects fluid and medication management decisions that must be coordinated together. Using standard materials unchanged ignores the documented cognitive impairment, directing education solely to the patient despite that impairment overlooks an identified safety need for support, and focusing only on heart failure without considering the coexisting renal disease misses an important factor affecting the overall self-care plan."},
{id:"P5036",d:"D7",c:"D7.C1",dok:1,q:"Which non-invasive test uses sound waves reflected off moving red blood cells to assess blood flow velocity in the carotid arteries?",
 o:["Cardiac magnetic resonance imaging","Coronary computed tomography angiography","Nuclear perfusion scan","Carotid duplex ultrasound"],a:3,
 why:"Carotid duplex ultrasound combines B mode imaging with Doppler technology to visualize the carotid arteries and measure blood flow velocity, which correlates with the degree of stenosis. Cardiac MRI and coronary CT angiography use magnetic fields or radiation based cross sectional imaging rather than Doppler flow velocity, and a nuclear perfusion scan evaluates myocardial blood flow, not carotid artery flow."},
{id:"P5037",d:"D7",c:"D7.C1",dok:1,q:"What is the primary purpose of an ankle brachial index (ABI) measurement?",
 o:["To screen for peripheral arterial disease by comparing blood pressure in the ankle to the arm","To assess pulmonary function","To measure right atrial pressure","To evaluate cardiac rhythm"],a:0,
 why:"The ankle brachial index compares systolic blood pressure at the ankle to that at the arm, and a reduced ratio indicates reduced arterial blood flow to the lower extremity consistent with peripheral arterial disease. It has no role in assessing pulmonary function, right atrial pressure, or cardiac rhythm, which are evaluated by entirely different tests."},
{id:"P5038",d:"D7",c:"D7.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient is scheduled for a dobutamine stress echocardiogram because they are unable to exercise. Which finding in the patients history would be most important to report before the test because it could contraindicate dobutamine use?",
 o:["A history of hypertension controlled on medication","A recent episode of sustained ventricular tachycardia","Mild osteoarthritis of the knee","A prior normal exercise stress test five years ago"],a:1,
 why:"Dobutamine is a potent inotropic and chronotropic agent that increases myocardial oxygen demand and can provoke significant arrhythmias, so recent sustained ventricular tachycardia is a major concern that should be reported and may contraindicate its use. Controlled hypertension and osteoarthritis do not typically preclude dobutamine stress testing, and a prior normal test from years ago does not affect the safety of the current procedure."},
{id:"P5039",d:"D7",c:"D7.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient with a history of asthma is scheduled for a pharmacologic nuclear stress test. Which stress agent should generally be avoided in this patient?",
 o:["Dobutamine, because it has no effect on the airways","Regadenoson, because it always causes bronchospasm in every patient regardless of history","Adenosine, because it can provoke bronchospasm","Exercise, because it is a pharmacologic agent"],a:2,
 why:"Adenosine and similar agents can trigger bronchospasm in patients with reactive airway disease, so a history of asthma should prompt selection of a different stress method, such as dobutamine, which acts on the heart rather than the airways. Dobutamine does not carry this bronchospasm risk, regadenoson carries a lower but not universal risk and is not accurately described as always causing bronchospasm, and exercise is a physical, not pharmacologic, stress method."},
{id:"P5040",d:"D7",c:"D7.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient is scheduled for a transesophageal echocardiogram (TEE). Which preparation is essential before the procedure?",
 o:["The patient should exercise on a treadmill immediately before the study","The patient must receive intravenous contrast dye","The patient should be fully anticoagulated with heparin regardless of indication","The patient must remain NPO for several hours before the procedure because of planned sedation and the risk of aspiration"],a:3,
 why:"Because a TEE involves passing a probe down the esophagus under sedation, patients must be NPO beforehand to reduce the risk of aspiration, similar to preparation for any sedated endoscopic procedure. A TEE does not involve treadmill exercise or intravenous contrast dye, and anticoagulation is not a universal preparation requirement but depends on the specific clinical indication."},
{id:"P5041",d:"D7",c:"D7.C1",dok:2,q:"Which patient preparation is most important before a coronary computed tomography angiography (CT angiography) study?",
 o:["Beta blockade to slow the heart rate for optimal image quality, along with screening for contrast allergy and renal function","Placement of a temporary pacemaker","Insertion of an arterial sheath in the femoral artery","Administration of a radioactive tracer several hours beforehand"],a:0,
 why:"Coronary CT angiography image quality depends heavily on a slow, regular heart rate, so beta blockade is often given beforehand, and because the study uses iodinated contrast, screening for contrast allergy and renal function is also essential preparation. A temporary pacemaker and arterial sheath are invasive measures not required for this non-invasive imaging study, and a radioactive tracer is used for nuclear perfusion imaging, not CT angiography."},
{id:"P5042",d:"D7",c:"D7.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient with a permanent pacemaker and a paced rhythm on the electrocardiogram needs evaluation for suspected coronary artery disease but has significant osteoarthritis limiting mobility. Which test is most appropriate?",
 o:["Standard exercise treadmill test with electrocardiogram interpretation alone","Pharmacologic stress test with nuclear or echocardiographic imaging","Ambulatory Holter monitor","Resting twelve lead electrocardiogram only"],a:1,
 why:"A paced rhythm obscures the ST segment changes needed to interpret a standard exercise electrocardiogram, and the patients mobility limits their ability to exercise adequately, so a pharmacologic stress test combined with imaging is needed to both stress the heart and directly visualize perfusion or wall motion. A standard exercise electrocardiogram alone would be uninterpretable due to the paced rhythm, a Holter monitor evaluates rhythm over time rather than inducible ischemia, and a resting electrocardiogram cannot reveal ischemia that only appears with stress."},
{id:"P5043",d:"D7",c:"D7.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient with syncope of unclear origin has a normal electrocardiogram, normal echocardiogram, and no arrhythmia captured on a 48 hour Holter monitor. Symptoms occur only a few times per year. Which monitoring strategy is most appropriate to capture the next event?",
 o:["A repeat 48 hour Holter monitor","A single resting electrocardiogram","An implantable loop recorder for long term continuous monitoring","An exercise stress test"],a:2,
 why:"When symptoms are infrequent and a short term Holter monitor has already failed to capture an event, an implantable loop recorder provides continuous monitoring for months to years, greatly increasing the chance of capturing the rhythm during a rare event. Repeating a 48 hour Holter is unlikely to coincide with an event that occurs only a few times a year, a single resting electrocardiogram only captures a moment in time, and a stress test evaluates for exercise induced ischemia or arrhythmia, not spontaneous syncope from an unknown cause."},
{id:"P5044",d:"D7",c:"D7.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient with recurrent unexplained syncope is being evaluated for a possible vasovagal or neurocardiogenic cause. Blood pressure and heart rate are monitored while the patient is moved from supine to an upright position on a motorized table. Which test is being described, and what response would confirm the diagnosis?",
 o:["Exercise stress test; ST depression confirms the diagnosis","Electrophysiology study; induction of ventricular tachycardia confirms the diagnosis","Carotid duplex ultrasound; reduced flow velocity confirms the diagnosis","Tilt table test; a drop in blood pressure with or without bradycardia reproducing the patients symptoms supports the diagnosis"],a:3,
 why:"The tilt table test evaluates the autonomic response to a change in position, and a significant drop in blood pressure, sometimes with bradycardia, that reproduces the patients typical symptoms supports a diagnosis of vasovagal or neurocardiogenic syncope. An exercise stress test evaluates for ischemia rather than positional autonomic response, an electrophysiology study evaluates for inducible arrhythmias through catheter based pacing, and carotid duplex evaluates arterial stenosis, not autonomic blood pressure regulation."},
{id:"P5045",d:"D7",c:"D7.C2",dok:1,q:"What does fractional flow reserve (FFR) measure during cardiac catheterization?",
 o:["The pressure gradient across a coronary stenosis to determine its physiologic significance","The ejection fraction of the left ventricle","The oxygen saturation of blood in the coronary sinus","The total cholesterol level in the coronary arteries"],a:0,
 why:"FFR is measured by comparing the pressure distal to a coronary stenosis with the pressure proximal to it during maximal hyperemia, providing an objective measure of whether a blockage is physiologically significant enough to warrant intervention. It does not measure ejection fraction, oxygen saturation, or cholesterol level, which are assessed by entirely different tests."},
{id:"P5046",d:"D7",c:"D7.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient is undergoing PCI with a drug eluting stent. Compared with a bare metal stent, what is a key nursing education point regarding antiplatelet therapy?",
 o:["Drug eluting stents require no antiplatelet therapy at all","Drug eluting stents generally require a longer duration of dual antiplatelet therapy to reduce the risk of stent thrombosis","Drug eluting stents require only aspirin for one week","Drug eluting stents eliminate the risk of restenosis entirely, so no follow up is needed"],a:1,
 why:"Because drug eluting stents release medication that slows the vessel healing and endothelialization process, a longer course of dual antiplatelet therapy is typically needed compared with bare metal stents to reduce the risk of stent thrombosis. These stents still require antiplatelet therapy, not none or only a brief one week course, and while drug eluting stents reduce restenosis compared with bare metal stents, they do not eliminate the risk entirely and patients still need follow up."},
{id:"P5047",d:"D7",c:"D7.C2",dok:2,q:"During PCI, a patient develops sudden slow or absent flow in the treated coronary artery despite the stent appearing well positioned with no visible dissection or major thrombus. What is this phenomenon called?",
 o:["Coronary artery dissection","Stent thrombosis","No reflow phenomenon, often related to distal microvascular obstruction","Coronary artery spasm at the guide catheter tip only"],a:2,
 why:"No reflow phenomenon describes markedly reduced or absent blood flow in the distal coronary artery after an otherwise successful intervention, thought to result from microvascular obstruction by debris, spasm, or inflammatory mediators rather than a problem at the stent site itself. A visible dissection or thrombus is explicitly absent in this scenario, ruling out those options, and this phenomenon is distal microvascular in nature rather than isolated to the guide catheter tip."},
{id:"P5048",d:"D7",c:"D7.C2",dok:2,q:"During a PCI procedure using unfractionated heparin, which laboratory test is used at the bedside to guide ongoing heparin dosing?",
 o:["Prothrombin time (PT)","Complete blood count","Basic metabolic panel","Activated clotting time (ACT)"],a:3,
 why:"Activated clotting time is measured at the bedside during catheterization procedures to titrate heparin dosing in real time and confirm adequate anticoagulation before and during the intervention. Prothrombin time is used to monitor warfarin therapy, not heparin during a procedure, and a complete blood count or metabolic panel does not provide the real time anticoagulation information needed during PCI."},
{id:"P5049",d:"D7",c:"D7.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient undergoing elective PCI for a stable coronary lesion has a calculated FFR of 0.90 across the lesion. What does this value suggest about the appropriate management?",
 o:["The lesion is not physiologically significant, and stenting may not be warranted based on this value alone","The lesion is critically significant and requires immediate stenting","The FFR value indicates the patient is having an acute myocardial infarction","The FFR value cannot be interpreted without an ejection fraction"],a:0,
 why:"An FFR value above the generally accepted threshold of about 0.80, such as 0.90, suggests that the stenosis is not causing significant flow limitation and that deferring stenting in favor of medical therapy may be appropriate, since studies show these lesions do not benefit from stenting. A value this high argues against, not for, critical significance requiring immediate stenting, FFR is a physiologic flow measurement unrelated to diagnosing an acute infarction, and it can be interpreted independently of ejection fraction."},
{id:"P5050",d:"D7",c:"D7.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient develops chest pain and ST elevation on the monitor within an hour of stent placement during PCI. Angiography shows the stent is patent but a thrombus is forming within it. What complication has occurred, and what is the priority action?",
 o:["Coronary artery spasm; the patient should receive only reassurance since this typically resolves spontaneously","Acute stent thrombosis; the patient needs emergent repeat catheterization and additional antithrombotic therapy","A normal post-procedure finding that requires no action","Contrast reaction; the patient should receive antihistamines and steroids only"],a:1,
 why:"New thrombus forming within a recently placed stent, accompanied by chest pain and ST elevation, indicates acute stent thrombosis, a medical emergency that requires urgent repeat catheterization and intensified antithrombotic therapy to restore flow and prevent myocardial damage. This finding is not a benign or expected post-procedure occurrence, coronary spasm would not typically show visible thrombus formation on angiography, and the presentation described is not consistent with an allergic contrast reaction, which would not explain thrombus within the stent."},
{id:"P5051",d:"D7",c:"D7.C2",dok:4,q:"A patient with diabetes, chronic kidney disease with a baseline creatinine of 2.1, and multivessel coronary artery disease is being evaluated for revascularization. The care team is weighing PCI against coronary artery bypass graft surgery. Which combination of factors most strongly favors surgical revascularization over PCI in this situation?",
 o:["The elevated creatinine alone, since contrast exposure during PCI is the only relevant risk to weigh","The presence of any coronary artery disease, since surgery is always preferred over PCI regardless of the number of vessels involved","Multivessel disease in a diabetic patient, since surgical revascularization has shown a mortality benefit over PCI in this population, despite the added risk that cardiopulmonary bypass carries for a patient with reduced renal function","Diabetes alone, since diabetic patients cannot tolerate PCI under any circumstances"],a:2,
 why:"Multiple large trials have shown that in diabetic patients with multivessel coronary artery disease, coronary artery bypass surgery offers a mortality advantage over PCI, and this benefit must be weighed against the real added risk that cardiopulmonary bypass poses to a patient with reduced renal function, making this a genuine risk versus benefit decision. Creatinine elevation is one relevant factor but not the only one, since vessel anatomy and diabetes status are also critical, surgery is not universally preferred over PCI for all coronary disease since single vessel disease is often treated with PCI, and diabetic patients routinely undergo PCI, so diabetes alone does not preclude it."},
{id:"P5052",d:"D7",c:"D7.C3",dok:1,q:"Following removal of a femoral arterial sheath after cardiac catheterization, which assessment finding requires immediate nursing action?",
 o:["A small area of ecchymosis smaller than a coin at the site","A palpable pedal pulse","A warm, well perfused extremity distal to the site","A new pulsatile, expanding mass at the puncture site"],a:3,
 why:"A pulsatile, expanding mass at the puncture site suggests an active bleed or forming pseudoaneurysm and requires immediate evaluation and intervention to prevent significant blood loss or further vascular injury. Small ecchymosis, a palpable pulse, and a warm well perfused extremity are all reassuring findings that reflect adequate hemostasis and distal perfusion rather than complications."},
{id:"P5053",d:"D7",c:"D7.C3",dok:2,q:"During assessment of a femoral access site two days after cardiac catheterization, the nurse notes a pulsatile mass with a palpable thrill and a continuous bruit on auscultation. What complication is most consistent with these findings?",
 o:["Arteriovenous fistula, from an abnormal connection between the artery and vein","Simple hematoma","Retroperitoneal hemorrhage","Radial artery occlusion"],a:0,
 why:"A continuous bruit with a palpable thrill and a pulsatile mass at the access site reflects turbulent, continuous flow between an artery and an adjacent vein, which is the hallmark of an arteriovenous fistula. A simple hematoma would not produce a bruit or thrill, retroperitoneal hemorrhage occurs internally and would not present as a palpable mass with a thrill at the groin, and radial artery occlusion is unrelated to a femoral access site."},
{id:"P5054",d:"D7",c:"D7.C3",dok:2,q:"A pulsatile mass is palpated at a femoral access site with a systolic bruit only, and ultrasound confirms a contained collection of blood communicating with the artery through a defect in the arterial wall. What complication does this describe?",
 o:["Arteriovenous fistula","Femoral pseudoaneurysm","Deep vein thrombosis","Simple ecchymosis"],a:1,
 why:"A pseudoaneurysm is a contained collection of blood outside the arterial wall that communicates with the artery through a defect, producing a pulsatile mass with a systolic bruit, and it is typically confirmed with ultrasound imaging. An arteriovenous fistula produces a continuous rather than systolic only bruit because it connects to a lower pressure vein, deep vein thrombosis would present with swelling and pain rather than a pulsatile mass, and simple ecchymosis does not involve a contained vascular collection."},
{id:"P5055",d:"D7",c:"D7.C3",dok:2,q:"During sheath removal after femoral cardiac catheterization, the patient becomes pale, diaphoretic, and reports nausea, with a heart rate that drops to 42 beats per minute and blood pressure that falls to 78 over 44. What is the most likely cause of this presentation?",
 o:["Acute myocardial infarction","Anaphylaxis to the contrast agent","A vasovagal reaction triggered by pressure at the sheath removal site","Retroperitoneal hemorrhage"],a:2,
 why:"Vasovagal reactions are common during sheath removal because manual pressure and pain at the site can trigger a vagal response, producing the classic combination of bradycardia, hypotension, pallor, diaphoresis, and nausea, and this typically responds quickly to fluids, leg elevation, and sometimes atropine. Myocardial infarction would more typically present with chest pain and would not characteristically cause this pattern of bradycardia, anaphylaxis would present with hives, wheezing, or angioedema rather than bradycardia, and while retroperitoneal hemorrhage can cause hypotension, it more typically causes tachycardia as a compensatory response rather than the bradycardia described here."},
{id:"P5056",d:"D7",c:"D7.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient six hours after femoral cardiac catheterization has a heart rate of 118 and blood pressure of 88 over 50, with a soft, nontender abdomen and a groin site that shows only mild bruising. The patient reports vague left flank discomfort. What complication should be suspected, and what is the priority nursing action?",
 o:["Simple groin hematoma; apply additional manual pressure to the groin site","Vasovagal reaction; elevate the legs and administer atropine","Pseudoaneurysm; apply an ultrasound guided compression device to the groin","Retroperitoneal hemorrhage; notify the provider immediately and prepare for fluid resuscitation and possible imaging"],a:3,
 why:"Flank or back discomfort combined with tachycardia and hypotension after femoral catheterization, even with an unremarkable groin appearance, is a hallmark presentation of retroperitoneal hemorrhage because bleeding tracks upward into the retroperitoneal space rather than externally, and this requires urgent provider notification and often imaging and fluid resuscitation. Applying pressure to the groin would not address bleeding occurring above the puncture site, the heart rate change here is tachycardic rather than the bradycardia typical of a vasovagal reaction, and a pseudoaneurysm would present as a palpable pulsatile groin mass rather than flank discomfort with a normal appearing groin."},
{id:"P5057",d:"D7",c:"D7.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient after cardiac catheterization via the radial artery has a normal Allen test result documented before the procedure, but three hours later the hand is cool, pale, and the patient reports numbness, though the compression band has already been removed. What should the nurse do first?",
 o:["Assess the radial and ulnar pulses and notify the provider promptly, since this suggests radial artery occlusion or spasm despite band removal","Reapply the compression band at the original pressure for another three hours","Reassure the patient that this is an expected finding after radial access","Elevate the arm and apply ice to the hand"],a:0,
 why:"A cool, pale, numb hand after radial access, even after the compression band has been removed, suggests possible radial artery occlusion or spasm compromising perfusion, and the priority is to assess pulses and notify the provider promptly since delayed recognition risks permanent hand ischemia. Reapplying a compression band would further restrict blood flow rather than help, this presentation is not an expected benign finding that warrants only reassurance, and elevating the arm with ice would not address the underlying vascular compromise and could worsen perfusion."},
{id:"P5058",d:"D7",c:"D7.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient two days after femoral cardiac catheterization reports increasing thigh swelling and the extremity is noted to be tense, painful with passive stretch, and the dorsalis pedis pulse is difficult to palpate by Doppler. What complication should be suspected?",
 o:["Superficial thrombophlebitis","Compartment syndrome from an expanding hematoma compressing the neurovascular structures of the thigh","A normal healing hematoma","Cellulitis at the puncture site"],a:1,
 why:"Tense swelling, pain with passive stretch, and diminished distal pulses in the extremity after catheterization are hallmark signs of compartment syndrome, typically caused by an expanding hematoma compressing the neurovascular bundle within a closed fascial compartment, and this is a limb threatening emergency requiring urgent evaluation. Superficial thrombophlebitis would present with localized redness and tenderness along a vein rather than tense compartment pressure and pulse changes, a normal healing hematoma would not cause pain with passive stretch or pulse loss, and cellulitis would present with warmth, redness, and possible fever rather than a tense, pulseless limb."},
{id:"P5059",d:"D7",c:"D7.C4",dok:1,q:"Which principle guides radiation safety for staff working in the cardiac catheterization laboratory?",
 o:["Exposing staff to the maximum tolerable dose to ensure adequate imaging","Avoiding all lead shielding to save time during procedures","ALARA, keeping radiation exposure as low as reasonably achievable through time, distance, and shielding","Standing as close as possible to the radiation source at all times"],a:2,
 why:"The ALARA principle guides radiation safety by minimizing staff exposure through limiting time near the radiation source, maximizing distance, and using shielding such as lead aprons. Deliberately maximizing exposure, avoiding shielding, or standing unnecessarily close to the source all directly contradict basic radiation safety practice."},
{id:"P5060",d:"D7",c:"D7.C4",dok:2,q:"A staff member in the catheterization laboratory wears a dosimeter badge during procedures. What is the primary purpose of this device?",
 o:["To measure the patients heart rate during the procedure","To detect contrast allergy reactions in staff","To measure the radiation dose delivered to the patients skin","To monitor and track the cumulative radiation exposure the staff member receives over time"],a:3,
 why:"A dosimeter badge is worn by staff to measure and track their personal cumulative radiation exposure over time, helping ensure exposure stays within safe occupational limits. It does not monitor a patients heart rate, detect allergic reactions, or measure the radiation dose to the patient, which are unrelated functions."},
{id:"P5061",d:"D7",c:"D7.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient with a known prior mild reaction to iodinated contrast is scheduled for a coronary angiogram. Which action is most appropriate before the procedure?",
 o:["Administer a premedication regimen with corticosteroids and antihistamines before contrast administration","Cancel the procedure permanently, since any prior reaction is an absolute lifelong contraindication","Proceed without any precautions since a mild reaction does not require any preparation","Substitute the iodinated contrast with a higher volume of the same agent"],a:0,
 why:"For patients with a history of a prior contrast reaction, a premedication protocol with corticosteroids and antihistamines given in advance reduces the risk and severity of a repeat reaction, allowing the procedure to proceed more safely. A prior mild reaction is not an absolute lifelong contraindication to ever receiving contrast again, proceeding with no precautions ignores a known risk factor, and increasing the volume of the same agent would increase, not decrease, the risk of a reaction."},
{id:"P5062",d:"D7",c:"D7.C4",dok:2,q:"Which sign, if it appeared within minutes of iodinated contrast administration during a catheterization procedure, would most urgently suggest an anaphylactoid contrast reaction requiring immediate treatment?",
 o:["Mild warmth or flushing lasting a few seconds","Sudden hives, facial swelling, and wheezing","A metallic taste in the mouth","A brief sensation of nausea that resolves spontaneously"],a:1,
 why:"Sudden hives, facial or airway swelling, and wheezing indicate a true anaphylactoid reaction with potential airway compromise and require immediate treatment with agents such as epinephrine, antihistamines, and corticosteroids along with airway support. Mild transient warmth, a metallic taste, or brief self resolving nausea are common, expected, benign sensations from contrast injection that do not indicate a dangerous allergic reaction."},
{id:"P5063",d:"D7",c:"D7.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient with type 2 diabetes taking metformin underwent coronary angiography with iodinated contrast two days ago. Baseline renal function was normal. What should guide the decision about when to resume the metformin?",
 o:["Metformin should never be restarted after any contrast exposure","Metformin can be resumed immediately regardless of renal function, since the baseline was normal","Confirming that renal function remains stable and has not declined before resuming metformin, per standard post-contrast protocols","Metformin should be replaced permanently with insulin after any contrast procedure"],a:2,
 why:"Because contrast can rarely cause a delayed decline in renal function, standard practice is to hold metformin around the time of contrast exposure and confirm that renal function remains stable before resuming it, reducing the risk of metformin accumulation and lactic acidosis if renal function were to decline. Metformin is not permanently discontinued after contrast exposure in a patient with normal renal function, resuming without confirming stable renal function skips a necessary safety check even though the baseline was normal, and there is no requirement to permanently switch to insulin after a single contrast exposure."},
{id:"P5064",d:"D7",c:"D7.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient develops a rising creatinine over the 48 hours following coronary angiography, consistent with contrast associated acute kidney injury. Which factor present before the procedure most likely contributed to this outcome?",
 o:["A normal baseline creatinine and no comorbidities","Use of a small volume of contrast during a straightforward diagnostic procedure","Adequate intravenous hydration given before the procedure","Pre-existing chronic kidney disease with an elevated baseline creatinine"],a:3,
 why:"Pre-existing renal impairment is one of the strongest risk factors for contrast associated acute kidney injury because damaged kidneys have less reserve capacity to handle the added stress of contrast exposure. A normal baseline creatinine without comorbidities, use of a smaller contrast volume, and adequate pre-procedure hydration are all factors that reduce, rather than increase, the risk of this complication."},
{id:"P5065",d:"D7",c:"D7.C4",dok:4,q:"A patient with a baseline creatinine of 2.8 and diabetes urgently needs coronary angiography for an evolving myocardial infarction. There is no time for extensive pre-procedure hydration. Which approach best balances the competing risks of delaying life saving intervention against the risk of contrast associated kidney injury?",
 o:["Proceed with angiography using the lowest effective contrast volume while providing whatever hydration and monitoring are feasible, since untreated myocardial infarction poses an immediate life threat that outweighs the risk of a potential future renal complication","Delay the angiogram until a full 12 hour hydration protocol is completed, since renal protection always takes priority over timing in myocardial infarction","Cancel the procedure entirely because the elevated creatinine is an absolute contraindication to any contrast exposure","Proceed with a standard, unrestricted contrast volume since time pressure removes any need to consider contrast dose"],a:0,
 why:"In an evolving myocardial infarction, timely reperfusion is a life saving priority that outweighs the longer term risk of contrast associated kidney injury, so the appropriate strategy is to proceed promptly while minimizing contrast volume and providing whatever supportive hydration and monitoring is feasible in the available time. Waiting for a full hydration protocol would dangerously delay treatment of an acute infarction, an elevated creatinine is a risk factor to be managed, not an absolute contraindication that should cancel a life saving procedure, and abandoning any effort to minimize contrast volume ignores a modifiable risk factor that remains relevant even under time pressure."},
{id:"P5066",d:"D7",c:"D7.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient is being evaluated for a Watchman device implantation. What is the primary purpose of this procedure?",
 o:["To replace a stenotic aortic valve","To occlude the left atrial appendage and reduce stroke risk in patients with atrial fibrillation who cannot tolerate long term anticoagulation","To repair a leaking mitral valve","To ablate an accessory conduction pathway"],a:1,
 why:"The Watchman device is implanted to close off the left atrial appendage, the site where most atrial fibrillation related clots form, offering an alternative stroke prevention strategy for patients who have a high bleeding risk or cannot tolerate long term anticoagulation. Replacing the aortic valve describes TAVR, repairing the mitral valve describes MitraClip, and ablating an accessory pathway describes catheter ablation for an arrhythmia such as Wolff Parkinson White syndrome, all of which are different procedures."},
{id:"P5067",d:"D7",c:"D7.C5",dok:2,q:"A MitraClip procedure is being performed for a patient with severe mitral regurgitation who is a poor surgical candidate. What does this device accomplish?",
 o:["It replaces the entire mitral valve with a mechanical prosthesis","It ablates the atrioventricular node","It clips together a portion of the mitral valve leaflets to reduce regurgitant flow without open heart surgery","It occludes the left atrial appendage"],a:2,
 why:"The MitraClip device is delivered via catheter to grasp and clip together the anterior and posterior mitral valve leaflets, reducing the regurgitant orifice and improving valve competence without the need for open heart surgery, making it an option for high risk surgical candidates. It does not replace the entire valve, which would be a surgical or transcatheter mitral valve replacement, it does not ablate the AV node, which is a rhythm control procedure, and it does not occlude the left atrial appendage, which describes a Watchman procedure."},
{id:"P5068",d:"D7",c:"D7.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient is undergoing catheter ablation for atrial fibrillation using pulmonary vein isolation. What is the therapeutic goal of this procedure?",
 o:["To permanently pace the ventricle at a fixed rate","To close a hole between the atria","To remove a thrombus from the left atrial appendage","To electrically isolate the pulmonary veins, a common source of the abnormal electrical triggers that initiate atrial fibrillation"],a:3,
 why:"Pulmonary vein isolation creates a ring of scar tissue around the pulmonary veins to electrically disconnect them from the rest of the atrium, since ectopic triggers originating in the pulmonary veins are a common cause of atrial fibrillation. This procedure does not involve permanent ventricular pacing, which would require a pacemaker, closing an atrial septal defect, which is a different structural procedure, or thrombus removal, which is not the goal of an ablation procedure targeting electrical triggers."},
{id:"P5069",d:"D7",c:"D7.C5",dok:3,q:"Several hours after catheter ablation for atrial fibrillation, a patient develops sudden hypotension, tachycardia, and muffled heart sounds. What complication should be suspected first, given the mechanism of this procedure?",
 o:["Cardiac tamponade from a perforation caused during catheter manipulation within the heart","A vasovagal reaction from sheath removal","An allergic reaction to the ablation catheter material","Expected post-procedure fatigue requiring no further evaluation"],a:0,
 why:"Because ablation catheters are manipulated within the thin walled atria to create lesions, perforation with resulting cardiac tamponade is a recognized and potentially life threatening complication, and the combination of hypotension, tachycardia, and muffled heart sounds strongly suggests this diagnosis. A vasovagal reaction typically causes bradycardia rather than tachycardia, an allergic reaction to catheter material is not a typical or expected complication of this procedure, and this constellation of findings is a medical emergency, not a benign expected finding after ablation."},
{id:"P5070",d:"D7",c:"D7.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with an ejection fraction of 28 percent, a QRS duration of 160 milliseconds with a left bundle branch block pattern, and persistent heart failure symptoms despite optimal medical therapy is being evaluated for a device. Which device is most appropriate, and why?",
 o:["A single chamber pacemaker, because the patient only needs backup pacing for bradycardia","Cardiac resynchronization therapy (CRT) with biventricular pacing, because the wide QRS with left bundle branch block indicates dyssynchronous ventricular contraction that biventricular pacing can help coordinate","An implantable loop recorder, because the priority is to monitor for an undiagnosed arrhythmia","A Watchman device, because the primary concern is stroke prevention"],a:1,
 why:"A reduced ejection fraction combined with a markedly widened QRS and a left bundle branch block pattern indicates that the ventricles are contracting in an uncoordinated, dyssynchronous fashion, and cardiac resynchronization therapy with biventricular pacing is specifically designed to coordinate this contraction and improve symptoms and outcomes in this population. A single chamber pacemaker would not address the dyssynchrony described, an implantable loop recorder is a diagnostic monitoring tool rather than a therapeutic device for this indication, and a Watchman device addresses stroke risk in atrial fibrillation, which is not the issue described in this scenario."},
{id:"P6056",d:"D8",c:"D8.C1",dok:1,q:"Where on the chest wall is the aortic valve best auscultated?",
 o:["Second intercostal space, right sternal border","Second intercostal space, left sternal border","Fourth intercostal space, left sternal border","Fifth intercostal space, midclavicular line"],a:0,
 why:"The aortic auscultation area is the second right intercostal space at the sternal border, where aortic valve sounds transmit best. The second left intercostal space corresponds to the pulmonic area, the fourth left intercostal space corresponds to the tricuspid area, and the fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line corresponds to the mitral, or apical, area."},
{id:"P6057",d:"D8",c:"D8.C1",dok:2,q:"During auscultation, a nurse notes that the split between the aortic and pulmonic components of S2 widens during inspiration and narrows during expiration. How should this finding be interpreted?",
 o:["This is a normal physiologic splitting pattern","This finding indicates a fixed split consistent with an atrial septal defect","This finding indicates paradoxical splitting consistent with severe aortic stenosis","This finding is always abnormal and requires immediate further workup"],a:0,
 why:"Physiologic S2 splitting widens with inspiration because increased venous return delays pulmonic valve closure, and narrows with expiration, which is a normal finding. A fixed split that does not change with respiration suggests an atrial septal defect, paradoxical splitting widens with expiration rather than inspiration and suggests conditions like severe aortic stenosis, and this respiratory variable pattern is normal rather than something that requires immediate workup."},
{id:"P6058",d:"D8",c:"D8.C1",dok:2,q:"When a nurse is uncertain whether a heart sound is S1 or S2, which technique helps distinguish them?",
 o:["Palpating the carotid pulse simultaneously, since S1 coincides with the carotid upstroke","Listening only at the pulmonic area, since S1 is never heard there","Measuring the patient blood pressure at the same time","Counting respirations during the same cardiac cycle"],a:0,
 why:"S1 occurs with mitral and tricuspid valve closure at the onset of systole, coinciding with the carotid upstroke, so simultaneous carotid palpation helps identify it. Listening only at the pulmonic area does not reliably distinguish timing, and blood pressure measurement or respiratory counting does not directly indicate which heart sound is being heard."},
{id:"P6059",d:"D8",c:"D8.C1",dok:3,q:"A nurse auscultates a patient and notes that the S2 split narrows during inspiration and widens during expiration, the opposite of the normal pattern. Which condition is most associated with this finding?",
 o:["Severe aortic stenosis with delayed aortic valve closure, or left bundle branch block","A normal, healthy young adult with no cardiac disease","An atrial septal defect with a fixed split","Mild physiologic variation requiring no further evaluation"],a:0,
 why:"Paradoxical splitting occurs when aortic valve closure is delayed enough to occur after pulmonic closure, commonly from severe aortic stenosis or left bundle branch block delaying left ventricular ejection, so the split reverses its normal respiratory behavior. This is not a normal healthy pattern, it differs from a fixed split that does not vary with respiration at all as seen with an atrial septal defect, and it should prompt further evaluation rather than being dismissed as mild variation."},
{id:"P6060",d:"D8",c:"D8.C2",dok:1,q:"An S4 heart sound is best described as occurring at which point in the cardiac cycle?",
 o:["Just before S1, during late diastole with atrial contraction","Just after S2, in early diastole","Simultaneously with S1","Simultaneously with S2"],a:0,
 why:"S4 results from atrial contraction pushing blood into a stiff, noncompliant ventricle late in diastole, occurring just before S1. A sound just after S2 in early diastole describes S3 instead, and S4 does not occur simultaneously with either S1 or S2."},
{id:"P6061",d:"D8",c:"D8.C2",dok:2,q:"A nurse hears a high pitched sound shortly after S2, followed by a low pitched diastolic murmur. What does this extra sound most likely represent?",
 o:["An opening snap from a stenotic mitral valve","A physiologic third heart sound","An ejection click from a bicuspid aortic valve","A pericardial friction rub"],a:0,
 why:"A high pitched sound shortly after S2 followed by a diastolic murmur is characteristic of mitral stenosis, caused by abrupt tensing of the stenotic but still mobile mitral leaflets, known as an opening snap. A physiologic S3 occurs later in diastole with a lower pitch and is not typically followed immediately by this type of murmur, an ejection click occurs in early systole rather than diastole, and a pericardial rub has a distinct scratchy, multi-component quality rather than a discrete snap."},
{id:"P6062",d:"D8",c:"D8.C2",dok:2,q:"An ejection click is best described as occurring at which point in the cardiac cycle, and with which condition is it classically associated?",
 o:["Early systole, shortly after S1, classically associated with a bicuspid aortic valve","Late diastole, just before S1, classically associated with mitral stenosis","Early diastole, just after S2, classically associated with heart failure","Mid systole, classically associated with mitral valve prolapse"],a:0,
 why:"An ejection click occurs in early systole immediately after S1, as a stenotic or bicuspid semilunar valve reaches its maximal excursion, and it is classically associated with a bicuspid aortic valve. The diastolic timings instead describe an opening snap or S4/S3, and a mid-systolic click, not an early systolic ejection click, is the classic finding in mitral valve prolapse."},
{id:"P6063",d:"D8",c:"D8.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient recovering from a recent myocardial infarction has a new scratchy, high pitched sound with up to three components heard best when the patient leans forward and holds their breath. What does this most likely represent?",
 o:["A pericardial friction rub","An S3 gallop","A mitral regurgitation murmur","A venous hum"],a:0,
 why:"A pericardial friction rub classically has a scratchy quality with up to three components corresponding to atrial systole, ventricular systole, and rapid ventricular filling, and it is best heard with the patient leaning forward, fitting a post infarction pericarditis presentation. An S3 gallop is a single low pitched extra sound in diastole, a mitral regurgitation murmur is a holosystolic blowing murmur with radiation to the axilla, and a venous hum is a continuous sound heard in the neck rather than this scratchy precordial finding."},
{id:"P6064",d:"D8",c:"D8.C3",dok:1,q:"On the standard murmur grading scale, which characteristic defines a grade IV murmur or higher?",
 o:["The murmur is associated with a palpable thrill","The murmur is barely audible even in a quiet room","The murmur is heard only in diastole","The murmur radiates to the neck"],a:0,
 why:"Grade IV and higher murmurs are loud enough to be accompanied by a palpable thrill on the chest wall, a key grading distinction. Being barely audible describes a low grade murmur, diastolic timing is a separate characteristic unrelated to intensity grading, and a radiation pattern to the neck does not itself define the grade either."},
{id:"P6065",d:"D8",c:"D8.C3",dok:2,q:"A diastolic murmur is auscultated on a routine cardiac exam. How should this finding generally be interpreted compared with an isolated soft systolic murmur?",
 o:["Diastolic murmurs are usually considered pathologic and warrant further cardiac evaluation","Diastolic murmurs are almost always innocent and require no further workup","Diastolic murmurs are graded on a different scale that does not reflect severity","Diastolic murmurs disappear with any change in patient position and can be disregarded"],a:0,
 why:"Unlike some soft, early systolic murmurs that can be innocent, diastolic murmurs are generally considered pathologic and warrant further evaluation for conditions such as aortic or mitral valve disease. Diastolic murmurs are not typically innocent, they are graded using the same intensity scale as systolic murmurs, and their behavior with position change does not make them safe to disregard."},
{id:"P6066",d:"D8",c:"D8.C3",dok:2,q:"A nurse asks a patient to perform sustained handgrip, or isometric exercise, during auscultation. Which murmur would be expected to become louder with this maneuver?",
 o:["Mitral regurgitation, because increased systemic vascular resistance increases the regurgitant volume across the valve","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, because increased afterload worsens dynamic outflow obstruction","Aortic stenosis, because increased afterload increases flow across the fixed valve","Mitral valve prolapse click, because it always moves earlier with increased afterload"],a:0,
 why:"Sustained handgrip raises systemic vascular resistance and afterload, increasing the pressure gradient driving regurgitant flow across an incompetent mitral valve and thus intensifying the murmur. Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy actually improves, becoming softer, with increased afterload because it opposes the dynamic obstruction, aortic stenosis murmur intensity does not reliably increase with this maneuver since it is a fixed obstruction, and mitral prolapse clicks move later rather than earlier with maneuvers that increase afterload or preload."},
{id:"P6067",d:"D8",c:"D8.C3",dok:3,q:"During the strain phase of the Valsalva maneuver, venous return and preload decrease. Which murmur is expected to become softer with this maneuver?",
 o:["Aortic stenosis, because reduced preload decreases the volume of blood ejected across the stenotic valve","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy, because reduced preload always increases outflow obstruction","Mitral valve prolapse, because reduced preload eliminates the click and murmur entirely","Ventricular septal defect, because reduced preload always makes the murmur louder"],a:0,
 why:"Most murmurs, including aortic stenosis, become softer during the Valsalva strain phase because reduced venous return and preload decrease the volume of flow across the valve. Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy is a well known exception where reduced preload allows greater dynamic outflow obstruction and makes the murmur louder rather than following the general pattern, mitral valve prolapse murmur and click typically move earlier and can lengthen rather than being eliminated with reduced preload, and most other murmurs generally soften rather than get louder with reduced preload."},
{id:"P6068",d:"D8",c:"D8.C4",dok:1,q:"Which description best characterizes the murmur of aortic stenosis?",
 o:["A crescendo decrescendo systolic murmur best heard at the right upper sternal border, radiating to the carotids","A blowing holosystolic murmur best heard at the apex, radiating to the axilla","A low pitched diastolic rumble best heard at the apex","A continuous machine like murmur heard throughout the cardiac cycle"],a:0,
 why:"Aortic stenosis produces a crescendo decrescendo, or diamond shaped, systolic ejection murmur that is loudest at the right second intercostal space and radiates to the carotid arteries. The blowing holosystolic murmur radiating to the axilla describes mitral regurgitation, a diastolic rumble at the apex describes mitral stenosis, and a continuous machine like murmur describes patent ductus arteriosus."},
{id:"P6069",d:"D8",c:"D8.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient with longstanding mitral stenosis is at increased risk for developing which arrhythmia due to progressive left atrial enlargement?",
 o:["Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular tachycardia","Complete heart block","Sinus bradycardia"],a:0,
 why:"Chronic pressure and volume overload from mitral stenosis enlarges the left atrium, creating a substrate for atrial fibrillation. Ventricular tachycardia is more associated with ventricular scar or cardiomyopathy, complete heart block involves the conduction system rather than atrial stretch, and sinus bradycardia is not a typical consequence of mitral stenosis."},
{id:"P6070",d:"D8",c:"D8.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient with severe aortic stenosis develops exertional angina. What is the significance of this symptom in terms of prognosis if the valve is not replaced?",
 o:["Onset of angina in severe aortic stenosis marks a significant decline in average survival without valve replacement, typically to around five years","Angina in aortic stenosis has no prognostic significance and can be safely monitored indefinitely","Angina is the least concerning of the classic symptoms and requires no change in management","Angina in aortic stenosis always indicates concurrent coronary artery disease and never occurs from the valve disease alone"],a:0,
 why:"The classic symptom triad of severe aortic stenosis, which includes angina, syncope, and heart failure, each marks a substantial drop in average survival without intervention, with angina onset associated with roughly a five year average survival if untreated. Angina is prognostically significant rather than something to simply monitor indefinitely, and it can occur from the valve disease itself through increased myocardial oxygen demand and reduced coronary flow reserve, even without independent obstructive coronary disease."},
{id:"P6071",d:"D8",c:"D8.C4",dok:3,q:"In mitral stenosis, the interval between S2 and the opening snap can help estimate severity. What does a shorter S2 to opening snap interval suggest?",
 o:["More severe stenosis, because higher left atrial pressure causes the mitral valve to open earlier and more forcefully","Less severe stenosis, because a shorter interval means the valve is opening more easily","No correlation exists between this interval and disease severity","A shorter interval indicates the valve has become regurgitant rather than stenotic"],a:0,
 why:"As mitral stenosis worsens, left atrial pressure rises, causing the stenotic valve to snap open earlier after S2, so a shorter S2 to opening snap interval is a recognized bedside indicator of more severe disease. It does not indicate the valve is opening more easily or that stenosis is milder, since the known correlation is inverse rather than absent, and a short interval does not itself indicate a change from stenosis to regurgitation."},
{id:"P6072",d:"D8",c:"D8.C5",dok:1,q:"Which description best characterizes the murmur of chronic aortic regurgitation?",
 o:["A high pitched, blowing decrescendo diastolic murmur best heard at the left sternal border with the patient leaning forward","A crescendo decrescendo systolic murmur radiating to the carotids","A mid diastolic rumble best heard at the apex","A continuous murmur heard throughout systole and diastole"],a:0,
 why:"Chronic aortic regurgitation classically produces a high pitched, blowing, decrescendo diastolic murmur best heard at the left sternal border with the patient sitting forward in expiration. The systolic crescendo decrescendo murmur radiating to the carotids describes aortic stenosis, a mid diastolic rumble at the apex describes mitral stenosis, and a continuous murmur describes conditions like patent ductus arteriosus."},
{id:"P6073",d:"D8",c:"D8.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient with chronic severe aortic regurgitation is noted to have a wide pulse pressure and bounding peripheral pulses on exam. What best explains this finding?",
 o:["Regurgitant flow back into the left ventricle during diastole lowers diastolic pressure while stroke volume increases systolic pressure","Reduced stroke volume from the incompetent valve lowers both systolic and diastolic pressure equally","Fluid overload from the regurgitant valve raises both systolic and diastolic pressure proportionally","The regurgitant valve causes a fixed, unchanging pulse pressure regardless of severity"],a:0,
 why:"In chronic aortic regurgitation, diastolic runoff of blood back into the ventricle lowers diastolic pressure while the resulting increased stroke volume raises systolic pressure, widening the pulse pressure and producing the bounding pulse. Stroke volume in compensated chronic regurgitation is typically increased rather than reduced, pressures do not rise proportionally together, and pulse pressure changes with the degree of regurgitation rather than staying fixed."},
{id:"P6074",d:"D8",c:"D8.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient with severe tricuspid regurgitation is examined for jugular venous findings. Which finding is most characteristic of this condition?",
 o:["A prominent, large v wave in the jugular venous pulse","A slow rise and fall of the jugular pulse with no distinct waves","Absent jugular venous pulsations entirely","A jugular venous pattern that disappears with inspiration"],a:0,
 why:"Tricuspid regurgitation causes retrograde systolic flow into the right atrium and venous system, producing a prominent, often giant v wave in the jugular pulse. An absent pulsation or a featureless pulse does not reflect this pathologic backflow, and jugular pulsations do not simply disappear with inspiration in this condition, since that respiratory pattern relates to a different, restrictive process."},
{id:"P6075",d:"D8",c:"D8.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient develops acute severe aortic regurgitation from infective endocarditis. Compared with chronic aortic regurgitation of similar severity, why is acute regurgitation poorly tolerated?",
 o:["The left ventricle has not had time to dilate and remodel, so left ventricular diastolic pressure rises rapidly, causing pulmonary edema","The left ventricle in acute regurgitation is already maximally dilated, offering no further compensation capacity","Acute regurgitation always involves a smaller regurgitant volume than chronic regurgitation","Acute regurgitation primarily affects the right side of the heart rather than the left"],a:0,
 why:"Chronic aortic regurgitation allows gradual eccentric left ventricular dilation that accommodates the regurgitant volume with a relatively normal diastolic pressure for a time, whereas in acute regurgitation the ventricle has not remodeled, so the same regurgitant volume causes a rapid rise in diastolic pressure and pulmonary edema. The ventricle is not already maximally dilated in the acute setting since that describes long standing chronic disease, regurgitant volume is not necessarily smaller in acute cases, and aortic regurgitation is a left sided rather than right sided valve lesion."},
{id:"P6076",d:"D8",c:"D8.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with severe chronic aortic regurgitation has a soft, low pitched diastolic rumble at the apex in addition to the expected diastolic murmur at the left sternal border. No opening snap is present. What does this apical finding most likely represent?",
 o:["An Austin Flint murmur, caused by the regurgitant aortic jet interfering with anterior mitral leaflet opening","Coexisting severe mitral stenosis, confirmed by the absence of an opening snap","A pericardial friction rub radiating from the base to the apex","An innocent flow murmur unrelated to the underlying valve disease"],a:0,
 why:"An Austin Flint murmur is a functional diastolic rumble at the apex caused when the regurgitant aortic jet strikes the anterior mitral leaflet, mimicking mitral stenosis, but it occurs without a true stenotic valve and characteristically lacks an opening snap. True mitral stenosis usually does produce an opening snap, so its absence argues against that diagnosis, a pericardial rub has a distinct scratchy multi-phasic quality rather than a rumble, and this finding is directly related to, not unrelated to, the underlying aortic valve disease."},
{id:"P6077",d:"D8",c:"D8.C6",dok:2,q:"A patient with a prosthetic heart valve is scheduled for a dental extraction. What is the appropriate recommendation regarding endocarditis prophylaxis?",
 o:["Antibiotic prophylaxis before the procedure is recommended, because a prosthetic valve is a high risk condition for infective endocarditis","Prophylaxis is unnecessary, because dental procedures do not increase endocarditis risk","Prophylaxis should be given only after the procedure is completed","All patients with any history of heart murmur require the same prophylaxis regimen"],a:0,
 why:"Current guidelines reserve endocarditis prophylaxis for the highest risk cardiac conditions, which include prosthetic heart valves, and recommend it before procedures that disrupt gingival tissue or oral mucosa, such as a dental extraction. Dental procedures do carry bacteremia risk in these patients, prophylaxis is timed before rather than after the procedure to be effective, and prophylaxis is not recommended for all murmurs, only for specific high risk cardiac conditions."},
{id:"P6078",d:"D8",c:"D8.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient has a mechanical aortic valve without additional risk factors. Which international normalized ratio target range is generally recommended, and why does it differ from a mechanical mitral valve?",
 o:["An INR target of about 2.0 to 3.0, because the aortic position has a lower thrombogenic risk than the mitral position","An INR target of about 2.5 to 3.5, because the aortic position always carries higher thrombotic risk than the mitral position","No anticoagulation is needed for a mechanical valve in the aortic position","An INR target of 4.0 to 5.0, because mechanical aortic valves are the most thrombogenic prosthesis"],a:0,
 why:"Mechanical valves in the aortic position generally require a lower INR target, around 2.0 to 3.0, than those in the mitral position, which target about 2.5 to 3.5, because the higher velocity aortic flow environment is associated with somewhat lower thrombogenic risk than the lower flow mitral position. All mechanical valves require lifelong anticoagulation, so no anticoagulation is not appropriate, and a target of 4.0 to 5.0 would be excessive and increase bleeding risk without added benefit."},
{id:"P6079",d:"D8",c:"D8.C6",dok:3,q:"A 28 year old woman who is planning future pregnancies needs valve replacement and asks about choosing between a mechanical and a bioprosthetic valve. Which counseling point is most important to this decision?",
 o:["A mechanical valve requires lifelong warfarin, which carries teratogenic risk and management challenges during pregnancy, while a bioprosthetic valve avoids this but is more likely to degenerate and require reoperation over time","Mechanical valves never require anticoagulation during pregnancy, so they are always preferred in women of childbearing age","Bioprosthetic valves last significantly longer than mechanical valves and never require reoperation","There is no meaningful difference between the two valve types for a woman planning pregnancy"],a:0,
 why:"This counseling weighs warfarin teratogenic risk and the complexity of anticoagulation management during pregnancy with a mechanical valve against a bioprosthetic valve freedom from long term anticoagulation but greater likelihood of structural degeneration and eventual reoperation, which is especially relevant in a young patient with a long life expectancy. Mechanical valves still require anticoagulation during pregnancy, posing the described risk, bioprosthetic valves generally have shorter durability than mechanical valves rather than longer, and there is in fact a significant, clinically important difference between the two options for this patient."},
{id:"P6080",d:"D8",c:"D8.C6",dok:4,q:"An 82 year old patient with severe symptomatic aortic stenosis has multiple comorbidities and is assessed as high surgical risk by a multidisciplinary heart team. Which factors together most strongly favor transcatheter aortic valve replacement over surgical aortic valve replacement in this patient?",
 o:["Advanced age, a high surgical risk score, and frailty, which together favor a less invasive approach with a shorter recovery","Young age and low surgical risk, which favor a transcatheter approach over surgery","A normal life expectancy with no comorbidities, which favors avoiding valve replacement altogether","A strong patient preference for open heart surgery despite high surgical risk, which should override the heart team assessment"],a:0,
 why:"The combination of advanced age, an elevated surgical risk score, and frailty in this patient supports a heart team recommendation for the less invasive transcatheter approach, given its comparable outcomes with shorter recovery in high risk older patients. Young age with low surgical risk would instead favor traditional surgical replacement given its long term durability, a normal life expectancy with no comorbidities does not describe this patient and would not argue against needed valve replacement, and while patient preference matters, it does not simply override a multidisciplinary risk based assessment when high surgical risk points toward the transcatheter option."},
{id:"P3041",d:"D9",c:"D9.C1",dok:1,q:"For a standard 12 lead ECG, where should the V1 electrode be placed?",
 o:["Fourth intercostal space at the right sternal border","Fourth intercostal space at the left sternal border","Fifth intercostal space at the left midclavicular line","Fifth intercostal space at the left midaxillary line"],a:0,
 why:"V1 is correctly placed at the fourth intercostal space at the right sternal border, which positions it directly over the right side of the heart near the septum. The fourth intercostal space at the left sternal border is the correct location for V2, the fifth intercostal space at the midclavicular line is the location for V4, and the fifth intercostal space at the midaxillary line is the location for V6, so each of those describes a different lead position."},
{id:"P3042",d:"D9",c:"D9.C1",dok:1,q:"For a standard 12 lead ECG, where should the V6 electrode be placed?",
 o:["Fifth intercostal space at the left midaxillary line","Fourth intercostal space at the right sternal border","Fifth intercostal space at the left anterior axillary line","Fourth intercostal space at the left midclavicular line"],a:0,
 why:"V6 is correctly placed at the fifth intercostal space in the left midaxillary line, level with V4, which places it over the lateral wall of the left ventricle. The right sternal border position belongs to V1, the anterior axillary line at the fifth intercostal space is the location for V5 rather than V6, and the fourth intercostal space at the midclavicular line does not correspond to any standard precordial lead position."},
{id:"P3043",d:"D9",c:"D9.C1",dok:2,q:"A 12 lead ECG shows a negative P wave and negative QRS complex in lead I, with an otherwise normal appearing precordial R wave progression. What technical problem should the nurse suspect first?",
 o:["Reversal of the right arm and left arm limb lead electrodes","A true finding of dextrocardia requiring immediate workup","Acute lateral wall ischemia","Left bundle branch block"],a:0,
 why:"A negative P wave and QRS in lead I, occurring with an otherwise normal precordial progression, is a classic sign of right arm and left arm limb lead reversal, since swapping these two electrodes inverts the lead I tracing while sparing the precordial leads. True dextrocardia would typically also show reversed precordial R wave progression rather than a normal one, acute ischemia would not selectively invert an entire lead in this global pattern, and left bundle branch block produces a specific QRS morphology change rather than global inversion limited to lead I."},
{id:"P3044",d:"D9",c:"D9.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient has anterior precordial ST depression with tall R waves in V1 through V3, and the provider wants to confirm a suspected posterior wall injury. Which additional leads should be obtained?",
 o:["Posterior leads V7 through V9","Right sided leads V3R through V6R","Only a repeat standard 12 lead ECG","Additional limb leads aVR and aVL only"],a:0,
 why:"When posterior wall injury is suspected based on anterior mirror image changes such as ST depression and tall R waves in V1 through V3, posterior leads V7 through V9 are obtained to directly visualize the posterior wall and confirm ST elevation there. Right sided leads V3R through V6R are used to evaluate the right ventricle rather than the posterior wall, simply repeating the standard 12 lead will not add posterior wall visualization, and aVR and aVL are standard limb leads that do not provide additional posterior information."},
{id:"P3045",d:"D9",c:"D9.C1",dok:3,q:"A 12 lead ECG on a patient with large breast tissue shows poor R wave progression across the precordial leads that does not match the patient prior baseline tracing or clinical presentation. What is the most likely explanation?",
 o:["Breast tissue displacing the precordial electrodes from their correct anatomic position, altering the recorded R wave progression","A new anteroseptal myocardial infarction must be present","The ECG machine requires recalibration","The patient has developed a new left bundle branch block"],a:0,
 why:"Large breast tissue can displace precordial electrodes from their intended anatomic landmarks, which commonly produces artifactual changes in R wave progression that do not reflect a true cardiac change, especially when the pattern does not match the patient baseline or symptoms. Jumping to a diagnosis of new infarction or new bundle branch block without correlating with symptoms or confirming lead placement risks a false positive interpretation, and machine recalibration is not the typical cause of localized precordial lead placement errors related to positioning over soft tissue."},
{id:"P3046",d:"D9",c:"D9.C2",dok:1,q:"In an adult with a normal heart rate, what is generally considered the upper limit of a normal corrected QT interval?",
 o:["Approximately 440 to 460 milliseconds","Approximately 200 milliseconds","Approximately 600 milliseconds","Approximately 120 milliseconds"],a:0,
 why:"A corrected QT interval up to roughly 440 to 460 milliseconds, with some variation by sex and reference source, is generally considered the upper limit of normal, beyond which the risk of dangerous ventricular arrhythmias such as torsades de pointes increases. A value near 200 milliseconds is far shorter than even a normal uncorrected QT interval, a value near 600 milliseconds represents severe, dangerous prolongation rather than a normal upper limit, and 120 milliseconds is closer to a normal PR interval or QRS duration rather than a QT interval."},
{id:"P3047",d:"D9",c:"D9.C2",dok:2,q:"On a 12 lead ECG, the QRS complex is predominantly positive in lead I and predominantly negative in lead aVF. How should the electrical axis be classified?",
 o:["Left axis deviation","Right axis deviation","Normal axis","Extreme or indeterminate axis"],a:0,
 why:"A positive QRS in lead I combined with a negative QRS in lead aVF places the mean electrical axis in the left superior quadrant, which is classified as left axis deviation. A normal axis would show a positive QRS in both lead I and lead aVF, right axis deviation would show a negative QRS in lead I with a positive QRS in aVF, and an extreme or indeterminate axis would show negative QRS complexes in both leads."},
{id:"P3048",d:"D9",c:"D9.C2",dok:2,q:"On a 12 lead ECG, the QRS complex is predominantly negative in lead I and predominantly positive in lead aVF. How should the electrical axis be classified?",
 o:["Right axis deviation","Left axis deviation","Normal axis","Extreme or indeterminate axis"],a:0,
 why:"A negative QRS in lead I paired with a positive QRS in lead aVF places the mean electrical axis in the right inferior quadrant, which defines right axis deviation. A normal axis requires a positive QRS in both leads, left axis deviation requires a positive lead I with a negative aVF, and an extreme or indeterminate axis would show negative complexes in both leads rather than the pattern described here."},
{id:"P3049",d:"D9",c:"D9.C2",dok:2,q:"A 12 lead ECG shows a deep S wave in V1 and a tall R wave in V5, with the sum of the two exceeding standard voltage thresholds used to screen for chamber enlargement. What does this finding suggest?",
 o:["Left ventricular hypertrophy","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Right atrial enlargement","Left bundle branch block"],a:0,
 why:"A deep S wave in V1 combined with a tall R wave in V5 or V6, when the summed voltage exceeds standard criteria, is a commonly used voltage based screening finding for left ventricular hypertrophy, reflecting increased left ventricular muscle mass. Right ventricular hypertrophy would more typically show a tall R wave in V1 rather than a deep S wave there, right atrial enlargement is identified by tall peaked P waves rather than QRS voltage, and left bundle branch block is defined by a specific QRS morphology and duration rather than simple voltage summation."},
{id:"P3050",d:"D9",c:"D9.C2",dok:3,q:"A nurse compares two 12 lead tracings. One shows a tall, peaked P wave in lead II measuring 3 mm, while the other shows a wide, notched P wave in lead II with two distinct peaks. How should these two findings be classified?",
 o:["The tall peaked P wave suggests right atrial enlargement, and the notched P wave suggests left atrial enlargement","The tall peaked P wave suggests left atrial enlargement, and the notched P wave suggests right atrial enlargement","Both findings represent the same condition, right atrial enlargement","Both findings represent normal variants with no clinical significance"],a:0,
 why:"A tall, peaked P wave in lead II, sometimes called P pulmonale, reflects increased right atrial forces and suggests right atrial enlargement, while a wide, notched P wave with two peaks, sometimes called P mitrale, reflects delayed left atrial conduction and suggests left atrial enlargement. These are distinct findings rather than the same condition, and both carry clinical significance related to underlying chamber enlargement rather than being incidental normal variants."},
{id:"P3051",d:"D9",c:"D9.C2",dok:3,q:"A nurse is evaluating a raw, uncorrected QT interval on a patient whose heart rate is 130 per minute. Why is it important to calculate a corrected QT interval rather than relying on the raw measurement alone in this situation?",
 o:["The QT interval naturally shortens at faster heart rates, so an uncorrected value can underestimate true repolarization risk without adjusting for rate","The QT interval naturally lengthens at faster heart rates, so an uncorrected value will always overestimate risk","Heart rate has no effect on the QT interval, so correction is unnecessary","QT correction is only relevant when the heart rate is below 60 per minute"],a:0,
 why:"The QT interval shortens as heart rate increases and lengthens as heart rate decreases, so at a fast rate like 130 per minute the raw QT interval may look falsely reassuring even if the underlying repolarization abnormality would be dangerous at a normal rate, which is why a rate corrected value such as the Bazett formula is used. It is not true that the raw value would always overestimate risk at fast rates, since the physiologic effect is a shortening rather than a lengthening, heart rate clearly does affect the QT interval, and correction is useful across a wide range of heart rates, not only when the rate is slow."},
{id:"P3052",d:"D9",c:"D9.C2",dok:3,q:"On a 12 lead ECG, the QRS complex is negative in both lead I and lead aVF. How should this axis be classified, and what does it typically prompt the nurse to consider?",
 o:["Extreme or indeterminate axis, which should prompt review for lead placement error or significant underlying cardiac or pulmonary disease","Normal axis, which requires no further evaluation","Left axis deviation, which is a benign and expected finding in most adults","Right axis deviation, which always indicates a normal variant in young patients"],a:0,
 why:"A QRS that is negative in both lead I and lead aVF falls into the extreme or indeterminate axis category, sometimes called the northwest axis, which is uncommon and should prompt the nurse to consider technical error such as lead misplacement as well as significant underlying pathology such as severe cardiomyopathy. A normal axis by definition shows a positive QRS in both of those leads, left axis deviation requires a positive lead I, and right axis deviation, while sometimes a normal variant in young or tall thin patients, still requires a positive aVF rather than a negative one."},
{id:"P3053",d:"D9",c:"D9.C3",dok:1,q:"A 12 lead ECG shows a QRS duration of 0.11 seconds with a morphology that does not clearly meet the specific criteria for either right or left bundle branch block. How should this finding be described?",
 o:["Nonspecific intraventricular conduction delay","Left bundle branch block","Right bundle branch block","Normal QRS morphology with no conduction abnormality"],a:0,
 why:"A QRS that is modestly widened but does not fit the specific morphologic pattern required for right or left bundle branch block is described as a nonspecific intraventricular conduction delay, indicating some degree of abnormal conduction without a clearly defined bundle branch pattern. Labeling it as either right or left bundle branch block would be inaccurate since the description states it does not meet the specific criteria for either, and calling the QRS entirely normal ignores that its duration and morphology are outside expected limits."},
{id:"P3054",d:"D9",c:"D9.C3",dok:2,q:"A 12 lead ECG shows an rsR prime pattern in lead V1 and a wide, slurred S wave in leads I and V6. What conduction abnormality does this represent?",
 o:["Right bundle branch block","Left bundle branch block","Left anterior fascicular block","Wolff Parkinson White pattern"],a:0,
 why:"An rsR prime pattern in V1, often described as a rabbit ear shape, along with a wide slurred S wave in the lateral leads I and V6, is the classic pattern of right bundle branch block, reflecting delayed activation of the right ventricle. Left bundle branch block instead shows a broad monophasic R wave in I and V6 with a deep QS in V1, left anterior fascicular block produces left axis deviation without this rsR prime pattern, and Wolff Parkinson White is identified by a short PR interval and a delta wave rather than this QRS morphology."},
{id:"P3055",d:"D9",c:"D9.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient with a known baseline left bundle branch block develops chest pain. Why can standard ST elevation criteria not be reliably applied to diagnose acute myocardial infarction in this situation?",
 o:["Left bundle branch block itself causes secondary ST and T wave changes that can mimic or obscure the changes of acute ischemia","Left bundle branch block prevents the heart from generating any ST segment at all","Left bundle branch block only affects the atria, so it should not affect ST segment interpretation","Left bundle branch block makes the QRS too narrow to assess accurately"],a:0,
 why:"Left bundle branch block alters the normal sequence of ventricular depolarization, which produces secondary, discordant ST and T wave changes that can either mimic acute ischemia or mask a true acute infarction, making standard ST elevation criteria unreliable, which is why specialized criteria such as Sgarbossa are used instead. The ST segment is still present and can still be evaluated, left bundle branch block is a ventricular conduction abnormality rather than an atrial one, and by definition left bundle branch block widens rather than narrows the QRS complex."},
{id:"P3056",d:"D9",c:"D9.C3",dok:2,q:"A 12 lead ECG shows left axis deviation, a small q wave in lead I, a small r wave in lead III, and a QRS duration within normal limits. What does this pattern most likely represent?",
 o:["Left anterior fascicular block","Right bundle branch block","Left bundle branch block","Complete heart block"],a:0,
 why:"Left axis deviation with a small q wave in lead I, a small r wave in lead III, and a QRS duration that remains normal or only minimally widened is the classic pattern of left anterior fascicular block, a type of hemiblock affecting one of the two fascicles of the left bundle. Right and left bundle branch block both produce a widened QRS beyond normal limits with distinct morphologies described elsewhere, and complete heart block is a disorder of AV conduction timing rather than a QRS morphology finding."},
{id:"P3057",d:"D9",c:"D9.C3",dok:3,q:"A 12 lead ECG shows right bundle branch block along with left axis deviation consistent with left anterior fascicular block. What is the clinical significance of this combined finding?",
 o:["This is a bifascicular block, which carries an increased risk of progressing to complete heart block","This combination is a normal variant with no increased risk","This combination confirms an acute inferior wall myocardial infarction","This combination rules out the possibility of any further conduction disease"],a:0,
 why:"The combination of right bundle branch block with a fascicular block affecting one of the two divisions of the left bundle is called bifascicular block, and because only one fascicle remains to conduct the impulse, there is an increased risk of progression to complete heart block, particularly if the patient develops symptoms of high grade block. This finding is not a benign normal variant, a QRS conduction pattern like this does not by itself diagnose an acute inferior infarction, which requires ST segment changes, and rather than ruling out further conduction disease, bifascicular block actually raises concern for it."},
{id:"P3058",d:"D9",c:"D9.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient with chest pain and no prior ECG on file is found to have a new appearing left bundle branch block along with ongoing ischemic symptoms. What is the most appropriate approach to management?",
 o:["Treat this presentation with high suspicion for acute coronary occlusion and apply Sgarbossa criteria while pursuing urgent cardiology evaluation","Disregard the ECG entirely since left bundle branch block can never indicate ischemia","Wait for a repeat ECG in 24 hours before taking any action","Assume this is a chronic finding and discharge the patient home if pain improves with rest"],a:0,
 why:"A new appearing left bundle branch block in a patient with active ischemic symptoms should be treated with a high suspicion for acute coronary occlusion, and applying Sgarbossa criteria to the tracing, along with urgent cardiology evaluation, helps identify whether concordant ST changes support that suspicion. Left bundle branch block does not exclude ischemia and can actually obscure it, waiting 24 hours for a repeat tracing delays time critical treatment of a possible acute coronary syndrome, and discharging the patient based on symptom improvement alone ignores the significant risk suggested by a new conduction abnormality with ischemic symptoms."},
{id:"P3059",d:"D9",c:"D9.C3",dok:4,q:"A patient with a known short PR interval and delta wave on baseline ECG, consistent with Wolff Parkinson White pattern, develops irregularly irregular wide complex tachycardia at a rate of 210 per minute. Why should AV nodal blocking agents such as adenosine, verapamil, or digoxin be avoided in this situation?",
 o:["Blocking the AV node can preferentially accelerate conduction down the accessory pathway, potentially precipitating ventricular fibrillation","These medications are contraindicated only because they are ineffective, not because they are dangerous","AV nodal blocking agents are the first line treatment of choice in this exact scenario","Wolff Parkinson White pattern eliminates any risk associated with atrial fibrillation, so medication choice does not matter"],a:0,
 why:"In a patient with an accessory pathway who develops atrial fibrillation conducting antegrade down that pathway, AV nodal blocking agents can suppress conduction through the normal AV node while leaving the accessory pathway unopposed, which can paradoxically accelerate ventricular rates and precipitate ventricular fibrillation, so these agents are avoided in favor of procainamide or electrical cardioversion. The concern here is genuine danger rather than simple ineffectiveness, these agents are specifically contraindicated rather than first line in this scenario, and Wolff Parkinson White pattern actually increases rather than eliminates the risk associated with atrial fibrillation conducting over the accessory pathway."},
{id:"P3060",d:"D9",c:"D9.C4",dok:1,q:"On a 12 lead ECG, which finding is most consistent with acute transmural myocardial injury rather than subendocardial ischemia?",
 o:["ST segment elevation","ST segment depression","A prolonged PR interval","A prolonged QT interval"],a:0,
 why:"ST segment elevation reflects acute, full thickness, transmural injury to the myocardial wall and is the hallmark finding of an ST elevation myocardial infarction. ST segment depression instead typically reflects subendocardial ischemia rather than full thickness injury, a prolonged PR interval reflects delayed AV conduction rather than myocardial injury, and a prolonged QT interval reflects a repolarization abnormality rather than acute injury of the ventricular wall."},
{id:"P3061",d:"D9",c:"D9.C4",dok:2,q:"A 12 lead ECG shows new ST segment elevation in leads V1 through V4. Which coronary territory does this most likely represent?",
 o:["Anterior wall, typically supplied by the left anterior descending artery","Inferior wall, typically supplied by the right coronary artery","Lateral wall, typically supplied by the circumflex artery","Posterior wall, typically supplied by the right coronary artery or circumflex artery"],a:0,
 why:"ST elevation in the anterior precordial leads V1 through V4 localizes to the anterior wall of the left ventricle, which is most commonly supplied by the left anterior descending artery. The inferior wall is reflected in leads II, III, and aVF rather than V1 through V4, the lateral wall is reflected in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6, and the posterior wall is identified using posterior leads V7 through V9 rather than the anterior precordial leads."},
{id:"P3062",d:"D9",c:"D9.C4",dok:2,q:"A 12 lead ECG shows new ST segment elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6. Which wall of the heart does this pattern represent?",
 o:["Lateral wall","Anterior wall","Inferior wall","Septal wall"],a:0,
 why:"ST elevation across leads I, aVL, V5, and V6 corresponds to the lateral wall of the left ventricle, which is typically supplied by the circumflex artery or a diagonal branch of the left anterior descending artery. The anterior wall is represented by leads V1 through V4, the inferior wall by leads II, III, and aVF, and the septal wall by leads V1 and V2 specifically rather than the lateral limb and precordial leads listed here."},
{id:"P3063",d:"D9",c:"D9.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient with ST elevation in the inferior leads also shows ST depression in leads I and aVL. What is the best explanation for the changes seen in I and aVL?",
 o:["Reciprocal changes, which are mirror image ST depressions in leads electrically opposite the area of infarction","A separate, unrelated lateral wall infarction occurring at the same time","Evidence that the inferior ST elevation is not a true infarction","Left bundle branch block affecting only leads I and aVL"],a:0,
 why:"ST depression appearing in leads that are electrically opposite the site of infarction, such as I and aVL relative to an inferior injury, is known as a reciprocal change and actually supports rather than contradicts the diagnosis of a true acute infarction. This does not represent a separate simultaneous infarction, since reciprocal changes are an expected mirror image finding rather than an independent injury, and left bundle branch block produces a specific widened QRS morphology across all leads rather than isolated ST depression limited to two leads."},
{id:"P3064",d:"D9",c:"D9.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient had an anterior STEMI three weeks ago. The current 12 lead ECG no longer shows ST elevation but does show new deep, wide Q waves in V1 through V4. What do these Q waves most likely represent?",
 o:["Evidence of prior myocardial necrosis from the completed infarction, rather than ongoing acute injury","An indication that the myocardial infarction is currently in its most acute, active phase","A normal, expected finding with no relationship to the prior infarction","Evidence that the infarction has fully reversed with no permanent damage"],a:0,
 why:"Pathologic Q waves that persist after the acute phase of infarction reflect the presence of electrically silent, necrotic myocardial tissue and indicate that irreversible damage has occurred, rather than indicating ongoing acute injury, which is instead reflected by ST elevation. The absence of ST elevation on this tracing argues against an ongoing acute active phase, deep Q waves are not a normal incidental finding, and their presence indicates permanent tissue loss rather than full reversal of the prior infarction."},
{id:"P3065",d:"D9",c:"D9.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient with chest pain has a 12 lead ECG showing 2 mm of ST segment depression and T wave inversion in V4 through V6, without any ST elevation in any lead. Troponin results are pending. How should this presentation be classified?",
 o:["Consistent with an acute coronary syndrome without ST elevation, warranting urgent evaluation and serial troponin measurement","Definitively ruled out for any cardiac cause since there is no ST elevation","An ST elevation myocardial infarction requiring immediate cardiac catheterization activation","A normal ECG requiring no further cardiac workup"],a:0,
 why:"ST depression and T wave inversion in the setting of chest pain, without ST elevation, are consistent with a non ST elevation acute coronary syndrome, which still requires urgent evaluation, serial troponin measurement, and close monitoring rather than being dismissed. The absence of ST elevation does not rule out a cardiac cause, since non ST elevation events are common and dangerous, this pattern does not meet criteria for the emergent cardiac catheterization activation reserved for ST elevation myocardial infarction, and these findings are clearly abnormal rather than representing a normal ECG."},
{id:"P3066",d:"D9",c:"D9.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient has new ST elevation in V2 through V5 along with ST depression in leads II, III, and aVF. How should the nurse interpret the changes seen in the inferior leads?",
 o:["Reciprocal ST depression from the anterior infarction, rather than a separate inferior process","A separate, simultaneous inferior wall infarction that must be treated independently","Proof that the anterior ST elevation is a false positive finding","Evidence of pericarditis rather than infarction"],a:0,
 why:"ST depression in the inferior leads occurring alongside anterior ST elevation is typically a reciprocal change related to the anterior infarction rather than a distinct, separate infarction, since the inferior leads are roughly electrically opposite the anterior wall. This does not indicate a false positive anterior finding, since reciprocal changes actually strengthen the case for a true infarction, and pericarditis typically produces diffuse concave ST elevation with PR depression rather than localized ST elevation paired with reciprocal depression in opposite leads."},
{id:"P3067",d:"D9",c:"D9.C4",dok:4,q:"A patient has new ST elevation isolated to leads V1 and V2 only, without involvement of V3 through V6, and a corresponding new right bundle branch block pattern is also present. How should this pattern be most precisely localized compared with an infarction that also involves V3 and V4?",
 o:["This more precisely localized pattern in V1 and V2 alone suggests a septal infarction, while extension into V3 and V4 would suggest a more extensive anteroseptal or anterior process","This is identical to an infarction pattern in V3 and V4 and should be localized the same way regardless of which leads are involved","ST elevation isolated to V1 and V2 always indicates a posterior infarction rather than a septal one","The presence of a new right bundle branch block means the ST elevation cannot be interpreted at all"],a:0,
 why:"ST elevation confined to V1 and V2 corresponds most precisely to the interventricular septum, so it is described as a septal infarction, whereas involvement extending into V3 and V4 reflects a larger area of anterior wall involvement and would be described as anteroseptal or anterior, showing that the precise leads involved refine the anatomic localization. These are not identical patterns since the extent of lead involvement changes the anatomic description, posterior infarction is identified using posterior leads or mirror image anterior depression rather than anterior elevation, and a new right bundle branch block does not prevent interpretation of ST elevation, since unlike left bundle branch block it does not typically obscure standard ST elevation criteria."},
{id:"P3068",d:"D9",c:"D9.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient with chest pain has a standard 12 lead ECG showing isolated ST depression in V1 through V3 with tall, broad R waves in those same leads, but no ST elevation anywhere on the standard tracing. Which additional finding on posterior leads V7 through V9 would confirm the suspected diagnosis?",
 o:["ST segment elevation of at least 0.5 mm on the posterior leads","Further ST depression on the posterior leads","A prolonged PR interval on the posterior leads","Absence of any P waves on the posterior leads"],a:0,
 why:"When anterior ST depression and tall R waves raise suspicion for posterior wall injury, obtaining ST elevation of at least 0.5 mm on posterior leads V7 through V9 confirms that the standard anterior findings are actually mirror image changes of a true posterior STEMI. Further ST depression on the posterior leads would not support the diagnosis, since the posterior wall itself needs to show elevation to confirm injury there, and neither PR interval changes nor absence of P waves are relevant findings for confirming posterior wall infarction."},
{id:"P3069",d:"D9",c:"D9.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient who is currently pain free after an episode of chest pain has a 12 lead ECG showing deeply inverted or biphasic T waves in leads V2 and V3, with minimal or no ST elevation and a normal or only mildly elevated troponin. What does this pattern most likely represent?",
 o:["Wellens syndrome, indicating critical stenosis of the left anterior descending artery","A normal variant requiring no further cardiac workup","An acute inferior wall myocardial infarction","Evidence that the left anterior descending artery is not involved at all"],a:0,
 why:"Deeply inverted or biphasic T waves in the anterior precordial leads, occurring in a pain free patient after a recent episode of chest pain, is the classic description of Wellens syndrome, which is strongly associated with critical proximal left anterior descending artery stenosis even when troponin is only mildly elevated. This pattern is not a benign normal variant and requires urgent cardiology evaluation, it does not correspond to the inferior leads used to identify an inferior infarction, and rather than excluding LAD involvement, this pattern specifically implicates the LAD."},
{id:"P3072",d:"D9",c:"D9.C5",dok:2,q:"In which clinical situations are Sgarbossa criteria specifically useful for identifying possible acute myocardial infarction?",
 o:["When a patient has a left bundle branch block or a ventricularly paced rhythm, which both obscure standard ST elevation criteria","Whenever any patient presents with chest pain, regardless of baseline QRS morphology","Only when the QRS complex is completely normal","Only in patients who already have a confirmed diagnosis of pericarditis"],a:0,
 why:"Sgarbossa criteria were developed specifically to help identify acute infarction in patients with left bundle branch block or a ventricularly paced rhythm, both of which produce secondary ST and T wave changes that make standard ST elevation criteria unreliable. They are not needed in every chest pain presentation regardless of QRS morphology, since standard criteria work well when the QRS is normal, and they are not relevant to pericarditis, which is a different condition altogether from the conduction abnormalities Sgarbossa criteria were designed to address."},
{id:"P3073",d:"D9",c:"D9.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient with resolved chest pain and deeply inverted T waves in V2 and V3, consistent with Wellens syndrome, has a normal troponin level. The provider is considering an outpatient exercise stress test. What should the nurse recommend instead?",
 o:["Avoiding provocative stress testing and instead advocating for urgent cardiology evaluation and likely cardiac catheterization","Proceeding with the stress test as planned, since normal troponin excludes significant disease","Discharging the patient home with instructions to follow up only if pain recurs","Repeating the ECG in one week to see if the T wave changes resolve on their own"],a:0,
 why:"Because Wellens syndrome reflects critical LAD stenosis that can progress to a large anterior infarction, provocative stress testing is considered dangerous in this setting and should be avoided in favor of urgent cardiology evaluation and likely catheterization, even when troponin is normal. A normal troponin does not exclude the severe underlying stenosis that Wellens syndrome represents, discharging the patient risks a subsequent major infarction, and waiting a week to repeat the ECG delays urgently needed intervention for a high risk finding."},
{id:"P3070",d:"D9",c:"D9.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with ongoing chest pain has a 12 lead ECG showing upsloping ST depression at the J point in the precordial leads with tall, symmetric T waves, but no T wave inversion. How does this pattern differ from Wellens syndrome, and what does it suggest?",
 o:["Unlike the deeply inverted T waves of Wellens syndrome seen after pain resolves, this pattern with upsloping depression and tall T waves during active pain, known as de Winter pattern, is also a marker of proximal LAD occlusion","This pattern is identical to Wellens syndrome and should be treated the same way with the same urgency label","This pattern indicates a benign, non ischemic cause and does not require urgent evaluation","This pattern only occurs in the inferior leads and reflects right coronary artery disease"],a:0,
 why:"The de Winter pattern, characterized by upsloping ST depression at the J point with tall, symmetric T waves occurring during active chest pain, is distinct from Wellens syndrome, which classically shows deeply inverted or biphasic T waves after pain has resolved, yet both patterns are recognized markers of proximal LAD occlusion requiring urgent intervention. These two patterns have distinct appearances and timing relative to symptoms even though both are STEMI equivalents, this pattern is not benign and does require urgent action, and it is described in the precordial leads reflecting anterior circulation rather than being an inferior lead finding tied to the right coronary artery."},
{id:"P3071",d:"D9",c:"D9.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with chest pain has diffuse ST depression across multiple leads along with ST elevation in lead aVR that is greater than the ST elevation in V1. What does this pattern raise concern for?",
 o:["Left main coronary artery or severe multivessel coronary disease","A benign, non cardiac cause of chest pain","An isolated, uncomplicated inferior wall infarction","Acute pericarditis"],a:0,
 why:"Diffuse ST depression across many leads combined with ST elevation in aVR that exceeds the elevation in V1 is a recognized pattern suggesting critical left main coronary artery disease or severe multivessel disease, both of which are high risk findings requiring urgent intervention. This pattern is not benign and should not be dismissed as non cardiac, it does not describe the localized inferior lead elevation seen with an isolated inferior infarction, and pericarditis typically produces diffuse concave ST elevation with PR depression rather than this pattern of diffuse depression with aVR elevation."},
{id:"P3074",d:"D9",c:"D9.C6",dok:1,q:"Which combination of ECG changes is most characteristic of hypokalemia?",
 o:["Flattened T waves, a prominent U wave, and ST segment depression","Tall, peaked T waves and a shortened QT interval","A widened QRS complex with loss of P waves","Diffuse ST elevation with PR depression"],a:0,
 why:"Hypokalemia classically produces flattened T waves, a prominent U wave that can be mistaken for part of the T wave, and ST segment depression, reflecting the effect of low extracellular potassium on myocardial repolarization. Tall peaked T waves with a short QT are instead associated with hyperkalemia, a widened QRS with loss of P waves reflects severe, advanced hyperkalemia rather than hypokalemia, and diffuse ST elevation with PR depression is the classic pattern of pericarditis rather than a potassium abnormality."},
{id:"P3075",d:"D9",c:"D9.C6",dok:2,q:"A patient taking digoxin has a 12 lead ECG showing a gently downsloping, scooped appearance of the ST segment in multiple leads, sometimes described as resembling a sagging curve. What is the most appropriate interpretation of this finding?",
 o:["This scooped ST segment appearance is a typical effect of digoxin and is not, by itself, evidence of ischemia","This finding always indicates digoxin toxicity requiring immediate discontinuation","This finding indicates an acute ST elevation myocardial infarction","This finding indicates the digoxin level is too low and should be increased"],a:0,
 why:"A gently downsloping, scooped ST segment is a well recognized effect of digoxin on the ECG, often described using a sagging curve comparison, and by itself reflects the drug effect on repolarization rather than ischemia or toxicity. This finding alone does not confirm toxicity, which is instead suggested by symptoms and specific rhythm disturbances such as certain tachyarrhythmias with block, it does not resemble the convex, dome shaped elevation seen with STEMI, and the presence of this expected effect does not indicate the dose is too low."},
{id:"P3077",d:"D9",c:"D9.C6",dok:2,q:"A 12 lead ECG on an otherwise healthy young adult with a family history of sudden cardiac death shows a coved ST segment elevation in leads V1 and V2 followed by a negative T wave, without evidence of structural heart disease. What pattern does this most likely represent?",
 o:["Brugada pattern, which carries a risk of sudden cardiac death from ventricular arrhythmias","Acute anteroseptal myocardial infarction","Normal early repolarization with no clinical significance","Left ventricular hypertrophy with strain pattern"],a:0,
 why:"A coved, dome shaped ST elevation in V1 and V2 followed by a negative T wave, occurring in a structurally normal heart, is the classic Brugada pattern, which is associated with an inherited risk of sudden cardiac death due to ventricular arrhythmias, and warrants further electrophysiology evaluation. This pattern is not caused by acute plaque rupture and infarction, it is not a benign incidental early repolarization variant given the described morphology and family history, and left ventricular hypertrophy with strain produces a different pattern typically involving the lateral leads rather than this coved anterior pattern."},
{id:"P3076",d:"D9",c:"D9.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient on digoxin therapy develops a new rhythm with an atrial rate of 180 per minute, but only every second P wave conducts to the ventricles, producing a ventricular rate of 90. The patient also reports nausea and visual changes with yellow tinged vision. What should the nurse suspect?",
 o:["Digoxin toxicity, given the combination of paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with block and classic systemic symptoms","A normal, expected therapeutic effect of digoxin with no cause for concern","Hypokalemia unrelated to the digoxin therapy","A new, unrelated primary atrial flutter"],a:0,
 why:"The combination of paroxysmal atrial tachycardia with AV block, along with systemic symptoms such as nausea and yellow tinged vision, is a classic presentation of digoxin toxicity and should prompt the nurse to hold the next dose and notify the provider for a digoxin level and further workup. This pattern is not a benign expected effect given the concerning combination of arrhythmia and systemic symptoms, hypokalemia would not independently explain the visual symptoms described, and while atrial flutter can occur on its own, the specific combination of tachycardia with block and these systemic symptoms points more specifically toward toxicity."},
{id:"P3078",d:"D9",c:"D9.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient started on a new antipsychotic medication develops a corrected QT interval of 510 milliseconds on a follow up ECG, with no prior baseline prolongation. What is the primary concern with this finding?",
 o:["Increased risk of torsades de pointes, a life threatening polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","Increased risk of atrial fibrillation only, with no ventricular risk","This finding is expected and requires no action","Increased risk of first degree AV block only"],a:0,
 why:"A significantly prolonged corrected QT interval, especially when newly caused by a QT prolonging medication, raises concern for torsades de pointes, a dangerous polymorphic ventricular tachycardia that can degenerate into ventricular fibrillation, so the medication should be reviewed and the patient closely monitored. QT prolongation is primarily a ventricular repolarization risk rather than an atrial fibrillation risk, this degree of prolongation is not an expected benign finding and does require action such as medication review, and the concern is a life threatening ventricular arrhythmia rather than a mild conduction delay like first degree block."},
{id:"P3079",d:"D9",c:"D9.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient with worsening renal failure has a 12 lead ECG showing tall, peaked T waves, a QRS complex that has widened compared with the prior tracing, and P waves that are no longer clearly visible. What does this progression indicate, and what immediate treatment is most appropriate?",
 o:["Severe hyperkalemia with impending risk of cardiac arrest, requiring immediate intravenous calcium gluconate to stabilize the cardiac membrane","Mild, early hyperkalemia that can be managed with oral potassium binders alone over the next several hours","Hypokalemia, requiring intravenous potassium replacement","A normal finding expected in chronic renal failure that does not require treatment"],a:0,
 why:"Progression from peaked T waves to a widening QRS with loss of visible P waves reflects severe, advancing hyperkalemia that carries a real risk of imminent cardiac arrest, and intravenous calcium gluconate is given immediately to stabilize the cardiac cell membrane while other measures to lower potassium are started. Oral potassium binders act too slowly to address this level of severity, the findings described are the opposite of what would be seen in hypokalemia, and this progression is a dangerous, actionable finding rather than an expected benign consequence of renal failure."},
{id:"P3080",d:"D9",c:"D9.C6",dok:4,q:"A patient who missed a scheduled dialysis session presents with a 12 lead ECG showing peaked T waves, a widened QRS, and absent P waves. A stat potassium level returns at 7.2. Emergent dialysis will not be available for at least 45 minutes. What should the nurse anticipate as the immediate priority intervention while arranging dialysis?",
 o:["Intravenous calcium gluconate immediately to stabilize the myocardium, followed by measures such as insulin with glucose to shift potassium intracellularly while awaiting dialysis","Withhold all treatment until dialysis can begin, since only dialysis can definitively lower the potassium level","Administer oral sodium polystyrene sulfonate as the sole immediate intervention","Administer a potassium sparing diuretic to reduce the potassium level quickly"],a:0,
 why:"With ECG changes showing severe hyperkalemia and a delay before dialysis is available, the immediate priority is intravenous calcium gluconate to stabilize the cardiac membrane and reduce arrhythmia risk, combined with temporizing measures such as insulin and glucose to shift potassium into cells, since waiting nearly an hour without any intervention would leave the patient at high risk for a fatal arrhythmia. Withholding all treatment ignores the immediate life threatening risk shown on the ECG, oral binders act far too slowly to address this level of severity within the needed timeframe, and a potassium sparing diuretic would be counterproductive since it works against potassium excretion rather than promoting it."},
{id:"P7001",d:"D10",c:"D10.C1",dok:1,q:"What is the correct formula for calculating arterial oxygen content (CaO2)?",
 o:["CaO2 = (Hgb x 1.34 x SaO2) + (0.003 x PaO2)","CaO2 = cardiac output x Hgb x SaO2","CaO2 = (Hgb x 1.34) divided by SaO2","CaO2 = 0.003 x PaO2 only"],a:0,
 why:"CaO2 is the sum of hemoglobin bound oxygen (Hgb x 1.34 x SaO2) and a small amount of dissolved oxygen (0.003 x PaO2). Option B confuses this with the delivery equation which includes cardiac output, option C performs an incorrect operation on the terms, and option D omits hemoglobin bound oxygen, which is the large majority of total oxygen content."},
{id:"P7002",d:"D10",c:"D10.C1",dok:2,q:"A critically ill patient has a hemoglobin of 7 g/dL and an SaO2 of 98 percent on supplemental oxygen with a PaO2 of 90 mmHg. Which single intervention would produce the largest increase in oxygen delivery (DO2)?",
 o:["Transfusing to raise hemoglobin from 7 to 10 g/dL","Increasing FiO2 to raise PaO2 from 90 to 300 mmHg","Increasing SaO2 from 98 percent to 100 percent","Increasing cardiac output by 0.3 liters per minute"],a:0,
 why:"Hemoglobin carries the vast majority of oxygen in blood, so raising it from 7 to 10 g/dL produces a much larger rise in CaO2 and DO2 than the other options. Raising an already high PaO2 adds only trivial dissolved oxygen since dissolved oxygen contributes just 0.003 mL per dL per mmHg, SaO2 is already near maximum with little room to improve, and a small cardiac output increase has a proportionally smaller effect than a roughly 40 percent rise in hemoglobin."},
{id:"P7004",d:"D10",c:"D10.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient is noted to have an elevated oxygen consumption (VO2). Which condition is the most likely cause?",
 o:["Fever with shivering","Deep sedation with mechanical ventilation","Hypothermia","Neuromuscular blockade"],a:0,
 why:"Fever and shivering raise metabolic rate and skeletal muscle activity, both of which increase tissue oxygen consumption. Deep sedation, hypothermia, and neuromuscular blockade all reduce metabolic demand and muscle activity, lowering VO2 rather than raising it."},
{id:"P7003",d:"D10",c:"D10.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient has a cardiac output of 5 L/min, hemoglobin of 10 g/dL, and SaO2 of 95 percent. Using DO2 = CO x CaO2 x 10, with CaO2 approximated as Hgb x 1.34 x SaO2, what is the approximate oxygen delivery in mL/min?",
 o:["About 640 mL/min","About 1270 mL/min","About 320 mL/min","About 64 mL/min"],a:0,
 why:"CaO2 = 10 x 1.34 x 0.95 = 12.73 mL/dL, and DO2 = 5 x 12.73 x 10 = about 637 mL/min. Option B doubles the correct value, option C is roughly half the correct value from a division error, and option D omits the x10 conversion factor needed to convert deciliters to liters."},
{id:"P7005",d:"D10",c:"D10.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient has an arterial oxygen content (CaO2) of 18 mL/dL and a mixed venous oxygen content (CvO2) of 14 mL/dL. What is the approximate oxygen extraction ratio?",
 o:["About 22 percent","About 78 percent","About 4 percent","About 44 percent"],a:0,
 why:"Extraction ratio equals (CaO2 minus CvO2) divided by CaO2, or 4 divided by 18, which is about 22 percent. Option B is the complement of the correct answer and represents the oxygen that remains unextracted rather than the extraction ratio itself, option C mistakes the raw content difference for a percentage without dividing by CaO2, and option D is roughly double the correct value from a denominator error."},
{id:"P7006",d:"D10",c:"D10.C2",dok:1,q:"What does the term P50 refer to on the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve?",
 o:["The partial pressure of oxygen at which hemoglobin is 50 percent saturated","The oxygen saturation reading at a PaO2 of exactly 50 mmHg","The percentage of cardiac output involved in oxygen delivery","The pressure at which 50 percent of oxygen exists in dissolved form in plasma"],a:0,
 why:"P50 is defined as the PaO2 at which hemoglobin is half saturated, and it is used to describe the position of the dissociation curve. Option B inverts the definition, option C describes an unrelated cardiac output concept, and option D confuses P50 with dissolved oxygen, which is a separate and minor component of oxygen content."},
{id:"P7007",d:"D10",c:"D10.C2",dok:2,q:"Which condition shifts the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve to the left, increasing the affinity of hemoglobin for oxygen?",
 o:["Hypothermia","Fever","Acidosis","Elevated 2,3-DPG"],a:0,
 why:"Hypothermia reduces metabolic activity and shifts the curve left, meaning hemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly at a given PaO2. Fever, acidosis, and elevated 2,3-DPG all shift the curve to the right, promoting easier oxygen unloading to tissues rather than tighter binding."},
{id:"P7008",d:"D10",c:"D10.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient with severe hyperventilation has a PaCO2 of 22 mmHg and a pH of 7.55. The pulse oximeter reads 99 percent. Why might this patient still show signs of tissue hypoxia despite a high oxygen saturation?",
 o:["Alkalosis shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve to the left, increasing hemoglobin affinity for oxygen and impairing its release to tissues","The pulse oximeter reading is falsely elevated by alkalosis and does not reflect true saturation","Alkalosis immediately raises 2,3-DPG, which worsens oxygen unloading further","Hyperventilation directly reduces cardiac output, which is the cause of the hypoxia"],a:0,
 why:"Alkalemia shifts the curve left so hemoglobin binds oxygen more tightly and releases less of it at the tissue level even though saturation looks normal. Pulse oximetry accurately reflects true SaO2 and is not falsely elevated by pH changes, 2,3-DPG rises only over hours to days as a compensatory response and would shift the curve right rather than worsening a left shift, and hyperventilation is not described here as directly lowering cardiac output."},
{id:"P7009",d:"D10",c:"D10.C3",dok:2,q:"An ABG shows pH 7.34, PaCO2 60 mmHg, and HCO3 32 mEq/L. How is this best classified?",
 o:["Chronic respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation","Acute respiratory acidosis, uncompensated","Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation","Acute metabolic acidosis"],a:0,
 why:"The elevated PaCO2 is the primary problem causing acidemia, and the markedly elevated HCO3 shows the kidneys have compensated over time, consistent with a chronic process. Option B is wrong because an acute uncompensated process would show a near normal HCO3, option C is wrong because the primary abnormality here is a high PaCO2 causing acidemia rather than a primary high HCO3 causing alkalemia, and option D is wrong because the pH is low from elevated CO2 rather than from a low HCO3."},
{id:"P7011",d:"D10",c:"D10.C3",dok:2,q:"An ABG shows pH 7.50, PaCO2 46 mmHg, and HCO3 34 mEq/L in a patient with three days of vomiting. How is this best classified?",
 o:["Metabolic alkalosis with partial respiratory compensation","Respiratory alkalosis, uncompensated","Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation","Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation"],a:0,
 why:"The elevated HCO3 is the primary abnormality driving the alkalemia, consistent with acid loss from vomiting, and the mildly elevated PaCO2 reflects partial respiratory compensation through hypoventilation. Option B is wrong because CO2 is elevated rather than decreased, option C is wrong because the pH is alkalemic and HCO3 is elevated rather than decreased, and option D is wrong because the pH would be acidic in a primary respiratory acidosis, which is not the case here."},
{id:"P7010",d:"D10",c:"D10.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient has the following electrolytes: sodium 138 mEq/L, chloride 96 mEq/L, and bicarbonate 12 mEq/L. What is the calculated anion gap and what does it suggest?",
 o:["30, consistent with a high anion gap metabolic acidosis","12, a normal anion gap","30, consistent with a normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis","8, within normal limits"],a:0,
 why:"Anion gap equals sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate, or 138 minus 108, which equals 30, well above the normal range of about 8 to 12 and indicating a high anion gap metabolic acidosis. Option B reflects an incorrect subtraction, option C uses the correct number but the wrong classification since a normal gap acidosis would not show this degree of elevation, and option D is simply an incorrect calculation."},
{id:"P7012",d:"D10",c:"D10.C3",dok:3,q:"A postoperative patient who has been vomiting and hyperventilating from pain has a pH of 7.55, PaCO2 of 30 mmHg, and HCO3 of 30 mEq/L. What does this ABG most likely represent?",
 o:["A mixed respiratory and metabolic alkalosis","A simple, fully compensated respiratory alkalosis","A simple, fully compensated metabolic alkalosis","A mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis"],a:0,
 why:"Both the low PaCO2 and the high HCO3 are pushing the pH in the alkaline direction at the same time, which is not how single process compensation works, indicating two independent alkalotic processes from vomiting and hyperventilation. Option B is wrong because compensation for a primary respiratory alkalosis would lower HCO3 rather than raise it, option C is wrong because compensation for a primary metabolic alkalosis would raise PaCO2 rather than lower it, and option D is wrong because both derangements here move the pH in the same alkaline direction rather than opposite directions."},
{id:"P7013",d:"D10",c:"D10.C3",dok:4,q:"A patient in diabetic ketoacidosis has sodium 140, chloride 100, bicarbonate 8, and a calculated anion gap of 32. Using delta gap reasoning, the expected bicarbonate based on the rise in anion gap above normal (32 minus 12 equals 20) would predict an HCO3 near 4, but the measured HCO3 is 8, higher than predicted. What does this discrepancy suggest?",
 o:["A concurrent metabolic alkalosis is present, offsetting part of the expected bicarbonate drop from the anion gap acidosis","The patient has a purely uncomplicated high anion gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate compensation","A concurrent normal anion gap (hyperchloremic) metabolic acidosis is also present","Laboratory error is the most likely explanation and the values should simply be repeated"],a:0,
 why:"When the measured bicarbonate is higher than the delta gap predicts, a second process is raising the bicarbonate back toward normal, such as a concurrent metabolic alkalosis from vomiting, diuretics, or nasogastric suction. Option B is wrong because an uncomplicated single process would show measured and predicted HCO3 values that closely match, option C is wrong because adding a second acidosis would push HCO3 even lower rather than higher than predicted, and option D dismisses a well established bedside reasoning tool rather than considering the more likely clinical explanation."},
{id:"P7014",d:"D10",c:"D10.C4",dok:1,q:"What is the main advantage of a Venturi mask compared with a standard nasal cannula for oxygen delivery?",
 o:["It delivers a precise, fixed FiO2 regardless of the patient breathing pattern","It delivers the highest possible FiO2 of any oxygen delivery device","It requires no humidification at any flow rate","It is better tolerated for long term home oxygen use"],a:0,
 why:"A Venturi mask uses color coded entrainment adapters to deliver a set, predictable FiO2 that does not vary with the patient respiratory rate or depth. Non-rebreather and high flow devices can deliver higher FiO2 than a Venturi mask, humidification needs still apply at some settings, and nasal cannula is generally better tolerated for long term home use than a Venturi mask."},
{id:"P7015",d:"D10",c:"D10.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient is placed on a non-rebreather mask. What minimum oxygen flow rate is required to keep the reservoir bag from collapsing during inspiration?",
 o:["10 to 15 liters per minute","1 to 2 liters per minute","4 to 6 liters per minute","25 to 30 liters per minute"],a:0,
 why:"A flow of 10 to 15 liters per minute keeps the reservoir bag inflated so the patient receives a high FiO2 with each breath. Flows of 1 to 2 or 4 to 6 liters per minute are too low and would allow the bag to collapse, reducing delivered oxygen concentration and risking carbon dioxide rebreathing, while 25 to 30 liters per minute exceeds what is needed and is not the standard recommended range."},
{id:"P7016",d:"D10",c:"D10.C4",dok:2,q:"What is a key physiologic benefit of high flow nasal cannula therapy compared with a standard nasal cannula?",
 o:["It washes out nasopharyngeal dead space and provides a small amount of positive airway pressure, improving oxygenation and reducing work of breathing","It delivers a lower FiO2 that is considered safer for patients with COPD","It eliminates the need for humidification because flows are so high","It cannot be used in patients who are in respiratory distress"],a:0,
 why:"High flow devices flush exhaled carbon dioxide from the upper airway and generate a small amount of positive pressure, both of which ease the work of breathing. Option B is wrong because high flow systems can deliver precisely titratable FiO2 up to nearly 100 percent, which is higher than standard cannula, option C is wrong because heated humidification is specifically required at these high flows, and option D is wrong because respiratory distress is a common indication for high flow therapy."},
{id:"P7017",d:"D10",c:"D10.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient on a 4 liter per minute nasal cannula has an SpO2 of 88 percent and increased work of breathing. The nurse increases the flow to 6 liters per minute with no improvement in SpO2 or work of breathing after 15 minutes. What is the most appropriate next step?",
 o:["Escalate to a higher FiO2 delivery device such as a non-rebreather mask or high flow system and notify the provider","Continue the nasal cannula at 6 liters per minute and reassess again in four hours","Decrease the flow rate since higher flows can cause oxygen toxicity within minutes","Switch to a simple face mask set at 2 liters per minute"],a:0,
 why:"Lack of improvement after a reasonable trial of increased flow warrants prompt escalation to a higher delivery device and provider notification rather than continued observation. Option B delays needed escalation for a patient who is not improving, option C is wrong because oxygen toxicity develops over much longer high FiO2 exposure and does not explain acute deterioration at this flow, and option D is wrong because 2 liters per minute is below the minimum effective flow for a simple face mask, risking carbon dioxide rebreathing and offering little advantage over the cannula."},
{id:"P7018",d:"D10",c:"D10.C5",dok:2,q:"In a patient with normal intravascular volume, how does the application of PEEP typically affect right ventricular afterload?",
 o:["It increases right ventricular afterload by raising pulmonary vascular resistance from alveolar overdistension","It decreases right ventricular afterload by uniformly improving lung compliance","It has no effect on the pulmonary circulation","It decreases right ventricular preload only, without affecting afterload"],a:0,
 why:"Overdistension of already open alveoli from PEEP compresses the adjacent pulmonary capillaries, raising pulmonary vascular resistance and right ventricular afterload. Option B is wrong because PEEP can worsen compliance in already open alveoli even while helping recruit collapsed regions, option C ignores well documented cardiopulmonary interactions, and option D is incomplete since PEEP affects afterload as well as preload."},
{id:"P7019",d:"D10",c:"D10.C5",dok:2,q:"How does spontaneous, negative pressure breathing affect venous return to the right heart during inspiration compared with positive pressure mechanical ventilation?",
 o:["Spontaneous inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure and augments venous return, while positive pressure inspiration raises intrathoracic pressure and reduces venous return","Both spontaneous and positive pressure inspiration reduce venous return to an equal degree","Spontaneous inspiration reduces venous return while positive pressure inspiration augments it","Neither has a measurable effect on venous return"],a:0,
 why:"Spontaneous breathing creates negative intrathoracic pressure that pulls blood toward the right heart, while positive pressure ventilation raises intrathoracic pressure and impedes venous return. Option B is wrong because the two mechanisms have opposite rather than equal effects, option C reverses the actual physiology, and option D ignores the hemodynamic effects that underlie phenomena such as PEEP induced hypotension."},
{id:"P7020",d:"D10",c:"D10.C5",dok:3,q:"A mechanically ventilated patient with severe asthma becomes progressively hypotensive and tachycardic. The ventilator flow waveform shows expiratory flow that does not return to zero before the next breath begins. What is the most appropriate immediate action?",
 o:["Briefly disconnect the patient from the ventilator to allow full exhalation, then decrease the respiratory rate or increase expiratory time","Increase the respiratory rate to improve minute ventilation","Give a rapid intravenous fluid bolus without changing any ventilator settings","Increase PEEP to counteract the falling blood pressure"],a:0,
 why:"The waveform shows air trapping and auto-PEEP from breath stacking, and briefly disconnecting the circuit allows trapped gas to escape while adjusting the rate or expiratory time prevents recurrence. Option B would shorten expiratory time further and worsen air trapping and hypotension, option C may provide temporary support but does not correct the underlying mechanical cause and delays the needed intervention, and option D would raise intrathoracic pressure further and worsen the hypotension rather than relieve it."},
{id:"P5001",d:"D11",c:"D11.C1",dok:1,q:"In hemodynamic terminology, preload refers to which of the following?",
 o:["The volume of blood stretching the ventricle at the end of diastole, just before contraction","The resistance the ventricle must overcome to eject blood","The intrinsic ability of the myocardium to contract independent of loading conditions","The heart rate multiplied by stroke volume"],a:0,
 why:"Preload is the ventricular wall stretch from end diastolic volume, reflecting the Frank Starling relationship. The resistance the ventricle overcomes describes afterload, not preload, and the intrinsic contractile property independent of loading describes contractility. Heart rate multiplied by stroke volume is the formula for cardiac output, not preload."},
{id:"P5002",d:"D11",c:"D11.C1",dok:2,q:"A patients arterial blood pressure is 132/63 mmHg. Using the standard formula, what is the mean arterial pressure?",
 o:["97 mmHg","86 mmHg","74 mmHg","63 mmHg"],a:1,
 why:"Mean arterial pressure is calculated as diastolic pressure plus one third of the pulse pressure, which weights diastole more heavily because the heart spends more time in that phase, giving 63 plus one third of 69, or 86 mmHg. Simply averaging systolic and diastolic pressure (97) ignores this weighting and overestimates the true mean, and reporting the diastolic value alone (63) ignores the systolic contribution entirely."},
{id:"P5003",d:"D11",c:"D11.C1",dok:2,q:"A patients afterload rises sharply while preload and contractility remain unchanged. What is the expected effect on stroke volume?",
 o:["Stroke volume increases because higher resistance improves coronary perfusion","Stroke volume stays the same because afterload only affects heart rate","Stroke volume decreases because the ventricle must generate more pressure to open the aortic valve and eject blood","Stroke volume increases because afterload stretches the ventricular wall"],a:2,
 why:"Increased afterload raises the pressure the ventricle must overcome to eject blood, so for a given contractility and preload, less blood is ejected and stroke volume falls. Afterload does not directly affect heart rate, and it is not the mechanism of wall stretch, which is described by preload, so both of those explanations misassign the physiologic mechanism."},
{id:"P5005",d:"D11",c:"D11.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient develops new onset atrial fibrillation with a rapid ventricular rate of 150 beats per minute. Which hemodynamic consequence is most directly explained by the shortened diastolic filling time?",
 o:["Reduced preload and subsequent decrease in stroke volume","Increased afterload from arterial vasoconstriction","Improved contractility from increased calcium influx","Decreased myocardial oxygen demand"],a:0,
 why:"A fast ventricular rate shortens diastole, the phase during which the ventricle fills, so less blood accumulates before the next contraction and preload and stroke volume fall. This mechanism does not involve arterial resistance or an increase in contractility, and tachycardia increases, not decreases, myocardial oxygen demand."},
{id:"P5004",d:"D11",c:"D11.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient receives 4 liters of crystalloid over several hours. Despite the increasing central venous pressure, stroke volume on serial measurements is falling. Which explanation is best supported by the Frank Starling principle?",
 o:["The fluid has increased contractility beyond a sustainable level","The fluid has decreased afterload, causing reflex tachycardia","The measurement error is likely because CVP and stroke volume are unrelated","The ventricle has been overstretched past the optimal point on the curve, so further volume no longer improves and may worsen contractile performance"],a:3,
 why:"The Frank Starling curve rises with increasing preload up to a point, then flattens or descends if the ventricle is overstretched, so excess volume can reduce stroke volume despite rising filling pressure. Fluid administration does not increase contractility, and while afterload changes can occur, they do not explain a falling stroke volume with rising CVP in this pattern, and CVP and stroke volume are physiologically linked through preload, so dismissing the relationship is not supported."},
{id:"P5006",d:"D11",c:"D11.C1",dok:4,q:"A patient with chronic systolic heart failure is started on a potent arterial vasodilator. Cardiac output improves even though heart rate, contractility, and preload are essentially unchanged. Which principle best explains this finding?",
 o:["The vasodilator increased contractility directly","Reducing afterload allows a weakened ventricle to eject a larger fraction of its preload, increasing stroke volume through a mechanism separate from the Frank Starling curve","The vasodilator increased venous return, raising preload","The improvement is explained by a leftward shift of the Frank Starling curve caused by increased calcium sensitivity"],a:1,
 why:"In a failing heart, stroke volume is highly sensitive to afterload, so lowering the resistance the diseased ventricle must pump against allows more effective ejection without any change in the intrinsic contractile state, preload, or heart rate. The claim that contractility increased directly conflicts with the stem, which states contractility is unchanged, the claim of increased venous return conflicts with the stated unchanged preload, and while calcium sensitivity can shift the curve, that mechanism is not what occurs with a pure arterial vasodilator."},
{id:"P5007",d:"D11",c:"D11.C2",dok:1,q:"What is the purpose of zeroing an arterial pressure transducer to atmospheric pressure before use?",
 o:["It measures the patients baseline cardiac output","It calibrates the catheter tip to detect arrhythmias","It eliminates the effect of atmospheric pressure so the monitor displays only the pressure generated by the patients blood","It sets the alarm limits for the monitor"],a:2,
 why:"Zeroing references the transducer to atmospheric pressure so the displayed waveform reflects only intravascular pressure generated by the patient, not the surrounding air pressure. It has no role in measuring cardiac output or detecting arrhythmias, and it is unrelated to setting alarm limits, which is a separate step performed on the monitor."},
{id:"P5008",d:"D11",c:"D11.C2",dok:1,q:"On a normal arterial waveform, what does the dicrotic notch represent?",
 o:["Opening of the mitral valve","Peak left ventricular systolic pressure","Onset of ventricular diastole before valve closure","Closure of the aortic valve"],a:3,
 why:"The dicrotic notch is a small deflection on the downslope of the arterial waveform caused by transient backflow of blood against the closing aortic valve. It is not related to mitral valve opening, and while it occurs near the transition to diastole, the notch itself specifically marks the valve closure event, not peak systolic pressure or the onset of diastole in general."},
{id:"P5009",d:"D11",c:"D11.C2",dok:2,q:"An arterial line waveform shows exaggerated systolic peaks, a narrow and sharp upstroke, and a diminished or absent dicrotic notch, with tubing that is short and free of air bubbles. What does this pattern suggest?",
 o:["An underdamped waveform caused by excessive catheter or tubing resonance","An overdamped waveform caused by a kinked catheter","A normal waveform with no artifact","A waveform inaccurately zeroed to atmospheric pressure"],a:0,
 why:"Underdamping occurs when the monitoring system resonates and overshoots, producing an exaggerated, spiked systolic peak with an artificially widened pulse pressure and a diminished dicrotic notch. Overdamping produces the opposite pattern of a blunted, rounded waveform, and a zeroing error would shift the entire waveform up or down rather than change its shape, so neither of those explains this presentation."},
{id:"P5010",d:"D11",c:"D11.C2",dok:2,q:"Which of the following is a common cause of an overdamped arterial waveform?",
 o:["Use of noncompliant, stiff pressure tubing","An air bubble in the pressure tubing","A catheter tip resting freely in the vessel lumen","A transducer positioned exactly at the phlebostatic axis"],a:1,
 why:"Air bubbles, blood clots, and kinks in the line absorb and blunt the pressure signal, producing an overdamped, rounded waveform with a falsely narrow pulse pressure. Noncompliant tubing actually helps prevent damping by transmitting the signal more faithfully, a freely positioned catheter tip is expected and not a cause of damping, and correct transducer placement at the phlebostatic axis is required for accuracy but does not itself cause damping."},
{id:"P5011",d:"D11",c:"D11.C2",dok:2,q:"A nurse notes that the arterial line waveform has become overdamped compared with the patients baseline tracing. What is the priority first action?",
 o:["Immediately remove the arterial catheter","Increase the pressure bag to 500 mmHg","Check the line for air bubbles, blood, or a kink and perform a square wave test","Silence the monitor alarm and document the finding"],a:2,
 why:"The first step when a waveform becomes damped is to assess the system for a mechanical cause, such as air, clot, or kinking, and confirm the finding with a square wave test before taking further action. Removing the catheter is premature without first troubleshooting, increasing bag pressure above the standard 300 mmHg does not address the underlying cause, and silencing the alarm without investigating ignores a change that affects the accuracy of pressure readings."},
{id:"P5012",d:"D11",c:"D11.C2",dok:3,q:"An arterial line waveform gradually loses its normal contour over several hours, becoming flatter with a narrowing pulse pressure, while the patients cuff blood pressure remains stable and the extremity is warm with a normal capillary refill. What is the most likely explanation?",
 o:["The patient is developing cardiogenic shock","The transducer has been leveled too high","The patient is developing sepsis","Partial thrombus formation at the catheter tip is dampening the pressure signal"],a:3,
 why:"A gradually worsening damped waveform with preserved perfusion and a stable cuff pressure points to a mechanical problem at the catheter tip, most often a small thrombus, rather than a true hemodynamic change. If shock or sepsis were developing, the cuff pressure and perfusion would also be expected to change, and a leveling error produces a constant offset in the numeric value rather than a progressive change in waveform shape."},
{id:"P5013",d:"D11",c:"D11.C2",dok:3,q:"A femoral arterial line waveform shows systolic pressure readings that are 20 mmHg higher than simultaneous cuff pressures, with a spiked, narrow systolic peak. A square wave test shows more than two oscillations before the tracing settles at baseline. What does this combination of findings indicate, and what is the appropriate response?",
 o:["The findings indicate underdamping; the falsely elevated systolic reading should not be treated without correlating with the mean arterial pressure, which remains accurate","The findings indicate overdamping; the pressure bag should be increased to 500 mmHg","The findings indicate a zeroing error; the transducer should be leveled lower","The findings indicate a normal waveform and no correlation is needed"],a:0,
 why:"More than two oscillations on a square wave test along with a spiked, narrow systolic peak are classic for an underdamped system, which artificially exaggerates the systolic pressure while the mean arterial pressure remains relatively accurate and should guide clinical decisions. Overdamping produces the opposite pattern of a blunted waveform and falsely low systolic readings, and increasing the pressure bag or relevelling the transducer do not correct resonance related overshoot."},
{id:"P5014",d:"D11",c:"D11.C3",dok:1,q:"On a right atrial pressure waveform, which component corresponds to atrial contraction?",
 o:["The c wave","The a wave","The v wave","The x descent"],a:1,
 why:"The a wave reflects the pressure rise generated by atrial contraction just before the tricuspid valve closes. The c wave reflects tricuspid valve closure and bulging into the atrium, the v wave reflects atrial filling against a closed tricuspid valve, and the x descent is a fall in pressure following the a wave, not the contraction itself."},
{id:"P5015",d:"D11",c:"D11.C3",dok:2,q:"A patient in complete heart block has intermittent, unusually tall spikes on the right atrial pressure tracing that occur irregularly and do not correspond consistently to the QRS complex. What do these spikes most likely represent?",
 o:["Giant v waves from severe tricuspid regurgitation","A normal finding unrelated to the rhythm","Cannon a waves from atrial contraction against a closed tricuspid valve during AV dissociation","Artifact from an underdamped catheter"],a:2,
 why:"In complete heart block, the atria and ventricles contract independently, so occasionally the atrium contracts while the tricuspid valve is closed by ventricular systole, producing an unusually tall cannon a wave. Giant v waves are a systolic finding from regurgitant flow through an incompetent tricuspid valve and would appear with every beat rather than intermittently, and this pattern is tied to the dissociated rhythm rather than simple catheter artifact."},
{id:"P5016",d:"D11",c:"D11.C3",dok:2,q:"Which right atrial pressure waveform finding is most characteristic of severe tricuspid regurgitation?",
 o:["A prominent a wave from forceful atrial contraction","A deep, prominent y descent only","Absence of any waveform","A large systolic v wave, sometimes fused with the c wave, from regurgitant flow into the atrium"],a:3,
 why:"In tricuspid regurgitation, blood is ejected backward from the right ventricle into the right atrium during systole, producing a large systolic v wave, sometimes described as fusing with the c wave into a single dominant systolic wave. A prominent a wave reflects forceful atrial contraction and is more typical of conditions like tricuspid stenosis, and an isolated y descent finding does not by itself characterize regurgitation."},
{id:"P5017",d:"D11",c:"D11.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient with acute right ventricular myocardial infarction develops jugular venous distension, hypotension, and clear lung sounds. The central venous pressure is elevated at 18 mmHg. What does this pattern of findings most likely reflect?",
 o:["Elevated right heart filling pressures from right ventricular failure with preserved left heart filling","Left ventricular failure with pulmonary edema","Hypovolemic shock","Cardiac tamponade unrelated to the infarct"],a:0,
 why:"Right ventricular infarction impairs the right ventricles ability to eject blood, causing blood to back up into the systemic venous system, raising central venous pressure and causing jugular venous distension, while the lungs remain clear because left sided filling is not elevated. Left ventricular failure would be expected to cause pulmonary congestion and crackles, hypovolemic shock would present with a low, not elevated, central venous pressure, and while tamponade can also elevate CVP, the clinical context here points specifically to right ventricular infarction."},
{id:"P5018",d:"D11",c:"D11.C3",dok:3,q:"A postoperative cardiac surgery patient has a central venous pressure that rises from 8 to 16 mmHg over one hour, accompanied by falling blood pressure, muffled heart sounds, and a narrowing pulse pressure on the arterial line, despite minimal chest tube drainage. What complication is most consistent with this pattern?",
 o:["Hypovolemic shock from ongoing bleeding","Cardiac tamponade from mediastinal blood accumulation","Sepsis","Acute right ventricular infarction unrelated to surgery"],a:1,
 why:"A rapid rise in central venous pressure combined with hypotension, muffled heart sounds, and a narrowing pulse pressure, especially with low chest tube output suggesting blood is accumulating around the heart rather than draining, is the classic presentation of cardiac tamponade after cardiac surgery. Hypovolemic shock from bleeding would typically present with a falling, not rising, central venous pressure, and while sepsis can cause hemodynamic instability, it does not explain the muffled heart sounds and rising filling pressure pattern described."},
{id:"P5019",d:"D11",c:"D11.C4",dok:1,q:"During insertion of a pulmonary artery catheter, which sequence of waveforms is expected as the catheter advances from the right atrium to its final resting position?",
 o:["Right atrium, pulmonary artery, right ventricle, wedge","Right ventricle, right atrium, wedge, pulmonary artery","Right atrium, right ventricle, pulmonary artery, pulmonary artery occlusion (wedge)","Pulmonary artery, right ventricle, right atrium, wedge"],a:2,
 why:"The catheter is flow directed by the balloon through the right heart chambers in anatomic and physiologic sequence, from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, then through the pulmonic valve into the pulmonary artery, and finally into a distal branch where the inflated balloon wedges. The other sequences reverse or scramble this order and do not reflect the actual path of blood flow through the right heart."},
{id:"P5020",d:"D11",c:"D11.C4",dok:2,q:"While a pulmonary artery catheter is being advanced through the right ventricle, the patient develops a run of nonsustained ventricular tachycardia on the monitor. What is the most appropriate immediate nursing action?",
 o:["Immediately defibrillate the patient","Notify the proceduralist and prepare to have the catheter advanced quickly through the ventricle or withdrawn, since irritation of the ventricular wall is the likely cause","Increase the balloon inflation volume","Discontinue all cardiac monitoring until the catheter is fully placed"],a:1,
 why:"Ventricular ectopy during right ventricular passage is a known, usually self limited complication caused by the catheter tip irritating the endocardium, and it typically resolves once the catheter is advanced into the pulmonary artery or withdrawn back into the atrium. Nonsustained ventricular tachycardia in this context does not require defibrillation, changing balloon inflation volume does not address the mechanical irritation, and monitoring should never be discontinued during catheter placement."},
{id:"P5021",d:"D11",c:"D11.C4",dok:2,q:"A pulmonary artery catheter is left with its balloon inflated for an extended period rather than only during intermittent wedge pressure measurement. What complication is this practice most likely to cause?",
 o:["Pulmonary infarction from prolonged occlusion of a distal pulmonary artery branch","Right ventricular perforation","Catheter knotting","Hemolysis of red blood cells"],a:0,
 why:"Leaving the balloon inflated for a prolonged period occludes blood flow to the distal lung segment supplied by that pulmonary artery branch, risking ischemia and infarction of lung tissue, which is why wedge readings should be brief and the balloon deflated immediately afterward. Right ventricular perforation and catheter knotting are related to catheter manipulation rather than prolonged balloon inflation, and hemolysis is not a typical complication of this practice."},
{id:"P5022",d:"D11",c:"D11.C4",dok:2,q:"A pulmonary artery catheter tracing shows a pulmonary artery pressure that increases from a baseline of 25/10 mmHg to 55/28 mmHg over several days, without a change in catheter position. What condition does this trend most likely suggest?",
 o:["Catheter migration into the wedge position","Worsening pulmonary hypertension","A damped waveform from a kinked catheter","Improvement in right ventricular function"],a:1,
 why:"A sustained rise in both systolic and diastolic pulmonary artery pressures over days, without any change in catheter position or waveform quality, reflects a true physiologic increase in pulmonary vascular pressures consistent with worsening pulmonary hypertension. Catheter migration into a wedge position would produce a wedge waveform rather than an elevated pulmonary artery tracing, a damped waveform would blunt rather than raise the pressure values, and worsening pulmonary pressures argue against, not for, improving right ventricular function."},
{id:"P5023",d:"D11",c:"D11.C4",dok:3,q:"A pulmonary artery catheter reading shows a pulmonary artery occlusion pressure of 22 mmHg, but the waveform tracing does not show the expected transition from a pulsatile pulmonary artery waveform to a lower amplitude, less pulsatile wedge waveform when the balloon is inflated. What should the nurse suspect?",
 o:["The elevated pressure confirms severe left ventricular failure","The balloon has ruptured","The catheter may be overwedged or malpositioned, and the reading may not be accurate","The reading confirms accurate wedge position and should be documented as obtained"],a:2,
 why:"A true wedge pressure requires a characteristic change in waveform morphology from the pulsatile pulmonary artery tracing to a dampened, lower frequency wedge tracing, and without that expected change, the numeric value cannot be trusted and may reflect overwedging or catheter malposition. The elevated number alone does not confirm heart failure without a valid waveform, a ruptured balloon would typically fail to generate any occlusion pressure rise at all, and documenting an unconfirmed reading as accurate could lead to inappropriate treatment decisions."},
{id:"P5024",d:"D11",c:"D11.C4",dok:3,q:"A pulmonary artery catheter has been in place for four days. The patient develops sudden hemoptysis, hypoxia, and a drop in blood pressure immediately after a wedge pressure measurement. What complication should be suspected first?",
 o:["Pneumothorax from catheter insertion","Catheter related bloodstream infection","Pulmonary embolism unrelated to the catheter","Pulmonary artery rupture from balloon inflation"],a:3,
 why:"Sudden hemoptysis and hemodynamic compromise occurring immediately after balloon inflation for a wedge measurement is a classic presentation of pulmonary artery rupture, a rare but life threatening complication, especially in catheters in place for several days. Pneumothorax is a complication of the original insertion, not of a wedge measurement days later, catheter related infection would present with fever and systemic signs rather than sudden hemoptysis tied temporally to balloon inflation, and the direct temporal link to the wedge measurement makes an unrelated pulmonary embolism far less likely."},
{id:"P5025",d:"D11",c:"D11.C5",dok:1,q:"Which formula correctly defines cardiac index?",
 o:["Cardiac output divided by body surface area","Stroke volume multiplied by heart rate","Mean arterial pressure minus central venous pressure, divided by cardiac output","Cardiac output multiplied by body surface area"],a:0,
 why:"Cardiac index normalizes cardiac output to the patients body surface area, allowing comparison across patients of different sizes, and is calculated as cardiac output divided by body surface area. Stroke volume multiplied by heart rate is the formula for cardiac output itself, not cardiac index, the mean arterial pressure and central venous pressure relationship describes systemic vascular resistance, and multiplying rather than dividing by body surface area would not produce a clinically meaningful normalized value."},
{id:"P5026",d:"D11",c:"D11.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient has a cardiac output of 5.4 L per minute and a body surface area of 1.8 square meters. What is the cardiac index?",
 o:["9.7 L/min/m2","3.0 L/min/m2","1.8 L/min/m2","5.4 L/min/m2"],a:1,
 why:"Cardiac index is calculated by dividing cardiac output by body surface area, so 5.4 divided by 1.8 equals 3.0 liters per minute per square meter, a normal value. Multiplying the two values instead of dividing, or simply reporting one of the two input numbers, does not follow the correct formula for this derived parameter."},
{id:"P5027",d:"D11",c:"D11.C5",dok:2,q:"A patients arterial oxygen content (CaO2) is 18 mL/dL and cardiac output is 5 L/min. Using the formula DO2 = CO x CaO2 x 10, what is the oxygen delivery?",
 o:["90 mL/min","360 mL/min","900 mL/min","1800 mL/min"],a:2,
 why:"Oxygen delivery is calculated by multiplying cardiac output by arterial oxygen content and a conversion factor of ten to reconcile the units, so 5 multiplied by 18 multiplied by 10 equals 900 mL of oxygen delivered per minute. The other values result from omitting the conversion factor, dividing instead of multiplying, or doubling the correct result, none of which follow the correct formula."},
{id:"P5028",d:"D11",c:"D11.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient has a mean pulmonary artery pressure of 30 mmHg, a pulmonary artery occlusion pressure of 10 mmHg, and a cardiac output of 4 L/min. Using the formula PVR equals mean PA pressure minus PAOP, divided by cardiac output, multiplied by 80, what is the pulmonary vascular resistance?",
 o:["200 dynes sec/cm5","80 dynes sec/cm5","20 dynes sec/cm5","400 dynes sec/cm5"],a:3,
 why:"Pulmonary vascular resistance is found by subtracting the pulmonary artery occlusion pressure from the mean pulmonary artery pressure to isolate the pressure drop across the pulmonary circulation, dividing by cardiac output, and multiplying by the conversion factor of 80, yielding 20 divided by 4 times 80, which equals 400 dynes sec per cm5, an elevated value. The distractor values come from stopping the calculation before applying the conversion factor or from arithmetic errors in the subtraction or division steps."},
{id:"P5029",d:"D11",c:"D11.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient has a mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2) of 45 percent despite a normal arterial oxygen saturation and hemoglobin. What does this low value most likely indicate?",
 o:["Increased oxygen extraction by the tissues relative to oxygen delivery, suggesting low cardiac output or high metabolic demand","Excess oxygen delivery relative to tissue needs","A falsely elevated hemoglobin level","Normal physiologic variation requiring no further evaluation"],a:0,
 why:"SvO2 reflects the balance between oxygen delivery and tissue oxygen consumption, and when delivery falls relative to demand, such as with low cardiac output or high metabolic demand, tissues extract more oxygen from the blood, leaving less oxygen in the venous return and lowering the SvO2. A low SvO2 indicates the opposite of excess delivery, hemoglobin is stated as normal in the stem so it cannot explain the finding, and a value this far below the normal range of about 60 to 80 percent is not a benign variant and warrants further assessment."},
{id:"P5030",d:"D11",c:"D11.C6",dok:2,q:"A patient in the early stage of septic shock typically shows which hemodynamic pattern before decompensation occurs?",
 o:["High systemic vascular resistance with a low cardiac index","Low systemic vascular resistance with a normal or elevated cardiac index","Low central venous pressure with a normal systemic vascular resistance","High cardiac index with high systemic vascular resistance"],a:1,
 why:"Early septic shock is a distributive shock state driven by widespread vasodilation from inflammatory mediators, producing a low systemic vascular resistance, while the heart often compensates with a normal or elevated cardiac index in a hyperdynamic response. A high systemic vascular resistance with a low cardiac index instead describes cardiogenic shock, and the combination of high resistance with high cardiac index does not represent a recognized shock pattern."},
{id:"P5031",d:"D11",c:"D11.C6",dok:2,q:"Which hemodynamic pattern is most consistent with hypovolemic shock from acute blood loss?",
 o:["High central venous pressure, high pulmonary artery occlusion pressure, and low cardiac index","Low systemic vascular resistance with a normal cardiac index","Low central venous pressure, low pulmonary artery occlusion pressure, low cardiac index, and high systemic vascular resistance","Normal filling pressures with a low cardiac index"],a:2,
 why:"Hypovolemic shock results from inadequate circulating volume, so both right and left sided filling pressures fall along with cardiac output, while the body compensates with reflex vasoconstriction that raises systemic vascular resistance. High filling pressures with a low cardiac index instead describe cardiogenic shock, and a low systemic vascular resistance is characteristic of distributive rather than hypovolemic shock."},
{id:"P5032",d:"D11",c:"D11.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient develops sudden dyspnea, hypotension, and jugular venous distension after a long surgical procedure. Hemodynamic monitoring shows an elevated central venous pressure, a normal to low pulmonary artery occlusion pressure, and a low cardiac index. Which shock state is best supported by this pattern?",
 o:["Hypovolemic shock","Cardiogenic shock from left ventricular failure","Distributive shock from sepsis","Obstructive shock, such as from a massive pulmonary embolism"],a:3,
 why:"Obstructive shock from a large pulmonary embolism causes a sudden mechanical block to right ventricular outflow, raising right sided filling pressure while left sided filling pressure remains normal or low because blood cannot reach the left heart, and cardiac output falls, a pattern distinct from left heart failure. Hypovolemic shock would show low filling pressures throughout, cardiogenic shock from left ventricular failure typically raises the pulmonary artery occlusion pressure along with the central venous pressure, and distributive shock produces a low, not elevated, systemic vascular resistance rather than this obstructive pattern."},
{id:"P5033",d:"D11",c:"D11.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient with a spinal cord injury at the T4 level develops hypotension and a heart rate of 48 beats per minute, with warm extremities. Hemodynamic monitoring shows a low systemic vascular resistance and a normal cardiac index. Which shock state does this presentation reflect?",
 o:["Neurogenic shock from loss of sympathetic vascular tone","Hypovolemic shock","Septic shock","Cardiogenic shock"],a:0,
 why:"Neurogenic shock results from disruption of sympathetic outflow after a high spinal cord injury, causing unopposed vagal tone with bradycardia and loss of vasomotor tone that produces vasodilation, warm extremities, and low systemic vascular resistance. Hypovolemic shock would present with tachycardia and vasoconstriction, septic shock typically presents with fever or infection and tachycardia rather than bradycardia, and cardiogenic shock would present with a low cardiac index and cool extremities rather than a normal index and warm skin."},
{id:"P5034",d:"D11",c:"D11.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient develops facial swelling, wheezing, and hypotension minutes after receiving intravenous contrast. Which hemodynamic pattern would be expected if invasive monitoring were obtained during this event?",
 o:["High systemic vascular resistance with a low cardiac index","Low systemic vascular resistance from massive histamine mediated vasodilation, similar in pattern to other distributive shock states","Elevated pulmonary artery occlusion pressure with pulmonary edema","Normal systemic vascular resistance with an elevated cardiac index"],a:1,
 why:"Anaphylaxis triggers massive histamine and mediator release causing profound vasodilation and capillary leak, producing a distributive shock pattern with low systemic vascular resistance, similar in hemodynamic profile to septic shock even though the trigger differs. High systemic vascular resistance with low cardiac output instead reflects cardiogenic shock, elevated filling pressures with pulmonary edema reflect left heart failure rather than an allergic reaction, and a normal resistance with elevated output does not match the profound vasodilation seen in anaphylaxis."},
{id:"P5035",d:"D11",c:"D11.C6",dok:4,q:"A patient in the intensive care unit has the following trend over six hours: cardiac index falls from 3.2 to 1.9, systemic vascular resistance rises from 1100 to 2000, pulmonary artery occlusion pressure rises from 12 to 24, and central venous pressure rises from 6 to 16, while urine output falls and the skin becomes cool and mottled. New muffled heart sounds are also noted. Which two conditions must be distinguished as the most likely explanation, and what bedside finding would help differentiate them?",
 o:["Hypovolemic shock versus septic shock; skin temperature is the only distinguishing feature needed","Cardiogenic shock from pump failure versus cardiac tamponade; pulsus paradoxus and equalization of diastolic pressures favor tamponade","Neurogenic shock versus anaphylactic shock; both would be excluded by the elevated systemic vascular resistance","Obstructive shock from pulmonary embolism versus hypovolemic shock; a normal pulmonary artery occlusion pressure would confirm pulmonary embolism"],a:1,
 why:"The pattern of falling cardiac index with rising filling pressures, rising resistance, and new muffled heart sounds is consistent with either primary pump failure or a mechanical restriction to filling such as tamponade, and these two must be distinguished clinically since both can show similarly elevated filling pressures with low output; pulsus paradoxus and equalization of right and left sided diastolic pressures on catheterization point toward tamponade rather than pump failure. Hypovolemic shock is excluded by the rising, not falling, filling pressures described in the stem, the elevated systemic vascular resistance argues against neurogenic or anaphylactic shock since both of those distributive states produce a low, not high, resistance, and the stem describes a rising, not normal, pulmonary artery occlusion pressure, which is inconsistent with using a normal value to confirm pulmonary embolism."},
{id:"P7021",d:"D12",c:"D12.C1",dok:1,q:"Which of the following is an appropriate indication for intra-aortic balloon pump placement?",
 o:["Cardiogenic shock refractory to medications after myocardial infarction","Severe aortic regurgitation","Suspected aortic dissection","End stage cardiomyopathy with no plan for any further intervention"],a:0,
 why:"Refractory cardiogenic shock is a classic indication for counterpulsation support. Aortic regurgitation and aortic dissection are absolute contraindications because balloon inflation worsens regurgitant flow and risks propagating a dissection, and end stage disease with no plan for further therapy is not an appropriate use since the device is meant to bridge patients to recovery or further treatment."},
{id:"P7022",d:"D12",c:"D12.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient with cardiogenic shock is being considered for an intra-aortic balloon pump. Which finding would be a contraindication to placement?",
 o:["Known abdominal aortic aneurysm","Elevated troponin","Hypotension","Tachycardia"],a:0,
 why:"An abdominal aortic aneurysm raises the risk of rupture or embolization as the balloon catheter moves through the aorta, making it a contraindication. Elevated troponin, hypotension, and tachycardia are expected findings in cardiogenic shock after myocardial infarction and are reasons that support rather than prohibit balloon pump use."},
{id:"P7027",d:"D12",c:"D12.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient on an intra-aortic balloon pump develops atrial fibrillation with an irregular rhythm, making ECG triggering unreliable. What is the most appropriate trigger mode to use instead?",
 o:["Arterial pressure triggering, using the upstroke of the arterial waveform","Pacer triggering only, regardless of the patient pacing status","Turning off the balloon pump until the rhythm converts","Increasing the balloon augmentation ratio to 1 to 1 regardless of trigger accuracy"],a:0,
 why:"Pressure triggering uses the arterial waveform upstroke and can maintain reliable timing when the ECG signal is irregular or unreliable. Pacer triggering only applies to patients who are actively being paced, stopping the device abandons needed hemodynamic support unnecessarily, and changing the augmentation ratio does not fix an unreliable trigger source."},
{id:"P7028",d:"D12",c:"D12.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient on an intra-aortic balloon pump is hemodynamically stable and being weaned from support. What is the typical method used to wean the device?",
 o:["Gradually decreasing the augmentation ratio from 1 to 1 to 1 to 2 to 1 to 3 while monitoring hemodynamics","Abruptly discontinuing the balloon pump once blood pressure normalizes","Gradually decreasing the balloon inflation volume while keeping the ratio at 1 to 1","Increasing the heart rate to reduce the number of augmented beats"],a:0,
 why:"Ratio weaning gradually reduces the frequency of augmented beats while allowing continued monitoring for hemodynamic tolerance. Abrupt discontinuation risks sudden hemodynamic collapse and thrombus formation from stagnant blood around the balloon, volume weaning is a less standard alternative used more selectively, and manipulating heart rate is not a recognized or safe method of weaning the device."},
{id:"P7023",d:"D12",c:"D12.C1",dok:3,q:"On an arterial waveform from a patient with an intra-aortic balloon pump, the balloon inflation artifact occurs well after the dicrotic notch instead of coincident with it. What effect does this timing error have on the patient?",
 o:["Suboptimal diastolic augmentation and reduced coronary perfusion benefit because part of diastole passes before augmentation begins","Premature closure of the aortic valve with increased afterload","Decreased coronary perfusion pressure from balloon inflation occurring during systole","Increased myocardial oxygen demand from inflation against a still open aortic valve"],a:0,
 why:"Late inflation wastes part of the diastolic period before augmentation starts, so the patient receives less benefit from the boosted diastolic coronary perfusion. Options B, C, and D each describe consequences of early inflation, a different timing error in which the balloon inflates while the aortic valve is still open, not the late inflation problem described here."},
{id:"P7024",d:"D12",c:"D12.C1",dok:3,q:"An intra-aortic balloon pump is set so the balloon deflates well before the onset of systole, seen on the waveform as the diastolic augmentation pressure dropping back to baseline before the next systolic upstroke begins. What is the main consequence of this timing error?",
 o:["Suboptimal afterload reduction and coronary augmentation, potentially allowing retrograde coronary and carotid blood flow","Increased afterload with a widened dicrotic notch","Increased myocardial oxygen demand from the balloon obstructing left ventricular ejection","Improved diastolic augmentation but increased balloon wear"],a:0,
 why:"Early deflation empties the aorta too soon, losing the pressure gradient that supports coronary and carotid flow and reducing the afterload reduction benefit of the next systole. Options B and D describe effects of late deflation, and option C describes early inflation, both different timing errors from the one described here."},
{id:"P7025",d:"D12",c:"D12.C1",dok:3,q:"An intra-aortic balloon pump deflates just after the onset of the next systolic contraction rather than immediately before it. What effect does this have on the patient?",
 o:["Increased afterload and myocardial oxygen demand because the left ventricle must eject against a still partially inflated balloon","Loss of diastolic augmentation with reduced coronary perfusion pressure","Retrograde flow into the coronary arteries during diastole","A widened pulse pressure with improved forward flow"],a:0,
 why:"When the balloon is still partially inflated as systole begins, the ventricle must eject against added resistance, raising afterload and myocardial oxygen demand. Option B describes early deflation, not late deflation, option C is not a recognized pattern for this fault, and option D is incorrect because late deflation raises resistance to ejection rather than improving forward flow."},
{id:"P7026",d:"D12",c:"D12.C1",dok:3,q:"A nurse caring for a patient on an intra-aortic balloon pump notes the balloon inflating before the aortic valve closes, augmenting pressure while the valve is still open. The patient develops new chest pain. What is the priority nursing action?",
 o:["Notify the provider immediately and anticipate a timing adjustment, since early inflation increases myocardial oxygen demand and can provoke ischemia","Increase the augmentation ratio to improve coronary perfusion","Document the finding and reassess again at the end of the shift","Decrease the patient heart rate with a beta blocker before addressing the balloon timing"],a:0,
 why:"Early inflation forces the ventricle to eject against a partially inflated balloon, raising myocardial oxygen demand, so new chest pain requires prompt provider notification and timing correction. Option B would worsen the problem by adding more pressure while the valve is still open, option C inappropriately delays action for a symptom suggesting ischemia, and option D treats a downstream effect rather than correcting the root mechanical cause first."},
{id:"P7029",d:"D12",c:"D12.C2",dok:1,q:"How does a percutaneous ventricular assist device such as an Impella provide hemodynamic support?",
 o:["An axial flow catheter pump pulls blood from the left ventricle and expels it into the ascending aorta, reducing ventricular workload and increasing cardiac output","It compresses the aorta rhythmically from outside the vessel to boost diastolic pressure","It pumps blood from the right atrium to the pulmonary artery to bypass the right ventricle","It fully replaces the native heart pumping function through a surgically implanted total artificial heart"],a:0,
 why:"The device uses an internal axial flow pump positioned across the aortic valve to actively unload the left ventricle and augment forward flow. Option B describes external counterpulsation, which is not how this device works, option C describes a different, right sided support concept, and option D describes a total artificial heart, a more invasive and different device altogether."},
{id:"P7030",d:"D12",c:"D12.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient with a TandemHeart percutaneous ventricular assist device requires monitoring of the transseptal cannula position. Which imaging modality is most commonly used at the bedside to confirm and reassess cannula position?",
 o:["Transthoracic or transesophageal echocardiography","Portable abdominal ultrasound","12 lead electrocardiography","Pulmonary artery catheter waveform analysis"],a:0,
 why:"Echocardiography allows direct visualization of the intracardiac cannula relative to the atrial septum and surrounding structures. Abdominal ultrasound is not relevant to intracardiac device position, a 12 lead ECG monitors electrical rhythm rather than mechanical position, and pulmonary artery catheter waveforms reflect hemodynamics rather than the physical location of the cannula."},
{id:"P7031",d:"D12",c:"D12.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient with a femorally inserted percutaneous ventricular assist device develops a cool, pale, pulseless leg on the side of insertion. What is the most likely cause and priority action?",
 o:["Limb ischemia from arterial obstruction by the device sheath, requiring immediate vascular assessment and possible distal perfusion catheter placement","A normal expected finding after femoral arterial cannulation that requires no intervention","Deep vein thrombosis, requiring anticoagulation alone without vascular assessment","A vasovagal reaction, requiring only reassurance and repositioning"],a:0,
 why:"A large bore arterial sheath can obstruct distal flow, and a cool, pale, pulseless limb signals limb ischemia requiring urgent vascular evaluation. This presentation is never a benign expected finding, the pattern described reflects arterial rather than venous occlusion so it does not fit deep vein thrombosis, and a vasovagal reaction affects heart rate and blood pressure globally rather than causing a focal pulseless limb."},
{id:"P7032",d:"D12",c:"D12.C3",dok:2,q:"When obtaining a blood pressure by Doppler in a patient with a continuous flow LVAD, the cuff is deflated slowly until the first Doppler sound returns. This value most closely approximates which parameter, and what is the generally recommended target?",
 o:["Mean arterial pressure, generally targeted between 70 and 90 mmHg","Systolic blood pressure, generally targeted above 140 mmHg","Diastolic blood pressure, generally targeted below 60 mmHg","Pulse pressure, generally targeted above 40 mmHg"],a:0,
 why:"Continuous flow produces minimal pulsatility, so the first Doppler sound during slow cuff deflation approximates mean arterial pressure rather than a discrete systolic reading. A target above 140 mmHg is far too high and raises bleeding and stroke risk, the technique does not reliably isolate a diastolic value, and pulse pressure is often narrow in these patients and is not the value obtained or targeted by this technique."},
{id:"P7033",d:"D12",c:"D12.C3",dok:2,q:"What is the priority nursing teaching for a patient being discharged with a durable LVAD driveline exit site?",
 o:["Stabilize the driveline to prevent tension and trauma, and keep the exit site clean and dry, watching for signs of infection","The exit site may be submerged in bath water once it is healed","The driveline can be temporarily disconnected from the controller if it becomes tangled","Any redness at the site is expected and does not need to be reported"],a:0,
 why:"Driveline stabilization and site care reduce trauma and infection risk, which is a leading complication in LVAD patients. Submerging the site increases infection risk and is contraindicated, disconnecting the driveline from the controller stops pump function and is a life threatening emergency, and redness or drainage can indicate infection and must always be reported rather than dismissed as expected."},
{id:"P7034",d:"D12",c:"D12.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient with a durable LVAD develops hypotension, a low flow alarm, and a rising pulsatility index on the controller. What does this pattern most likely suggest?",
 o:["Hypovolemia or a suction event, from inadequate left ventricular filling","Pump thrombosis, from clot formation within the rotor","An outflow graft obstruction from kinking","Aortic regurgitation, causing recirculation of flow"],a:0,
 why:"Inadequate ventricular filling from hypovolemia can cause a suction event, producing hypotension, low flow, and a rising pulsatility index as the pump intermittently loses inflow. Pump thrombosis classically shows rising power with a low, not rising, pulsatility index along with hemolysis, an outflow obstruction typically produces low flow with a low rather than rising pulsatility index from the fixed restriction, and aortic regurgitation produces a different recirculation pattern rather than this acute hypotensive suction presentation."},
{id:"P7035",d:"D12",c:"D12.C3",dok:4,q:"A durable LVAD patient becomes acutely hypotensive with an alarm sounding, but the pump hum is present on auscultation and the patient is conscious and speaking. Which sequence of actions best reflects appropriate priority?",
 o:["Check the controller for alarm details and pump parameters first, assess the driveline and connections, then evaluate the patient for hypovolemia, arrhythmia, or tamponade before considering pump exchange equipment","Immediately activate the emergency pump exchange protocol and disconnect the current controller","Perform chest compressions immediately since hypotension in an LVAD patient always indicates cardiac arrest","Give an empiric fluid bolus and antiarrhythmic medication simultaneously without checking the device first"],a:0,
 why:"A stepwise approach that starts with the controller and device connections before moving to clinical causes is appropriate because the pump hum indicates the device is still functioning, so a mechanical failure is not confirmed yet. Pump exchange is reserved for confirmed device failure rather than a first response, chest compressions are not automatically indicated in a conscious, speaking patient with a present pump hum, and giving multiple empiric treatments without first identifying the cause risks missing a correctable device related problem."},
{id:"P7036",d:"D12",c:"D12.C4",dok:1,q:"Which patient is the most appropriate candidate for venovenous ECMO rather than venoarterial ECMO?",
 o:["A patient with severe isolated respiratory failure and adequate cardiac function","A patient in cardiogenic shock after a large myocardial infarction","A patient in cardiac arrest requiring hemodynamic support","A patient with severe biventricular heart failure awaiting transplant"],a:0,
 why:"Venovenous ECMO provides gas exchange support only and requires adequate native cardiac function to circulate blood, making it suitable for isolated respiratory failure. Cardiogenic shock, cardiac arrest, and severe biventricular heart failure all require circulatory support that only venoarterial ECMO can provide."},
{id:"P7037",d:"D12",c:"D12.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient on peripheral femoral venoarterial ECMO with recovering native lung function shows cyanosis and a lower oxygen saturation in the fingers of the right hand compared with the toes. What phenomenon does this represent?",
 o:["Differential hypoxia (harlequin syndrome), from the patient own poorly oxygenated blood ejected by a recovering heart mixing with well oxygenated ECMO return blood in the lower body","A normal expected finding that requires no further evaluation","Peripheral vasoconstriction from hypothermia affecting only the upper body","An arterial line malfunction on the right side"],a:0,
 why:"As native cardiac function recovers, the heart can eject the patient own less oxygenated blood into the upper body while the femoral ECMO return supplies well oxygenated blood to the lower body, producing this regional saturation split. This is a recognized physiologic complication requiring evaluation, not a benign or expected finding, hypothermia related vasoconstriction would not selectively lower hand saturation compared to toe saturation in this classic pattern, and equipment should be checked but the consistent, physiologically explainable pattern points to differential hypoxia rather than simple malfunction."},
{id:"P7038",d:"D12",c:"D12.C4",dok:2,q:"On venoarterial ECMO, the post-oxygenator blood gas shows a rising PaCO2 and the circuit pressure drop across the oxygenator increases over several hours. What does this most likely indicate?",
 o:["Oxygenator dysfunction from clot formation or plasma leak, reducing gas exchange efficiency","Normal variation that requires no action","Cannula malposition causing recirculation","Patient recovery of native lung function"],a:0,
 why:"A rising post oxygenator PaCO2 together with an increasing pressure drop across the membrane points to a failing artificial lung from clot or plasma leak. This trend is abnormal and requires action rather than being normal variation, cannula malposition and recirculation affect saturation patterns at the cannulas rather than the pressure drop across the oxygenator itself, and native lung recovery would not worsen the circuit own post oxygenator gas exchange values."},
{id:"P7039",d:"D12",c:"D12.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient on mechanical circulatory support after cardiac surgery develops a sudden drop in device flow, muffled heart sounds, and rising central venous pressure with mediastinal chest tube output that abruptly decreases. What complication should be suspected first?",
 o:["Cardiac tamponade from mediastinal bleeding with clot obstructing chest tube drainage","Pulmonary embolism","Pneumothorax","Hypoglycemia"],a:0,
 why:"The combination of muffled heart sounds, rising central venous pressure, falling device flow, and sudden cessation of chest tube output despite ongoing bleeding is classic for tamponade from clotted mediastinal blood. Pulmonary embolism does not typically cause muffled heart sounds or sudden chest tube cessation, pneumothorax usually presents with absent breath sounds and tracheal shift rather than this pattern, and hypoglycemia does not explain muffled heart sounds or a sudden device flow drop tied to chest tube findings."},
{id:"P7040",d:"D12",c:"D12.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient on ECMO who is anticoagulated with heparin develops a falling platelet count five days into therapy along with a new limb clot. What complication should be suspected and what is the appropriate next step?",
 o:["Heparin induced thrombocytopenia, requiring discontinuation of heparin and initiation of a non-heparin anticoagulant","Disseminated intravascular coagulation, requiring an increased heparin dose","Expected hemodilution from the ECMO circuit, requiring no change in therapy","Vitamin K deficiency, requiring vitamin K administration"],a:0,
 why:"A falling platelet count with a new thrombotic event several days after starting heparin is the classic presentation of heparin induced thrombocytopenia, treated by stopping heparin and starting an alternative anticoagulant. Disseminated intravascular coagulation typically causes both bleeding and clotting with low fibrinogen and would not be treated by increasing heparin, circuit hemodilution alone does not explain a new thrombotic event, and vitamin K deficiency causes a bleeding tendency from low clotting factors rather than thrombocytopenia with new clot formation."},
{id:"P7041",d:"D13",c:"D13.C1",dok:1,q:"Which condition is a standard indication for permanent pacemaker implantation?",
 o:["Symptomatic sinus bradycardia causing fatigue and dizziness","Asymptomatic first degree AV block","Sinus tachycardia during exercise","A single episode of vasovagal syncope in a healthy young adult with no documented bradycardia"],a:0,
 why:"Symptomatic bradycardia causing fatigue or dizziness is a recognized indication for pacing because it reflects hemodynamically significant slow heart rate. Asymptomatic first degree block rarely warrants pacing, sinus tachycardia during exercise is a normal physiologic response rather than an arrhythmia needing pacing, and an isolated vasovagal episode without documented significant bradycardia in an otherwise healthy young adult is not a standard pacing indication."},
{id:"P7042",d:"D13",c:"D13.C1",dok:2,q:"A device is programmed so the first letter of its NBG code is V. What does this indicate about the pacemaker?",
 o:["The ventricle is the chamber being paced","The ventricle is the chamber being sensed","The device responds to sensed activity by triggering another impulse","The device has rate responsive pacing enabled"],a:0,
 why:"The first letter of the NBG code identifies the chamber being paced. Option B describes the function of the second letter, option C describes the response function indicated by the third letter, and option D describes the rate modulation feature indicated by the fourth letter, so each of these describes a different position in the code than the one being asked about."},
{id:"P7043",d:"D13",c:"D13.C1",dok:2,q:"A pacemaker is programmed with an R in the fourth position of its code, for example VVIR. What does this fourth position indicate?",
 o:["The device can automatically increase its paced rate in response to patient activity or metabolic demand","The device paces and senses in both chambers","The device is programmed to a fixed rate regardless of activity","The device delivers biventricular pacing"],a:0,
 why:"The fourth position of the NBG code indicates rate modulation, allowing the paced rate to rise with activity or metabolic demand. Dual chamber pacing and sensing is described by the first two letters rather than the fourth, a fixed rate is the opposite of what rate responsiveness means, and biventricular pacing is a separate feature of cardiac resynchronization devices, not something indicated by the fourth NBG letter."},
{id:"P7044",d:"D13",c:"D13.C2",dok:1,q:"What does a pacemaker programmed to VVI mode do?",
 o:["It paces and senses the ventricle only, and inhibits pacing when it senses an intrinsic ventricular beat","It paces and senses both the atrium and ventricle, tracking atrial activity","It paces the atrium only and senses nothing","It delivers a fixed paced rate regardless of any sensed intrinsic activity"],a:0,
 why:"VVI mode paces and senses in the ventricle only, and withholds pacing when it detects an intrinsic ventricular beat, making it a demand mode. Option B describes DDD mode, option C describes an atrial only pacing mode without sensing, and option D describes an asynchronous mode such as VOO, none of which describe VVI."},
{id:"P7045",d:"D13",c:"D13.C2",dok:2,q:"AAI pacing mode is most appropriate for which patient?",
 o:["A patient with sinus node dysfunction and normal AV node conduction","A patient with complete heart block","A patient with chronic atrial fibrillation","A patient with frequent premature ventricular contractions requiring ventricular backup pacing"],a:0,
 why:"AAI paces and senses the atrium only, which is appropriate when the sinus node is diseased but AV conduction to the ventricle remains normal. It would not help complete heart block since atrial impulses could not reliably reach the ventricle, it is not useful in chronic atrial fibrillation because there is no organized atrial rhythm to pace or sense, and it provides no ventricular backup pacing for a patient with ventricular ectopy."},
{id:"P7046",d:"D13",c:"D13.C2",dok:2,q:"In a DDD pacemaker, what is the function of the programmed AV delay?",
 o:["It is the timed interval between a sensed or paced atrial event and delivery of a ventricular output, mimicking the normal PR interval","It is the minimum time the device waits before sensing any atrial activity","It is the interval between two consecutive ventricular paced beats","It determines only the upper rate limit of the device"],a:0,
 why:"The AV delay mimics the natural PR interval by timing the interval from an atrial event to the resulting ventricular output. Option B describes a refractory period concept rather than the AV delay, option C describes the pacing cycle length or lower rate interval, and option D describes a separate, unrelated programmed parameter."},
{id:"P7047",d:"D13",c:"D13.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient with a DDD pacemaker develops sinus tachycardia during exercise that exceeds the programmed upper rate limit. The rhythm strip shows the paced AV delay progressively lengthening over several beats until a ventricular paced beat is dropped, then the cycle repeats. What is this pattern called and why does it occur?",
 o:["Pacemaker Wenckebach, a normal protective response that prevents ventricular pacing from tracking the atrium 1 to 1 above the programmed upper rate","Failure to capture, from inadequate output","Pacemaker mediated tachycardia, from retrograde conduction","Cross talk, from the ventricular lead sensing the atrial pacing output"],a:0,
 why:"Pacemaker Wenckebach is a built in protective behavior that progressively lengthens the AV delay and drops beats to prevent the ventricle from tracking the atrium one to one above the programmed upper rate. Failure to capture would show spikes without a resulting QRS rather than a gradually lengthening delay, pacemaker mediated tachycardia is a re-entrant loop sustained by retrograde P waves rather than a rate limiting response, and cross talk is inappropriate sensing between leads, a different mechanism than upper rate behavior."},
{id:"P7048",d:"D13",c:"D13.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient with a dual chamber pacemaker develops a burst of atrial fibrillation. To prevent the device from rapidly pacing the ventricle in response to the fast, irregular atrial signals, the device automatically changes from a tracking mode to a non-tracking mode until the atrial fibrillation resolves. What feature is being described?",
 o:["Automatic mode switching","Rate responsiveness","Hysteresis","Magnet mode"],a:0,
 why:"Automatic mode switching detects a fast, irregular atrial rhythm and temporarily changes the device to a non-tracking mode to prevent rapid ventricular pacing. Rate responsiveness adjusts pacing rate based on activity or metabolic demand rather than arrhythmia detection, hysteresis allows a lower rate before pacing resumes after an intrinsic beat, and magnet mode is a temporary asynchronous response to an external magnet, not an automatic arrhythmia detection algorithm."},
{id:"P7049",d:"D13",c:"D13.C3",dok:1,q:"What does normal ventricular capture on a pacemaker rhythm strip look like?",
 o:["A pacing spike immediately followed by a wide QRS complex representing depolarization of the paced ventricle","A pacing spike with no QRS complex following it","A QRS complex occurring without any preceding pacing spike, unrelated to device timing","Two pacing spikes occurring back to back with no intervening QRS"],a:0,
 why:"Normal capture is shown by a pacing spike immediately followed by a wide QRS from ventricular depolarization spreading through myocardial tissue rather than the native conduction pathway. A spike with no following QRS describes failure to capture, an unrelated QRS without a preceding spike may simply reflect a normally sensed intrinsic beat rather than capture, and two spikes with no intervening QRS suggests a malfunction rather than a normal pattern."},
{id:"P7050",d:"D13",c:"D13.C3",dok:2,q:"A pacemaker sensitivity setting is programmed so the device is overly sensitive to small electrical signals. What problem can this cause?",
 o:["Oversensing of nonpacing signals such as T waves or myopotentials, leading to inappropriate inhibition of pacing","Failure to capture the ventricle","Failure to sense true intrinsic beats, causing competitive pacing","A fixed asynchronous pacing rate regardless of intrinsic activity"],a:0,
 why:"An overly sensitive setting can detect nonpacing electrical signals such as T waves or muscle artifact and inappropriately inhibit pacing output. Failure to capture relates to output energy rather than sensitivity, failure to sense true beats describes the opposite problem of undersensing which occurs when sensitivity is set too low, and asynchronous pacing is unrelated to a sensitivity setting error."},
{id:"P7051",d:"D13",c:"D13.C3",dok:3,q:"A nurse is evaluating a paced rhythm strip to confirm adequate ventricular capture. Which combination of findings confirms capture is occurring appropriately?",
 o:["Each pacing spike is consistently followed by a wide QRS complex, and the palpated pulse and blood pressure correspond to the paced rate","Pacing spikes are present at the programmed rate, regardless of whether a QRS follows each one","The monitored heart rate matches the programmed rate, even if intermittent spikes are not followed by a QRS","A normal narrow QRS follows every pacing spike, confirming the impulse originated from the native conduction system"],a:0,
 why:"Consistent capture is confirmed when every spike produces a wide paced QRS and this electrical activity correlates with an actual palpable pulse and measurable blood pressure. Spikes without a consistent following QRS indicate failure to capture rather than confirmation of it, a matching monitored rate can be misleading if intrinsic beats coincidentally match the paced rate while some spikes fail to capture, and a paced QRS is typically wide rather than narrow since the impulse spreads through myocardium rather than the native conduction system."},
{id:"P7055",d:"D13",c:"D13.C4",dok:2,q:"In a dual chamber pacemaker, the ventricular lead inappropriately senses the atrial pacing output as an intrinsic ventricular event, causing the device to inhibit its ventricular pacing output. What is this phenomenon called?",
 o:["Cross talk","Pacemaker mediated tachycardia","Undersensing","Magnet mode"],a:0,
 why:"Cross talk occurs when one lead inappropriately senses the paced output of the other lead as an intrinsic event. Pacemaker mediated tachycardia describes a retrograde conduction re-entry loop, a different mechanism, undersensing describes the device failing to detect true intrinsic signals rather than inappropriately detecting a signal that is not a true ventricular event, and magnet mode describes a temporary response to an external magnet rather than lead to lead interference."},
{id:"P7056",d:"D13",c:"D13.C4",dok:2,q:"How does undersensing differ from oversensing in a pacemaker?",
 o:["Undersensing causes the device to fail to detect intrinsic cardiac activity, resulting in inappropriate extra pacing spikes, while oversensing causes the device to detect nonintrinsic signals and inappropriately withhold pacing","Undersensing and oversensing produce identical rhythm strip findings and cannot be distinguished clinically","Undersensing always requires immediate device replacement, while oversensing never requires any intervention","Oversensing only occurs with unipolar leads and never occurs with bipolar leads"],a:0,
 why:"Undersensing and oversensing produce opposite and distinguishable patterns, inappropriate extra spikes competing with intrinsic beats versus inappropriately withheld spikes. The two malfunctions produce different, not identical, strip findings, both may be managed with troubleshooting or reprogramming rather than automatic replacement and oversensing can also be clinically significant in a dependent patient, and while unipolar leads are more prone to myopotential oversensing, bipolar systems can still oversense from other sources such as electromagnetic interference."},
{id:"P7052",d:"D13",c:"D13.C4",dok:3,q:"A pacemaker dependent patient has a rhythm strip showing long pauses without any pacing spikes, and is found to have significant skeletal muscle tremor from shivering. Electrical artifact from the muscle activity correlates with each pause. What is the most likely explanation?",
 o:["Oversensing of myopotential (muscle) artifact, which the device misinterprets as intrinsic cardiac activity and inappropriately inhibits pacing","Failure to capture from inadequate lead contact","A normal expected response to shivering that requires no intervention","Battery depletion causing the device to stop functioning"],a:0,
 why:"Muscle artifact correlating precisely with pacing pauses in a pacemaker dependent patient points to oversensing of myopotentials being mistaken for intrinsic cardiac activity. Failure to capture would show spikes present without a following QRS rather than an absence of spikes altogether, this pattern represents a potentially serious malfunction in a dependent patient rather than a benign expected finding, and isolated pauses tied precisely to muscle artifact are more consistent with oversensing than with a global battery failure."},
{id:"P7053",d:"D13",c:"D13.C4",dok:3,q:"A pacemaker dependent patient rhythm strip shows a prolonged asystolic pause with no pacing spikes visible at all, despite the patient having no intrinsic heart rate above the programmed lower rate. What malfunction does this represent and what is a possible cause?",
 o:["Failure to output, which can result from a fractured or dislodged lead, battery depletion, or a loose connector","Failure to capture, from an elevated capture threshold","Undersensing, from inappropriate detection of intrinsic beats","Cross talk, from one lead sensing the other chamber paced output"],a:0,
 why:"A complete absence of pacing spikes in a dependent patient without an adequate intrinsic rhythm describes failure to output, which can be caused by lead fracture, dislodgement, battery depletion, or a loose connection. Failure to capture would show spikes present with no resulting QRS rather than no spikes at all, undersensing produces inappropriately timed extra spikes rather than none, and cross talk causes inappropriate inhibition from misread signals between leads rather than a total absence of output."},
{id:"P7054",d:"D13",c:"D13.C4",dok:4,q:"A patient with a dual chamber pacemaker develops a sustained paced rhythm at the programmed upper rate limit after a premature ventricular contraction conducts retrogradely to the atrium, is sensed by the atrial lead, and triggers another ventricular paced beat, perpetuating the cycle. What is this phenomenon called and what is an effective way to terminate it?",
 o:["Pacemaker mediated tachycardia, which can be terminated by applying a magnet to temporarily convert the device to an asynchronous mode, interrupting the retrograde tracking loop","Pacemaker Wenckebach, which requires no intervention and resolves spontaneously within seconds","Failure to sense, treated by increasing the sensitivity setting further","Cross talk, treated by decreasing the atrial output energy"],a:0,
 why:"This retrograde conduction re-entry loop is pacemaker mediated tachycardia, and a magnet converts the device to asynchronous pacing, interrupting the atrial sensing that sustains the cycle. Pacemaker Wenckebach is a rate limiting response to sinus tachycardia rather than a retrograde loop and would not reliably self terminate here, increasing sensitivity would make the device more likely to sense the retrograde P wave and could worsen rather than fix the loop, and cross talk is inappropriate sensing between lead outputs, a different mechanism that would not be corrected by lowering atrial output."},
{id:"P7057",d:"D13",c:"D13.C5",dok:2,q:"Which finding is a standard indication for a primary prevention implantable cardioverter defibrillator?",
 o:["Left ventricular ejection fraction of 30 percent or less due to prior myocardial infarction","A single episode of asymptomatic sinus bradycardia","Well controlled hypertension with a normal ejection fraction","A first degree AV block found incidentally on a routine ECG"],a:0,
 why:"A significantly reduced ejection fraction from prior infarction carries an elevated risk of sudden cardiac death, which is the basis for primary prevention defibrillator implantation. Asymptomatic sinus bradycardia is at most a pacing consideration rather than a defibrillator indication, controlled hypertension with normal ejection fraction does not carry the sudden death risk that justifies an ICD, and an isolated first degree AV block is a conduction delay rather than an indication for defibrillator therapy."},
{id:"P7058",d:"D13",c:"D13.C5",dok:2,q:"Which patient is most likely to benefit from cardiac resynchronization therapy with biventricular pacing?",
 o:["A patient with heart failure, an ejection fraction of 30 percent, and a wide QRS complex from left bundle branch block","A patient with heart failure and a narrow, normal QRS complex","A patient with a normal ejection fraction and no heart failure symptoms","A patient with isolated sinus bradycardia and no evidence of heart failure"],a:0,
 why:"CRT benefits patients with reduced ejection fraction and ventricular dyssynchrony, which is reflected by a wide QRS from bundle branch block. A narrow QRS indicates dyssynchrony is not present, a normal ejection fraction without heart failure symptoms does not meet criteria for CRT, and isolated bradycardia without heart failure or dyssynchrony is better addressed with standard single or dual chamber pacing."},
{id:"P7059",d:"D13",c:"D13.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with an ICD receives a shock while resting comfortably with no symptoms beforehand. Device interrogation shows the shock was triggered by intermittent, erratic high frequency signals recorded only on the shocking lead, with a normal underlying rhythm on the surface ECG obtained just before the event. What is the most likely explanation?",
 o:["Lead fracture causing electrical noise that was misinterpreted as a rapid ventricular arrhythmia","An appropriate shock for sustained ventricular fibrillation","Inappropriate shock for rapidly conducted atrial fibrillation","A normal response to strenuous exercise"],a:0,
 why:"Erratic, isolated high frequency noise confined to one lead with a normal rhythm just before the event is characteristic of lead fracture artifact being misread as a dangerous arrhythmia. A normal rhythm before the event argues against a true sustained ventricular arrhythmia, rapidly conducted atrial fibrillation would show an organized or fibrillatory conducted rhythm rather than erratic isolated lead noise, and the patient was resting, so exercise related tachycardia would show a physiologic rhythm rather than erratic noise on one lead."},
{id:"P7060",d:"D13",c:"D13.C5",dok:3,q:"A heart failure patient with a CRT device shows minimal symptomatic improvement at follow up. Device interrogation reveals the percentage of biventricular pacing is only 85 percent due to frequent intrinsic conduction and premature ventricular contractions competing with paced beats. What is the most likely explanation for the lack of benefit?",
 o:["CRT benefit requires a high percentage of biventricular pacing, generally over 90 to 98 percent, so competing intrinsic beats are reducing the resynchronization effect","The device is malfunctioning and needs immediate replacement","The ejection fraction has normalized, so CRT is no longer needed","The lack of improvement is unrelated to pacing percentage and reflects only medication nonadherence"],a:0,
 why:"CRT benefit depends on consistently high biventricular pacing, and competing intrinsic and ectopic beats lowering the percentage to 85 percent reduces the resynchronization effect. An 85 percent pacing rate reflects a programming and rhythm interaction issue to be optimized rather than evidence of hardware malfunction requiring replacement, nothing in the scenario shows the ejection fraction has normalized, and while medication adherence matters, the scenario specifically identifies a known cause tied to pacing percentage that should not be dismissed."},
{id:"P7061",d:"D13",c:"D13.C6",dok:1,q:"Which situation is an appropriate indication for temporary transvenous pacing?",
 o:["Symptomatic bradycardia unresponsive to medications while awaiting permanent pacemaker placement","Asymptomatic sinus bradycardia in a well conditioned athlete","Stable sinus rhythm with no symptoms","A single premature atrial contraction noted on telemetry"],a:0,
 why:"Symptomatic bradycardia that does not respond to medications is an appropriate bridge indication for temporary pacing until a permanent device can be placed. Athletic bradycardia without symptoms is a normal physiologic finding, a stable asymptomatic rhythm does not require pacing support, and an isolated premature atrial contraction is a benign, common finding not associated with hemodynamic compromise."},
{id:"P7062",d:"D13",c:"D13.C6",dok:2,q:"A patient has temporary epicardial pacing wires in place after cardiac surgery. What is an important nursing consideration for these wires?",
 o:["Keep the exposed wire ends insulated and secured, and never allow them to be handled with wet or ungloved hands to prevent microshock","Remove the wires as soon as the patient is out of bed for the first time","Connect both atrial and ventricular wires directly to a 12 lead ECG machine at all times","Leave the wire ends uncovered and exposed to allow visual inspection at any time"],a:0,
 why:"Insulating exposed wire ends and avoiding wet or ungloved handling prevents microshock, since these wires create a direct, low resistance pathway to the heart. Removal timing depends on the surgeon order and the patient rhythm stability rather than simply on ambulation status, epicardial wires connect to a pacing generator or are capped rather than to a 12 lead ECG machine, and leaving the ends uncovered increases rather than decreases the risk of microshock."},
{id:"P7064",d:"D13",c:"D13.C6",dok:2,q:"When applying external defibrillation pads to a patient with an implanted pacemaker or ICD, what is an important consideration for pad placement?",
 o:["Place pads at least one inch away from the device generator, using anterior-posterior placement if possible to avoid damaging the device or reducing shock effectiveness","Place one pad directly over the device generator for the most effective current delivery","Pad placement does not matter since implanted devices are shielded from external current","Only anterolateral placement should ever be used, regardless of device location"],a:0,
 why:"Keeping distance from the generator and considering anterior-posterior placement helps avoid damaging the device and altering the current pathway during defibrillation. Placing a pad directly over the generator can damage the device and reduce shock effectiveness, implanted devices are not fully shielded from external defibrillation current, and rigidly requiring anterolateral placement regardless of device location ignores the reason anterior-posterior placement is often preferred."},
{id:"P7065",d:"D13",c:"D13.C6",dok:3,q:"A magnet is placed over an implanted cardioverter defibrillator, as opposed to a standard permanent pacemaker. What is the expected response?",
 o:["The device suspends tachyarrhythmia detection and shock therapy, but in most devices does not change the underlying pacing mode or rate","The device converts to a fixed asynchronous pacing mode at a fixed rate, identical to the response of a standard permanent pacemaker","The device permanently disables all future shock therapy until reprogrammed by a technician","The device increases its pacing rate to a rapid fixed rate to overdrive suppress arrhythmias"],a:0,
 why:"Magnet application on an ICD typically suspends shock therapy without changing the pacing mode, unlike a standard pacemaker where the magnet does change pacing behavior to an asynchronous mode. Equating the ICD response to the classic pacemaker magnet response is incorrect since the two devices generally behave differently, the suspension of shock therapy is temporary and lasts only while the magnet remains in place rather than being a permanent change requiring reprogramming, and there is no rapid overdrive pacing response triggered by magnet application in ICDs."},
{id:"P7063",d:"D13",c:"D13.C6",dok:4,q:"A pacemaker dependent patient with an ICD is scheduled for abdominal surgery requiring electrocautery. Which combination of perioperative actions best protects the patient?",
 o:["Suspend the ICD tachyarrhythmia therapies before surgery with a magnet or reprogramming, reprogram the pacemaker to an asynchronous mode if pacemaker dependent, use bipolar cautery in short bursts if possible, and have external defibrillation equipment immediately available","Leave all device settings unchanged since electrocautery has no effect on implanted devices","Remove the device entirely before any surgery involving electrocautery","Apply a magnet only after surgery is complete, with no other precautions needed"],a:0,
 why:"Electrocautery can cause oversensing that leads to inappropriate inhibition of pacing in a dependent patient or inappropriate ICD shocks, so suspending shock therapy, ensuring asynchronous pacing if needed, using bipolar cautery in short bursts, and having defibrillation equipment ready together provide comprehensive protection. Leaving settings unchanged ignores a well documented electrocautery risk, removing the device is unnecessary and would leave the patient without needed pacing or defibrillation support, and applying the magnet only after surgery defeats its protective purpose since the risk is present during the procedure."},
{id:"P4001",d:"D14",c:"D14.C1",dok:1,q:"Which laboratory test is used to monitor the therapeutic effect of an unfractionated heparin infusion?",
 o:["aPTT","INR","platelet count","fibrinogen"],a:0,
 why:"aPTT reflects intrinsic pathway inhibition by unfractionated heparin and is used to titrate the infusion to a therapeutic range. INR monitors vitamin K antagonist therapy such as warfarin, not heparin. Platelet count is trended to screen for heparin induced thrombocytopenia but does not measure anticoagulant effect. Fibrinogen is relevant to thrombolytic therapy monitoring, not heparin dosing."},
{id:"P4002",d:"D14",c:"D14.C1",dok:2,q:"A patient receiving enoxaparin for DVT treatment has a creatinine clearance of 25 mL per minute. What action should the nurse anticipate?",
 o:["No adjustment needed since enoxaparin is not renally cleared","Dose reduction of enoxaparin due to renal clearance","Switch to a higher dose because renal impairment reduces bioavailability","Discontinue and start aspirin instead"],a:1,
 why:"Enoxaparin is renally cleared and accumulates in severe renal impairment, raising bleeding risk, so dose reduction or an alternative agent is anticipated when clearance falls below 30 mL per minute. It is incorrect to assume no adjustment is needed because clearance of enoxaparin depends heavily on the kidneys. Increasing the dose would compound the accumulation risk rather than correct it. Aspirin is an antiplatelet, not an equivalent anticoagulant for DVT treatment, so substituting it is not appropriate."},
{id:"P4003",d:"D14",c:"D14.C1",dok:2,q:"Which reversal agent is specifically indicated for life threatening bleeding in a patient taking apixaban?",
 o:["Protamine sulfate","Vitamin K","Andexanet alfa","Idarucizumab"],a:2,
 why:"Andexanet alfa is a factor Xa decoy protein approved to reverse apixaban and rivaroxaban in serious bleeding. Protamine sulfate reverses heparin and partially reverses enoxaparin, not factor Xa inhibitors. Vitamin K reverses warfarin by restoring vitamin K dependent clotting factor synthesis. Idarucizumab is a monoclonal antibody fragment specific to dabigatran, a direct thrombin inhibitor."},
{id:"P4004",d:"D14",c:"D14.C1",dok:2,q:"Protamine sulfate is administered for reversal of which anticoagulant?",
 o:["Warfarin","Apixaban","Clopidogrel","Unfractionated heparin"],a:3,
 why:"Protamine sulfate is a positively charged protein that binds and neutralizes negatively charged heparin, making it the appropriate reversal agent. Warfarin reversal requires vitamin K and, for urgent situations, clotting factor replacement, not protamine. Apixaban is a direct factor Xa inhibitor reversed by andexanet alfa rather than protamine. Clopidogrel is an antiplatelet agent with no direct pharmacologic reversal agent in routine use."},
{id:"P4005",d:"D14",c:"D14.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient is prescribed dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin and ticagrelor after placement of a drug eluting coronary stent. What is the primary purpose of continuing both agents rather than aspirin alone?",
 o:["Reduce the risk of stent thrombosis during endothelialization","Prevent recurrence of the original coronary plaque rupture only","Lower blood pressure to protect the stented vessel","Replace the need for long term statin therapy"],a:0,
 why:"Until the stent struts endothelialize, the exposed metal surface is thrombogenic, and dual antiplatelet therapy with two different mechanisms reduces the risk of stent thrombosis more effectively than aspirin alone. It is not primarily aimed at preventing new plaque rupture elsewhere, though that is a secondary benefit of antiplatelet therapy generally. Antiplatelet agents do not lower blood pressure. Statin therapy addresses lipid management and plaque stability and is not interchangeable with antiplatelet therapy."},
{id:"P4006",d:"D14",c:"D14.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient on clopidogrel for a coronary stent placed three months ago is scheduled for urgent non cardiac surgery in two days. The surgical team asks the nurse to clarify the bleeding risk of proceeding without stopping the drug. Which response best reflects appropriate reasoning?",
 o:["Clopidogrel should be stopped immediately since surgical bleeding risk always outweighs stent risk","Stopping clopidogrel this early after stent placement risks acute stent thrombosis and should be discussed with cardiology before surgery proceeds","Clopidogrel has no effect on platelet function after three months, so surgery can proceed without concern","Only aspirin needs to be held before surgery, clopidogrel can be continued freely"],a:1,
 why:"Discontinuing dual antiplatelet therapy within the recommended minimum duration after a drug eluting stent significantly raises the risk of stent thrombosis, so the decision requires a shared risk discussion between surgery and cardiology rather than a unilateral stop. Automatically stopping clopidogrel ignores the serious cardiac risk of premature discontinuation. Clopidogrel remains pharmacologically active on platelets for the duration of therapy and does not simply lose effect at three months. Holding aspirin alone while continuing clopidogrel does not resolve the core question of whether dual therapy interruption is safe at this stage."},
{id:"P4007",d:"D14",c:"D14.C1",dok:3,q:"A patient with a mechanical mitral valve on long term warfarin reports missing several doses over the past week due to travel and now has an INR of 1.3. Which nursing analysis is most appropriate?",
 o:["An INR of 1.3 is within the safe range for a mechanical valve and no action is needed","The low INR indicates warfarin resistance requiring a switch to a direct oral anticoagulant","The subtherapeutic INR places the patient at increased risk for valve thrombosis and requires prompt provider notification for dose correction","The patient should be told to double the next dose to catch up"],a:2,
 why:"Mechanical heart valves require a higher therapeutic INR range than atrial fibrillation, so an INR of 1.3 is significantly subtherapeutic and raises the risk of thrombus formation on the valve, warranting prompt provider notification. This value is not within a safe range for a mechanical valve. A single low reading after missed doses reflects nonadherence, not true pharmacologic resistance, and direct oral anticoagulants are not approved for mechanical valves. Instructing a patient to double a dose without provider guidance risks an unsafe rebound in anticoagulation and is not within independent nursing action."},
{id:"P4008",d:"D14",c:"D14.C1",dok:4,q:"A patient on chronic warfarin therapy for atrial fibrillation is admitted with a urinary tract infection and started on trimethoprim sulfamethoxazole. Three days later the patient reports gum bleeding and bruising, and the INR is 5.8. The patient also reports reduced oral intake due to nausea for the past four days. Which combination of factors best explains this presentation and directs the priority nursing action?",
 o:["The elevated INR is solely due to the urinary tract infection itself and warfarin should be continued at the same dose","The gum bleeding is unrelated to the INR and reflects a dental issue requiring only a dental referral","Trimethoprim sulfamethoxazole has no known interaction with warfarin, so the INR rise must be a laboratory error","Both the antibiotic interaction and reduced dietary vitamin K intake have potentiated the effect of warfarin, requiring the provider be notified to hold warfarin and assess for bleeding"],a:3,
 why:"Trimethoprim sulfamethoxazole inhibits warfarin metabolism and displaces it from protein binding, and reduced oral intake lowers dietary vitamin K, both of which push the INR higher and explain the bleeding signs, so the priority is provider notification, holding the dose, and a bleeding assessment. Infection alone does not typically raise INR to this degree without a contributing drug or nutritional factor, and continuing the same dose would worsen an already dangerous supratherapeutic state. Gum bleeding in the setting of a markedly elevated INR is a systemic bleeding sign, not an isolated dental problem, so dismissing it delays needed intervention. Trimethoprim sulfamethoxazole is a well documented enzyme inhibitor that significantly potentiates warfarin, so attributing the result to lab error ignores a known and dangerous interaction."},
{id:"P4009",d:"D14",c:"D14.C2",dok:1,q:"Amiodarone is classified as which type of antiarrhythmic agent?",
 o:["Class III","Class I","Class II","Class IV"],a:0,
 why:"Amiodarone is classified as a Class III antiarrhythmic because its primary action is potassium channel blockade that prolongs repolarization, though it also has properties of the other classes. Class I agents work primarily through sodium channel blockade, such as lidocaine and procainamide. Class II agents are beta blockers that slow conduction through the AV node. Class IV agents are calcium channel blockers such as diltiazem and verapamil."},
{id:"P4010",d:"D14",c:"D14.C2",dok:2,q:"When administering IV adenosine for a stable narrow complex tachycardia, which administration technique is correct?",
 o:["Slow infusion over 30 minutes through a peripheral line","Rapid IV push followed immediately by a rapid saline flush","Rapid push through a central line only, never peripheral","Administration diluted in 100 mL over 10 minutes"],a:1,
 why:"Adenosine has an extremely short half life of only a few seconds, so it must be given as a rapid IV push immediately followed by a rapid saline flush to ensure the drug reaches the heart before it is metabolized. A slow infusion would allow the drug to be broken down before reaching systemic circulation in an effective concentration. It can be given through a peripheral line as long as it is proximal and followed by a rapid flush, so a central line is not a requirement. Diluting the dose and infusing it slowly defeats the purpose of achieving a high, rapid bolus concentration needed for effect."},
{id:"P4011",d:"D14",c:"D14.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient is started on oral amiodarone for recurrent atrial fibrillation. Which baseline studies should the nurse anticipate before therapy begins?",
 o:["Renal function tests and a fasting lipid panel only","A bone density scan and hemoglobin A1c","Thyroid function tests, liver function tests, and a chest x-ray or pulmonary function tests","Serum potassium only, since amiodarone has no other organ toxicity"],a:2,
 why:"Amiodarone contains iodine and can cause thyroid dysfunction, hepatotoxicity, and pulmonary fibrosis, so baseline thyroid function, liver function, and a pulmonary assessment are needed before starting therapy. Renal function and lipid panels are not the primary organs affected by amiodarone toxicity. Bone density and hemoglobin A1c are unrelated to the known toxicity profile of amiodarone. While electrolyte status matters for arrhythmia management generally, amiodarone has significant thyroid, liver, and pulmonary toxicity risks beyond potassium alone."},
{id:"P4012",d:"D14",c:"D14.C2",dok:2,q:"A patient stabilized on digoxin for atrial fibrillation is started on amiodarone for rhythm control. Which nursing consideration is most important?",
 o:["Amiodarone has no interaction with digoxin and no dose change is needed","Amiodarone will require an increased digoxin dose to maintain therapeutic effect","The interaction only matters if the patient also has renal impairment","Amiodarone inhibits digoxin clearance and typically requires a digoxin dose reduction to prevent toxicity"],a:3,
 why:"Amiodarone inhibits transport proteins and reduces renal and biliary clearance of digoxin, commonly raising digoxin levels, so a dose reduction of digoxin is typically anticipated when amiodarone is added. Assuming no interaction exists ignores a well documented and clinically significant drug interaction. Because amiodarone raises digoxin levels rather than lowering them, an increased digoxin dose would worsen toxicity risk rather than maintain safe therapeutic effect. While renal impairment further raises digoxin levels, the amiodarone interaction itself is significant regardless of baseline renal function."},
{id:"P4013",d:"D14",c:"D14.C2",dok:2,q:"Lidocaine is primarily indicated for treatment of which type of arrhythmia?",
 o:["Ventricular arrhythmias","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus bradycardia","Supraventricular tachycardia"],a:0,
 why:"Lidocaine is a Class I sodium channel blocker used primarily for ventricular arrhythmias such as ventricular tachycardia, particularly when amiodarone is unavailable or contraindicated. It is not a first line agent for atrial fibrillation, which is typically managed with rate or rhythm control agents targeting atrial and AV nodal tissue. Lidocaine does not treat bradycardia and has no role in increasing heart rate. It is not used for supraventricular tachycardia, which responds to agents like adenosine or AV nodal blockers."},
{id:"P4014",d:"D14",c:"D14.C2",dok:3,q:"A patient with a creatinine clearance of 35 mL per minute is prescribed sotalol for atrial fibrillation. Which nursing analysis best reflects the priority concern?",
 o:["Sotalol is not renally cleared, so no adjustment is necessary","Renal impairment reduces sotalol clearance, increasing the risk of QT prolongation and torsades de pointes, so dose adjustment and continuous cardiac monitoring are needed","The renal impairment primarily increases the risk of hepatotoxicity from sotalol","Sotalol should be given at a higher dose to compensate for reduced absorption in renal impairment"],a:1,
 why:"Sotalol is renally eliminated and accumulates in renal impairment, which raises the risk of excessive QT prolongation and the potentially fatal arrhythmia torsades de pointes, so dose reduction and continuous monitoring during initiation are essential safety measures. Clearance of sotalol is, in fact, significantly dependent on the kidneys, so assuming no adjustment is needed is incorrect. The major organ toxicity concern with sotalol is cardiac, specifically proarrhythmia, not hepatotoxicity. Increasing the dose in the setting of reduced clearance would worsen drug accumulation and raise the risk of dangerous arrhythmia rather than compensate for any absorption issue, since the concern is elimination, not absorption."},
{id:"P4015",d:"D14",c:"D14.C3",dok:1,q:"Which vasopressor is recommended as first line therapy for septic shock according to standard practice?",
 o:["Phenylephrine","Dopamine","Norepinephrine","Vasopressin"],a:2,
 why:"Norepinephrine is the recommended first line vasopressor for septic shock because it provides potent alpha-1 mediated vasoconstriction with modest beta-1 effect, raising blood pressure with a favorable arrhythmia risk profile compared to alternatives. Phenylephrine is typically reserved as a second line or adjunct option because its pure alpha effect can reflexively lower heart rate and cardiac output. Dopamine is associated with a higher risk of arrhythmias and is no longer recommended first line. Vasopressin is generally added as a second agent to reduce norepinephrine dose requirements, not used as initial therapy."},
{id:"P4021",d:"D14",c:"D14.C3",dok:1,q:"Vasoactive infusions such as norepinephrine and vasopressin are preferably administered through which type of access?",
 o:["A central venous line","A short peripheral IV in the hand","A peripherally inserted midline catheter","A subcutaneous port"],a:0,
 why:"Central venous access is preferred for vasoactive infusions because these medications cause potent vasoconstriction and can cause severe tissue necrosis if they infiltrate a peripheral vein. A short peripheral IV in the hand carries a high risk of infiltration and tissue injury given the small, fragile veins in that location. A midline catheter is not intended for vasoactive infusions and does not eliminate the extravasation risk associated with these potent vasoconstrictors. Subcutaneous administration is not an appropriate route for vasoactive infusions, which require direct intravenous delivery into central circulation."},
{id:"P4016",d:"D14",c:"D14.C3",dok:2,q:"A norepinephrine infusion infiltrates into the surrounding tissue at the peripheral IV site. Which medication should the nurse anticipate administering to prevent tissue necrosis?",
 o:["Protamine sulfate","Naloxone","Flumazenil","Phentolamine"],a:3,
 why:"Phentolamine is an alpha adrenergic antagonist that is injected locally into the infiltrated area to counteract the intense vasoconstriction caused by extravasated norepinephrine and prevent tissue necrosis. Protamine sulfate reverses heparin and has no role in vasopressor extravasation. Naloxone reverses opioid effects and is unrelated to catecholamine extravasation injury. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine sedation and has no antidote role for vasopressor infiltration."},
{id:"P4018",d:"D14",c:"D14.C3",dok:2,q:"Milrinone is being considered for a patient with acute decompensated heart failure. Which mechanism explains why milrinone can cause significant hypotension?",
 o:["It is a pure alpha-1 agonist that causes reflex bradycardia","It is a phosphodiesterase-3 inhibitor that causes both inotropy and peripheral vasodilation","It works only on beta-1 receptors with no vascular effect","It blocks calcium channels in vascular smooth muscle exclusively"],a:1,
 why:"Milrinone inhibits phosphodiesterase-3, increasing intracellular cyclic AMP in both cardiac and vascular smooth muscle, which produces increased contractility along with significant peripheral vasodilation, and this vasodilation is what commonly causes hypotension. It is not an alpha-1 agonist, which would cause vasoconstriction rather than vasodilation. It does not act solely on beta-1 receptors, since its vascular effects are central to both its benefit and its hypotension risk. It is not a direct calcium channel blocker, even though its downstream cellular effects influence calcium handling."},
{id:"P4019",d:"D14",c:"D14.C3",dok:2,q:"Vasopressin is added as a second agent in a patient already on a high dose norepinephrine infusion for septic shock. Which statement about vasopressin dosing is correct?",
 o:["Vasopressin should be titrated upward rapidly to replace norepinephrine entirely","Vasopressin has no role once norepinephrine is already at a high dose","Vasopressin is typically given at a fixed low dose rather than titrated like catecholamine vasopressors","Vasopressin is dosed based on the same weight based scale as norepinephrine"],a:2,
 why:"Vasopressin is typically administered at a fixed low dose as an adjunct to catecholamine vasopressors because higher doses do not improve outcomes and increase the risk of ischemic complications, so it is not titrated in the same way as norepinephrine. It is added to allow the norepinephrine dose to be reduced, not to be rapidly escalated as a replacement. Adding vasopressin when catecholamine doses are rising is a recognized strategy to improve blood pressure while limiting catecholamine related harm, so it does have a role at that point. It is not dosed on a weight based titration scale the way norepinephrine is."},
{id:"P4017",d:"D14",c:"D14.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient in septic shock is receiving norepinephrine titrated to a mean arterial pressure goal. The blood pressure has been at goal for two hours, and the provider orders the nurse to begin weaning the infusion. Which finding would most indicate the wean should be paused?",
 o:["Mean arterial pressure drops below 65 mmHg with the first dose reduction","Heart rate remains stable at 82 beats per minute after the first dose reduction","Urine output has been consistently above 0.5 mL per kilogram per hour","The patient remains alert and oriented after the dose reduction"],a:0,
 why:"A drop in mean arterial pressure below the target of 65 mmHg with a dose reduction indicates the patient cannot yet tolerate weaning and the infusion should be increased back or the wean paused to maintain adequate organ perfusion. A stable heart rate after a dose reduction is a reassuring sign, not a reason to pause. Adequate urine output reflects preserved renal perfusion and supports continuing the wean rather than stopping it. Maintained alertness is also a reassuring perfusion indicator and does not indicate a need to pause weaning."},
{id:"P4020",d:"D14",c:"D14.C3",dok:3,q:"A patient with new onset atrial fibrillation and a rapid ventricular response is started on a dobutamine infusion for cardiogenic shock. Twenty minutes later the heart rate rises from 110 to 160 beats per minute with worsening irregularity. Which analysis best explains this change?",
 o:["The heart rate change is unrelated to dobutamine and reflects disease progression alone","Dobutamine has no chronotropic effect and the rhythm change must be due to another cause","The rising heart rate indicates the dobutamine dose is too low and should be increased","The beta-1 stimulating effect of dobutamine can increase myocardial irritability and exacerbate atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response"],a:3,
 why:"Dobutamine stimulates beta-1 receptors, which increases heart rate and myocardial irritability and can worsen the ventricular response in atrial fibrillation, making the medication a likely contributor to this change. Dismissing the temporal relationship between starting the infusion and the rhythm change overlooks a well known drug effect. Dobutamine does have a significant chronotropic effect, so claiming it has none is inaccurate. Increasing the dose in this situation would further stimulate beta-1 receptors and likely worsen the tachyarrhythmia rather than help."},
{id:"P4022",d:"D14",c:"D14.C3",dok:4,q:"A patient in cardiogenic shock is on norepinephrine at 20 mcg per minute and the dose has been increasing over the past three hours to maintain a mean arterial pressure of 65 mmHg. Lactate has risen from 2.4 to 5.1 mmol per L, urine output has fallen to 15 mL over the last two hours, and extremities are now cool and mottled to the knees. Which combination of actions best reflects the priority nursing response?",
 o:["Continue current management and reassess in four hours since the mean arterial pressure goal is currently being met","Notify the provider immediately to discuss escalation of shock management, since rising lactate, falling urine output, and worsening perfusion despite an already high and rising vasopressor dose indicate inadequate tissue perfusion","Independently add a second vasopressor without provider notification since the trend is already clear","Attribute the mottling solely to the norepinephrine infusion site and reposition the IV"],a:1,
 why:"A rising lactate, falling urine output, and worsening peripheral perfusion despite an escalating vasopressor dose and an achieved blood pressure target together indicate that macrocirculatory pressure is being maintained while tissue perfusion is failing, a pattern that requires prompt provider notification to consider additional interventions such as another agent or source control. Continuing without escalation ignores multiple converging signs of worsening shock and delays needed care. Adding a second vasopressor is a medical decision requiring a provider order except in specific protocol driven situations, so acting independently outside of an established protocol is not appropriate. Mottling in shock reflects systemic vasoconstriction and poor perfusion, not a local IV site issue, so attributing it to the infusion site misses the systemic significance of the finding."},
{id:"P4023",d:"D14",c:"D14.C4",dok:1,q:"A patient reports a burning or tingling sensation under the tongue after taking sublingual nitroglycerin for chest pain. How should the nurse interpret this finding?",
 o:["It indicates an allergic reaction requiring immediate discontinuation","It is a sign of overdose requiring naloxone","It is an expected sign that the tablet is potent and being absorbed","It indicates the tablet should be swallowed instead of dissolved"],a:2,
 why:"A burning or tingling sensation under the tongue is an expected finding with sublingual nitroglycerin and is often used as an indicator that the tablet is still potent and being absorbed properly. It is not an allergic reaction and does not require the medication to be stopped. Naloxone reverses opioids, not nitrate effects, and nitroglycerin overdose presents with hypotension and headache rather than a picture treated with naloxone. Sublingual tablets are specifically designed to dissolve under the tongue for rapid absorption and should not be swallowed."},
{id:"P4024",d:"D14",c:"D14.C4",dok:2,q:"A patient on a continuous IV nitroglycerin infusion for unstable angina has required progressively higher doses over 24 hours to control chest pain. What is the most likely explanation?",
 o:["The patient has developed an allergy that reduces drug effectiveness","The infusion pump is underdosing due to a calibration error","This pattern indicates improvement in the underlying coronary disease","Development of nitrate tolerance with continuous infusion"],a:3,
 why:"Continuous nitroglycerin infusions commonly lead to nitrate tolerance within 24 hours, requiring escalating doses to achieve the same effect, which is why intermittent nitrate free intervals are used when feasible with other formulations. An allergy would not present as gradually reduced effectiveness requiring dose escalation but rather as a hypersensitivity reaction. While pump malfunction is possible, it is a far less likely and less clinically expected explanation than known pharmacologic tolerance. Needing higher doses to control ongoing chest pain is inconsistent with improvement or resolution of angina."},
{id:"P4026",d:"D14",c:"D14.C4",dok:2,q:"Verapamil is being considered for rate control in a patient with atrial fibrillation who also has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. Which concern should the nurse raise?",
 o:["Verapamil has no effect on cardiac contractility and is safe in any degree of heart failure","Verapamil has a strong negative inotropic effect that can worsen heart failure symptoms","Verapamil is the preferred agent specifically because it improves ejection fraction","Verapamil only affects blood pressure and has no relevance to heart failure management"],a:1,
 why:"Verapamil is a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker with a pronounced negative inotropic effect, which can worsen symptoms and outcomes in patients with reduced ejection fraction heart failure, making it a medication to use cautiously or avoid in this population. It does have a clinically significant effect on contractility, so stating it has none is inaccurate. It is not preferred in reduced ejection fraction heart failure and does not improve ejection fraction, since other rate control agents or beta blockers with proven benefit are generally favored. Its relevance extends beyond blood pressure because its effect on cardiac contractility directly impacts heart failure status."},
{id:"P4025",d:"D14",c:"D14.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient started on lisinopril for hypertension develops a persistent dry cough after several weeks. Which explanation and action best reflects appropriate clinical reasoning?",
 o:["The cough is likely due to bradykinin accumulation from ACE inhibition, and switching to an angiotensin receptor blocker is a reasonable option","The cough indicates pulmonary edema and diuretic dosing should be increased instead","ACE inhibitors do not cause cough, so an alternate cause unrelated to the medication should be investigated exclusively","The cough will resolve on its own without any change in therapy and requires no follow-up"],a:0,
 why:"ACE inhibitors block the breakdown of bradykinin, and bradykinin accumulation in the airways is the recognized mechanism behind the characteristic dry cough, so switching to an angiotensin receptor blocker, which does not significantly affect bradykinin, is a reasonable strategy. A new dry cough without other signs such as dyspnea or crackles is not the typical presentation of pulmonary edema, so increasing diuretic dosing does not address the actual cause. ACE inhibitor cough is a well documented and common adverse effect, so dismissing the medication as a possible cause ignores established pharmacology. Because the cough is a known drug effect that resolves upon discontinuation, providing no follow-up plan misses an opportunity to manage a bothersome and medication related symptom."},
{id:"P4027",d:"D14",c:"D14.C4",dok:3,q:"A patient with confirmed variant Prinzmetal angina caused by coronary artery vasospasm is being considered for a new antianginal medication. Which class of medication should be avoided or used with caution due to the potential to worsen vasospasm?",
 o:["Calcium channel blockers","Long acting nitrates","Nonselective beta blockers","Statins"],a:2,
 why:"Nonselective beta blockers can leave alpha adrenergic vasoconstrictor activity unopposed, potentially worsening coronary vasospasm in Prinzmetal angina, so they are used cautiously or avoided in this condition. Calcium channel blockers are actually a mainstay treatment for vasospastic angina because they directly relax coronary smooth muscle. Long acting nitrates are also commonly used to reduce vasospasm and are not the concerning class here. Statins address atherosclerotic risk and lipid management and have no direct link to worsening coronary vasospasm."},
{id:"P4028",d:"D14",c:"D14.C5",dok:1,q:"Which class of medication has been shown to reduce mortality in patients with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction and is part of standard guideline directed therapy?",
 o:["Short acting nitrates","Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers","NSAIDs","Beta blockers"],a:3,
 why:"Evidence based beta blockers such as carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, and bisoprolol have been shown to reduce mortality in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction and are a cornerstone of guideline directed medical therapy. Short acting nitrates provide symptomatic relief but are not part of the mortality reducing foundational therapy. Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as verapamil and diltiazem are generally avoided in reduced ejection fraction heart failure due to negative inotropic effects. NSAIDs are avoided in heart failure because they promote sodium and fluid retention and can worsen the condition."},
{id:"P4029",d:"D14",c:"D14.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient currently taking an ACE inhibitor for heart failure is being transitioned to sacubitril valsartan. What safety step must occur before starting the new medication?",
 o:["A washout period of at least 36 hours after the last ACE inhibitor dose to reduce angioedema risk","No washout period is needed since the two drugs can be given together","The ACE inhibitor should be doubled first to ease the transition","A 36 hour washout is required only if the patient has a history of diabetes"],a:0,
 why:"Combining an ACE inhibitor with sacubitril valsartan significantly raises the risk of life threatening angioedema due to combined effects on bradykinin, so a washout period of at least 36 hours is required between stopping the ACE inhibitor and starting the new medication. Giving them together defeats the purpose of the washout and increases angioedema risk rather than easing transition. Doubling the ACE inhibitor dose before stopping it has no role in this transition and would increase adverse effect risk. The washout requirement is based on the pharmacologic interaction itself and applies regardless of diabetes history."},
{id:"P4031",d:"D14",c:"D14.C5",dok:2,q:"A patient with heart failure is started on spironolactone in addition to an ACE inhibitor. Which laboratory value requires close monitoring due to the combined effect of these two medications?",
 o:["Sodium","Calcium","Potassium","Glucose"],a:2,
 why:"Both spironolactone, a potassium sparing diuretic, and ACE inhibitors reduce potassium excretion, so combining them significantly raises the risk of hyperkalemia and requires close potassium monitoring. Sodium is monitored in heart failure generally but is not the specific concern created by combining these two medications. Calcium is not significantly affected by either medication. Glucose is not a primary concern with this drug combination, unlike with corticosteroids or certain diuretics affecting glucose metabolism."},
{id:"P4030",d:"D14",c:"D14.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient admitted with acutely decompensated heart failure and significant volume overload is not currently on a beta blocker. The nurse notes an order to start a beta blocker today. Which analysis best reflects appropriate clinical reasoning?",
 o:["Beta blockers should always be started immediately on admission regardless of volume status","Beta blockers should generally be held until the patient is euvolemic and hemodynamically stable, since starting during acute decompensation can worsen symptoms","Beta blockers have no effect on volume status or hemodynamics and can be started at any time","The order should be carried out immediately since delaying beta blocker therapy always worsens long term outcomes"],a:1,
 why:"Initiating or increasing a beta blocker during an episode of acute decompensation with significant volume overload can further reduce contractility and worsen symptoms, so therapy is generally held until the patient is stabilized and near euvolemic before starting or resuming this class of medication. Starting immediately regardless of volume status ignores the risk of acutely worsening a decompensated state. Beta blockers directly affect heart rate and contractility, so claiming no hemodynamic effect is inaccurate. While beta blockers do improve long term outcomes once appropriately initiated, giving them during unstable acute decompensation is not the recommended timing and can cause short term harm."},
{id:"P4032",d:"D14",c:"D14.C5",dok:3,q:"A patient with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction and no history of diabetes is started on an SGLT2 inhibitor as part of guideline directed therapy. Which nursing consideration is most important for patient teaching?",
 o:["Explaining that this medication is only used for glucose control and has no cardiac benefit","Reassuring the patient that no monitoring is needed since the drug is only for non-diabetic patients","Instructing the patient that hypoglycemia is the primary risk to watch for","Educating about genitourinary infection risk and signs of euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis, which can occur even without diabetes"],a:3,
 why:"SGLT2 inhibitors are now used in heart failure regardless of diabetes status because of proven cardiovascular benefit, but they carry a risk of genital and urinary infections and the risk of euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis, a condition where ketoacidosis occurs with a normal or only mildly elevated glucose, so patient education on these signs is essential. The medication does have a proven cardiac benefit independent of glucose control, so describing it as only for glucose control is inaccurate. Because of the infection and ketoacidosis risks, ongoing monitoring and teaching are needed rather than no monitoring at all. Hypoglycemia is not the primary concern with SGLT2 inhibitors when used alone, especially in a non-diabetic patient, since these agents cause glucose loss through urine rather than directly lowering blood glucose to dangerous levels in this population."},
{id:"P4033",d:"D14",c:"D14.C6",dok:2,q:"A patient on statin therapy reports new muscle pain and weakness. Which laboratory value should the nurse anticipate being drawn to evaluate for statin induced myopathy?",
 o:["Creatine kinase","Troponin","D-dimer","Amylase"],a:0,
 why:"Creatine kinase is the laboratory marker used to evaluate for statin induced myopathy or rhabdomyolysis when a patient reports new muscle pain or weakness. Troponin is a marker of myocardial injury and is not used to assess skeletal muscle toxicity from statins. D-dimer is used to evaluate for thromboembolic conditions and has no role in evaluating statin related muscle symptoms. Amylase is used to evaluate pancreatic conditions, not muscle injury from statins."},
{id:"P4034",d:"D14",c:"D14.C6",dok:2,q:"A patient stable on simvastatin is prescribed clarithromycin for a respiratory infection. What is the primary concern with this combination?",
 o:["Clarithromycin has no interaction with statins of any kind","Clarithromycin inhibits the enzyme that metabolizes simvastatin, increasing the risk of myopathy and rhabdomyolysis","The combination increases the risk of hyperkalemia","Clarithromycin reduces statin absorption, making the statin less effective"],a:1,
 why:"Clarithromycin inhibits the enzyme responsible for metabolizing simvastatin, which can cause statin levels to rise significantly and increases the risk of myopathy and rhabdomyolysis. This is a well documented, clinically important interaction, so assuming no interaction exists is incorrect. The concern with this combination is muscle toxicity, not hyperkalemia, which is not a recognized effect of this interaction. The interaction increases statin levels through reduced metabolism rather than reducing absorption or effectiveness."},
{id:"P4036",d:"D14",c:"D14.C6",dok:2,q:"A patient remains with an LDL cholesterol above goal despite maximally tolerated statin therapy. The provider adds evolocumab. What is the mechanism of action of this medication?",
 o:["It blocks cholesterol absorption in the small intestine","It inhibits HMG-CoA reductase in the liver","It binds bile acids in the intestine to increase cholesterol excretion","It inhibits PCSK9, increasing the number of LDL receptors available to clear LDL from the blood"],a:3,
 why:"Evolocumab is a PCSK9 inhibitor that prevents degradation of LDL receptors, allowing more receptors to remain on the liver cell surface to clear LDL cholesterol from the bloodstream, making it useful as an add-on when statins alone are insufficient. Blocking intestinal cholesterol absorption describes the mechanism of ezetimibe, not evolocumab. Inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase describes the mechanism of statins themselves, which the patient is already taking. Binding bile acids in the intestine describes the mechanism of bile acid sequestrants, a different drug class entirely."},
{id:"P4035",d:"D14",c:"D14.C6",dok:3,q:"A patient is admitted following an acute myocardial infarction. Baseline LDL cholesterol is only mildly elevated at 110 mg per dL. Which statin approach reflects current evidence based practice?",
 o:["Withhold statin therapy since the LDL is only mildly elevated","Start only a low intensity statin since the LDL is close to goal already","Start a high intensity statin regardless of the baseline LDL level, since post-MI benefit is tied to plaque stabilization, not just LDL lowering","Delay statin initiation until an outpatient follow-up visit in one month"],a:2,
 why:"Current evidence supports starting a high intensity statin in essentially all patients after an acute myocardial infarction regardless of the baseline LDL level, because the benefit relates to plaque stabilization and reduced cardiovascular events, not simply to the degree of LDL elevation. Withholding therapy because the LDL is only mildly elevated ignores the plaque stabilizing and anti-inflammatory benefits independent of the starting LDL value. Using only a low intensity statin does not provide the more robust risk reduction demonstrated with high intensity therapy in this population. Delaying initiation to an outpatient visit misses the early benefit of starting therapy during the acute hospitalization, which is the recommended timing."},
{id:"P4037",d:"D14",c:"D14.C7",dok:2,q:"Which practice is a core safety strategy for reducing dosing errors with high alert medications such as heparin and insulin infusions?",
 o:["Independent double-check of the dose, pump settings, and calculation by a second qualified nurse","Relying on the calculation of a single nurse to save time in urgent situations","Programming the infusion pump without confirming the order first","Allowing verbal orders for high alert medication rate changes without written confirmation"],a:0,
 why:"Independent double-checks by a second qualified nurse for the dose, pump programming, and calculation are a core safety strategy shown to reduce errors with high alert medications, which have a narrow margin of safety and high potential for harm if dosed incorrectly. Relying on a single nurse calculation removes a critical safety redundancy specifically designed to catch errors before they reach the patient. Programming a pump without confirming the order first bypasses a fundamental safety check and risks administering an incorrect dose. Accepting verbal orders for rate changes without written or electronic confirmation increases the risk of miscommunication with high alert medications."},
{id:"P4038",d:"D14",c:"D14.C7",dok:3,q:"A weight based heparin infusion protocol is used for a patient admitted with a pulmonary embolism. The nurse notices the ordered bolus dose does not match the calculated dose based on the documented weight of the patient. Which action best reflects appropriate safety reasoning?",
 o:["Administer the ordered dose as written since it came from the provider","Hold the bolus and clarify the discrepancy with the ordering provider before administering","Adjust the dose independently to match the protocol without notifying the provider","Administer half the ordered dose as a compromise between the two values"],a:1,
 why:"Weight based heparin protocols exist specifically to standardize dosing and reduce error, so when the ordered dose does not match the calculated protocol dose, the nurse should hold the medication and clarify with the provider before proceeding, since heparin is a high alert medication with serious bleeding risk if dosed incorrectly. Administering the dose as written despite a known discrepancy bypasses an important safety check meant to catch exactly this kind of error. Independently changing the dose without provider communication oversteps the role of the nurse in medication ordering, even when following a protocol, because the discrepancy needs to be resolved with the prescriber. Splitting the difference between two uncertain values is not a safe or evidence based way to resolve a dosing discrepancy."},
{id:"P4039",d:"D14",c:"D14.C7",dok:3,q:"A patient on an insulin drip for diabetic ketoacidosis has a blood glucose that drops from 210 to 68 mg per dL within one hour, faster than expected based on the titration protocol. The patient is diaphoretic and shaky. Which nursing analysis and action best reflects safe practice?",
 o:["Continue the current insulin rate since the glucose is trending toward a normal range","Increase the insulin rate slightly to smooth out the correction curve","Recognize this as a hypoglycemic event requiring the insulin infusion to be paused and treatment per hypoglycemia protocol initiated immediately","Wait until the next scheduled glucose check in one hour to reassess"],a:2,
 why:"A rapid drop in glucose to 68 mg per dL with diaphoresis and shakiness indicates symptomatic hypoglycemia, a dangerous complication of insulin infusions that requires the infusion to be paused and hypoglycemia treatment initiated right away per protocol. Continuing the current rate would allow the glucose to fall further and worsen a symptomatic hypoglycemic episode. Increasing the insulin rate would be directly dangerous and worsen the hypoglycemia. Waiting a full hour for the next scheduled check ignores the current symptomatic presentation of the patient and delays urgently needed treatment."},
{id:"P4040",d:"D14",c:"D14.C7",dok:3,q:"A nurse is reviewing a medication administration record and notices an order for hydralazine 10 mg IV that could easily be misread as another look alike sound alike drug name. Which system level safety strategy is designed to reduce this type of error?",
 o:["Relying solely on memory of drug names to prevent confusion","Allowing abbreviations for all high alert medication names to save documentation time","Removing the medication name from the electronic order and using only a numeric code","Tall man lettering and standardized order sets that distinguish look alike sound alike medication names"],a:3,
 why:"Tall man lettering, such as capitalizing distinguishing letters, along with standardized order sets and alerts, is a system level strategy specifically designed to reduce confusion between look alike sound alike medication names. Relying on memory alone removes the systemic safety net that catches errors regardless of individual recall or fatigue. Using abbreviations for high alert medications actually increases the risk of misinterpretation and is generally discouraged by safety organizations. Removing the medication name entirely and replacing it with only a numeric code would eliminate an important visual and contextual check that helps staff verify the correct drug."}
];
